Items 2–4 pass; the inventory partition and its difference from Task 0's are recorded.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: the merge of Tasks 0–19.\n\n---\n\n## Task 21: post-merge tracer\n2100→\n**Production files**: none\n**Test files**: none\n**Task shape**: `operational` — it runs the gate, the check and the assembler that Tasks 5–10\nbuilt.\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-8\" step 3, spec FR-8 steps 2, 2a and 3, in Task 20's fixture project.\nStep 2: a real forbidden write through `hmad-dispatch exec agy` with the operator's global\n`~/.gemini/config/hooks.json` loaded is refused. Step 2a: a delay handler (matcher `*`, `timeout`\nT, sleeping at least 2T, then printing a `deny`) is added to the fixture's `.agents/hooks.json`,\n2110→with `shasum -a 256 .agents/hooks.json` recorded before; agy is told to write one named non-test\nfile; the file's existence, the wall time and agy's message are read independently; one outcome\nfrom step 2a's table (`timeout-blocks`, `timeout-allows`, `timeout-not-enforced`,\n`session-error`) is named; the delay handler is removed and the after-hash compared. Step 3: the\nRED/GREEN readings with re-run, revert and mutation results, and where agy's files landed. Every\nreading is committed under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, stamped with the sha and\n`agy --version`.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-8.1 (the real refusal; the RED/GREEN readings with re-run, revert and mutation results):\n2120→  committed and stamped.\n- [ ] AC-8.2: the step-2a reading names exactly one outcome consistent with its three readings,\n  shows the delay hook removed and the before and after hashes equal; `session-error` is re-run\n  and never counts. On `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD gate\" carries the fail-open\n  residual and the operator question is raised before the gate is called verified.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 20.\n\n---\n\n2130→## Task 22: post-merge adoption and the derived timeout\n\n**Production files**: `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`, `.agents/hooks.json` (new)\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py`\n**Task shape**: `new-behaviour`\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-8\" steps 4–5. This repository's `.agents/hooks.json` holding FR-5's\nexact entry is committed only after Task 21's refusal passed (`git check-ignore -v\n.agents/hooks.json` prints nothing at `4e4b5ded`, so no `git add -f` is needed; the file is absent\ntoday), and step 2 is re-run once here. The assembler's `TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS[\"agy\"]` becomes\n2140→`max(900, 3 × slower turn)` rounded up to a multiple of 60, over the readings Task 21 committed.\nThe committed probe `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr8_timeout_formula.py` recomputes\nthe figure from those readings and asserts the assembler's default equals it (a probe, not a suite\ntest, so archiving the probe directory cannot break the suite).\n\n**Tests**:\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_default_timeouts[agy-1500]` is re-pointed:\n   its id becomes `agy-` followed by the figure the formula probe prints, and its expected value\n   becomes that figure.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_agy_wired_writes_the_prompt` (Task 10 item 6)\n2150→   pins `--timeout 1500` in the command block. Its expected `--timeout` becomes the same figure.\n\nBoth expectations change in the RED commit. Task 10's `TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS[\"agy\"]` is still `1500`\nat RED, so both nodes fail there.\n\n**RED split**: when the derived figure is not 1500, 2 F: the re-pointed `test_agent_default_timeouts`\nid and `test_agent_agy_wired_writes_the_prompt`. When it is 1500, 0 F: this Task changes no code\nand no test, and records that.\n\n**Anchors**: the edit touches only the `\"agy\"` value of `TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS`, on which no row sits\n2160→(Task 10 adds `assemble_tdd_agy.json`, and none of its rows anchors on that value).\n`--check-anchors` over the 8 specs naming the assembler by then: Task 10's 7, plus\n`new_caller_carveout_copies.json`, whose `fr9-copy-L5` row names it (Task 13).\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-8.1 (the default timeout equals FR-8's formula over the readings): the formula probe\n  prints `PROBE: PASS`.\n- [ ] This repository's step-2 re-run is committed and stamped.\n- [ ] The feature is recorded COMPLETE only after AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 pass (spec D-13).\n\n2170→**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 21.\n\n---\n\n## Mutation-row ledger\n\nDesign v1.4 (`2cd5d7f1`) §\"Mutation rows (AC-X)\" lists 60 new rows, counted from its list: FR-1 3;\nFR-2 4 (3 `_cause` branches and the other-peer step); FR-3 4; FR-6 2; FR-4 19 (14 single bullets,\nv1.4's \"T2a removed\" among them, 3 kinds, 2 class-(c) `cwd` rows); FR-5 8 (7 reasons and `UNWIRED`\nreturning 2); FR-7 10 (3 append rows, 2 `APPEND_ONLY`, 5 warning and `property_valid` rows); FR-9\n2180→10 (8 + 2). 3 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 19 + 8 + 10 + 10 = 60.\n\n| Task | design rows | wire rows (cut + converse) | doc-pin rows (plan §\"Success Criteria\") | discipline and decision rows |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 4 (FR-1 3, FR-7 1) | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 2 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 3 | 4 | 3 (2 + 1) | 0 | 0 |\n| 4 | 2 | 0 | 0 | 2 (signal discipline, I4) |\n| 5 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 1 (signal discipline) |\n| 6 | 11 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n2190→| 7 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 9 | 7 | 0 | 0 | 1 (connection, I3) |\n| 10 | 1 | 2 (1 + 1) | 0 | 1 (marker discipline) |\n| 11 | 5 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 12 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 13 | 8 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 14 | 2 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 15 | 0 | 0 | 4 | 0 |\n| 16 | 0 | 0 | 8 | 0 |\n| 17 | 0 | 0 | 6 | 0 |\n2200→| total | 60 | 5 | 18 | 5 |\n\nDesign rows: 4 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 4 + 11 + 4 + 7 + 1 + 5 + 4 + 8 + 2 = 60, equal to the design's\nlist. Wire rows: 3 + 2 = 5. Doc-pin rows: 4 + 8 + 6 = 18. Discipline and connection rows:\n2 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5, none of them in the design's list (Task 4's two Codex self-check verdict rows\nand Task 9's budget-connection row from audit cycle 2, Task 5's signal-discipline and Task 10's\nmarker rows from cycle 1). OD-2's row is now a design row (design v1.4). All new rows:\n60 + 5 + 18 + 5 = 88. Existing rows\nchanged, not new: the 31 re-pointed in Task 4, of which `CX-FOLD` also takes a new `find` and\n`CX-FORCE-SCRIPT` a new `replace`; and the re-anchors of Tasks 2, 3 and 10 (`w10a-grok-by-default`\n2210→named; the others as `--check-anchors` finds them). Each Task states which existing rows its\nedits touch.\n\n## Decisions (closed)\n\n- **OD-1 (Task 17)**, closed 2026-10-16. Operator: option (A), a transcription test over the\n  written procedure. Spec FR-1 and design FR-7 stand unchanged.\n- **OD-2 (Task 6)**, closed 2026-10-16. Operator: a class-(b) target naming an existing directory\n  is refused with `kind=judge-error` under `S` (`active`, `unknown`) and allowed under `inactive`.\n  Task 6 implements it as T2a, with item 10 and row `od2-directory-target-check-dropped`. Spec v1.10\n2220→  (`c283bb63`) records it as D-16, design v1.4 (`2cd5d7f1`) as T2a.\n\nNo decision is open.\n\n## Residuals carried from the design\n\nEach stays where the design states it (design §\"Residuals\"); the Tasks above add no fixture that\nclaims to close one. The ones a Task touches: the `which` matrix (Task 3's regression guard); the\nverb reads the root only (Task 2); the class-(a) judgement and the six-line shell composition\n(Tasks 5, 7, 8); a sibling `overwrite`, the review-leg agy, worktree state (Task 21 observes, does\n2230→not settle); an equivalent command spelling (Task 9); a writer that bypasses `_mutate` (Task 11);\nprose application, a seventh surface (Tasks 13, 18); a later `KINDS` member (Task 5's kind scan\nreads `KINDS` live); T4's empty-string arm (Task 6); the author-before-review ordering (Task 17).\n\nAdded by this plan, not by the design:\n- OD-2's T2a covers an existing directory only. A target ending in `/` that names no existing\n  directory goes through T3–T7 as a file name (Task 6).\n- The staging step is prose that the orchestrator follows. Task 16's tests pin its words, not its\n  execution (the same residual as AC-7.3 under OD-1 (A)).\n\n2240→## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial implementation plan draft (2026-10-16), Phase 5a, from design v1.3 (4e4b5ded), spec v1.9 (4bef0e76), plan v1.6 (8e242d06); tree read at 4e4b5ded. 23 Tasks (0-19 pre-merge, 20-22 post-merge per D-13); wiring Tasks 3 and 10 with WIRE/WIRE-PIN; 59 design mutation rows plus 3 wire-cut and 14 doc-pin rows; OD-1 (AC-7.3 surface vs spec FR-1 \"no script reads it\") and OD-2 (directory target).\n- v1.1: Impl-plan audit cycle 1 (2026-10-16), answering codex p1 (9 must, 3 should) and agy p2 (2 must, 2 should) against v1.0 (fc6b497e), tree at 683777df. Grammar probe fr3_state_line_grammar.py updated (derived/shipped modes, --control, --hook) so Task 3's post-GREEN re-run passes. Harness cwd convention (scripts/ from h-mad/). agy --self-check: verdict FAILs exit 0, policy-import exit 2. Task 16 staging step with record=failed halt step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded and its discriminating control. Task 20 requires all six samples (5 class-(b) + run_command), RED split derived from captured keys, capture-only gate. Task 10 converse wire row, [H-MAD] halt marker. Task 3 converse wire row. Task 11, 9, 10 RED assertions on refusal text, not argparse exit 2. Task 2 status fixtures expect reason=status. Task 22 two timeout tests. Task 4 CX-FOLD find and CX-FORCE-SCRIPT replace. Task 15 FR-9 prompt bullets. hook_wiring.json 12 rows. OD-1 closed (A); OD-2 closed: directory target judge-error under S (Task 6 T2a). Rows: 59 design + 5 wire + 18 doc-pin + 3 discipline/decision = 85.\n- v1.2: Impl-plan audit cycle 2, final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16), answering codex p1 (4 must, 1 should; agy p2 clean) against v1.1 (fc92becf), tree at 84ec2133, design v1.4 (2cd5d7f1), spec v1.10 (c283bb63). I1: Task 6 item 1 gains number-inactive and list-inactive, the discriminators for fr4-class-b-target-presence-only (null-inactive does not discriminate: canonicalise treats a falsy target as the root); 34->35 nodes with the t2a-directory deny-row id. I2: Task 20 item 3 uses the root .venv interpreter under the captured workdir key, workdir-root and workdir-outside (ls cannot discriminate); 14 nodes. I3: Task 9 item 4 patches h_mad_tdd_judge.JUDGE_BUDGET_S to 41.1 (timeouts 61 and 62), check_agy reads the module attribute at call time, row fr5-timeout-floor-hardcoded-60; 18 nodes. I4: Task 4 fixes both Codex --self-check verdict FAILs (parser, shell-policy) to stdout with exit 0, policy-import keeps exit 2; no existing test pinned exit 2; 2 tests, 2 rows; Task 4 shape new-behaviour; residual removed. I5: Task 20 suite metadata in h-mad/tests/fixtures/agy-payloads/capture.json. Design v1.4 alignment: FR-4 19 design rows (T2a), deny table 17 rows, owed lines marked recorded. Rows: 60 design + 5 wire + 18 doc-pin + 5 discipline/connection = 88.\n\nPaired audited design: # Design: agy-coder-fallback\n\n> **Slug note.** The slug predates brainstorm v1.1. agy is a **peer** Phase-5 implementer with\n> Codex's standing, not a fallback (spec §\"Slug note\").\n\n2250→Sources: spec v1.6 (`c853b207`), plan v1.4 (`29ae3e7b`), brainstorm v1.1. Every \"currently\" claim\nbelow was read from the tree at `29ae3e7b`. `h-mad/` has not changed since `a8d9ba99`\n(`git diff --stat a8d9ba99 29ae3e7b -- h-mad | wc -l` prints `0`), so the spec's and plan's\n`h-mad/` readings still describe the code this design changes. Code is cited as `path` plus a\nsymbol, a heading or a content predicate, never a line number.\n\nv1.1 answers design audit cycle 1. Every premise v1.1 adds was re-read at `200ffd52`, where\n`h-mad/` is still unchanged (`git diff --stat 29ae3e7b 200ffd52 -- h-mad | wc -l` prints `0`).\n\nv1.2 is the final corrective revision after design audit cycle 2 (round cap reached; not\n2260→re-audited). It changes only what cycle 2 filed. Every premise it adds was read at `cff71b49`,\nwhere `h-mad/` is still unchanged (`git diff --stat 200ffd52 cff71b49 -- h-mad | wc -l` prints `0`).\n\nv1.3 records operator decision OD-E2 (option (a), a durable author record; spec v1.9 D-15) and changes only what it\nneeds: FR-1's fifth property, FR-7's author record and its readers, and the sections that list them.\nEvery premise it adds was read at `3ad735f7`, where `h-mad/` is still unchanged\n(`git diff --stat cff71b49 3ad735f7 -- h-mad | wc -l` prints `0`).\n\n## Executive Summary\n\n2270→The judge (`h-mad/scripts/h_mad_tdd_judge.py`) gains the one function that decides who authors a\nrecord, and it carries that decision to the Claude hook as a new **per-record** field on the\n`TDD-STATE:` line. The hook stops reading `PATH` and the Codex env var itself. The Codex gate's\nshell policy, its operator allowance, its phase-5 activity predicate and its production predicate\nmove into `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, the module both existing gates already share,\nand both the Codex gate and the new agy gate import them from there. A new\n`h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` gates agy's tools through a closed classification, so an unknown\ntool is denied. `h_mad_hook_wiring.py --agy` prints `AGY-GATE:`, and the assembler halts on\nanything but `WIRED`. The state writer gains a locked `--append`, and the record gains an append-only\n`implementation_authors` list that FR-7 attribution reads as a module's author set. Phase 7 warns on non-independent,\n2280→unrecorded and malformed review entries for every implementer. The FR-9 carve-out stays applied by\nprose, and one test binds its six copies to one canonical block.\n\n## Overview — the design obligations and where each is settled\n\n| Obligation | Decision | Section |\n|---|---|---|\n| DO-1 / DO-1a — `TDD-STATE:` shape | a fifth comma field per record, `codex\\|agy\\|peers-out\\|invalid:<pct>`, between the state file and the fallback tag; no header field moves or is added | §\"FR-3\" |\n| DO-2 — read-only allowlist | 11 names, checked against the 58-name `init.tools` inventory | §\"FR-4\" |\n| DO-3 — `review_independence` add | a writer operation, `--append KEY=JSON`, run inside the writer's existing locked read-modify-write | §\"FR-7\" |\n2290→| DO-4 — invalid `.ts` on both paths | a stdlib RFC 3339 predicate in `h_mad_state_validate.py`, applied outside the validator backend, scoped to `implementer_switch.ts` | §\"FR-1\" |\n| DO-5 — the moved names | 17 functions and 8 constants move into `h_mad_target_identity.py`; the Codex gate imports every one of them by name | §\"FR-6\" |\n| DO-6 — installed path vs worktree | no override in code; tests point `HOME` at a fixture; FR-8 runs after the merge to main | §\"FR-5\", §\"FR-8\" |\n| DO-7 — where the carve-out is applied | it stays applied by prose; the corpus checks the rule's text; one equivalence test with six per-copy locators | §\"FR-9\" |\n\nThe two operator questions v1.0 raised are decided (§\"Operator decisions\"): the shared policy\nlives in `h_mad_target_identity.py` (OQ-A, spec D-12), and FR-8 is a post-merge stage, with the\nfeature recorded COMPLETE only after it (OQ-B, spec D-13). OD-E2, raised by v1.2, is decided as a\ndurable author record (spec D-15, §\"FR-7\").\n\n2300→## Architecture Overview\n\n```\n                  docs/.bkit-memory.json (the root's chain)\n                                │\n             h_mad_tdd_judge.py ── resolve_implementer(record)   (FR-2, the only resolver)\n               │        │                 │\n     `state` verb   `implementer` verb   in-process: read_chain / judge","absolute_path":"/Users/kimhawk/.grok/sessions/%2FUsers%2Fkimhawk%2F.local%2Fstate%2Fh-mad%2Fgrok-audit-surface%2Frun-ab2c85dc2e25-20261024T132452Z%2Froots%2Fgas-root-d9-s2v1-oesswhhq/01a16e92-2377-7a81-9b1e-b1c30274d035/prompts/prompt_0.txt","offset":1308,"limit":1000,"raw_output":"9. `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_docstring_drops_the_any_halt_rule`. 1 node, F.\n10. `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent` (existing,\n    extended for AC-9.1): the WIRED fixture is provisioned in its `tmp_path` root once before the\n    loop; the loop becomes `(\"codex\", \"grok\", \"agy\")`; the assertion becomes\n    `len(hashes) == 3 and len(set(hashes)) == 1` (today it asserts `hashes[0] == hashes[1]`).\n    1 node, F.\n\n**RED split**: 18 nodes; 16 F, 2 P (1 + 7 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 3 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 18). The WIRE-PIN\nfails on an assertion that the check was not run; items 2–10 fail for the absent `agy` choice,\ntable and docstring, which is new behaviour.\n\n**Regression guards**: `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_grok_explicit_timeout_wins`\n(the test of row `t1-timeout-judged-by-value`),\n`tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_grok_block_names_exec_grok_with_1500` (row\n`wr3-assembler-drops-agent`),\n`tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py::TestCli::test_a_clean_assembly_prints_pass_and_the_command_block`\n(row `w10a-grok-by-default`), and the rest of `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`.\n\n**Mutation rows** — new spec `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/assemble_tdd_agy.json`, 4 rows:\n| name | mutant | test |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fr5-unwired-exits-2` | the `UNWIRED` verdict branch returns 2 | `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_agy_unwired_halts_with_exit_0` |\n| `wire-check-not-run` (wire-cut) | the check subprocess call deleted, the check and `assemble` kept | `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_agy_runs_the_wiring_check_before_assembling` |\n| `wire-check-runs-for-every-agent` (converse) | the `args.agent == \"agy\"` condition on the check run replaced by `True` | `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_grok_block_names_exec_grok_with_1500` |\n| `halt-marker-dropped` (marker discipline) | the `[H-MAD]` marker print removed from the `UNWIRED` branch | `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_agy_unwired_halts_with_exit_0` |\n\n**Both directions.** `wire-check-not-run` cuts the wire, and `wire-check-runs-for-every-agent`\nforces it. The converse's killer is an existing regression guard. It runs\n`run_cli(..., \"--agent\", \"grok\")` with `root = tmp_path`, which holds no `.agents/hooks.json`. Under\nthe mutant the real check runs, prints `AGY-GATE: UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file` (FR-5's first\nreason, whatever `HOME` holds), and the assembler halts with no command block, so the test's\n`hmad-dispatch exec grok` assertion fails.\n`tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_codex_default_timeout_is_900` fails the same\nway for codex. Neither test changes, so the RED counts do not move.\n\n**Anchors (E7)**: `assemble_tdd_agent.json` `w10a-grok-by-default` is re-anchored in this Task:\n`find` `ap.add_argument(\"--agent\", choices=(\"codex\", \"grok\", \"agy\"), default=\"codex\")`, `replace`\nthe same with `default=\"grok\"`, `test` unchanged. `t1-timeout-judged-by-value` (`find`\n`args.timeout is not None`) and `wr3-assembler-drops-agent` (`find` `        agent=args.agent,`)\nneed no re-anchor if the line forms above are kept. No other row among the 60 naming the\nassembler holds the docstring line (`2 on any HALT`), the `--agent` line or the timeout line, so\nthese three are the only existing rows this Task's edits touch. `--check-anchors` over the 6 specs naming the\nassembler (`assemble_tdd.json`, `assemble_tdd_agent.json`, `assemble_tdd_env_known.json`,\n`assemble_tdd_mutation_rows.json`, `fill_slots.json`, `grok_wire_reverts.json`) plus\n`assemble_tdd_agy.json`.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-5.1 (`--agent agy` halts with no prompt on each reason): item 2 passes.\n- [ ] AC-5.2 (the assembler's half of each row, token and exit asserted separately; a mutant\n  returning 2 on `UNWIRED` killed): items 2–6 pass and `fr5-unwired-exits-2` is caught.\n- [ ] AC-9.1 (`test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent` covers `codex`, `grok`, `agy`): item 10\n  passes.\n- [ ] The WIRE-PIN fails when only the wire is cut (`wire-check-not-run`), and the codex/grok\n  regression guards fail when the wire is forced (`wire-check-runs-for-every-agent`).\n- [ ] Marker discipline: every new halt path prints the `[H-MAD]` marker after its token, as\n  items 1–5 assert; `halt-marker-dropped` is caught.\n- [ ] AC-X: `assemble_tdd_agy.json` → `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`; `ANCHORS: ANCHORS_OK` over the 7\n  assembler specs.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 9.\n\n---\n\n## Task 11: the writer's `--append`\n\n**Production files**: `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_write.py`\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py`\n**Task shape**: `new-behaviour`\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-7\", \"The add\". `--append KEY=JSON` is repeatable and decoded with\n`json.loads`, no string fallback (a failure prints the writer's `ERROR:` line, exit 2). `--set` fields and\n`--append` entries apply inside one `_mutate` call through `merge_fields`, which gains an\n`appends` keyword. `apply` keeps its existing statements byte for byte (`record =\n{**records[feature], **fields}`, then the owner-heartbeat `if` and its assignment) and runs the\nappends after them, each reading `current = record.get(key, [])` from the updated `record`. A key\nnamed by both `--set` and `--append` is refused (`--set and --append name the same key: <key>`).\n`APPEND_ONLY = (\"review_independence\", \"implementation_authors\")`; a key of `fields` in it raises\n`StateWriteError` (`<key> is append-only: use --append`) in a check at the top of `merge_fields`,\nbefore `def apply`.\nThe `STATE-WRITE: OK` line lists appended keys with set keys.\n\n**Code structure**:\n```python\nAPPEND_ONLY = (\"review_independence\", \"implementation_authors\")\ndef merge_fields(state_file: Path, feature: str, fields: dict[str, Any], *,\n                 session_id: str | None = None, now: str | None = None,\n                 appends: list[tuple[str, Any]] | None = None) -> dict: ...\n```\n\n**Tests** (in `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py`, new class `TestAppend`, using the file's\n`run` helper):\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_append_keeps_both_earlier_entries` — a\n   record holding two `review_independence` entries; one append; both earlier entries compared as\n   `json.dumps` text.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_two_appends_in_one_call_keep_order`.\n3. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_set_and_append_on_the_same_key_are_refused`\n   — the key is `production_paths_needing_red_tests` (a schema `array` of strings, not in\n   `APPEND_ONLY`, so the `APPEND_ONLY` check cannot refuse it first and mask a removed same-key\n   guard): `--set production_paths_needing_red_tests=[] --append production_paths_needing_red_tests=[\"a\"]`.\n   Asserts stderr carries the exact diagnostic\n   `--set and --append name the same key: production_paths_needing_red_tests`, stderr does not carry\n   `unrecognized arguments`, exit 2, and the store is byte-identical. Read at `4e4b5ded` on a\n   scratch store: the current writer exits 2 with\n   `error: unrecognized arguments: --append production_paths_needing_red_tests=[\"a\"]` and leaves the\n   store unchanged. So a key-and-exit-only assertion passes at RED, and the exact diagnostic is\n   what makes this item fail there.\n4. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_append_only_key_refuses_set` —\n   `review_independence`, `implementation_authors`, each alone: `--set <key>=[]` exits 2 with the\n   append-only message and the store unchanged. 2 nodes.\n5. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_append_only_refusal_applies_to_library_callers`\n   — `merge_fields` raises `StateWriteError`.\n6. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_append_value_is_strict_json` — a value that\n   is not JSON; stderr starts with the writer's `ERROR: ` line naming the `--append` item and does\n   not carry `unrecognized arguments`; exit 2; store unchanged.\n7. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_append_to_non_array_refused` — stderr\n   carries `cannot append to a non-array value`; exit 2; store unchanged.\n8. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_refused_entry_leaves_store_byte_identical` —\n   `review-independence-bad-leg`, `implementation-authors-gemini`. Each id asserts that stderr\n   carries the writer's schema refusal (`would not validate`, from `_refusal`) and not\n   `unrecognized arguments`, that the exit is 2, and that the store is byte-identical. 2 nodes.\n\nItems 3 and 6–8 each assert the refusal's own text. At RED every `--append` run exits 2 on\nargparse's `unrecognized arguments` with the store unchanged, so the exit and the store alone\nwould pass there.\n9. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_ok_line_lists_appended_keys`.\n10. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_owner_append_refreshes_the_heartbeat`.\n11. `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_append_implementation_authors_entry`.\n\n**RED split**: 13 nodes, 13 F, 0 P (1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1). Items 3 and 6–8\nassert the refusal's message. Item 4 runs against Task 1's schema, which accepts\n`--set review_independence=[]` at RED. So none of them passes at RED on argparse's own refusal.\n\n**Regression guards**: the rest of `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py`, including\n`tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestHeartbeatBeats::test_a_set_by_the_owner_refreshes_the_heartbeat`\nand `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestHeartbeatBeats::test_a_set_by_a_bystander_does_not_manufacture_liveness`.\n\n**Mutation rows** — new spec `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/state_write_append.json`, 5 rows:\n| name | mutant | test |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fr7-append-from-empty` | each appended value built from `[]` | `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_append_keeps_both_earlier_entries` |\n| `fr7-append-reads-prior` | accumulation reads `prior[key]` instead of the updated `record` | `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_two_appends_in_one_call_keep_order` |\n| `fr7-same-key-refusal-dropped` | the same-key `--set`/`--append` refusal removed | `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_set_and_append_on_the_same_key_are_refused` |\n| `fr7-append-only-drops-review-independence` | `review_independence` removed from `APPEND_ONLY` | `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_append_only_key_refuses_set[review_independence]` |\n| `fr7-append-only-drops-implementation-authors` | `implementation_authors` removed from `APPEND_ONLY` | `tests/test_h_mad_state_write.py::TestAppend::test_append_only_key_refuses_set[implementation_authors]` |\n\n**Anchors (E7)**: `heartbeat_beat.json` `the-owners-set-no-longer-beats` and\n`a-bystanders-set-beats-too` anchor on `merge_fields`' owner-heartbeat `if` and its assignment,\nwhich stay byte for byte, so no re-anchor. These are the only rows inside `merge_fields`. The\nwriter's other rows sit in the nested `apply` functions of the beat, release, lane and\ndrop-undeclared paths, and none of them sits in `main`'s argument parsing. `--check-anchors` over the 4 specs naming the writer\n(`heartbeat_beat.json`, `lane_liveness.json`, `release_ownership_guard.json`,\n`state_undeclared_keys.json`) plus `state_write_append.json`.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-7.1 (an add keeps both earlier entries; a refused entry leaves the store unchanged):\n  items 1, 2 and 8 pass.\n- [ ] AC-1.3 (a refused `--append implementation_authors=` entry leaves the store byte-identical):\n  item 8 `implementation-authors-gemini` passes.\n- [ ] Design `APPEND_ONLY` fixtures: item 4, once per key, passes.\n- [ ] AC-X: `state_write_append.json` → `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`; `ANCHORS: ANCHORS_OK` over the 5\n  writer specs.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 1.\n\n---\n\n## Task 12: the Phase-7 review-independence warnings\n\n**Production files**: `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py`\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py`\n**Task shape**: `new-behaviour`\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-7\", \"The warnings\". `check` (today `def check(record, analysis_path,\nspec_dirs=None, …)`, returning `{\"ready\": not blockers, \"blockers\": …, \"warnings\": …}`) calls a\nnew `_review_independence_warnings(record)` and extends `warnings`; it never touches `blockers`.\nWell-formed is `schema_property_valid(\"review_independence\", [entry])` and `entry[\"independent\"] ==\n(entry[\"reviewer\"] != entry[\"implementer\"])`. `review_independence_malformed` per malformed entry\nand once for a non-list field; `review_non_independent` per well-formed `independent=false` entry,\nnaming leg, module and agent; `review_independence_unrecorded` when `record.get(\"archreview\")` is in\n`{\"READY_TO_MERGE\", \"WITH_FIXES\", \"NO\"}` and no well-formed entry has `leg == \"6a-prime\"`, with no\nimplementer condition, reading neither `implementer`, `implementer_switch` nor\n`implementation_authors`; its detail names the sorted distinct `implementer` values of the\nwell-formed 5e entries, or `authors=unrecorded`. `main`'s existing `  WARNING <code>: <detail>`\nloop prints them.\n\n**Tests** (in `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py`, new class\n`TestReviewIndependence`):\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_non_independent_entry_warns`. F.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_malformed_entry_warns` —\n   `bad-leg`, `lying-boolean`, `field-not-a-list`. 3 nodes, F.\n3. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_unrecorded_fires_for_each_implementer`\n   — `codex-field-absent`, `codex`, `agy` (through `implementer`), `grok`, `claude` (through\n   `implementer_switch.to`). 5 nodes, F.\n4. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_unrecorded_fires_for_each_archreview_value`\n   — `READY_TO_MERGE`, `WITH_FIXES`, `NO`. 3 nodes, F.\n5. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_unrecorded_is_quiet` —\n   `has-6a-prime-entry`, `archreview-absent`, `SKIPPED_NO_PANE`, `SKIPPED_OPERATOR_OVERRIDE`.\n   4 nodes, P (the warning does not exist at RED; these pin its silence at GREEN).\n6. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_malformed_6a_prime_entry_is_not_recorded`\n   — both warnings. F.\n7. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_unrecorded_detail_names_entry_authors_not_routing`\n   — `implementer_switch.to == \"agy\"`, one well-formed 5e entry with `implementer` `codex`; the\n   detail names `codex` and not `agy`. F.\n8. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_unrecorded_detail_without_5e_entries_says_unrecorded`. F.\n9. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_warnings_never_move_ready`. P.\n10. `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_warning_lines_print_through_main`. F.\n\n**RED split**: 21 nodes; 16 F, 5 P (1 + 3 + 5 + 3 + 4 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 21; P = 4 in item 5\nand item 9).\n\n**Mutation rows** — new spec `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/phase7_review_independence.json`, 4 rows:\n| name | mutant | test |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fr7-consistency-check-dropped` | the `independent` consistency check removed | `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_malformed_entry_warns` |\n| `fr7-unrecorded-agy-only` | the trigger restricted to `implementer == \"agy\"` | `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_unrecorded_fires_for_each_implementer` |\n| `fr7-malformed-6a-prime-counted` | a malformed 6a-prime entry counted as recorded | `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_malformed_6a_prime_entry_is_not_recorded` |\n| `fr7-detail-reads-switch` | the detail text reads `implementer_switch.to` | `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py::TestReviewIndependence::test_unrecorded_detail_names_entry_authors_not_routing` |\n\n**Anchors**: `--check-anchors` over `phase7_analysis_anchor.json`,\n`phase7_preconditions_gates.json` and `phase7_review_independence.json`. Existing rows touched:\nnone. The call is added immediately before `check`'s line starting `return {\"ready\": not blockers`,\non which no row sits. The 4 rows inside `check` and the 3 inside `main` are not on the edited\nlines.\n\n**Regression guards** (D5d-5, `acf_5d_task12_decisions.md`): five existing\n`test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` tests assert no warnings or the exact pre-FR-7 set on the\nshared `READY` record, which carries no `review_independence`; they read a `REVIEWED` fixture\n(a recorded independent 6a-prime entry) so FR-7's `review_independence_unrecorded` does not fire.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-7.1 (each warning from its own fixture; none moves `ready`): items 1, 2, 3 and 9 pass.\n- [ ] AC-7.2: items 3, 4 and 5 pass.\n- [ ] Design switch/recovery fixture: item 7 passes.\n- [ ] AC-X: `phase7_review_independence.json` → `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 1 (`property_valid`).\n\n---\n\n## Task 13: the six carve-out copies\n\n**Production files**: `h-mad/references/codex-implementer-prompt.md`, `h-mad/SKILL.md`, `h-mad/references/failure-recovery.md`, `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_new_caller_carveout_copies.py` (new)\n**Task shape**: `new-behaviour`\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-9\". The canonical block (spec FR-9's quoted block, three sentences)\nis appended after six context sentences, each occurring once in its file at `4e4b5ded` (Python\n`str.count`): prompt RED item 1 after `Say which case each failing test is.`; the prompt's WIRE-PIN\nparagraph after `report that and stop rather than accepting it.`; the SKILL.md 5d bullet after\n``halt `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>`.``; the `failure-recovery.md` 5d row inside the remedy\ncell's quotes after `only the failure mode can.`; `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` `assemble()` inside the\nwiring branch's `lines.append(...)` holding `The WIRE-PIN is this task's single load-bearing test.`\n(the literal `\"symbol.\"` becomes `\"symbol. \"` and the block follows as adjacent literals;\n`never a missing` stays); the prompt's GREEN paragraph after `keeping tests green.`.\n\nThe test holds `CANONICAL` once and compares `collapse(span).count(collapse(CANONICAL)) == 1` with\n`collapse(s) = \" \".join(s.split())`, through the design's six locators L1–L6. Each locator raises\nwhen its anchor matches zero spans or more than one. All six locators select exactly one span on\nthe `4e4b5ded` tree, and each span contains its context sentence (checked for this plan with\nscratch code since deleted).\n\n**Tests** (all in `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_new_caller_carveout_copies.py`):\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_new_caller_carveout_copies.py::test_copy_carries_the_canonical_block` — `L1`\n   … `L6`. 6 nodes, F.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_new_caller_carveout_copies.py::test_locator_refuses_zero_or_many_spans` — each\n   locator × `zero` and `two`, over doctored copies of its source text. 12 nodes, P (they exercise\n   the test's own locators, which exist at RED).\n\n**RED split**: 18 nodes; 6 F, 12 P.\n\n**Regression guards (doc-pin census)**:\n`tests/test_h_mad_agent_definitions.py::test_implementer_scopes_the_import_error_red_rule_to_wiring_tasks`\n(RED item 1's sentences), `tests/test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py` (`HALT_TOKENS`, the\nwiring-bullet anchor and `failure mode per test, not only the count`),\n`tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py::TestWiringShape::test_the_pin_is_told_what_its_red_must_be`\n(`never a missing`), and every other census pin on the prompt. Expected pins moved: none.\n\n**Mutation rows** — new spec `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/new_caller_carveout_copies.json`, 8 rows\nhere, each with `symbol` (the enclosing heading for a `.md` file, `assemble` for the `.py` file)\nand `test` `tests/test_h_mad_new_caller_carveout_copies.py::test_copy_carries_the_canonical_block`,\neach run alone:\n- 6 per-copy rows (`fr9-copy-L1` … `fr9-copy-L6`): `valid **only** when` → `valid when` in that\n  copy, with a `find` that starts at the copy's context sentence; the `L5` row's `find` is written\n  against the committed source bytes, including the adjacent-literal split.\n- 2 per-sentence rows on the `failure-recovery.md` copy: `fr9-L4-sentence-2` deletes\n  `The RED report must name both. `; `fr9-L4-sentence-3` changes `delete the call only, keep the\n  caller and the callee` to `delete the callee only, keep the caller`.\n\n**Anchors**: `--check-anchors` over every spec naming `SKILL.md` (16 specs, 57 rows at\n`4e4b5ded`), `references/failure-recovery.md` (`archreview_done_marker_recovery.json`) and the 7\nassembler specs. Existing rows touched: none. No row's `find` holds any of the six context\nsentences, the `\"symbol.\"` literal or `never a missing`. No row names\n`references/codex-implementer-prompt.md`. The 2 `failure-recovery.md` rows sit outside the 5d row.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-9.1 (the rule on all six surfaces; the three pin files green): item 1 and the\n  regression guards pass.\n- [ ] AC-9.4: item 1 passes and the 8 rows → `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`, each run alone.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 10 (both edit `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`).\n\n---\n\n## Task 14: the FR-9 differential corpus\n\n**Production files**: none\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_wire_pin_new_caller_corpus.py` (new)\n**Task shape**: `gate` — a verdict over the rule text Task 13 placed; it writes no production file.\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-9\", \"The differential corpus\". Five cases (`new-caller`,\n`missing-callee`, `both-missing`, `unrelated-import`, `other-symbol`), each a fixture repository in\n`tmp_path` with a real pytest RED run by `sys.executable`. Callee resolution is executed (a\nsubprocess runs `import` and `getattr` at the RED base), never parsed. `old_rule` and `new_rule`\ntranscribe the two texts. The spec's AC-9.2 names four cases; the fifth is the plan's, and the pass\ncondition is over all five. The module exposes `run_corpus(base: Path) -> list[dict]`, which the\ncommitted probe `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr9_corpus_reading.py` imports from\n`h-mad/tests` to print the cases' exception lines and verdicts, stamped with the sha; the probe\ncarries one reading.\n\n**Tests**:\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_wire_pin_new_caller_corpus.py::test_corpus_case_red_is_real` — one id per case;\n   the exception line names the declared missing symbol, and callee resolution is recorded.\n   5 nodes.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_wire_pin_new_caller_corpus.py::test_changed_verdicts_are_exactly_new_caller`. 1 node.\n3. `tests/test_h_mad_wire_pin_new_caller_corpus.py::test_transcription_is_tied_to_the_canonical_words`\n   — `CANONICAL`, imported from the equivalence test, contains `the missing symbol is the caller\n   the task creates` and `the callee resolves at the RED base`. 1 node.\n\n**RED split**: 7 nodes, 0 F, 7 P (5 + 1 + 1). A failure is a finding against Task 13's text or the\ntranscription, not something to fix here.\n\n**Mutation rows** — `new_caller_carveout_copies.json`, 2 rows here (`file`\n`tests/test_h_mad_wire_pin_new_caller_corpus.py`, `test`\n`tests/test_h_mad_wire_pin_new_caller_corpus.py::test_changed_verdicts_are_exactly_new_caller`,\neach run alone): `fr9-corpus-drops-callee-condition` (then `both-missing` changes) and\n`fr9-corpus-drops-caller-condition` (then `other-symbol` and `unrelated-import` change). The spec\nthen holds 8 + 2 = 10 rows.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-9.2: items 1–2 pass; the changed set is exactly `{new-caller}`.\n- [ ] The probe prints its reading and is committed with one reading at one sha.\n- [ ] AC-X: `new_caller_carveout_copies.json` (10 rows) → `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 13.\n\n---\n\n## Task 15: the implementer-neutral prompt\n\n**Production files**: `h-mad/references/codex-implementer-prompt.md`\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_implementer_neutral_prompt.py` (new)\n**Task shape**: `new-behaviour`\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-10\", \"The prompt\". The opening sentence (`You are Codex implementing\nmodule …` at `4e4b5ded`, pinned by no test: `git ls-files 'h-mad/tests/*.py' | xargs grep -ln\n'You are Codex implementing'` prints nothing) names \"the Phase-5 implementer\" and no implementer\nby name (spec FR-9, first bullet). Line 9 at `4e4b5ded` reads ``You are Codex implementing module\n`<INLINE_MODULE_NAME>` for feature `<INLINE_FEATURE>`.``. Its two template slots stay.\n\nThe one `Hook: ` line (line 19 at `4e4b5ded`) names each gate by implementer, as spec FR-9's\nsecond bullet requires:\n- Codex: `h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py`, guarding `apply_patch` and shell writes;\n- agy: `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py`. For agy the line states that the class-(b) tools are how writes are\n  made, naming all five: `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content`, `multi_replace_file_content`,\n  `sed_file`, `notebook_edit` (design §\"FR-4\" class (b));\n- grok: no write-time gate, in those words;\n- Claude: `h-mad-tdd-gate.sh`, guarding `Write` and `Edit` (kept from today's line).\nThe line keeps every token `test_hook_line_names_both_gates` pins (`h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py`,\n`apply_patch`, `shell`, `h-mad-tdd-gate.sh`, `Write`, `Edit`). It introduces no `<`, `>` or `->` on\nthe line or its bullets, so the spec's `→` is written as a colon or as words. The filename stays\n(plan \"Tradeoffs kept\").\n\n**Tests**:\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_implementer_neutral_prompt.py::test_prompt_opening_names_no_implementer` — the\n   first line starting `You are` contains `the Phase-5 implementer` and none of `Codex`, `agy`,\n   `grok`, `Claude` (case-insensitive). F.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_implementer_neutral_prompt.py::test_hook_line_names_the_agy_gate` — the `Hook: `\n   line names `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py`. F.\n3. `tests/test_h_mad_implementer_neutral_prompt.py::test_hook_line_names_the_agy_write_tools` — the\n   `Hook: ` line contains `agy` and each of the five class-(b) names. F.\n4. `tests/test_h_mad_implementer_neutral_prompt.py::test_hook_line_says_grok_has_no_write_time_gate`\n   — the `Hook: ` line contains `grok` and `no write-time gate`. F.\n\nEach test reads the `Hook: ` line through `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_docs._hook_block`'s rule (the one\nline starting `Hook: `), so a clause moved onto a bullet fails items 2–4.\n\n**RED split**: 4 nodes, 4 F.\n\n**Regression guards (doc-pin census, the prompt)**: `tests/test_h_mad_tdd_gate_docs.py`\n(`_hook_block`: exactly one `Hook: ` line; `test_hook_line_names_both_gates`;\n`test_hook_bullets_state_the_shipped_rule`; both forbid `<` and `>`),\n`tests/test_h_mad_extract_verdict.py::test_both_codex_prompts_spell_the_label`,\n`tests/test_h_mad_portable_timeout.py`, and the 23 literals across the 8 files the census lists.\nExpected pins moved: none.\n\n**Mutation rows** — new spec `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/implementer_neutral_prompt.json`, 4 rows\n(doc pins, plan §\"Success Criteria\"), each with `symbol` the prompt's enclosing heading:\n- `fr10-prompt-opening-names-codex` (the opening restored to `You are Codex implementing`) →\n  `tests/test_h_mad_implementer_neutral_prompt.py::test_prompt_opening_names_no_implementer`;\n- `fr10-hook-line-drops-agy-gate` (the `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` name deleted) →\n  `tests/test_h_mad_implementer_neutral_prompt.py::test_hook_line_names_the_agy_gate`;\n- `fr10-hook-line-drops-agy-write-tools` (the class-(b) clause deleted) →\n  `tests/test_h_mad_implementer_neutral_prompt.py::test_hook_line_names_the_agy_write_tools`;\n- `fr10-hook-line-drops-grok` (the grok clause deleted) →\n  `tests/test_h_mad_implementer_neutral_prompt.py::test_hook_line_says_grok_has_no_write_time_gate`.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-9.1 and spec FR-9 first bullet (the opening names \"the Phase-5 implementer\" and no\n  implementer): item 1 passes.\n- [ ] Spec FR-9 second bullet and FR-10 (the `Hook:` line: Codex, agy with its class-(b) tools,\n  grok with no write-time gate): items 2–4 and the regression guards pass.\n- [ ] The 4 rows → `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 13 (both edit the prompt).\n\n---\n\n## Task 16: SKILL.md authorship surfaces and `agy-runtime.md`\n\n**Production files**: `h-mad/SKILL.md`, `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md`\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py` (new)\n**Task shape**: `new-behaviour`\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-10\". SKILL.md §\"Codex authors Phase 5 — enforced, not just\ninstructed\" becomes implementer-neutral, states FR-2 and FR-3, and names `agy-authorship`,\n`implementer-invalid` and `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer`; the heading and the sentence\n`Phase 5 implementation is **Codex's job**` stay (or `_phase_5e_text` and `PHASE5_HEADING` move in\nthe same commit). It also states the re-run trigger: after any agy upgrade, re-run FR-8 step 2 and\nstep 2a and re-take the class-(a) inventory. §\"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e (`hmad-dispatch exec`)\n— default for one-shot\" gains a line holding both `exec agy` and \"implement\", plus the\n`TDD-IMPLEMENTER:` staging step below. The teammate-audit bullet starting\n`- **It does not license Claude to write Phase 5 production code` separates audit-leg selection\nfrom resolved authorship, keeping its anchor prefix and the tokens `ACTIVE`, `every`,\n`unavailable` and `exhausted`. `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD gate\" names `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` and\n`AGY-GATE:` and keeps the halt. Its two `SessionStart` surfaces (2 matching lines at `4e4b5ded`,\none under §\"Hooks\" and one in the §\"Construct mapping\" `hook-event` row) are re-checked against\nthe installed agy (`agy --version` prints `1.4.0` at `4e4b5ded`; its bundled `hooks.md` has 3\nlines naming `SessionStart`) and corrected together, stamping that version.\n\n**The staging step** (spec FR-2 \"CLI verb\", design §\"FR-2\" \"The `implementer` verb\"). It is one\nbullet that starts `- **Stage the implementer.**`, which occurs once in SKILL.md after this Task\nand nowhere at `4e4b5ded`. It states:\n- the command, run before every 5d and 5e dispatch, from the project root:\n  `python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer --root . --feature \"$FEATURE\" --record`.\n  Read the `TDD-IMPLEMENTER:` token, never `$?`, because the verb always exits 0;\n- a line that ends ` record=failed`, whatever comes before it: **do not dispatch.** Halt\n  `step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded` and print the marker\n  `[H-MAD] <feature> halt step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded`, because a switch that is not in\n  state is the silent switch the operator decision forbids (spec FR-2). This branch is checked\n  first, before the token's other fields are read;\n- `switch=none`: stage `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --agent` with the resolved peer (`codex` or `agy`);\n- a peer switch (`switch=codex->agy` or `switch=agy->codex`, with `reason=`): stage the resolved\n  peer, and print the token line on the dispatch;\n- `switch=peers-out`: today's fallback path (grok or Claude), unchanged;\n- `invalid field=`: fix the record and never dispatch. Halt `step5:implementer_invalid` and print\n  its `[H-MAD]` marker;\n- `no-record` or `unreadable`: halt `step5:implementer_unresolved` and print its `[H-MAD]`\n  marker. The feature name or root is wrong, or the state file cannot be read.\nThe names `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved` originated in this plan;\nspec v1.10 (`c283bb63`) FR-2 and design v1.4 (`2cd5d7f1`) §\"FR-2\" adopt them.\n\n**Tests** (all in `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py`; section locators are\n`docsections.titled_section`, and the bullet locator is `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_docs.py`\n`_skill_gate_bullet`'s split):\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py::test_phase5_authorship_section_names_the_resolver_and_agy_authorship`. F.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py::test_phase5_authorship_section_states_the_agy_upgrade_trigger`. F.\n3. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py::test_exit_code_dispatch_section_lists_exec_agy_as_an_implementer`\n   — a line holding `exec agy` and, case-insensitively, `implement`; and `TDD-IMPLEMENTER:`. F.\n4. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py::test_teammate_bullet_separates_audit_selection_from_authorship`\n   — names `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer` and `agy-authorship`. F.\n5. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py::test_agy_runtime_tdd_gate_names_the_agy_gate_and_keeps_the_halt`\n   — `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate`, `AGY-GATE` and `step5:agy_tdd_hook_unverified`. F.\n6. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py::test_agy_runtime_sessionstart_claim_is_rechecked_and_stamped`\n   — neither surface still says there is no `SessionStart`, and both stamp `1.4.0`. F.\n7. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py::test_staging_step_runs_the_verb_with_record_and_names_every_branch`\n   — the bullet starting `- **Stage the implementer.**` (exactly one) contains\n   `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer`, `--record`, one sentence holding both `never` and `$?`, and\n   each branch token: `record=failed`, `switch=none`, `switch=peers-out`, `invalid field=`,\n   `no-record`, `unreadable`. F.\n8. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py::test_staging_step_halts_on_record_failed_before_any_dispatch`\n   — runs the module's `record_failed_branch_refuses_dispatch(bullet)` on the staging bullet and\n   asserts `True`. That function takes the branch span: the text from the bullet's first\n   `record=failed` to the first `switch=none` after it, with whitespace collapsed. A bullet where\n   `switch=none` does not follow `record=failed` returns `False`, so the `record=failed` branch must\n   be listed first. The span must hold `do not dispatch` (case-insensitive),\n   `step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded`, `[H-MAD]` and `first`. F.\n9. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_authorship_docs.py::test_record_failed_check_rejects_a_procedure_that_dispatches`\n   — the discriminating control. `record_failed_branch_refuses_dispatch` returns `False` on four\n   doctored bullets: one whose `record=failed` branch says to stage the resolved peer and\n   dispatch; one with the halt sentence deleted; one that keeps the halt but drops `[H-MAD]`; and\n   one that lists the `record=failed` branch after `switch=none`. P (it exercises the test's own\n   function, which exists at RED).\n\n**RED split**: 9 nodes, 8 F, 1 P (item 9).\n\n**Regression guards (doc-pin census)**: `tests/test_grok_fallback_docs.py` (`PHASE5_HEADING`,\n`TEAMMATE_HEADING`, the exit-code heading and tokens),\n`tests/test_h_mad_mutation_harness.py::test_skill_documents_the_precheck_is_automatic`\n(`_phase_5e_text`'s two `str.index` anchors), `tests/test_h_mad_tdd_gate_docs.py` (the anchor\nsentence and tokens, `_skill_gate_bullet`, §\"The TDD gate\" tokens, `rc 0`, no `exit-code protocol`),\n`tests/test_h_mad_context_budget_docs.py` (`hmad-dispatch exec agy`), `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n(`hmad-dispatch exec codex`). Expected pins moved: none.\n\n**Mutation rows** — new spec `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/agy_authorship_docs.json`, 8 rows (doc\npins), one per F item, each deleting the pinned sentence: `fr10-authorship-section`,\n`fr10-upgrade-trigger`, `fr10-exit-code-exec-agy`, `fr10-teammate-bullet`,\n`fr10-agy-runtime-tdd-gate`, `fr10-sessionstart-stamp`, `fr10-staging-drops-record-flag` (the\n`--record` flag deleted from the staging command) and `fr10-staging-dispatches-after-record-failed`\n(the `record=failed` halt sentence replaced by \"stage the resolved peer\"). Each `test` is its\nitem's node ID, items 1–8 in that order.\n\n**Anchors**: `--check-anchors` over the 16 specs naming `SKILL.md`; `agy-runtime.md` has no rows.\nExisting rows touched: none. No SKILL.md row's `find` lies in §\"Codex authors Phase 5\", in\n§\"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e\" or in the teammate-audit bullet (sweep at `4e4b5ded`, by offset\nspan).\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] Plan §\"Success Criteria\" FR-10 baselines (0 at `d90decc6`/`9c3b799d`) are non-zero: items\n  1, 3, 4 and 5 pass.\n- [ ] Spec §\"Owed\" `SessionStart`: item 6 passes.\n- [ ] Spec FR-2 \"CLI verb\" (the caller halts `step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded` on\n  `record=failed` and never dispatches): items 7–9 pass, and\n  `fr10-staging-dispatches-after-record-failed` is caught.\n- [ ] The 8 rows → `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`; `ANCHORS: ANCHORS_OK` over the SKILL.md specs.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 13 (both edit SKILL.md).\n\n---\n\n## Task 17: the review-independence procedure and AC-7.3's surface\n\n**Production files**: `h-mad/SKILL.md`\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py` (new)\n**Task shape**: `new-behaviour`\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-7\" (the orchestrator's step, attribution, the author append) and\n§\"FR-10\". SKILL.md Phase-7 closure item 7 (located by the `str.index` slice from\n`7. **Closure (autonomous)**` to the next `\\n## Phase 5`) carries every `WARNING` line\n`h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` prints and adds the `--append review_independence=` step after\neach 5e-review, 5e-verify and 6a-prime leg, for every implementer. The attribution text states that\nan entry's `implementer` comes from the dispatch, never from `implementer_switch.to` or\n`implementer`, and that a module's author set is read from `implementation_authors` only, with the\nthree closed no-entry cases. The 5e bullet (`- **5e** — GREEN dispatch`, to the next `- **5f** —`)\nappends an `--append implementation_authors=` entry holding the module and the accepted\ndispatch's agent (design FR-7's JSON form) at GREEN acceptance and at each\naccepted 5e fix dispatch, before the leg that reviews the code. The 6a-prime bullet (`- **6a-prime**\n— architectural review via agy`) does the same for a fix after `WITH_FIXES`. The 5d bullet\n(`- **5d** — RED dispatch`) gains none. Each of these four prefixes occurs once in SKILL.md at\n`4e4b5ded`.\n\n**OD-1, decided (operator, 2026-10-16): option (A).** AC-7.3 is met by the transcription test\nbelow, not by a script surface. The text that follows records the reasoning.\nAttribution is orchestrator prose (design FR-7),\nand spec FR-1 says `implementation_authors` \"is read by the orchestrator's FR-7 attribution step\nonly; no script reads it\". A production function that computes an entry's `implementer` and\n`independent` from `implementation_authors` would therefore contradict spec FR-1 and design\nFR-7 (\"The writer does not check attribution\").\n- **(A), decided and built here:** a transcription test, on\n  the FR-9 corpus precedent. A test-local `attribute(record, leg, module, reviewer)` transcribes\n  the SKILL.md step; each AC-7.3 fixture runs it on a durable record, and also runs that fixture's\n  named mutant transcription, asserting the mutant gives the wrong outcome (so the fixture is RED\n  against it). One test ties the transcription to the SKILL.md words. Residual: this shows what the\n  rule's text selects, not that any orchestrator applies it.\n- **(B), not chosen:** a script surface, for example a writer operation that derives `implementer`\n  and `independent` from `implementation_authors` before appending. It would need spec FR-1 and\n  design FR-7 revised first.\n\n**Tests** (all in `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py`):\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py::test_closure_step_carries_every_warning_line` —\n   `review_non_independent`, `review_independence_unrecorded`, `review_independence_malformed`. F.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py::test_closure_step_appends_review_independence_for_every_implementer`. F.\n3. `tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py::test_attribution_step_names_the_dispatch_as_the_source`. F.\n4. `tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py::test_5e_bullet_appends_the_author_at_green_acceptance`. F.\n5. `tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py::test_6a_prime_fix_appends_the_author`. F.\n6. `tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py::test_5d_bullet_appends_no_author`. P (a negative\n   pin; it bites at GREEN).\n7. `tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py::test_attribution_transcription` — AC-7.3's five\n   fixtures: `routing-is-not-authorship` (the entry it yields is fed to `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.check`,\n   and `review_non_independent` fires), `multi-author-recovered`, `pre-field-feature`,\n   `red-is-not-authorship`, `unaccepted-dispatches-author-nothing`. 5 nodes, P (the transcription\n   lives in the test).\n8. `tests/test_h_mad_review_attribution_docs.py::test_transcription_is_tied_to_the_skill_text` —\n   the SKILL.md step contains `implementation_authors`, `implementer_switch.to` in a \"never\"\n   clause, and the no-entry rule. F.\n\n**RED split**: 12 nodes; 6 F (items 1–5, 8), 6 P (item 6, and item 7's 5).\n\n**Regression guards (doc-pin census)**: `tests/test_h_mad_archreview_pane_halt.py` (the item-7\nslice and its `SKIPPED_NO_PANE` sentence), `tests/test_h_mad_phase7_integrate_docs.py`\n(`must name a LOCAL branch`, `git branch main origin/main`),\n`tests/test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py` (the 5d wiring bullet). Expected pins moved: none.\n`phase7_integrate_blockers_documented.json`'s three rows sit inside item 7, which this Task only\nadds to. They are the only rows in the four spans this Task edits (sweep at `4e4b5ded`, by offset\nspan). `baseline_sha.json` `skill-stops-naming-the-wrong-command` has a `find` that starts exactly\nat `- **5f** —`. The 5e bullet's addition therefore goes before the newline that ends the 5e\nbullet, and that `find` stays unique.\n\n**Mutation rows** — new spec `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/review_attribution_docs.json`, 6 rows (doc\npins), one per F item, each deleting the pinned sentence: `fr7-docs-warning-lines`,\n`fr7-docs-review-append`, `fr7-docs-dispatch-source`, `fr7-docs-5e-author-append`,\n`fr7-docs-6a-prime-author-append`, `fr7-docs-transcription-words`; each `test` is its item's node\nID.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] Plan §\"Success Criteria\" (Phase-7 closure `WARNING`, baseline 0): item 1 passes.\n- [ ] Design AC-7.1 row (the dispatch as the source): item 3 passes.\n- [ ] Design §\"Owed\" (the author append at 5e GREEN acceptance and at each accepted fix): items\n  4–6 pass.\n- [ ] AC-7.3, under OD-1 (A): items 7 and 8 pass.\n- [ ] The 6 rows → `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 12 (item 7's first fixture runs `check`), Task 16 (both edit\nSKILL.md).\n\n---\n\n## Task 18: pre-merge re-measurement\n\n**Production files**: none\n**Test files**: none\n**Task shape**: `gate`\n\n**Description**: On the shipped tree, before the merge:\n- `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr9_union_sweep.py` — AC-9.1's sweep: the line grep\n  over every tracked file under `h-mad/` and the joined reading (`ast` `str` constants with\n  whitespace collapsed, plus `tokenize` `COMMENT` tokens of each `.py`), with the plan's regex\n  `missing.symbol|tests the callee|ImportError.*AttributeError|AttributeError.*ImportError`\n  (case-insensitive), reporting the union and `unparsed=N`. Every hit file is read by hand and\n  listed with whether it states the rule with the carve-out.\n- Re-run Task 0's probes. M-2 and M-3 move by construction (Tasks 1–17 add rows); the readings\n  are recorded, not compared with Task 0's. M-4 must read 0 Codex-gate rows inside a moved span.\n- `git grep -n 'review_independence_unrecorded'` over the files this feature edited; each hit is\n  read by hand to confirm none states the trigger as agy-only (plan §\"Success Criteria\").\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-9.1: the sweep hits no file that states the rule without the carve-out; `unparsed=0`, or\n  each unparsed file read by hand.\n- [ ] The census readings are committed with the sha.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Tasks 1–17.\n\n---\n\n## Task 19: the incident replay\n\n**Production files**: none\n**Test files**: none\n**Task shape**: `gate`\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-9\", \"The incident replay\"; plan stage 6. A committed probe,\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr9_incident_replay.py`, replays HemaSuite\n`notebooklm-integration-interface` T4 WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_resolver.py` at `531a6296b`),\nT5 WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_initialize.py` at `7aa0d9dec`) and T6 WIRE-PIN 2\n(`test_notebooklm_status_report.py` at `c2d671f6b`) through `git archive` exports, so the live\nclone is never checked out. At `4e4b5ded` HemaSuite `HEAD` is `02d52e4e7`, and each of the three\ncommits is an ancestor of it (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`). For each case the probe records the\ndirect-call form's RED at the task commit's parent with the callee resolving by import, the old\nverdict (halt) and the new verdict (valid) using Task 14's `old_rule`/`new_rule`; for T5 and T6 the\ncommitted scaffolded pin's `AssertionError` RED and the old verdict on it (valid); a wire-cut\nre-run at the task commit in which the committed pin fails; and the citation (M4.1, M5.1, the\nWIRE 2 revert). It stamps both repositories' shas. A case that cannot be reproduced is recorded\nwith its failing command and counted.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-9.3: all three cases reproduced, each with the five records above; a non-reproduced\n  case is counted and fails the AC.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 14.\n\n---\n\n## Task 20: post-merge payload capture\n\n**Production files**: `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py`\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_agy_tdd_gate.py`\n**Task shape**: `new-behaviour`\n\n**v1.3 amendment (D-FR8-1, operator decision 2026-10-17) — supersedes the six-tool scope below.**\nThe capture gate stopped at 3 of 6: on agy `1.4.0`, `multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file` and\n`notebook_edit` are listed in `init.tools` but were never declared to the model across the initial\ninstruction and three re-instructions on three model families\n(`docs/03-analysis/agy-coder-fallback.authoring/fr8_capture_attempts/README.md`, `b7d34327`).\nThe required set becomes the three captured tools: `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content`,\n`run_command`. The three uncaptured class-(b) tools keep no `PATH_KEYS` entry, so under active\nstep5 they still deny `kind=judge-error` (fail-closed); a later agy that declares them reopens\nthe capture. Tests, as amended:\n- item 1: one id per captured tool (3 nodes);\n- item 2: one id per captured class-(b) tool, `write_to_file` and `replace_file_content` (2 nodes);\n- item 3: unchanged (2 nodes); item 4: unchanged (1 node).\nTotal 8 nodes. Captured keys: `write_to_file` → `TargetFile`, `replace_file_content` →\n`TargetFile`, `run_command` → `CommandLine` / `Cwd`. **RED split: 1 F, 7 P** over the 8 new\nnodes — the `replace_file_content` item-2 id is F (its key is not in `PATH_KEYS` at the RED base);\nthe rest P. Two existing expectations move in the same RED commit:\n`test_classification_table_is_the_recorded_inventory`'s `PATH_KEYS` equality gains\n`replace_file_content` (a second F), and `test_class_b_tools_without_a_recorded_key_deny_under_active`\ndrops its `replace_file_content` id. Module RED reading: 2 failed, 101 passed.\nGREEN adds `\"replace_file_content\": (\"TargetFile\",)` to `PATH_KEYS`. AC-8.1 reads \"all three\nsamples\" for \"all six samples\".\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-8\" step 1, on main after the merge. In a throwaway fixture project\ncreated beside this checkout (never under `/tmp`), with its own `docs/.bkit-memory.json` step5\nrecord and FR-5's exact `.agents/hooks.json` entry, a temporary capture hook records one real\n`toolCall` envelope **for each of the six required tools**: the five class-(b) tools\n(`write_to_file`, `replace_file_content`, `multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file`,\n`notebook_edit`) and `run_command` (spec FR-8 step 1: \"one real `toolCall` per class-(b) tool and\nper `run_command`\"). It also records `init.tools` and `agy --version`. Each sample is the hook's\nwhole stdin as received, unedited, committed under `h-mad/tests/fixtures/agy-payloads/` as six\nfiles named by the tool: `write_to_file.json`, `replace_file_content.json`,\n`multi_replace_file_content.json`, `sed_file.json`, `notebook_edit.json`, `run_command.json`.\nBeside them, `h-mad/tests/fixtures/agy-payloads/capture.json` holds the metadata the suite reads\n(audit cycle 2, I5): one object per tool, keyed by tool name, with `workspacePaths_present`\n(`true` or `false`), `path_keys` (the class-(b) key or keys naming the target, for the five\nclass-(b) tools), and, for `run_command`, `command_key` and `workdir_key` (the envelope keys\nnaming the command and its working directory). The human-readable capture record\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr8_payload_capture.md` holds, per tool: the\ninstruction given to agy that produced the call, the stream-json line showing it, and whether\n`workspacePaths` is present in the envelope. No suite test reads the probe directory, so\narchiving it cannot break the suite (as Task 22 keeps its formula probe out of the suite). It also holds the sha, `agy --version`, and the\n`ls` reading showing the capture hook removed (probe-then-delete). The fixture project's\n`notebook_edit` sample needs an `.ipynb` file in the project, which the fixture creates.\n\n**Capture gate.** All six samples must exist before the RED dispatch. A missing sample (agy\nrefuses the instruction, or never routes the edit through that tool) is not dropped: re-instruct\nup to three times, then stop the Task and report which tool could not be captured, with its three\ninstructions. The capture does not pass on a subset.\n\n`PATH_KEYS` gains each class-(b) key the capture shows that it does not already hold. If\n`run_command`'s envelope names its command or workdir under a key other than `CommandLine` or\n`Cwd`, class (c)'s reads change to the captured keys, and that is a finding against design\n§\"FR-4\", reported before GREEN. If the `run_command` sample carries no workdir key at all,\n`capture.json`'s `workdir_key` is `null`, item 3's `workdir-outside` id cannot be built, and that\nis reported as a finding against design §\"FR-4\" before the RED dispatch. `class_inventory.py` (Task 0) is re-run with the capture as its\nsecond log.\n\n**Tests**:\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_tdd_gate.py::test_capture_holds_every_required_sample` — one id per\n   required tool (6): the fixture file exists, parses as JSON, names that tool in its `toolCall`,\n   and `h-mad/tests/fixtures/agy-payloads/capture.json` holds a boolean `workspacePaths_present`\n   for it that agrees with the sample (the key is in the envelope exactly when it is `true`).\n   6 nodes.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_tdd_gate.py::test_captured_payload_is_judged_by_its_recorded_key` — one id\n   per class-(b) tool (5): the captured payload, with its path values aimed at a production `.py`\n   without a failing test under `active`, denies with the judge's kind (not `judge-error`). 5 nodes.\n3. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_tdd_gate.py::test_captured_run_command_is_judged_by_its_recorded_keys` —\n   the captured `run_command` envelope, `active`, in a fixture root holding `.venv/bin/python` (a\n   regular file) and `.venv/pyvenv.cfg` (Task 7 item 6's root-venv fixture), and an existing\n   directory `elsewhere` beside the root, outside it. The value under the captured `command_key`\n   is set to `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_x.py`; the value under the captured\n   `workdir_key` is set per id (audit cycle 2, I2):\n   - `workdir-root`: `str(root)` → allowed;\n   - `workdir-outside`: `str(elsewhere)` → deny `kind=shell-not-admitted` (a relative venv\n     interpreter under a `cwd` outside the root, design §\"FR-4\" class (c), `M:F2-ESCAPE`).\n   2 nodes. A reader that misses the captured workdir key falls back to the root, so\n   `workdir-outside` allows and fails; a reader that misses the captured command key denies\n   `judge-error` (`CommandLine` missing), so both ids fail. `ls` (v1.1's fixture) cannot do this:\n   the shared policy admits `ls` under every `cwd`. Controlled pair, run at `84ec2133` against the\n   live `_safe_shell_command` loaded from `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py`, on a scratch root\n   with that `.venv` and an outside directory, a reader on the captured key against one that\n   ignores it (`cwd` defaulting to the root): the venv command under `workdir-outside` gives\n   `False` read against `True` ignored, the other three cells (venv under `workdir-root`, `ls`\n   under both) agree at `True`. The scratch was deleted.\n4. `tests/test_h_mad_agy_tdd_gate.py::test_class_a_names_are_in_the_recaptured_inventory`. 1 node.\n\n**RED split**, derived from the committed capture before the RED dispatch, and stated in it:\n- item 1: 6 P. The capture is committed first; this item is the capture gate's regression pin;\n- item 2: an id fails at RED exactly when its captured path key is not in `PATH_KEYS` at the RED\n  base. Only `write_to_file` → `TargetFile` is there, from Task 5. So `write_to_file` is P when\n  its captured key is `TargetFile`, and F otherwise; each of the other four is F when its captured\n  key is new and P when the capture shows a key already mapped;\n- item 3: both ids P when the captured `command_key` and `workdir_key` are `CommandLine` and\n  `Cwd` (Task 7's reads). When `command_key` differs, both F. When only `workdir_key` differs,\n  `workdir-outside` F and `workdir-root` P;\n- item 4: P, unless the re-capture drops a class-(a) name, which is a finding for the design.\nTotal 14 nodes (6 + 5 + 2 + 1). F is the number of item-2 and item-3 ids the rules above mark F, and P is 14 minus F.\nWhen F is 0, the Task is **capture-only**: no production change, the RED dispatch's gate is\n\"0 F, 14 P\", and GREEN is the RED tree.\n\n**Mutation rows**: none new. A `PATH_KEYS` entry is data: removing one fails its own item-2 node,\nand the no-target guard keeps its Task 6 row.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-8.1 (the payload capture, stamped with the sha and `agy --version`): all six samples and\n  `capture.json` are committed under `h-mad/tests/fixtures/agy-payloads/`, and the capture record,\n  with the capture hook's removal, under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`. Item 1\n  passes.\n- [ ] Items 2–4 pass; the inventory partition and its difference from Task 0's are recorded.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: the merge of Tasks 0–19.\n\n---\n\n## Task 21: post-merge tracer\n\n**Production files**: none\n**Test files**: none\n**Task shape**: `operational` — it runs the gate, the check and the assembler that Tasks 5–10\nbuilt.\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-8\" step 3, spec FR-8 steps 2, 2a and 3, in Task 20's fixture project.\nStep 2: a real forbidden write through `hmad-dispatch exec agy` with the operator's global\n`~/.gemini/config/hooks.json` loaded is refused. Step 2a: a delay handler (matcher `*`, `timeout`\nT, sleeping at least 2T, then printing a `deny`) is added to the fixture's `.agents/hooks.json`,\nwith `shasum -a 256 .agents/hooks.json` recorded before; agy is told to write one named non-test\nfile; the file's existence, the wall time and agy's message are read independently; one outcome\nfrom step 2a's table (`timeout-blocks`, `timeout-allows`, `timeout-not-enforced`,\n`session-error`) is named; the delay handler is removed and the after-hash compared. Step 3: the\nRED/GREEN readings with re-run, revert and mutation results, and where agy's files landed. Every\nreading is committed under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, stamped with the sha and\n`agy --version`.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-8.1 (the real refusal; the RED/GREEN readings with re-run, revert and mutation results):\n  committed and stamped.\n- [ ] AC-8.2: the step-2a reading names exactly one outcome consistent with its three readings,\n  shows the delay hook removed and the before and after hashes equal; `session-error` is re-run\n  and never counts. On `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD gate\" carries the fail-open\n  residual and the operator question is raised before the gate is called verified.\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 20.\n\n---\n\n## Task 22: post-merge adoption and the derived timeout\n\n**Production files**: `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`, `.agents/hooks.json` (new)\n**Test files**: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py`\n**Task shape**: `new-behaviour`\n\n**Description**: Design §\"FR-8\" steps 4–5. This repository's `.agents/hooks.json` holding FR-5's\nexact entry is committed only after Task 21's refusal passed (`git check-ignore -v\n.agents/hooks.json` prints nothing at `4e4b5ded`, so no `git add -f` is needed; the file is absent\ntoday), and step 2 is re-run once here. The assembler's `TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS[\"agy\"]` becomes\n`max(900, 3 × slower turn)` rounded up to a multiple of 60, over the readings Task 21 committed.\nThe committed probe `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr8_timeout_formula.py` recomputes\nthe figure from those readings and asserts the assembler's default equals it (a probe, not a suite\ntest, so archiving the probe directory cannot break the suite).\n\n**Tests**:\n1. `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_default_timeouts[agy-1500]` is re-pointed:\n   its id becomes `agy-` followed by the figure the formula probe prints, and its expected value\n   becomes that figure.\n2. `tests/test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py::test_agent_agy_wired_writes_the_prompt` (Task 10 item 6)\n   pins `--timeout 1500` in the command block. Its expected `--timeout` becomes the same figure.\n\nBoth expectations change in the RED commit. Task 10's `TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS[\"agy\"]` is still `1500`\nat RED, so both nodes fail there.\n\n**RED split**: when the derived figure is not 1500, 2 F: the re-pointed `test_agent_default_timeouts`\nid and `test_agent_agy_wired_writes_the_prompt`. When it is 1500, 0 F: this Task changes no code\nand no test, and records that.\n\n**Anchors**: the edit touches only the `\"agy\"` value of `TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS`, on which no row sits\n(Task 10 adds `assemble_tdd_agy.json`, and none of its rows anchors on that value).\n`--check-anchors` over the 8 specs naming the assembler by then: Task 10's 7, plus\n`new_caller_carveout_copies.json`, whose `fr9-copy-L5` row names it (Task 13).\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- [ ] AC-8.1 (the default timeout equals FR-8's formula over the readings): the formula probe\n  prints `PROBE: PASS`.\n- [ ] This repository's step-2 re-run is committed and stamped.\n- [ ] The feature is recorded COMPLETE only after AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 pass (spec D-13).\n\n**Dependencies on other tasks**: Task 21.\n\n---\n\n## Mutation-row ledger\n\nDesign v1.4 (`2cd5d7f1`) §\"Mutation rows (AC-X)\" lists 60 new rows, counted from its list: FR-1 3;\nFR-2 4 (3 `_cause` branches and the other-peer step); FR-3 4; FR-6 2; FR-4 19 (14 single bullets,\nv1.4's \"T2a removed\" among them, 3 kinds, 2 class-(c) `cwd` rows); FR-5 8 (7 reasons and `UNWIRED`\nreturning 2); FR-7 10 (3 append rows, 2 `APPEND_ONLY`, 5 warning and `property_valid` rows); FR-9\n10 (8 + 2). 3 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 19 + 8 + 10 + 10 = 60.\n\n| Task | design rows | wire rows (cut + converse) | doc-pin rows (plan §\"Success Criteria\") | discipline and decision rows |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 4 (FR-1 3, FR-7 1) | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 2 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 3 | 4 | 3 (2 + 1) | 0 | 0 |\n| 4 | 2 | 0 | 0 | 2 (signal discipline, I4) |\n| 5 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 1 (signal discipline) |\n| 6 | 11 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 7 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 9 | 7 | 0 | 0 | 1 (connection, I3) |\n| 10 | 1 | 2 (1 + 1) | 0 | 1 (marker discipline) |\n| 11 | 5 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 12 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 13 | 8 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 14 | 2 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 15 | 0 | 0 | 4 | 0 |\n| 16 | 0 | 0 | 8 | 0 |\n| 17 | 0 | 0 | 6 | 0 |\n| total | 60 | 5 | 18 | 5 |\n\nDesign rows: 4 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 4 + 11 + 4 + 7 + 1 + 5 + 4 + 8 + 2 = 60, equal to the design's\nlist. Wire rows: 3 + 2 = 5. Doc-pin rows: 4 + 8 + 6 = 18. Discipline and connection rows:\n2 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5, none of them in the design's list (Task 4's two Codex self-check verdict rows\nand Task 9's budget-connection row from audit cycle 2, Task 5's signal-discipline and Task 10's\nmarker rows from cycle 1). OD-2's row is now a design row (design v1.4). All new rows:\n60 + 5 + 18 + 5 = 88. Existing rows\nchanged, not new: the 31 re-pointed in Task 4, of which `CX-FOLD` also takes a new `find` and\n`CX-FORCE-SCRIPT` a new `replace`; and the re-anchors of Tasks 2, 3 and 10 (`w10a-grok-by-default`\nnamed; the others as `--check-anchors` finds them). Each Task states which existing rows its\nedits touch.\n\n## Decisions (closed)\n\n- **OD-1 (Task 17)**, closed 2026-10-16. Operator: option (A), a transcription test over the\n  written procedure. Spec FR-1 and design FR-7 stand unchanged.\n- **OD-2 (Task 6)**, closed 2026-10-16. Operator: a class-(b) target naming an existing directory\n  is refused with `kind=judge-error` under `S` (`active`, `unknown`) and allowed under `inactive`.\n  Task 6 implements it as T2a, with item 10 and row `od2-directory-target-check-dropped`. Spec v1.10\n  (`c283bb63`) records it as D-16, design v1.4 (`2cd5d7f1`) as T2a.\n\nNo decision is open.\n\n## Residuals carried from the design\n\nEach stays where the design states it (design §\"Residuals\"); the Tasks above add no fixture that\nclaims to close one. The ones a Task touches: the `which` matrix (Task 3's regression guard); the\nverb reads the root only (Task 2); the class-(a) judgement and the six-line shell composition\n(Tasks 5, 7, 8); a sibling `overwrite`, the review-leg agy, worktree state (Task 21 observes, does\nnot settle); an equivalent command spelling (Task 9); a writer that bypasses `_mutate` (Task 11);\nprose application, a seventh surface (Tasks 13, 18); a later `KINDS` member (Task 5's kind scan\nreads `KINDS` live); T4's empty-string arm (Task 6); the author-before-review ordering (Task 17).\n\nAdded by this plan, not by the design:\n- OD-2's T2a covers an existing directory only. A target ending in `/` that names no existing\n  directory goes through T3–T7 as a file name (Task 6).\n- The staging step is prose that the orchestrator follows. Task 16's tests pin its words, not its\n  execution (the same residual as AC-7.3 under OD-1 (A)).\n\n## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial implementation plan draft (2026-10-16), Phase 5a, from design v1.3 (4e4b5ded), spec v1.9 (4bef0e76), plan v1.6 (8e242d06); tree read at 4e4b5ded. 23 Tasks (0-19 pre-merge, 20-22 post-merge per D-13); wiring Tasks 3 and 10 with WIRE/WIRE-PIN; 59 design mutation rows plus 3 wire-cut and 14 doc-pin rows; OD-1 (AC-7.3 surface vs spec FR-1 \"no script reads it\") and OD-2 (directory target).\n- v1.1: Impl-plan audit cycle 1 (2026-10-16), answering codex p1 (9 must, 3 should) and agy p2 (2 must, 2 should) against v1.0 (fc6b497e), tree at 683777df. Grammar probe fr3_state_line_grammar.py updated (derived/shipped modes, --control, --hook) so Task 3's post-GREEN re-run passes. Harness cwd convention (scripts/ from h-mad/). agy --self-check: verdict FAILs exit 0, policy-import exit 2. Task 16 staging step with record=failed halt step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded and its discriminating control. Task 20 requires all six samples (5 class-(b) + run_command), RED split derived from captured keys, capture-only gate. Task 10 converse wire row, [H-MAD] halt marker. Task 3 converse wire row. Task 11, 9, 10 RED assertions on refusal text, not argparse exit 2. Task 2 status fixtures expect reason=status. Task 22 two timeout tests. Task 4 CX-FOLD find and CX-FORCE-SCRIPT replace. Task 15 FR-9 prompt bullets. hook_wiring.json 12 rows. OD-1 closed (A); OD-2 closed: directory target judge-error under S (Task 6 T2a). Rows: 59 design + 5 wire + 18 doc-pin + 3 discipline/decision = 85.\n- v1.2: Impl-plan audit cycle 2, final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16), answering codex p1 (4 must, 1 should; agy p2 clean) against v1.1 (fc92becf), tree at 84ec2133, design v1.4 (2cd5d7f1), spec v1.10 (c283bb63). I1: Task 6 item 1 gains number-inactive and list-inactive, the discriminators for fr4-class-b-target-presence-only (null-inactive does not discriminate: canonicalise treats a falsy target as the root); 34->35 nodes with the t2a-directory deny-row id. I2: Task 20 item 3 uses the root .venv interpreter under the captured workdir key, workdir-root and workdir-outside (ls cannot discriminate); 14 nodes. I3: Task 9 item 4 patches h_mad_tdd_judge.JUDGE_BUDGET_S to 41.1 (timeouts 61 and 62), check_agy reads the module attribute at call time, row fr5-timeout-floor-hardcoded-60; 18 nodes. I4: Task 4 fixes both Codex --self-check verdict FAILs (parser, shell-policy) to stdout with exit 0, policy-import keeps exit 2; no existing test pinned exit 2; 2 tests, 2 rows; Task 4 shape new-behaviour; residual removed. I5: Task 20 suite metadata in h-mad/tests/fixtures/agy-payloads/capture.json. Design v1.4 alignment: FR-4 19 design rows (T2a), deny table 17 rows, owed lines marked recorded. Rows: 60 design + 5 wire + 18 doc-pin + 5 discipline/connection = 88.\n\nPaired audited design: # Design: agy-coder-fallback\n\n> **Slug note.** The slug predates brainstorm v1.1. agy is a **peer** Phase-5 implementer with\n> Codex's standing, not a fallback (spec §\"Slug note\").\n\nSources: spec v1.6 (`c853b207`), plan v1.4 (`29ae3e7b`), brainstorm v1.1. Every \"currently\" claim\nbelow was read from the tree at `29ae3e7b`. `h-mad/` has not changed since `a8d9ba99`\n(`git diff --stat a8d9ba99 29ae3e7b -- h-mad | wc -l` prints `0`), so the spec's and plan's\n`h-mad/` readings still describe the code this design changes. Code is cited as `path` plus a\nsymbol, a heading or a content predicate, never a line number.\n\nv1.1 answers design audit cycle 1. Every premise v1.1 adds was re-read at `200ffd52`, where\n`h-mad/` is still unchanged (`git diff --stat 29ae3e7b 200ffd52 -- h-mad | wc -l` prints `0`).\n\nv1.2 is the final corrective revision after design audit cycle 2 (round cap reached; not\nre-audited). It changes only what cycle 2 filed. Every premise it adds was read at `cff71b49`,\nwhere `h-mad/` is still unchanged (`git diff --stat 200ffd52 cff71b49 -- h-mad | wc -l` prints `0`).\n\nv1.3 records operator decision OD-E2 (option (a), a durable author record; spec v1.9 D-15) and changes only what it\nneeds: FR-1's fifth property, FR-7's author record and its readers, and the sections that list them.\nEvery premise it adds was read at `3ad735f7`, where `h-mad/` is still unchanged\n(`git diff --stat cff71b49 3ad735f7 -- h-mad | wc -l` prints `0`).\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThe judge (`h-mad/scripts/h_mad_tdd_judge.py`) gains the one function that decides who authors a\nrecord, and it carries that decision to the Claude hook as a new **per-record** field on the\n`TDD-STATE:` line. The hook stops reading `PATH` and the Codex env var itself. The Codex gate's\nshell policy, its operator allowance, its phase-5 activity predicate and its production predicate\nmove into `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, the module both existing gates already share,\nand both the Codex gate and the new agy gate import them from there. A new\n`h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` gates agy's tools through a closed classification, so an unknown\ntool is denied. `h_mad_hook_wiring.py --agy` prints `AGY-GATE:`, and the assembler halts on\nanything but `WIRED`. The state writer gains a locked `--append`, and the record gains an append-only\n`implementation_authors` list that FR-7 attribution reads as a module's author set. Phase 7 warns on non-independent,\nunrecorded and malformed review entries for every implementer. The FR-9 carve-out stays applied by\nprose, and one test binds its six copies to one canonical block.\n\n## Overview — the design obligations and where each is settled\n\n| Obligation | Decision | Section |\n|---|---|---|\n| DO-1 / DO-1a — `TDD-STATE:` shape | a fifth comma field per record, `codex\\|agy\\|peers-out\\|invalid:<pct>`, between the state file and the fallback tag; no header field moves or is added | §\"FR-3\" |\n| DO-2 — read-only allowlist | 11 names, checked against the 58-name `init.tools` inventory | §\"FR-4\" |\n| DO-3 — `review_independence` add | a writer operation, `--append KEY=JSON`, run inside the writer's existing locked read-modify-write | §\"FR-7\" |\n| DO-4 — invalid `.ts` on both paths | a stdlib RFC 3339 predicate in `h_mad_state_validate.py`, applied outside the validator backend, scoped to `implementer_switch.ts` | §\"FR-1\" |\n| DO-5 — the moved names | 17 functions and 8 constants move into `h_mad_target_identity.py`; the Codex gate imports every one of them by name | §\"FR-6\" |\n| DO-6 — installed path vs worktree | no override in code; tests point `HOME` at a fixture; FR-8 runs after the merge to main | §\"FR-5\", §\"FR-8\" |\n| DO-7 — where the carve-out is applied | it stays applied by prose; the corpus checks the rule's text; one equivalence test with six per-copy locators | §\"FR-9\" |\n\nThe two operator questions v1.0 raised are decided (§\"Operator decisions\"): the shared policy\nlives in `h_mad_target_identity.py` (OQ-A, spec D-12), and FR-8 is a post-merge stage, with the\nfeature recorded COMPLETE only after it (OQ-B, spec D-13). OD-E2, raised by v1.2, is decided as a\ndurable author record (spec D-15, §\"FR-7\").\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\n```\n                  docs/.bkit-memory.json (the root's chain)\n                                │\n             h_mad_tdd_judge.py ── resolve_implementer(record)   (FR-2, the only resolver)\n               │        │                 │\n     `state` verb   `implementer` verb   in-process: read_chain / judge\n","total_lines":4240}},"locations":[]}
{"type":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"call-7ff6ad45-bf66-4fbd-9947-667549568e2e-2","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"2308→               │        │                 │\n   TDD-STATE: line   TDD-IMPLEMENTER:     ├── h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py ──┐\n2310→               │        │                 └── h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py ────┤ both import the\n   h-mad-tdd-gate.sh  orchestrator ──► h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --agent    │ gate policy from\n   (Claude's writes)                    └─ agy: h_mad_hook_wiring.py --agy\n                                                                        │\n                                          h_mad_target_identity.py ◄────┘\n```\n\n- The judge is the only place `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE`, `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE` and `PATH` are read\n  for authorship. It runs as a child of the Claude hook, so it sees the hook's environment.\n- The Claude hook parses the line and refuses. It re-derives nothing.\n2320→- The agy gate and the Codex gate share one copy of the shell policy, the allowance file rules,\n  the phase-5 activity predicate and the production predicate.\n\n## Detailed Design\n\n### FR-1 — state fields, and the timestamp refusal (DO-4)\n\n**Schema.** Five properties are added to `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json`. `required` does\nnot change, and `additionalProperties` stays `false`. Every construct below is in the bundled\nvalidator's supported set (`_MiniDraft7`'s docstring, the `Deliberately supports ONLY` paragraph):\n2330→`type`, `enum`, `required`, `properties`, `additionalProperties`, `items`, and (v1.3, for\n`implementation_authors`) `minLength`. No `oneOf`, `anyOf`,\n`const` or `pattern` is used, so the two validator paths cannot disagree on structure.\n\n**Property order.** The five are appended, in the order listed below, after `fallback_agent`, which is the schema's last\nproperty today (`list(properties)` ends `…, 'codex_status', 'fallback_agent'` at `200ffd52`).\n`test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts`\nasserts `list(properties).index(\"fallback_agent\") == list(properties).index(\"codex_status\") + 1`,\nso any new property placed between those two breaks AC-1.1. Rule over the axis: no property is\ninserted anywhere before `fallback_agent`.\n2340→\n- `implementer`: `enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", null]`.\n- `agy_status`: `enum` `[\"available\", \"unavailable\", \"exhausted\", null]`.\n- `implementer_switch`: `type` `[\"object\", \"null\"]`. Properties `from` and `to` take `enum`\n  `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`, `reason` takes `enum` `[\"env\", \"status\", \"cli-absent\"]`, and\n  `ts` is `type` `string` with `\"format\": \"date-time\"` as an annotation only. All four are\n  `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`.\n- `review_independence`: `type` `array`. Its `items` is an object with `leg`\n  (`enum` `[\"5e-review\", \"5e-verify\", \"6a-prime\"]`), `module` (`type` `[\"string\", \"null\"]`),\n  `reviewer` and `implementer` (each `enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`) and `independent`\n2350→  (`type` `boolean`). All five are `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`.\n- `implementation_authors` (v1.3, operator decision OD-E2, spec FR-1 D-15): `type` `array`. Its\n  `items` is an object with `module` (`type` `string`, `minLength` `1`: it is the `--module` value\n  the assembler requires) and `agent` (`enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`). Both are\n  `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`. The array is a log, not a set: duplicates\n  are kept, and array order is acceptance order (spec FR-1 D-15). One entry is one accepted implementation dispatch; §\"FR-7\" says who writes it and when,\n  and how attribution reads it. `module` is a `string`, not `[\"string\", \"null\"]` as in\n  `review_independence`, because an implementation dispatch is always for one module (5e stages\n  one module per GREEN). It is placed last, after `review_independence`, so the AC-1.1 index\n  assertion on `fallback_agent` is untouched. `minLength` is in `_MiniDraft7`'s supported set (its\n2360→  docstring's `Deliberately supports ONLY` paragraph names it, and its `_ok` checks it on a\n  `str`), and the schema already uses it, so the two validator paths cannot disagree on its\n  structure. Fixtures: AC-1.3 (§\"Test Plan\").\n\n**The `.ts` refusal: a predicate outside the validator backend.** The option chosen is the third of\nthe three the spec lists, placed in the validator module rather than in the writer, so that a\nhand-edited record is refused by `h_mad_state_validate.py` too.\n- **The probe it rests on.** Reading at `29ae3e7b`, the plan's command\n  (`/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3 -B -c '… Draft7Validator(s).is_valid(x) … v._MiniDraft7(s).is_valid(x)'`\n  with `s={\"type\":\"string\",\"format\":\"date-time\"}` and `x=\"not-a-date\"`) prints\n2370→  `Draft7Validator: True` and `bundled: True`. `format` is enforced on neither path, so declaring it\n  refuses nothing.\n- **The predicate.** `h_mad_state_validate.py` gains `rfc3339_datetime(value) -> bool`:\n  - `re.fullmatch` with `re.ASCII` over\n    `([0-9]{4})-([0-9]{2})-([0-9]{2})[Tt]([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2})(\\.[0-9]+)?([Zz]|[+-]([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2}))`;\n  - then `datetime.datetime(y, mo, d, h, mi, s)` must construct, which refuses `2026-02-30`;\n  - then an offset's hours must be at most 23 and its minutes at most 59;\n  - a non-`str` is refused.\n\n  `fullmatch` and `[0-9]` are deliberate: `$` admits a trailing newline, and `\\d` admits non-ASCII\n2380→  digits. A scratch probe at `29ae3e7b`, since deleted, returned these results under both\n  `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3` (3.11.8) and `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` (3.15.0):\n  - refused: `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`, `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z`, an offset of `+24:00`, a seconds\n    field of 60, a value with\n    full-width digits, the writer's form followed by `\\n`, `5` and `None`;\n  - accepted: `2026-10-16T07:05:09Z` and `2026-10-16T07:05:09.123+09:00`.\n- **Where it applies.** A private `_semantic_ok(record)` returns `False` exactly when `record` is\n  a `dict` whose `implementer_switch` is a `dict` whose `ts` fails `rfc3339_datetime`; it returns\n  `True` for anything else, a non-`dict` record included, so it never raises.\n  `classify` keeps its existing first statement (`if not isinstance(record, dict): return\n2390→  \"invalid\"`, which is what `test_h_mad_state_validate_fallback.py` `CORPUS`'s\n  `(\"not an object\", [\"nope\"])` row exercises), then calls `_semantic_ok` and returns `invalid`\n  when it fails, before either schema tier is tried. That runs whichever\n  backend `_make_validator` chose, so both paths refuse. The writer refuses through `classify` as it\n  does today (`_mutate`: `classify(record) != \"strict\"`), and it prints its existing refusal.\n- **No library format checker is used.** The `rfc3339-validator` hazard therefore does not bind,\n  and AC-1.2's package-absent fixture is not owed, since AC-1.2 makes it conditional on that\n  mechanism. Recorded for the reader: at `29ae3e7b`, `rfc3339_validator` imports in\n  `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3`, and `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` has no `jsonschema`.\n- **Not schema-wide.** `started_ts`, `owner_heartbeat_ts` and `halt_ts` stay annotation-only, so\n2400→  no existing record can change class. The `classify_store` run the spec asks of a schema-wide\n  mechanism is not owed. A reading anyway, taken 2026-10-16 with this repository at `29ae3e7b` and\n  HemaSuite at `77fb16f51` (both files are untracked, so the sha does not pin their content): this\n  repository's `docs/.bkit-memory.json` has 54 records and HemaSuite's has 7. Zero records in either file carry\n  any of the four FR-1 keys. That zero is load-bearing, not incidental: the strict schema has\n  `additionalProperties: false`, so the writer has refused those keys until now. A hand-edited\n  record is the residual. The fifth key, `implementation_authors` (v1.3), has the same zero for\n  the same load-bearing reason: one reading on 2026-10-16, this repository at `3ad735f7` and\n  HemaSuite at `02d52e4e7`, over each file's `orchestrator_state` object, gives 54 and 8 records,\n  and 0 records in either file carry any of the five keys.\n2410→- **A seconds field of 60 is refused, as spec FR-1 requires (D-14).** RFC 3339 admits a leap\n  second; spec v1.7 FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`, because every `.ts`\n  is machine-written by h-mad's own writer through `datetime`, which cannot hold second 60. The predicate refuses it at the `datetime` construction\n  step, and a fixture pins `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` refused and `2016-12-31T23:59:59Z` accepted on\n  both paths.\n\n**Fixtures (AC-1.2).** One fixture per value per path, with the bundled path forced through the\nexisting `_FORCE_FALLBACK` seam. Refused: `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`, `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z`, and\n`2026-10-16T00:00:00Zjunk` and `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z`. `…Zjunk` kills a `fullmatch` → `match`\nmutation. Accepted: the writer's `_utc_now` form and `2016-12-31T23:59:59Z`. Each alternative of the zone group has its own fixture, run with that\n2420→branch alone: `…Z`, `…z`, `…+09:00` accepted, and `…+24:00` refused.\n\n**The `fallback_agent` description, and the same class on `codex_status`.** Spec FR-1 names the\n`fallback_agent` description and its `state-schema.md` row. The class is \"a surface that says what\nan unavailable Codex lets Claude do\". It has two more members in the tree:\n- the `codex_status` schema `description`, which ends \"permitting Claude to write the fallback\n  (still test-first)\" and \"One switch, both effects\";\n- its `state-schema.md` row, \"An unavailable or exhausted status permits a Phase-5 authoring\n  fallback\".\n\n2430→Both are false once agy is available. All four surfaces are rewritten to the FR-2 condition.\n`test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts` keeps\nits four substrings, for example \"read only when codex is out and agy is out too\". No test pins the\n`codex_status` wording: `git ls-files 'h-mad/tests/*.py' | xargs grep -ln 'permitting Claude\\|One switch, both effects'`\nprints nothing at `29ae3e7b`. The spec does not list the `codex_status` pair; see §\"Owed by other\ndocuments\".\n\n### FR-2 — `resolve_implementer` and the `implementer` verb\n\n**Record.** `Record` gains two trailing fields with defaults: `implementer: str = \"absent\"` and\n2440→`agy_status: str = \"available\"`. The defaults keep every positional four-argument construction\nvalid. One exists in the tree: the replace text of a `codex_gate_judge_wiring.json` row\n(`git ls-files -z h-mad | xargs -0 grep -l 'Record('` → `h_mad_tdd_judge.py` and that spec,\n2 files). `_active_records` fills both through two helpers:\n- `_implementer_tag(state)` returns `absent`, `null`, `codex`, `agy` or `invalid:<json>`, built like\n  `_fallback_tag`;\n- `agy_status` is read the way `codex_status` is: `\"available\"` when the value is `None` or the key\n  is absent, else `str(value)`.\n\n**Resolution.** `resolve_implementer(record, env=None, which=None) -> Implementer` is the only\n2450→resolver. `env` defaults to `os.environ` and `which` to `shutil.which`. `Implementer` is a\n`NamedTuple` with these fields: `resolved` (`codex|agy|grok|claude|invalid`), `chosen`\n(`codex|agy`, or `None` when invalid), `reason` (`env|status|cli-absent`, or `None`),\n`invalid_field` (`implementer|fallback_agent`, or `None`) and `invalid_value` (the JSON text, or\n`\"\"`).\n1. An `invalid:` implementer tag gives `resolved=\"invalid\"` and `invalid_field=\"implementer\"`.\n2. `chosen` is `agy` for the tag `agy`, else `codex`.\n3. `_cause(peer)` returns the first cause in FR-2's order:\n   - `env`: `env.get(\"HMAD_<PEER>_UNAVAILABLE\", \"\") != \"\"`;\n   - `status`: the record's `<peer>_status` is in `ESCAPE_STATUSES`;\n2460→   - `cli-absent`: `which(peer)` is `None`.\n\n   It returns `None` when the peer is available.\n4. If `chosen` is available it is resolved. Otherwise the other peer is resolved if it is\n   available. Otherwise the record's fallback tag decides: `grok` resolves `grok`; `absent`, `null`\n   and `claude` resolve `claude`; an invalid tag gives `invalid_field=\"fallback_agent\"`.\n   `reason` is `_cause(chosen)` whenever the resolved author is not `chosen`.\n\n`shutil.which` replaces the hook's `command -v codex`. The equivalence is checked by the existing\ndifferential `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py` `test_absent_null_claude_match_the_base_hook`,\n2470→which compares the current hook's bytes with the hook at `8ef6009f` over the PATH × env × status\nmatrix. Residual: that matrix holds only the PATH forms the test builds. Other forms, such as an\nempty `PATH` element or a non-executable `codex` file, are not compared.\n\n**The `implementer` verb.** It is a new `implementer` subparser in `main`, with `--root`,\n`--feature` and `--record`. It has no `--target`. It reads `<root>/docs/.bkit-memory.json` through\n`_state_at`, so it has the same reader and the same error names as the chain. It looks up\n`orchestrator_state[feature]` **whatever its phase**, because staging must not depend on the record\nalready being in `step5`. Exactly one line is printed:\n\n2480→| condition | line |\n|---|---|\n| file absent, or the key absent | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: no-record feature=<pct>` |\n| `_state_at` gives `unreadable`, or the record is not an object | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: unreadable file=<pct> error=<name>` (`not-an-object` for the second) |\n| `invalid_field` is set | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: invalid field=<field> value=<pct>` |\n| `resolved == chosen` | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <resolved> chosen=<chosen> switch=none` |\n| a peer switch | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <resolved> chosen=<chosen> switch=<chosen>-><resolved> reason=<reason>` |\n| peers out | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <grok\\|claude> chosen=<chosen> switch=peers-out reason=<reason>` |\n\n`<pct>` is the judge's existing `_enc`. With `--record`, any switch other than `none` calls\n2490→`h_mad_state_write.merge_fields(state_file, feature, {\"implementer_switch\": {...}})` with\n`from=chosen`, `to=resolved`, `reason` and `ts=_utc_now()`. That is the same write path `--set`\nuses. The write is skipped when the stored value has the same `from`, `to` and `reason`. A\n`StateWriteError` or an `OSError` appends ` record=failed` to the line. The verb always exits 0\nafter printing a line, and the caller reads the token.\n\n**The caller's halts (v1.4).** The orchestrator (SKILL.md, §\"FR-10\") reads the token and never\ndispatches on these three outcomes, adopting the impl-plan's names:\n- a line ending ` record=failed`, whatever precedes it, read before any other field →\n  `step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded` (the name spec FR-2 \"CLI verb\" already gives);\n2500→- `invalid field=` → `step5:implementer_invalid`;\n- `no-record` or `unreadable` → `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n\nEach prints its `[H-MAD] <feature> halt <name>` marker. The verb itself raises no halt; the names\nlive in the caller's prose, and the impl-plan's SKILL.md Task pins them.\n\nResidual: the verb reads the root's own state file. The gate folds the whole chain from the target\nup to the root. A nested sub-project state file that holds a record with the same key is read by\nthe gate and not by the verb.\n\n2510→### FR-3 — the `TDD-STATE:` line (DO-1, DO-1a) and the hook's fold\n\n**The shape.** Each record gains a fifth comma field, the **author field**, between the state file\nand the fallback tag. The header does not change.\n\n```\nTDD-STATE: active codex-escape=<yes|no> blocker=<N> records=<N> fallback=<fold> record=<key>,<codex_status>,<state_file>,<author>,<fallback_tag> …\n```\n\n- `<author>` is one of `codex`, `agy`, `peers-out` or `invalid:<pct-json>`. `format_state_line`\n2520→  computes it per record through `resolve_implementer`, evaluating `env` and `which` once per call.\n  `codex` and `agy` mean that peer is resolved. `invalid:` means the record's `implementer` is\n  invalid. `peers-out` means both peers are out, and the fallback tag then decides.\n- The new `STATE_ACTIVE_RE`, which is the one literal in `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh` matching\n  `^STATE_ACTIVE_RE='(.*)'$`:\n\n  ```\n  ^TDD-STATE: active codex-escape=(yes|no) blocker=(0|[1-9][0-9]*) records=([1-9][0-9]*) fallback=(none|(grok|invalid):([1-9][0-9]*))( record=[^ ,]+,[^ ,]+,[^ ,]+,(codex|agy|peers-out|invalid:[^ ,]+),(absent|null|grok|claude|invalid:[^ ,]+))+$\n  ```\n\n2530→  The new capture group follows `group(4)`, and groups 1 to 6, which the hook reads, keep their\n  numbers. No group before it changes, and the grammar uses no construct that Python `re` and POSIX\n  ERE read differently.\n\n**P-1..P-5 hold, checked against candidate lines.** The committed probe\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr3_state_line_grammar.py` derives the new grammar from\nthe hook's current `STATE_ACTIVE_RE` literal (it refuses if that literal's tail has drifted), builds\nlines in this shape, and matches each one with Python `re.fullmatch` and with `[[ =~ ]]` under\nevery bash named on its command line. Invocation, from the repository root:\n`python3 docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr3_state_line_grammar.py /bin/bash /opt/homebrew/bin/bash`.\n2540→One reading at `200ffd52`: `PROBE: PASS`, under `/bin/bash` 3.2.57, the hook's shebang, and\nHomebrew bash 5.3.20. A control copy that expects the today-shape line to match prints\n`PROBE: FAIL`, so the probe can fail. What it shows:\n- P-1: the header is unchanged, so ` records=1 fallback=grok:1 record=` stays contiguous.\n- P-2: Python and both bash versions gave the same groups 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6 on a grok fold, an agy\n  record and a two-record line whose first record has an invalid implementer, and `group(4)`\n  equalled the fold.\n- P-3: the fallback tag is still the last comma token.\n- P-4: the header is unchanged.\n- P-5: fields 0, 1 and 2 after the first ` record=` are still the key, the status and the file.\n2550→\nA today-shape line, and a line whose author field is `grok`, matched neither engine. So a missing\nor unknown author field refuses `judge-error`, which is the rule FR-3 requires.\n\n**Why the author field's value is right whatever `PATH` holds.** P-1 and P-2 run in-process with\nthe ambient `PATH`, where `agy` resolves (`which agy` → `/Users/kimhawk/.local/bin/agy` at\n`29ae3e7b`). Neither assertion reads the author field, and the grammar admits all four values, so\nboth stay byte-identical and green. The `fallback=` fold is still computed over the records alone\n(`_fallback_fold` is untouched).\n\n2560→**The hook.** `_read_state` keeps its header checks unchanged: the record count, escape against\nblocker, and both range checks. `codex-escape=` and `blocker=` are no longer decision inputs. They\nstay because P-4 pins them and because they still check the line's integrity. In the record loop,\nthe author field is taken by stripping three leading fields (`${rest#*,}` three times, then `%%,*`).\nThe key (`%%,*`), the file (the third field) and the tag (`##*,`) are read exactly as P-5 needs.\nThe loop sets two values:\n- `INVALID_RECORD`: the first record whose author starts with `invalid:`;\n- `PEER_RECORD` and `PEER`: the first record whose author is `codex` or `agy`.\n\nThe `BLOCKER_RECORD` capture, which only the old authorship block read, is removed. After\n2570→`_read_state --target`, the block that tested `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE`, `ESCAPE` and\n`command -v codex` is replaced by FR-3's precedence:\n1. **`INVALID_RECORD` set → `implementer-invalid`.** The text is new:\n   `implementer=$VALUE is not valid (valid: codex|agy|null) — refusing the write, fail-closed.` and\n   then ``  Fix it: python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_write.py --feature $KEY --set implementer=codex|agy|null \"$FILE\"``.\n   `$VALUE` is the decoded JSON.\n2. **`PEER_RECORD` set → `codex-authorship` or `agy-authorship`.** The `codex-authorship` text is\n   the current literal, byte for byte. Only the variables it interpolates are renamed, from\n   `BLOCKER_KEY`/`BLOCKER_FILE` to `PEER_KEY`/`PEER_FILE`, so the rendered bytes for a given record\n   are unchanged. The `agy-authorship` text is new and mirrors it. It names\n2580→   `hmad-dispatch exec agy <promptfile>` with `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --agent agy`, the\n   `--set agy_status=exhausted` command for `$PEER_KEY`/`$PEER_FILE`,\n   `export HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE=1 for a one-off`, and `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer` as the way to\n   see who resolves.\n3. **Otherwise, the existing `fallback_agent` block runs unchanged**, including the `M:F1`\n   tag-agreement check. It is reached only when every record's author is `peers-out`, which is\n   FR-3's \"both peers out\" condition. The comment above it,\n   `codex-authorship has already refused every case where codex is available`, is rewritten to\n   name both peers.\n\n2590→**Fixture consequence (AC-3.2).** Two literals are re-shaped, and nothing else in either file\nchanges:\n- `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_judge.py` `_tree_b`: the default `state_line` gains `,peers-out` before\n  `,absent`;\n- `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py` `RECORD`: it becomes\n  `\" record=feat,exhausted,/x/docs/.bkit-memory.json,peers-out,\"`.\n\n`test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `ACTIVE_RE` becomes the grammar above. The new fixture is a today-shape\nline → `judge-error`.\n\n2600→**No gate test is unreachable.** This was checked by enumerating the stub sites at `29ae3e7b`:\n- `_tree_b(` matches 9 lines in `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_judge.py`, one `def` and 8 call sites. One of\n  the 8 passes its own `state_line`, whose lines are none of them `TDD-STATE: active`.\n- `_stub_outcome(` matches 6 lines in `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py`, one `def` and 5 call\n  sites.\n\nEvery one reaches its asserted outcome with author `peers-out`:\n- the judge-verb stubs reach the judge exactly as today;\n- `missing-field`, `unknown-kind` and `position-zero` still fail the grammar on their header;\n- the range check and the tag-agreement check still run, because `peers-out` reaches step 3.\n2610→\nThe real-judge gate tests build `PATH` as `<bin_dir>:/usr/bin:/bin` (`_gate`'s `path_tail`), with\nno `agy`, so their outcomes are FR-2's codex-only outcomes. So no contradiction is reported here.\n\n**Mutation rows that follow the line (R-1).** At `29ae3e7b`:\n- 56 rows name the hook and 36 name the judge;\n- M-3's pattern matches 8 rows in 3 specs (`claude_gate_judge_wiring.json` 5,\n  `tdd_judge_chain.json` 2, `tdd_gate_fallback_agent.json` 1), which reproduces the plan's figure;\n- 13 rows anchor inside the regions this design rewrites. In the hook that is 8: 7 in `_read_state`\n  and 1 in the authorship block. In the judge it is 5: 1 in `_active_records` and 4 in\n2620→  `format_state_line`.\n\nThe re-anchoring Task reads every row of both files, not only these, because the regions above are\na proxy. The census is owed as a committed probe (§\"Measurements\").\n\n### FR-6 — the shared gate policy (DO-5)\n\n**Placement: `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module. This is OQ-A, decided\nby the operator (spec D-12).** The tree holds hand-built trees that copy the Codex gate beside a stub judge\nand, at most, the identity module:\n2630→- `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason` copies the identity\n  module in and expects `kind=judge-timeout`;\n- the same file's `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]` builds one with a raising judge and **no**\n  identity module, and expects `kind=judge-error`.\n\nThe committed probe `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr6_import_failure_pair.py`\nrebuilds both trees in a temporary directory it removes, and runs the gate as those tests do.\nInvocation, from the repository root:\n`python3 docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr6_import_failure_pair.py`. One reading at\n`200ffd52`, `PROBE: PASS`:\n2640→- `timeout-tree/unmodified`: exit 0, a deny with `kind=judge-timeout`;\n- `timeout-tree/new-module` (one top-level `from h_mad_gate_policy import _safe_shell_command`\n  added): exit 1, no stdout, `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'h_mad_gate_policy'`;\n- `error-tree/unmodified`: exit 0, a deny with `kind=judge-error`;\n- `error-tree/G2-mutant` (row `G2`'s `replace` applied): exit 1, no stdout,\n  `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'h_mad_target_identity'`.\n\nThe last pair is what the binding below rests on: today the `judge-error` tree's deny is produced\nby `main`'s `except Exception as exc:  # M:G2` catching the identity module's import failure,\nraised from `_load_identity` inside `_main_guarded`. That is the only route by which row `G2`'s\n2650→test reaches `# M:G2`.\n\nSo a **new** module that the gate imports breaks a test that FR-6 says passes unmodified. The\nidentity module is in that tree already, as a copy of the live file, so the move keeps the test\ngreen. It also keeps the spec's letter: a shared module, imported by both gates. The cost is\ncohesion. The module's docstring, `Canonical filesystem identity shared by the H-MAD TDD gates.`,\nis widened to say that it also holds the policy both write gates apply.\n\n**The moved set.** It is 25 names, each moved byte for byte except where noted.\n- The 10 functions of `_safe_shell_command`'s call closure, re-derived with `ast` at `29ae3e7b` as\n2660→  the plan's M-4 does: `_contained_venv_executable`, `_cwd_escapes_root`, `_load_judge`,\n  `_option_value`, `_path_within`, `_payload_cwd_base`, `_safe_argv`, `_safe_hmad_script`,\n  `_safe_shell_command` and `_trusted_executable`.\n- 7 more functions the agy gate needs, so that none of them is copied:\n  - the operator allowance: `_shell_allowance`, `_operator_allowed`, `_is_shell_allowance`, and\n    `_read_regular_text`, which `_shell_allowance` reads through;\n  - the phase-5 activity predicate: `_state_status` and `_any_phase5_status`;\n  - the production predicate: `_is_production_python`. Its `_load_identity().fold_py_suffix` call\n    becomes a direct call to the module's own `fold_py_suffix`, the one non-verbatim edit.\n- The 8 module constants that those functions read: `SIMPLE_SHELL_COMMAND`, `READ_ONLY_COMMANDS`,\n2670→  `SAFE_DISPATCH_VERBS`, `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS`, `TEMP_OUTPUT_OPTIONS`, `SHELL_ALLOW_FILE`,\n  `TRUSTED_BIN_DIRS` and `MALFORMED_WORKDIR`.\n\nNot moved: `_load_identity`, which loads this very module; `_relative_target`, `_tmp_report_target`\nand `_command_cwd`, which are Codex-payload specific (see the binding rule); `_main_guarded`'s\ncomposition of safe-command → allowance → operator check, which carries the Codex-specific refusal\ntext and the `M:F2-WIRE` and `M:SA-WIRE` anchors; and the self-check's `unsafe` tuple in `main`.\n\nThe moved functions' `Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]` still names `h-mad/`, because\n`scripts/` and `hooks/` are siblings. So `hmad-dispatch` and the scripts root resolve as before.\n2680→\n**Binding.** The Codex gate inserts its `scripts` directory into `sys.path`, as `_load_judge`\nalready does, and imports all 25 names by name at module level, inside\n`try: … except Exception as exc: _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR = exc` (`_POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR = None`\nbefore it).\n- **The failure is re-raised inside `_main_guarded`, never answered in `main`.** The first\n  statement of `_main_guarded` is `if _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR is not None: raise\n  _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR`. The existing `except Exception as exc:  # M:G2` in `main` turns it into\n  the same `kind=judge-error` deny it gives today. A deny issued in `main` before `_main_guarded`\n  would also print `judge-error`, but `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]` would then pass with\n2690→  `# M:G2` mutated, so row `G2` would survive (§\"FR-6\" probe, `error-tree/G2-mutant`). Rule over\n  the axis: no new early return is added on any path between `main`'s entry and the `try` that\n  holds `# M:G2`; every new failure is raised inside `_main_guarded`.\n- **`--self-check` under a failed import.** The self-check branch runs before that `try` and\n  calls `_safe_shell_command`. When `_POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR` is set it prints\n  `CODEX-TDD-GATE: FAIL policy-import` to stderr and returns 2, instead of raising `NameError`.\n- **Fixtures.** `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]`, unmodified, is the import-failure fixture:\n  its tree has no identity module, so the guarded import fails and the gate still prints one\n  deny. A new fixture copies the gate beside a real judge and **no** identity module and runs\n  `--self-check`, expecting `FAIL policy-import` and exit 2.\n2700→\nOf the moved names, an existing test reaches these four through the Codex gate module, and\nbinding all 25 covers them:\n- `_safe_shell_command`: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` calls `module._safe_shell_command`;\n- `_payload_cwd_base`: the same file monkeypatches it on the module, then calls\n  `module._relative_target`. `_relative_target` stays in the Codex gate, so it reads the patched\n  binding;\n- `SIMPLE_SHELL_COMMAND`: read from `runpy.run_path(...)` globals, in the same file;\n- `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS`: read as `hook.SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS` in `test_h_mad_codex_runtime.py`.\n\n2710→The four were found with `grep -l \"\\b<name>\\b\"` over `git ls-files 'h-mad/tests/*.py'`, once per\nmoved name. Residual: a test that builds a name at run time is not found by that grep.\n\n**Rows re-pointed (R-3, AC-6.1).** At `29ae3e7b`, 62 rows name the Codex gate. A row is counted when\nits `find` starts inside a moved definition's span. 31 rows qualify, in 4 specs:\n`codex_gate_judge_wiring.json` 21, `codex_gate_shell_allowance.json` 6,\n`assemble_tdd_mutation_rows.json` 2 and `tdd_gate_differential.json` 2. 0 anchors are absent and 0\nare non-unique. The plan's 19, which is the 10-function closure alone, is a subset. The 12 added\nrows come from the allowance functions, `_read_regular_text`, `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS` and\n`_is_production_python`. Each re-pointed row's `file` becomes `h_mad_target_identity.py` under\n2720→`h-mad/scripts/` (written relative to `h-mad/`, as every row's `file` is),\nand each spec holding one is gated on `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n- **The identity module's own rows are rechecked too.** Adding the moved code can make one of the\n  module's existing `find` strings occur twice, and the harness then refuses it. The move Task runs\n  the anchor precheck over every spec naming that file.\n- **AC-6.1's \"one definition\".** A test asserts that each of the 17 moved functions has exactly one\n  `def` across `git ls-files h-mad`.\n\n### FR-4 — the agy write gate (DO-2)\n\n2730→**File and contract.** `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` reads stdin and prints exactly one JSON\nobject, `{\"decision\": \"allow\"}` or `{\"decision\": \"deny\", \"reason\": \"<text> kind=<kind>\"}`, then\nexits 0. `main` builds the decision inside one `try` and prints from a `finally` that emits a\n`judge-error` deny if nothing was decided. The judge, the identity module and the policy are\nimported inside that `try`. So a failed import still yields JSON (AC-4.2).\n\n**The order of evaluation.**\n1. Parse stdin, read with a 2 s bound like the Claude hook's `READER` (the Codex gate's `_payload`\n   calls `json.load(sys.stdin)` unbounded). `toolCall.name` must be a `str`, matched exactly and\n   case-sensitively. **A malformed payload** is exactly: stdin that is not a JSON object, a\n2740→   `toolCall` that is not an object, or a `toolCall.name` that is not a `str`. A `toolCall.args`\n   that is absent or not an object is read as `{}`, as the Codex gate's `_targets` reads a\n   non-`dict` `tool_input`; it is not malformed.\n2. **Class (a) is allowed before any state is read.** Class (a) is allowed whether or not step5 is\n   active, so no state read can change its answer.\n3. **The root.** It is `git -C <handler cwd>/.. rev-parse --show-toplevel`, canonicalised with the\n   identity module's `canonical_directory`.\n   A root that cannot be resolved, or a malformed payload, denies `kind=judge-error`. After the\n   root is resolved the gate calls `os.chdir(root)` once, so that every relative path the shared\n   policy resolves against `Path.cwd()` starts from the root rather than from `<root>/.agents`\n2750→   (class (c) refines this per command).\n4. **Activity.** `phase5_status = _any_phase5_status(root)`, the predicate the Codex gate computes\n   before it looks at the tool. It is an **input** to classes (b), (c) and (d), never a global\n   shortcut: the Codex gate judges a production `.py` target through `read_chain` even when the\n   `rglob` scan reads `inactive` (its `for raw in targets:  # M:G5` loop has no status filter, and\n   row `G5`'s test `test_unsearchable_docs_on_the_chain_denies_although_the_scan_is_inactive`\n   kills a mutant that adds one). `rglob` skips a directory it cannot search, so an `inactive` scan\n   does not prove the chain inactive. v1.0's \"`inactive` allows\" before class (b) was that mutant.\n5. **The `workspacePaths` cross-check, only under `S`** (`S` stands for `phase5_status in\n   {\"active\", \"unknown\"}` throughout this section). When `S` holds, `workspacePaths` is a list,\n2760→   and no entry resolves inside the root, the gate denies `kind=judge-error`. When `phase5_status`\n   is `inactive` the check stands down (spec FR-4 §\"Project root\", AC-4.1), and evaluation goes\n   on to step 6. v1.1 made this deny unconditional, so `run_command` `ls` with\n   `workspacePaths=[]` and an inactive state was refused (design audit cycle 2).\n6. **Classes (b), (c) and (d)**, as below. For (c) and (d), `inactive` allows and `unknown`\n   denies `kind=judge-error`. The Codex gate also refuses a shell command under `unknown`, but its\n   reason carries no `kind=` token (`H-MAD state is unreadable; refusing shell execution\n   fail-closed.`); the agy gate adds the kind, and the Codex gate has no counterpart for class (d).\n   Class (b) passes the status into its per-target sequence. An exception anywhere after step 2\n   denies `kind=judge-error`.\n2770→\n**The deny kinds: a closed set of five gate kinds plus the judge's.** Every deny the gate prints\ncarries exactly one `kind=` token, from this list:\n- `judge-error`: the gate could not reach a judgement (the rows of the table below that say so);\n- `tool-not-admitted`: class (d) under `active` (spec FR-4's table);\n- `target-outside-root`: T4's refusal;\n- `allowance-file-write`: T5's refusal;\n- `shell-not-admitted`: class (c)'s policy refusal (`_safe_shell_command` false and\n  `_operator_allowed` false);\n- at T7 only, the judge's own `verdict.kind`, passed through unchanged. It is a member of\n2780→  `h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `KINDS`, which holds 12 names at `cff71b49` (`red-measured`,\n  `no-test-resolved`, `test-missing`, `venv-escapes-root`, `pytest-missing`, `pytest-error`,\n  `no-tests-ran`, `no-summary`, `test-passing`, `timeout`, `judge-timeout`, `judge-error`).\n\nResidual: a kind the judge adds to `KINDS` later is passed through without a change here, so the\nclosed set over the gate's own denies is five names, and over T7 it is whatever `KINDS` holds.\nWhere the Codex gate's deny carries a kind (`judge-error` on an unresolvable target, an unreadable\nchain, a bad root; the judge's kind), the agy gate's carries the same one. Where the Codex deny\ncarries none (out-of-root target, allowance-file write, no write target, shell under `unknown`,\nan unreadable allowance file, the shell policy: 6 of the 13 `_deny(` call sites in\n2790→`h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py` at `cff71b49`, counted from `grep -n '_deny(' ` minus the\n`def _deny` line, with the one multi-line call read whole, as spec v1.8 FR-4 counts them), the\nagy kind is the one in the table below. Spec v1.8 FR-4 §\"Every deny carries a kind\" owns\n`judge-error`, `tool-not-admitted` and the judge's kind; the other three names are this design's.\n\n**Every deny, and when it is gated on step5.** The rule over the class: a deny that depends on\nthe payload's tool or targets fires only under `S`, except the two that follow the Codex gate in\nrunning whatever the scan reads (T7, because the chain is the authority, `M:G5`); a deny that\nfires before the status can be known, or because judging failed, is unconditional.\n\n2800→| Deny | When | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| malformed payload (step 1) | always: no tool is named, so no class applies | `judge-error` |\n| root cannot be resolved (step 3) | always: the status cannot be read without a root (a deliberate difference, below) | `judge-error` |\n| an exception after step 2, and the `finally` with nothing decided | always, as the Codex gate's `M:G2` | `judge-error` |\n| `workspacePaths` names nothing inside the root (step 5) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| class (b) with no target (no recognised key, or no recognised value that is a non-empty `str`) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T2 unresolvable | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T2a an existing directory (OD-2) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T4 outside the root, not admitted | `S` | `target-outside-root` |\n2810→| T5 the allowance file | `S` | `allowance-file-write` |\n| T7 chain unreadable | whatever the status (`M:G5`) | `judge-error` |\n| T7 judge non-ALLOW | whatever the status; reached only on an `active` chain | the judge's kind |\n| class (c) `CommandLine` missing or not a `str` | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) under `unknown` | `unknown` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) `_shell_allowance` returns a `str` (the allowance file unreadable or unparsable) | `active` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) policy refusal | `active` | `shell-not-admitted` |\n| class (d) | `active` | `tool-not-admitted` |\n| class (d) | `unknown` | `judge-error` |\n\n2820→The table is the whole set: 17 rows (v1.4 adds T2a), counted from this table, and every `deny` this section\nnames elsewhere is one of them. Under `inactive` the only denies left are the three unconditional\nrows and the two T7 rows; AC-4.1's \"allows every tool\" holds for every payload whose root resolves\nand whose chain is not `active` or `unreadable`. The T7 exception is v1.1's (step 4): an\n`inactive` scan does not prove the chain inactive.\n\n**Class (a), the read-only allowlist: 11 names.** `view_file`, `list_dir`, `grep_search`,\n`find_by_name`, `command_status`, `read_url_content`, `search_web`, `ask_question`, `finish`,\n`wait` and `wait_5_seconds`.\n- **The rule over the class.** A tool is in (a) only if it appears in a recorded `init.tools`\n2830→  inventory, and only if it writes nothing to the filesystem, starts no process, and drives no\n  other agent, MCP server or browser.\n- `finish` and the two waits are included because denying them leaves a headless agy unable to\n  end or pace its turn, and they touch nothing.\n- **Inventory.** The `init.tools` array of\n  `docs/03-analysis/probes/multi-host-runtime/rehearsal/live-agy-2026-10-01.log` holds 58 distinct\n  names (parsed as the first `init` event's `tools`, at `29ae3e7b`). All 11 are in it.\n- **That log carries no agy version.** Its `init` object has only `cwd`, `tools` and\n  `permission_mode`. The installed agy reports `1.4.0` (spec v1.7 FR-8 §\"Version\" records the\n  same at `200ffd52`; spec v1.6 and plan v1.4 read `1.3.6`).\n2840→  So the inventory is re-taken, with `agy --version`, by FR-8 step 1 (§\"FR-8\").\n- **Residual.** Membership is judged from the tool's name and agy's documentation, not observed. A\n  tool whose name reads as read-only but which writes would be admitted. The 11 names are the whole\n  exposure, and egress over the network is outside this gate.\n\n**Class (b), path-write: 5 names.** `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content`,\n`multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file` and `notebook_edit`.\n- **Path keys.** A per-tool table, `PATH_KEYS`, holds only observed keys. At `29ae3e7b` it holds\n  `write_to_file` → `TargetFile`. That key was seen in stream-json `tool_info.parameters` in 4\n  committed logs under `docs/03-analysis/probes/multi-host-runtime/rehearsal/`\n2850→  (`agy-G7-tool-write-then-link-read.log`, `agy-L3-unmapped-tool.log`, `agy-L9-no-step-index.log`,\n  `agy-L11-duplicate-index.log`), and never in a hook payload.\n- **Fail-closed.** The call's targets are the values of the keys from its table entry that are\n  present **and are a non-empty `str`** (spec v1.8 FR-4 §\"Target keys\"). A recognised key whose\n  value is anything else (`\"\"`, `null`, a number, a list) counts as no path key, and `\"\"` is never\n  canonicalised: v1.1 passed `\"\"` to T1, which design audit cycle 2 (agy) traced to the root\n  directory, no production name at T6, and an allow during active RED. A class-(b) call\n  with no target is denied `kind=judge-error` under `S` and allowed under `inactive`, as the Codex\n  gate denies a `Write`/`Edit`/`apply_patch` with no target (`[path] if path else []`, then\n  `not targets and phase5_status in {\"active\", \"unknown\"}`). That covers the other four tools until\n2860→  FR-8 step 1 records their keys. The non-`str` arm is stricter than the Codex gate, which\n  stringifies a truthy non-`str` value (`str(args.get(\"file_path\") or …)`) and judges it as a path;\n  that is a deliberate difference toward refusal.\n- **Judging a target: the Codex gate's per-target sequence, step for step.** The class is \"every\n  rule the Codex gate's `_main_guarded` applies to one target\" (its `_relative_target` and the\n  body of `for raw in targets:  # M:G5`), not the two rules the audit named. Each recognised\n  key's value `raw` goes through these steps, in this order, and the first that decides wins.\n  `S` stands for `phase5_status in {\"active\", \"unknown\"}`.\n  - **T1 canonicalise.** `canonicalise(str(root), str(Path(raw).expanduser()), str(root))`, the\n    Codex gate's call with the root as the base: agy sends no payload cwd.\n2870→  - **T2 unresolvable.** `resolved.unresolvable` → if `S`, deny `kind=judge-error`; else allow.\n    This runs before containment, as in `_relative_target`, which returns an unresolvable identity\n    before it tests `relative_to(root)`.\n  - **T2a an existing directory (OD-2, operator, 2026-10-16; v1.4).** `Path(resolved.target).is_dir()`\n    → if `S`, deny `kind=judge-error` with a reason naming the directory target; else allow. It runs\n    right after T2 and before T3, so an out-of-root directory under `S` denies `judge-error` too.\n    Without it the sequence allows a directory under `active`: `canonicalise` gives a directory\n    target (`.`, `src/`, `src`, the root itself) `unresolvable=False` and `names=()`, so T2 does not\n    fire, T3 and T5 pass, and T6 has no name to judge (scratch call at `84ec2133`, deleted). A\n    symlink to a directory is a directory here, since `is_dir` follows it. Residual: a target\n2880→    ending in `/` that names no existing directory is not a directory; `canonicalise` gives it\n    `names=('nodir',)` (same scratch call), and it goes through T3–T7 as a file name.\n  - **T3 containment, before anything that assumes it.** `Path(resolved.target).relative_to(root)`\n    raising `ValueError` means outside the root, and the target goes to T4 and never to T5–T7.\n    The order is load-bearing: `_is_shell_allowance` calls `candidate.relative_to(root)` without\n    catching `ValueError`, so calling it on an outside target raises (a scratch call at\n    `200ffd52` on a canonicalised `/tmp/audit-report.txt` raised `ValueError`), and the step-6\n    catch would turn every `/tmp` write into `judge-error`. The moved helper stays byte for byte;\n    the order makes it safe.\n  - **T4 outside the root.** Allowed when **no** name in `resolved.names` folds to `.py` under\n2890→    `fold_py_suffix`, and `resolved.target` is within `/tmp` by `_path_within`, or within the\n    payload's `artifactDirectoryPath` when that is a non-empty `str` (anything else is treated as\n    absent: `Path(\"\").resolve() == Path.cwd()` held in a scratch call at `200ffd52`, so an empty\n    value would make the allowance the process's working directory instead of anything the payload\n    named). Otherwise, if `S`, deny `kind=target-outside-root`; else allow. The artifact\n    allowance is computed before `S` is read, as `artifact = Path(adp) if isinstance(adp, str)\n    and adp else None` with `adp = payload.get(\"artifactDirectoryPath\")`, so a non-`str` value\n    never reaches `Path` (`Path(5)`, `Path([1])`, `Path({})` and `Path(True)` each raise\n    `TypeError`, scratch call at `cff71b49`, deleted).\n  - **T5 the allowance file.** If `S` and `_is_shell_allowance(root, resolved)`, deny\n2900→    `kind=allowance-file-write`. The helper\n    already tests the target and every same-parent hard-link name, casefolded.\n  - **T6 production, over every name.** For each `name` in `resolved.names`, `relative =\n    (Path(resolved.target).parent / name).relative_to(root).as_posix()`; collect those for which\n    `_is_production_python(relative)` holds. None → allow. Otherwise take `min` of the collected\n    `(name, absolute, relative)` tuples, as the Codex gate does. Judging only `resolved.target`\n    would miss production: `resolved.target` is `names[0]`, so with `src/test_a.py` hard-linked to\n    `src/z.py` the identity is `names=('test_a.py', 'z.py')` and the target names the test file\n    (scratch call at `200ffd52`: target-only `False`, any-name `True`).\n  - **T7 the chain.** `chain = read_chain(root, absolute)`. `chain.value == \"unreadable\"` → deny\n2910→    `kind=judge-error`; `chain.value != \"active\"` → allow; else `judge(root, absolute,\n    chain.records)`, and a non-ALLOW denies with the judge's kind. T6–T7 run whatever\n    `phase5_status` is (step 4).\n- **Deliberate differences from the Codex gate's target sequence**, each failing toward refusal or\n  forced by the payload:\n  - the base for a relative `raw` is the root, never a payload cwd (agy sends none);\n  - T4 admits `artifactDirectoryPath` as well as `/tmp` (spec FR-4);\n  - T4 tests every name in `resolved.names` for `.py`, where the Codex gate's `_tmp_report_target`\n    tests only the spelled path's resolved name. The spelled name and the referent's name are both\n    same-directory links, so they are in `names`, and the agy refusal set contains the Codex one;\n2920→  - a root that cannot be canonicalised denies outright (step 3); the Codex gate inspects the\n    component and allows when its scan reads `inactive` (`M:CX-ROOT-ENTER`);\n  - (v1.2) every deny carries a kind from the closed set above, where 6 of the Codex gate's 13 do\n    not; spec v1.8 FR-4 adopts the four above and this one as its fifth;\n  - (v1.2) a recognised path key whose value is a truthy non-`str` is no target here (spec v1.8\n    FR-4 §\"Target keys\"), where the Codex gate stringifies it;\n  - (v1.4) T2a refuses an existing-directory target under `S` (OD-2); the Codex gate's per-target\n    sequence has no such step (its three `is_dir` calls test a root candidate or a payload cwd,\n    never a write target).\n  Residual: any rule the Codex gate gains later is not inherited, because the per-target\n2930→  composition is written in both gates, like the shell composition below. The target\n  differential test (§\"Test Plan\", AC-4.1) is what catches that drift.\n\n**Class (c): `run_command`.** The command is `args.CommandLine`. A missing or non-`str` value\ndenies `kind=judge-error` under `S` and is allowed under `inactive` (v1.1 denied it whatever the\nstatus, against AC-4.1; design audit cycle 2). The Codex gate has no counterpart: a shell tool\nwith no command reaches none of its shell denies.\n- **The command's directory, `cwd`, computed as the Codex gate's `_command_cwd` computes it**,\n  with the root standing in for the session cwd agy does not send. `args.Cwd` was observed beside\n  `CommandLine` in 4 of 630 stream-json `run_command` parameter objects across the committed\n2940→  probes (counted as objects, not distinct calls).\n  - `Cwd` present and not a `str` (`0`, a list, a dict, `true`) → `MALFORMED_WORKDIR`;\n  - `Cwd` absent, `None` or `\"\"` → the root, as `_command_cwd` returns the session for an empty\n    workdir;\n  - a relative `Cwd` → `str(root / Path(Cwd).expanduser())`, joined against the root, never\n    against the handler's directory;\n  - an absolute `Cwd` → `str(Path(Cwd).expanduser())`.\n\n  `cwd` is therefore always `MALFORMED_WORKDIR` or an absolute `str`, so the process directory\n  cannot change what `_cwd_escapes_root` and `_payload_cwd_base` decide.\n2950→- **The process directory, for relative script and argument tokens.** `_safe_argv` resolves a\n  relative script, and `_path_within` a relative argument, against `Path.cwd()`. The shell runs the\n  command in `cwd`, so the gate resolves them there: when `cwd` is a `str` naming an existing\n  directory, the gate calls `os.chdir(cwd)`; in every other case (`MALFORMED_WORKDIR`, a path that\n  does not exist or is not a directory, or a `chdir` that raises `OSError`) it stays in the root\n  (step 3) and still judges with that `cwd`. **No case denies `judge-error`** — v1.0's \"a failed\n  change denies `judge-error`\" diverged from the Codex gate, which never changes directory, still\n  admits a read-only command such as `ls` under a bad workdir, and refuses a relative `.venv`\n  token there as a policy refusal through `_cwd_escapes_root` (`M:F2-ESCAPE`, `M:F2-MALFORMED`).\n  Why not stay in the root always: a relative script token would then be judged at\n2960→  `root/<token>` while the shell runs `<cwd>/<token>`, and a test-directory file named like an\n  h-mad script would be judged as the real one.\n- **The composition is the Codex gate's**, reading the shared names: `phase5_status == \"unknown\"`\n  denies `kind=judge-error`; `active` runs `_safe_shell_command(command, root, cwd)`, then\n  `_shell_allowance` (a `str` result denies `kind=judge-error`), then `_operator_allowed` (false\n  denies `kind=shell-not-admitted`); `inactive` allows. The refusal tells agy to use the\n  class-(b) tools, and names `SHELL_ALLOW_FILE`.\n- **Fixtures matching the Codex gate's workdir tests**, each run through the agy gate with `Cwd`\n  in place of `workdir`: `test_a_workdir_outside_the_root_stays_refused`'s three shapes\n  (`outside-absolute`, `dotdot-escape`, `absent` = `str(root / \"no-such-dir\")`) deny a relative\n2970→  `.venv` interpreter with the root's own venv as the positive control;\n  `test_a_present_but_malformed_workdir_fails_closed`'s four values deny; `Cwd=\"\"` behaves as\n  absent; and `ls` under each bad `Cwd` is allowed. Each of these denies carries\n  `kind=shell-not-admitted`, never `kind=judge-error`.\n- **Residual of the process directory.** When `cwd` does not exist the gate judges relative tokens\n  against the root, but the shell cannot run the command there either.\n- **Residual.** This six-line composition is the one piece of policy written in both gates. It\n  carries different refusal text, and each copy has its own mutation rows. A differential test pins\n  them equal: a corpus of commands × allowance-file states, judged by both gates, with the same\n  admit/deny in every cell.\n2980→\n**Class (d): everything else → deny, `kind=tool-not-admitted`** when `phase5_status` is\n`active` (`unknown` denies `kind=judge-error` and `inactive` allows, step 6). At the inventory above that is\n41 names: `ask_custom_permission`, `ask_permission`, `browser_click_element`,\n`browser_drag_pixel_to_pixel`, `browser_get_dom`, `browser_get_network_request`, `browser_input`,\n`browser_list_network_requests`, `browser_mouse_down`, `browser_mouse_up`, `browser_move_mouse`,\n`browser_press_key`, `browser_refresh_page`, `browser_resize_window`, `browser_scroll`,\n`browser_scroll_dom`, `browser_select_option`, `browser_subagent`, `call_mcp_tool`,\n`capture_browser_console_logs`, `capture_browser_screenshot`, `click_browser_pixel`,\n`define_subagent`, `delete_knowledge`, `execute_browser_javascript`, `generate_image`,\n2990→`invoke_subagent`, `list_browser_pages`, `list_permissions`, `list_resources`, `manage_inbox`,\n`manage_subagents`, `manage_task`, `notebook_execution`, `open_browser_url`, `read_browser_page`,\n`read_resource`, `run_workflow`, `schedule`, `send_command_input` and `send_message`. The 41 are\n58 − 11 − 5 − 1, derived as a set difference over the parsed inventory. Any name absent from the\ninventory, which includes tools added later, also falls here.\n\n**`--self-check`.** It checks four things: the classifier puts `view_file` in (a) and\n`call_mcp_tool` in (d); `_safe_shell_command` refuses `python3 -c \"open('x','w').write('x')\"`; the\npolicy names import; and `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` is readable. It prints exactly one line on stdout.\n**Rule over the axis (v1.4, orchestrator decision):** a FAIL that is a verdict of a check that ran\n3000→prints its token and exits 0, like PASS; the token carries the verdict. Only a FAIL meaning the\ncheck could not run exits 2. So `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS` and the two verdict FAILs,\n`FAIL classification` and `FAIL shell-policy`, exit 0. `FAIL policy-import` (the judge, the\nidentity module or the policy names do not import, or `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` cannot be read) exits 2.\nFR-6's Codex `FAIL policy-import` exits 2 for the same reason. FR-5's wiring check is unaffected:\nit requires the exact line `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS` with exit 0, so a FAIL line with exit 0 still reads\nas `self-check-failed`. The Codex gate's own `--self-check` breaks this rule today: its verdict\nFAILs `FAIL parser` and `FAIL shell-policy` print to stderr and return 2. The impl-plan's FR-6\nTask (Task 4) corrects `FAIL shell-policy` by orchestrator decision. `FAIL parser` is the same\nclass and is owed (§\"Owed by other documents\"). Residual: a self-check FAIL added later is\n3010→classed by this rule, not listed here. AC-6.1's parity with the Codex list is a test, not this self-check.\nThe test locates the `unsafe` tuple assigned inside the Codex gate's `main` with `ast`, requires\nexactly one, and asserts that the agy gate refuses each member.\n\n**Overwrite residual.** agy merges handlers, and a sibling's `overwrite` is a shallow merge into the\ntool's arguments (hooks.md §\"PreToolUse Contract\"). The gate judges the arguments it receives. A\nsibling that rewrites a path key after this gate judged it is not seen. At `29ae3e7b` the operator's\nglobal `~/.gemini/config/hooks.json` holds one `PreToolUse` group, `orca-status` with matcher `*`,\nwhose script prints `{\"decision\":\"ask\"}` for `PreToolUse` and never `overwrite`.\n\n3020→**Roles.** The gate cannot tell an implementing agy from a reviewing agy. During active step5, a\n5e-review agy in a wired project is therefore held to the same tool and shell rules, as a Codex\nverifier is today. 6a-prime runs after step5 ends, so it is unaffected.\n\n**Inherited residual.** The gate decides \"active\" from the root's own state, as both existing gates\ndo. `docs/.bkit-memory.json` is untracked in this repository and in HemaSuite\n(`git ls-files docs/.bkit-memory.json` prints nothing in either at `29ae3e7b`). How Phase 5c\nprovisions state inside a worktree was not read for this design, so whether the three gates stand\ndown in a worktree is not claimed either way.\n\n3030→**Hook timeout.** The floor is `math.ceil(JUDGE_BUDGET_S + 20)`, read from the module. At\n`29ae3e7b` `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` is `40.0`, so the floor is 60.\n\n### FR-5 — the wiring check, the assembler, and the worktree (DO-6)\n\n**The check.** `h_mad_hook_wiring.py` gains `--agy`. It calls a new `check_agy(project_root)`,\nprints the one line that function returns, and exits with the code beside it. Without `--agy`,\nbehaviour is unchanged. The reasons are evaluated in FR-5's order, and the first one found is\nprinted:\n1. `<root>/.agents/hooks.json` is absent → `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file`, exit 0. When it is\n3040→   present but not JSON, or not a JSON object, the line is `UNREADABLE file=<pct> error=<name>`\n   with exit 2, and the name is `not-an-object` for the second case.\n2. There is no top-level key `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate`, or its value is not an object → `no-entry`.\n3. `enabled` is present and is not `true` → `disabled`.\n4. No `PreToolUse` group has a `matcher` of exactly `*` or `\"\"` → `matcher-narrow`. An absent or\n   empty `PreToolUse` counts here too.\n5. No handler in such a group has a `command` whose `shlex.split` equals\n   `[\"python3\", \"~/.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py\"]` → `bad-command`.\n6. That handler's `timeout` is not an `int` ≥ the floor; an absent timeout means agy's default of\n   30 → `timeout-below-floor`.\n3050→7. `<python3 on PATH> <expanduser(path)> --self-check` must exit 0 and print exactly\n   `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS`, within 30 s. Otherwise → `self-check-failed`. When it passes, the line is\n   `AGY-GATE: WIRED`.\n\nResidual: an equivalent spelling of the command (an absolute path, `$HOME`) reads `bad-command`.\nThat fails toward the halt.\n\n**The assembler.** `--agent` gains `agy`, and the timeout default becomes a table,\n`{\"codex\": 900, \"grok\": 1500, \"agy\": 1500}`, with `--timeout` still winning. The choices line\nbecomes `ap.add_argument(\"--agent\", choices=(\"codex\", \"grok\", \"agy\"), default=\"codex\")`, and the\n3060→timeout line keeps the substring `args.timeout is not None` exactly once\n(`timeout = args.timeout if args.timeout is not None else TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS[args.agent]`), so the\nexisting mutation anchors on it survive (§\"Mutation rows\", \"Existing rows the edits move\"). For `--agent agy`,\n`main` runs the check before `assemble`, as a subprocess:\n`[sys.executable, AGY_WIRING_CHECK, \"--agy\", \"--project-root\", <root>]`, with a 60 s bound.\n`AGY_WIRING_CHECK` is a module constant and the tests' seam. Then FR-5's table applies:\n- `WIRED` → continue to PASS;\n- `UNWIRED` → print `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified`, then the check line indented by\n  two spaces, as existing HALT details are. Write no prompt, exit 0;\n- `UNREADABLE` → the same two lines, exit 2;\n3070→- no `AGY-GATE:` line, or a timeout → `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token`,\n  exit 2.\n\nThe verdict branch does not raise `Halt`, because every `Halt` exits 2. The module docstring's\n`exit 0 on PASS, 2 on any HALT` becomes: `exit 0 on PASS and on HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified after\nAGY-GATE: UNWIRED; 2 on every other HALT`. A test pins that sentence, and a test pins that the old\none is absent. `command_block`'s existing prefix rewrite already emits `hmad-dispatch exec agy`, and\n`exec agy` accepts `--model`, `--effort` and `--timeout` (`hmad-dispatch.sh` `_cmd_exec`).\n\n**DO-6: no override path in code.**\n3080→- **Tests.** Tests of `check_agy` set `HOME` to a fixture directory holding\n  `.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` as a symlink to the gate under test.\n  `os.path.expanduser` honours `HOME`, so the shipped code has no worktree switch.\n- **Any test that runs the assembler with `--agent agy` and expects a prompt** needs that `HOME`\n  fixture **and** a `<root>/.agents/hooks.json` holding FR-5's exact entry in its project root.\n  A bare `tmp_path` reads `no-hooks-file` and halts with no prompt written, which is the designed\n  outcome, not a defect. The fixture is called the **WIRED fixture** below. The check runs in a\n  subprocess, so `AGY_WIRING_CHECK` cannot be monkeypatched from a test that also runs the\n  assembler as a subprocess (as `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py` `run_cli` does); the fixture is\n  the only seam such a test has.\n3090→- **This feature's own Phase-5 worktree.** It ships no `.agents/hooks.json`, so `--agent agy` there\n  reads `no-hooks-file` and halts. That is correct: this feature's implementer is Codex.\n- **An adopting project's worktree.** Its tracked `.agents/hooks.json` reaches the worktree, and\n  the installed path is the skills repository's main checkout. So the check reads `WIRED` once this\n  feature has merged.\n- **This repository's own `.agents/hooks.json`** is committed only by FR-8's post-merge stage, and\n  only after its real refusal passes (§\"FR-8\"). Until then the halt stands here mechanically,\n  because the file is absent. Evidence that tracking it needs no `git add -f`: at `29ae3e7b`,\n  `.git/info/exclude` holds `/.agents/skills/`, and `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints\n  nothing.\n3100→\n### FR-7 — the `review_independence` add (DO-3) and the Phase-7 warnings\n\n**The add.** `h_mad_state_write.py` gains `--append KEY=JSON`, which is repeatable.\n- **Strict decoding.** `json.loads` with no string fallback. A decode failure prints `ERROR: …`\n  and exits 2, and the store is unchanged.\n- **One write, accumulated in command-line order.** `--set` fields and `--append` entries apply\n  inside **one** `_mutate` call, through `merge_fields`, which gains an `appends` parameter. Its\n  `apply` keeps its existing statements byte for byte (`record = {**records[feature], **fields}`,\n  then the owner-heartbeat `if` and its assignment) and runs the appends after them, so the two\n3110→  `heartbeat_beat.json` rows anchored on that `if` still match once (§\"Mutation rows\").\n  `appends` is the list of `(key, entry)` pairs in the order given. Below, `prior` is\n  `records[feature]` and `record` is the merged dict; for each pair in order, `current = record.get(key, [])`\n  is read from the **updated** `record`, and `record[key] = list(current) + [entry]`. Two\n  `--append` flags on one key in one call therefore keep every earlier entry and add both, in\n  order. Reading `prior[key]` instead would keep only the last addition.\n- **`--set` and `--append` on the same key in one call are refused** (`--set and --append name\n  the same key: <key>`), exit 2, store unchanged. `--set` replaces a whole value and `--append`\n  adds to it, so the pair has no single reading; refusing is the defined interaction.\n- **The append-only keys refuse `--set` (v1.3).** `review_independence` and\n3120→  `implementation_authors` are append-only: an entry, once written, is never removed or altered.\n  The writer operations that could replace a declared value are `--set` on the command line and\n  `merge_fields` / `set_fields` for library callers (`set_fields` calls `merge_fields`);\n  `create_feature` leaves an existing record alone and `drop_undeclared` drops only undeclared\n  keys, so neither can touch a declared one. The rule is therefore one check at the top of\n  `merge_fields`, before its `def apply`: a key of `fields` in the module constant\n  `APPEND_ONLY = (\"review_independence\", \"implementation_authors\")` raises `StateWriteError`\n  (`<key> is append-only: use --append`), exit 2, store unchanged. It sits outside `apply`, so\n  `apply`'s statements and the heartbeat rows anchored on them are unmoved. `--append` itself goes\n  through the `appends` parameter, never through `fields`, so it is not refused. Residual,\n3130→  exactly: a writer that bypasses `_mutate` (the same residual as below), and a key added to the\n  schema later as an array of history that is not added to `APPEND_ONLY`.\n- **Refusals.** An existing value that is present and is not a list is refused\n  (`cannot append to a non-array value`). The result is validated by `classify`, so FR-1's item\n  schema applies on both validator paths. A refusal raises before `_atomic_write` and prints the\n  writer's existing `ERROR:` refusal with exit 2.\n- **Heartbeat.** An owner's `--session-id` refreshes the heartbeat, as `merge_fields` does.\n- **Output.** The `STATE-WRITE: OK` line lists the appended keys with the set keys.\n- **Lost-entry detection is not needed, and that is why this mechanism was chosen.** `_mutate`\n  holds `fcntl.flock` on the sidecar lock across load, apply and write, so no other writer that\n3140→  goes through `_mutate` can interleave between the read and the write. Residual: a writer that\n  bypasses `_mutate`, such as a hand edit or another tool's raw write, can still race. That is true\n  of every field.\n- **The orchestrator's step (SKILL.md, FR-10).** After each 5e-review, 5e-verify and 6a-prime leg,\n  it runs `--append review_independence='{\"leg\":…,\"module\":…,\"reviewer\":…,\"implementer\":…,\"independent\":…}'`.\n  `independent` is `reviewer != implementer`. This applies for every implementer.\n- **Attribution: `implementer` is read from the dispatch, as spec FR-7 §\"Attribution\" requires.**\n  It is never read from `implementer_switch.to` or from the `implementer` field, which record\n  routing, not authorship. Counter-example the rule exists for: Codex out → agy dispatched → switch\n  stored; Codex back → Codex dispatched with `switch=none`, and the stored `.to` still says `agy`.\n3150→  - **The authors of a module** are the agents of that module's **accepted implementation\n    dispatches** (spec v1.8 FR-7, E4): the 5e GREEN dispatch the orchestrator accepted, and every\n    later fix dispatch for that module it accepted. A 5d RED dispatch is never one: it writes\n    tests, not the reviewed code (`codex-implementer-prompt.md` assigns GREEN to 5e, `For GREEN\n    phase (5e):`). A dispatch that halted, timed out or was rejected authored nothing. The agent of\n    a dispatch is the value of the `TDD-IMPLEMENTER:` token the orchestrator resolved just before\n    staging it, which is the `--agent` it passed to `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` (`--phase green` for\n    the 5e GREEN dispatch; `--phase` takes `red` or `green`); for the Claude fallback, where no\n    dispatch leaves the session, it is the same token's `claude` value.\n  - **The durable author record (v1.3, OD-E2 option (a), spec FR-1 D-15).** Each accepted\n3160→    implementation dispatch is recorded as one `implementation_authors` entry, through the same\n    `--append` operation as above:\n    `--append implementation_authors='{\"module\":\"<module>\",\"agent\":\"<agent>\"}'`. The writer gains\n    no code for it beyond FR-1's schema entry and the `APPEND_ONLY` refusal: `--append` is\n    key-agnostic, and `classify` applies the item schema on both validator paths.\n  - **Where in the procedure the append happens.** The rule over the axis is an ordering:\n    **every accepted implementation dispatch for a module is appended before any review leg of\n    its code runs** (spec FR-1 D-15), and so before that leg's `review_independence` entry. Its\n    members in the procedure:\n    - **5e GREEN.** In SKILL.md's 5e bullet, when the orchestrator accepts a module's GREEN\n3170→      dispatch, that is when it reads the `STATUS:` token `DONE` or `DONE_WITH_CONCERNS` and stops\n      re-dispatching GREEN for that module. It appends there, before the module's 5e spec-review\n      leg runs. A GREEN that is\n      re-dispatched (the path toward `step5e:green_unreachable:<module>`) is not accepted and is\n      not appended.\n    - **Every accepted fix dispatch** for the module, wherever it is dispatched (a 5e fix after a\n      review or verification finding, or a fix after a 6a-prime `WITH_FIXES`): appended once it\n      is accepted, before the next leg that reviews the fixed code.\n    - **Never 5d RED.** The 5d bullet gains no append.\n\n3180→    An accepted GREEN that a later step halts on (`step5e:verify_failed:<module>`, a failed revert\n    test) keeps its entry, because entries are append-only and its code is still in the tree. That\n    direction is the safe one: an extra author can only turn an entry `independent=false`, never\n    `true`. Residual, exactly: the order is orchestrator prose (SKILL.md, FR-10), and no code\n    refuses a `review_independence` append made before the author append it should follow. A\n    refused author append (an invalid entry) prints the writer's `ERROR:` refusal with the store\n    unchanged; the orchestrator corrects the entry and appends again before the review leg runs\n    (spec FR-1).\n  - **Reading the complete author set.** For module `M`, the set is the `agent` values of the\n    well-formed entries of the record's `implementation_authors` whose `module == M`. Well-formed\n3190→    is `property_valid(\"implementation_authors\", [entry])` (§\"The warnings\" below), the definition\n    the orchestrator applies, since no script reads the field (spec FR-1). The latest\n    accepted implementation dispatch is the last such entry in array order, since `--append` adds\n    at the end and the orchestrator appends at acceptance. The set is read from this field and\n    from nothing else: never from earlier `review_independence` entries, which are a projection of\n    it with one `implementer` per entry. Counter-example: with authors `[agy, codex]`, a grok\n    5e-review stores `implementer=codex` and a Codex 5e-verify stores `implementer=codex`, so a\n    set rebuilt from those entries is `{codex}` and an agy 6a-prime would read as independent\n    (design audit cycle 2, codex). The orchestrator's in-context list of dispatches is not read\n    either: the record is the only source, so a resumed orchestrator reads exactly what an\n3200→    uninterrupted one reads. v1.2 kept the set \"in the procedure\" and is replaced by this.\n  - **A 5e leg** reviews one module. If the reviewer is in that module's author set,\n    `implementer` is the reviewer and `independent` is `false`. Otherwise `implementer` is the\n    agent of the module's latest entry, and `independent` is `true` (spec v1.8 FR-7, S5).\n  - **6a-prime** reviews the whole diff. The orchestrator writes one 6a-prime entry per module,\n    with `module` set, applying the same rule to that module's complete author set.\n  - **When no author can be read, no entry is written.** The leg writes no `review_independence`\n    entry for module `M`, rather than one whose `independent` it cannot establish, in exactly\n    these cases:\n    - the record has no `implementation_authors` field: a record whose implementation dispatches\n3210→      were accepted before this field existed (spec v1.8 FR-7's \"no entry\" rule, kept for those\n      records only). An absent field is never read as an empty author set, which would make\n      every reviewer independent; AC-7.3's pre-field fixture pins this (spec v1.9);\n    - the field is present and holds no well-formed entry for `M`: an author append that was\n      skipped, the residual of the ordering rule above;\n    - the field is present and is not a list, or holds an entry that is not well-formed: a hand\n      edit, since the writer refuses both. One malformed entry may be the lost record of `M`'s\n      author, so the set is not established for any module of that record.\n\n    A resumed orchestrator whose dispatch context is gone is **not** a member: it reads the field\n3220→    like any other. Residual, as the spec states: `review_independence_unrecorded` fires only when\n    no `leg=6a-prime` entry exists at all, so one module missing among several recorded ones has\n    no warning of its own.\n  - **AC-7.3's multi-author recovered fixture, from durable state.** The record holds\n    `implementation_authors` `[{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"agy\"},{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"codex\"}]`. A\n    grok 5e-review: grok is not in `{agy, codex}`, so the entry is `implementer=codex` (the latest\n    entry) and `independent=true`. A Codex 5e-verify: codex is in the set, so the entry is\n    `implementer=codex` and `independent=false`. Then the dispatch context is discarded, and only\n    the record is kept. An agy 6a-prime: agy is in the set read from the record, so the entry is\n    `implementer=agy` and `independent=false`, the outcome AC-7.3 expects. The mutant AC-7.3 names\n3230→    (authors recovered from earlier entries' `implementer` values only) yields `{codex}` and\n    `independent=true`, so the fixture is RED against it. The other three v1.8 AC-7.3 fixtures follow\n    from the same field: a 5d RED appends nothing, so the agy reviewer of a Codex GREEN reads\n    `{codex}`; a rejected agy GREEN appends nothing, so the same holds; and the author set never\n    reads `implementer_switch.to`. The pre-field fixture (spec v1.9) runs the same reviews on a\n    record with no `implementation_authors`, and the 6a-prime leg writes no entry for the module;\n    its mutant reads the absent field as an empty set, so the agy reviewer reads as independent.\n    Whether attribution gets a code surface for these fixtures to\n    drive is a separate open decision, still the impl-plan's (§\"Owed by other documents\").\n  - The writer does not check attribution; the Phase-7 consistency check below verifies only that\n3240→    `independent == (reviewer != implementer)`.\n  - Residual: a module's authors are the agents of accepted dispatches only. Code a reviewer wrote\n    during its own leg (outside an implementation dispatch) is not attributed to it.\n\n**The warnings.** `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` `check` calls a new\n`_review_independence_warnings(record)` and extends `warnings`. It never touches `blockers`, so\n`ready` does not move.\n- **Well-formed means validated by the schema, not by a second copy of the value sets.**\n  `h_mad_state_validate.py` gains `property_valid(name, value)`, which validates `value` against the\n  strict schema's `properties[name]` through `_make_validator`, on both paths. For\n3250→  `implementer_switch` it also applies `rfc3339_datetime` to `ts`, and only when `value` is a\n  `dict`: it calls `_semantic_ok({\"implementer_switch\": value})`, the same predicate `classify`\n  uses, so `None` (the schema's `[\"object\", \"null\"]`) and every other non-`dict` go to the schema\n  alone and never raise (v1.1 applied the predicate unguarded; design audit cycle 2). An entry is well-formed when\n  `property_valid(\"review_independence\", [entry])` holds **and** `entry[\"independent\"] ==\n  (entry[\"reviewer\"] != entry[\"implementer\"])`. A boolean that contradicts its own two agents is\n  malformed, so it is never read as `independent=true`.\n- **`review_independence_malformed`** is emitted for each entry that is not well-formed, and once\n  when the field is present and not a list.\n- **`review_non_independent`** is emitted for each well-formed entry with `independent=false`,\n3260→  naming the leg, the module and the agent.\n- **`review_independence_unrecorded`** is emitted when `record.get(\"archreview\")` is in\n  `{\"READY_TO_MERGE\", \"WITH_FIXES\", \"NO\"}` and no **well-formed** entry has `leg == \"6a-prime\"`.\n  - The code path has no implementer condition (D-11), and it reads neither `implementer` nor\n    `implementer_switch`, nor `implementation_authors` (an authorship record no script reads,\n    spec FR-1 D-15), so AC-7.2's per-implementer fixtures (`grok` and `claude` through\n    `implementer_switch.to`) all reach the same branch.\n  - The detail text names the agents the record's entries attest (a projection, which is why it\n    is only detail text and never an author set the attribution reads): the distinct `implementer` values\n    of the well-formed 5e entries, sorted, or `authors=unrecorded` when there are none. It never\n3270→    names `implementer_switch.to` or `implementer`: they record routing, and the same attribution\n    rule as the entries applies to every surface that names an author.\n  - A malformed 6a-prime entry does not count as recorded, so it raises both warnings.\n- Each warning prints through the existing `  WARNING <code>: <detail>` loop in `main`.\n\n### FR-8 — the tracer, after the merge (DO-6, R-2) — OQ-B, spec D-13\n\n**Why after the merge.** The tracer needs agy to run the gate at the installed path,\n`~/.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/` → `/Users/kimhawk/orca/skills/h-mad`\n(`ls -la ~/.gemini/config/skills` at `29ae3e7b`). That path is main, and main gains the gate only\n3280→when this feature merges. An override flag would ship a code path that points the check away from\nthe installed gate. A hand edit of the symlink would also change every live session.\n\n**The stage.** It runs after Phase 7's integrate and before the feature is recorded COMPLETE.\n1. **A fixture project.** It is a throwaway git repository with one module task. It has its own\n   `docs/.bkit-memory.json` step5 record and its own `.agents/hooks.json`, holding FR-5's exact\n   entry. It is created beside this checkout, never under `/tmp`, because agy's trust decision for\n   `.agents/hooks.json` may depend on location.\n2. **Step 1, payload capture.** The real class-(b) payload keys are recorded, and so is\n   `init.tools` with `agy --version`. They land as one commit on main that fills `PATH_KEYS` and\n3290→   re-checks the class-(a) inventory. That commit carries tests built from the captured payloads,\n   written RED then GREEN, and it lands before step 2. The capture hook is then deleted.\n   **D-FR8-1 (2026-10-17):** on agy `1.4.0` only `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content` and\n   `run_command` are declared to the model; `multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file` and\n   `notebook_edit` appear in `init.tools` only. Step 1 captures the three declared tools; the\n   other three keep no `PATH_KEYS` entry and deny under active step5 (impl-plan Task 20 v1.3).\n3. **Steps 2, 2a and 3** in the fixture project, as FR-8 states them. Step 2a's delay hook is\n   added to, and removed from, the fixture's `hooks.json`, and the before and after\n   `shasum -a 256` are recorded.\n4. **This repository's adoption.** `.agents/hooks.json` is committed here, and step 2 is re-run\n3300→   once in this repository to settle trust at this location.\n5. The derived timeout replaces `agy`'s `1500` in the assembler table, as FR-8's formula sets.\n\nEvery reading is stamped with the sha and `agy --version` under\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`.\n\n**AC-8.1's formula check is a probe check, not a suite test.** A committed script asserts that the\nassembler's `agy` default equals `max(900, 3 × slower)` rounded up to a multiple of 60 over the"}}],"rawOutput":{"type":"ReadFile","FileContent":{"content":"2308→               │        │                 │\n   TDD-STATE: line   TDD-IMPLEMENTER:     ├── h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py ──┐\n2310→               │        │                 └── h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py ────┤ both import the\n   h-mad-tdd-gate.sh  orchestrator ──► h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --agent    │ gate policy from\n   (Claude's writes)                    └─ agy: h_mad_hook_wiring.py --agy\n                                                                        │\n                                          h_mad_target_identity.py ◄────┘\n```\n\n- The judge is the only place `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE`, `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE` and `PATH` are read\n  for authorship. It runs as a child of the Claude hook, so it sees the hook's environment.\n- The Claude hook parses the line and refuses. It re-derives nothing.\n2320→- The agy gate and the Codex gate share one copy of the shell policy, the allowance file rules,\n  the phase-5 activity predicate and the production predicate.\n\n## Detailed Design\n\n### FR-1 — state fields, and the timestamp refusal (DO-4)\n\n**Schema.** Five properties are added to `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json`. `required` does\nnot change, and `additionalProperties` stays `false`. Every construct below is in the bundled\nvalidator's supported set (`_MiniDraft7`'s docstring, the `Deliberately supports ONLY` paragraph):\n2330→`type`, `enum`, `required`, `properties`, `additionalProperties`, `items`, and (v1.3, for\n`implementation_authors`) `minLength`. No `oneOf`, `anyOf`,\n`const` or `pattern` is used, so the two validator paths cannot disagree on structure.\n\n**Property order.** The five are appended, in the order listed below, after `fallback_agent`, which is the schema's last\nproperty today (`list(properties)` ends `…, 'codex_status', 'fallback_agent'` at `200ffd52`).\n`test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts`\nasserts `list(properties).index(\"fallback_agent\") == list(properties).index(\"codex_status\") + 1`,\nso any new property placed between those two breaks AC-1.1. Rule over the axis: no property is\ninserted anywhere before `fallback_agent`.\n2340→\n- `implementer`: `enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", null]`.\n- `agy_status`: `enum` `[\"available\", \"unavailable\", \"exhausted\", null]`.\n- `implementer_switch`: `type` `[\"object\", \"null\"]`. Properties `from` and `to` take `enum`\n  `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`, `reason` takes `enum` `[\"env\", \"status\", \"cli-absent\"]`, and\n  `ts` is `type` `string` with `\"format\": \"date-time\"` as an annotation only. All four are\n  `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`.\n- `review_independence`: `type` `array`. Its `items` is an object with `leg`\n  (`enum` `[\"5e-review\", \"5e-verify\", \"6a-prime\"]`), `module` (`type` `[\"string\", \"null\"]`),\n  `reviewer` and `implementer` (each `enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`) and `independent`\n2350→  (`type` `boolean`). All five are `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`.\n- `implementation_authors` (v1.3, operator decision OD-E2, spec FR-1 D-15): `type` `array`. Its\n  `items` is an object with `module` (`type` `string`, `minLength` `1`: it is the `--module` value\n  the assembler requires) and `agent` (`enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`). Both are\n  `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`. The array is a log, not a set: duplicates\n  are kept, and array order is acceptance order (spec FR-1 D-15). One entry is one accepted implementation dispatch; §\"FR-7\" says who writes it and when,\n  and how attribution reads it. `module` is a `string`, not `[\"string\", \"null\"]` as in\n  `review_independence`, because an implementation dispatch is always for one module (5e stages\n  one module per GREEN). It is placed last, after `review_independence`, so the AC-1.1 index\n  assertion on `fallback_agent` is untouched. `minLength` is in `_MiniDraft7`'s supported set (its\n2360→  docstring's `Deliberately supports ONLY` paragraph names it, and its `_ok` checks it on a\n  `str`), and the schema already uses it, so the two validator paths cannot disagree on its\n  structure. Fixtures: AC-1.3 (§\"Test Plan\").\n\n**The `.ts` refusal: a predicate outside the validator backend.** The option chosen is the third of\nthe three the spec lists, placed in the validator module rather than in the writer, so that a\nhand-edited record is refused by `h_mad_state_validate.py` too.\n- **The probe it rests on.** Reading at `29ae3e7b`, the plan's command\n  (`/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3 -B -c '… Draft7Validator(s).is_valid(x) … v._MiniDraft7(s).is_valid(x)'`\n  with `s={\"type\":\"string\",\"format\":\"date-time\"}` and `x=\"not-a-date\"`) prints\n2370→  `Draft7Validator: True` and `bundled: True`. `format` is enforced on neither path, so declaring it\n  refuses nothing.\n- **The predicate.** `h_mad_state_validate.py` gains `rfc3339_datetime(value) -> bool`:\n  - `re.fullmatch` with `re.ASCII` over\n    `([0-9]{4})-([0-9]{2})-([0-9]{2})[Tt]([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2})(\\.[0-9]+)?([Zz]|[+-]([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2}))`;\n  - then `datetime.datetime(y, mo, d, h, mi, s)` must construct, which refuses `2026-02-30`;\n  - then an offset's hours must be at most 23 and its minutes at most 59;\n  - a non-`str` is refused.\n\n  `fullmatch` and `[0-9]` are deliberate: `$` admits a trailing newline, and `\\d` admits non-ASCII\n2380→  digits. A scratch probe at `29ae3e7b`, since deleted, returned these results under both\n  `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3` (3.11.8) and `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` (3.15.0):\n  - refused: `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`, `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z`, an offset of `+24:00`, a seconds\n    field of 60, a value with\n    full-width digits, the writer's form followed by `\\n`, `5` and `None`;\n  - accepted: `2026-10-16T07:05:09Z` and `2026-10-16T07:05:09.123+09:00`.\n- **Where it applies.** A private `_semantic_ok(record)` returns `False` exactly when `record` is\n  a `dict` whose `implementer_switch` is a `dict` whose `ts` fails `rfc3339_datetime`; it returns\n  `True` for anything else, a non-`dict` record included, so it never raises.\n  `classify` keeps its existing first statement (`if not isinstance(record, dict): return\n2390→  \"invalid\"`, which is what `test_h_mad_state_validate_fallback.py` `CORPUS`'s\n  `(\"not an object\", [\"nope\"])` row exercises), then calls `_semantic_ok` and returns `invalid`\n  when it fails, before either schema tier is tried. That runs whichever\n  backend `_make_validator` chose, so both paths refuse. The writer refuses through `classify` as it\n  does today (`_mutate`: `classify(record) != \"strict\"`), and it prints its existing refusal.\n- **No library format checker is used.** The `rfc3339-validator` hazard therefore does not bind,\n  and AC-1.2's package-absent fixture is not owed, since AC-1.2 makes it conditional on that\n  mechanism. Recorded for the reader: at `29ae3e7b`, `rfc3339_validator` imports in\n  `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3`, and `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` has no `jsonschema`.\n- **Not schema-wide.** `started_ts`, `owner_heartbeat_ts` and `halt_ts` stay annotation-only, so\n2400→  no existing record can change class. The `classify_store` run the spec asks of a schema-wide\n  mechanism is not owed. A reading anyway, taken 2026-10-16 with this repository at `29ae3e7b` and\n  HemaSuite at `77fb16f51` (both files are untracked, so the sha does not pin their content): this\n  repository's `docs/.bkit-memory.json` has 54 records and HemaSuite's has 7. Zero records in either file carry\n  any of the four FR-1 keys. That zero is load-bearing, not incidental: the strict schema has\n  `additionalProperties: false`, so the writer has refused those keys until now. A hand-edited\n  record is the residual. The fifth key, `implementation_authors` (v1.3), has the same zero for\n  the same load-bearing reason: one reading on 2026-10-16, this repository at `3ad735f7` and\n  HemaSuite at `02d52e4e7`, over each file's `orchestrator_state` object, gives 54 and 8 records,\n  and 0 records in either file carry any of the five keys.\n2410→- **A seconds field of 60 is refused, as spec FR-1 requires (D-14).** RFC 3339 admits a leap\n  second; spec v1.7 FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`, because every `.ts`\n  is machine-written by h-mad's own writer through `datetime`, which cannot hold second 60. The predicate refuses it at the `datetime` construction\n  step, and a fixture pins `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` refused and `2016-12-31T23:59:59Z` accepted on\n  both paths.\n\n**Fixtures (AC-1.2).** One fixture per value per path, with the bundled path forced through the\nexisting `_FORCE_FALLBACK` seam. Refused: `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`, `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z`, and\n`2026-10-16T00:00:00Zjunk` and `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z`. `…Zjunk` kills a `fullmatch` → `match`\nmutation. Accepted: the writer's `_utc_now` form and `2016-12-31T23:59:59Z`. Each alternative of the zone group has its own fixture, run with that\n2420→branch alone: `…Z`, `…z`, `…+09:00` accepted, and `…+24:00` refused.\n\n**The `fallback_agent` description, and the same class on `codex_status`.** Spec FR-1 names the\n`fallback_agent` description and its `state-schema.md` row. The class is \"a surface that says what\nan unavailable Codex lets Claude do\". It has two more members in the tree:\n- the `codex_status` schema `description`, which ends \"permitting Claude to write the fallback\n  (still test-first)\" and \"One switch, both effects\";\n- its `state-schema.md` row, \"An unavailable or exhausted status permits a Phase-5 authoring\n  fallback\".\n\n2430→Both are false once agy is available. All four surfaces are rewritten to the FR-2 condition.\n`test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts` keeps\nits four substrings, for example \"read only when codex is out and agy is out too\". No test pins the\n`codex_status` wording: `git ls-files 'h-mad/tests/*.py' | xargs grep -ln 'permitting Claude\\|One switch, both effects'`\nprints nothing at `29ae3e7b`. The spec does not list the `codex_status` pair; see §\"Owed by other\ndocuments\".\n\n### FR-2 — `resolve_implementer` and the `implementer` verb\n\n**Record.** `Record` gains two trailing fields with defaults: `implementer: str = \"absent\"` and\n2440→`agy_status: str = \"available\"`. The defaults keep every positional four-argument construction\nvalid. One exists in the tree: the replace text of a `codex_gate_judge_wiring.json` row\n(`git ls-files -z h-mad | xargs -0 grep -l 'Record('` → `h_mad_tdd_judge.py` and that spec,\n2 files). `_active_records` fills both through two helpers:\n- `_implementer_tag(state)` returns `absent`, `null`, `codex`, `agy` or `invalid:<json>`, built like\n  `_fallback_tag`;\n- `agy_status` is read the way `codex_status` is: `\"available\"` when the value is `None` or the key\n  is absent, else `str(value)`.\n\n**Resolution.** `resolve_implementer(record, env=None, which=None) -> Implementer` is the only\n2450→resolver. `env` defaults to `os.environ` and `which` to `shutil.which`. `Implementer` is a\n`NamedTuple` with these fields: `resolved` (`codex|agy|grok|claude|invalid`), `chosen`\n(`codex|agy`, or `None` when invalid), `reason` (`env|status|cli-absent`, or `None`),\n`invalid_field` (`implementer|fallback_agent`, or `None`) and `invalid_value` (the JSON text, or\n`\"\"`).\n1. An `invalid:` implementer tag gives `resolved=\"invalid\"` and `invalid_field=\"implementer\"`.\n2. `chosen` is `agy` for the tag `agy`, else `codex`.\n3. `_cause(peer)` returns the first cause in FR-2's order:\n   - `env`: `env.get(\"HMAD_<PEER>_UNAVAILABLE\", \"\") != \"\"`;\n   - `status`: the record's `<peer>_status` is in `ESCAPE_STATUSES`;\n2460→   - `cli-absent`: `which(peer)` is `None`.\n\n   It returns `None` when the peer is available.\n4. If `chosen` is available it is resolved. Otherwise the other peer is resolved if it is\n   available. Otherwise the record's fallback tag decides: `grok` resolves `grok`; `absent`, `null`\n   and `claude` resolve `claude`; an invalid tag gives `invalid_field=\"fallback_agent\"`.\n   `reason` is `_cause(chosen)` whenever the resolved author is not `chosen`.\n\n`shutil.which` replaces the hook's `command -v codex`. The equivalence is checked by the existing\ndifferential `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py` `test_absent_null_claude_match_the_base_hook`,\n2470→which compares the current hook's bytes with the hook at `8ef6009f` over the PATH × env × status\nmatrix. Residual: that matrix holds only the PATH forms the test builds. Other forms, such as an\nempty `PATH` element or a non-executable `codex` file, are not compared.\n\n**The `implementer` verb.** It is a new `implementer` subparser in `main`, with `--root`,\n`--feature` and `--record`. It has no `--target`. It reads `<root>/docs/.bkit-memory.json` through\n`_state_at`, so it has the same reader and the same error names as the chain. It looks up\n`orchestrator_state[feature]` **whatever its phase**, because staging must not depend on the record\nalready being in `step5`. Exactly one line is printed:\n\n2480→| condition | line |\n|---|---|\n| file absent, or the key absent | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: no-record feature=<pct>` |\n| `_state_at` gives `unreadable`, or the record is not an object | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: unreadable file=<pct> error=<name>` (`not-an-object` for the second) |\n| `invalid_field` is set | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: invalid field=<field> value=<pct>` |\n| `resolved == chosen` | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <resolved> chosen=<chosen> switch=none` |\n| a peer switch | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <resolved> chosen=<chosen> switch=<chosen>-><resolved> reason=<reason>` |\n| peers out | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <grok\\|claude> chosen=<chosen> switch=peers-out reason=<reason>` |\n\n`<pct>` is the judge's existing `_enc`. With `--record`, any switch other than `none` calls\n2490→`h_mad_state_write.merge_fields(state_file, feature, {\"implementer_switch\": {...}})` with\n`from=chosen`, `to=resolved`, `reason` and `ts=_utc_now()`. That is the same write path `--set`\nuses. The write is skipped when the stored value has the same `from`, `to` and `reason`. A\n`StateWriteError` or an `OSError` appends ` record=failed` to the line. The verb always exits 0\nafter printing a line, and the caller reads the token.\n\n**The caller's halts (v1.4).** The orchestrator (SKILL.md, §\"FR-10\") reads the token and never\ndispatches on these three outcomes, adopting the impl-plan's names:\n- a line ending ` record=failed`, whatever precedes it, read before any other field →\n  `step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded` (the name spec FR-2 \"CLI verb\" already gives);\n2500→- `invalid field=` → `step5:implementer_invalid`;\n- `no-record` or `unreadable` → `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n\nEach prints its `[H-MAD] <feature> halt <name>` marker. The verb itself raises no halt; the names\nlive in the caller's prose, and the impl-plan's SKILL.md Task pins them.\n\nResidual: the verb reads the root's own state file. The gate folds the whole chain from the target\nup to the root. A nested sub-project state file that holds a record with the same key is read by\nthe gate and not by the verb.\n\n2510→### FR-3 — the `TDD-STATE:` line (DO-1, DO-1a) and the hook's fold\n\n**The shape.** Each record gains a fifth comma field, the **author field**, between the state file\nand the fallback tag. The header does not change.\n\n```\nTDD-STATE: active codex-escape=<yes|no> blocker=<N> records=<N> fallback=<fold> record=<key>,<codex_status>,<state_file>,<author>,<fallback_tag> …\n```\n\n- `<author>` is one of `codex`, `agy`, `peers-out` or `invalid:<pct-json>`. `format_state_line`\n2520→  computes it per record through `resolve_implementer`, evaluating `env` and `which` once per call.\n  `codex` and `agy` mean that peer is resolved. `invalid:` means the record's `implementer` is\n  invalid. `peers-out` means both peers are out, and the fallback tag then decides.\n- The new `STATE_ACTIVE_RE`, which is the one literal in `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh` matching\n  `^STATE_ACTIVE_RE='(.*)'$`:\n\n  ```\n  ^TDD-STATE: active codex-escape=(yes|no) blocker=(0|[1-9][0-9]*) records=([1-9][0-9]*) fallback=(none|(grok|invalid):([1-9][0-9]*))( record=[^ ,]+,[^ ,]+,[^ ,]+,(codex|agy|peers-out|invalid:[^ ,]+),(absent|null|grok|claude|invalid:[^ ,]+))+$\n  ```\n\n2530→  The new capture group follows `group(4)`, and groups 1 to 6, which the hook reads, keep their\n  numbers. No group before it changes, and the grammar uses no construct that Python `re` and POSIX\n  ERE read differently.\n\n**P-1..P-5 hold, checked against candidate lines.** The committed probe\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr3_state_line_grammar.py` derives the new grammar from\nthe hook's current `STATE_ACTIVE_RE` literal (it refuses if that literal's tail has drifted), builds\nlines in this shape, and matches each one with Python `re.fullmatch` and with `[[ =~ ]]` under\nevery bash named on its command line. Invocation, from the repository root:\n`python3 docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr3_state_line_grammar.py /bin/bash /opt/homebrew/bin/bash`.\n2540→One reading at `200ffd52`: `PROBE: PASS`, under `/bin/bash` 3.2.57, the hook's shebang, and\nHomebrew bash 5.3.20. A control copy that expects the today-shape line to match prints\n`PROBE: FAIL`, so the probe can fail. What it shows:\n- P-1: the header is unchanged, so ` records=1 fallback=grok:1 record=` stays contiguous.\n- P-2: Python and both bash versions gave the same groups 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6 on a grok fold, an agy\n  record and a two-record line whose first record has an invalid implementer, and `group(4)`\n  equalled the fold.\n- P-3: the fallback tag is still the last comma token.\n- P-4: the header is unchanged.\n- P-5: fields 0, 1 and 2 after the first ` record=` are still the key, the status and the file.\n2550→\nA today-shape line, and a line whose author field is `grok`, matched neither engine. So a missing\nor unknown author field refuses `judge-error`, which is the rule FR-3 requires.\n\n**Why the author field's value is right whatever `PATH` holds.** P-1 and P-2 run in-process with\nthe ambient `PATH`, where `agy` resolves (`which agy` → `/Users/kimhawk/.local/bin/agy` at\n`29ae3e7b`). Neither assertion reads the author field, and the grammar admits all four values, so\nboth stay byte-identical and green. The `fallback=` fold is still computed over the records alone\n(`_fallback_fold` is untouched).\n\n2560→**The hook.** `_read_state` keeps its header checks unchanged: the record count, escape against\nblocker, and both range checks. `codex-escape=` and `blocker=` are no longer decision inputs. They\nstay because P-4 pins them and because they still check the line's integrity. In the record loop,\nthe author field is taken by stripping three leading fields (`${rest#*,}` three times, then `%%,*`).\nThe key (`%%,*`), the file (the third field) and the tag (`##*,`) are read exactly as P-5 needs.\nThe loop sets two values:\n- `INVALID_RECORD`: the first record whose author starts with `invalid:`;\n- `PEER_RECORD` and `PEER`: the first record whose author is `codex` or `agy`.\n\nThe `BLOCKER_RECORD` capture, which only the old authorship block read, is removed. After\n2570→`_read_state --target`, the block that tested `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE`, `ESCAPE` and\n`command -v codex` is replaced by FR-3's precedence:\n1. **`INVALID_RECORD` set → `implementer-invalid`.** The text is new:\n   `implementer=$VALUE is not valid (valid: codex|agy|null) — refusing the write, fail-closed.` and\n   then ``  Fix it: python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_write.py --feature $KEY --set implementer=codex|agy|null \"$FILE\"``.\n   `$VALUE` is the decoded JSON.\n2. **`PEER_RECORD` set → `codex-authorship` or `agy-authorship`.** The `codex-authorship` text is\n   the current literal, byte for byte. Only the variables it interpolates are renamed, from\n   `BLOCKER_KEY`/`BLOCKER_FILE` to `PEER_KEY`/`PEER_FILE`, so the rendered bytes for a given record\n   are unchanged. The `agy-authorship` text is new and mirrors it. It names\n2580→   `hmad-dispatch exec agy <promptfile>` with `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --agent agy`, the\n   `--set agy_status=exhausted` command for `$PEER_KEY`/`$PEER_FILE`,\n   `export HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE=1 for a one-off`, and `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer` as the way to\n   see who resolves.\n3. **Otherwise, the existing `fallback_agent` block runs unchanged**, including the `M:F1`\n   tag-agreement check. It is reached only when every record's author is `peers-out`, which is\n   FR-3's \"both peers out\" condition. The comment above it,\n   `codex-authorship has already refused every case where codex is available`, is rewritten to\n   name both peers.\n\n2590→**Fixture consequence (AC-3.2).** Two literals are re-shaped, and nothing else in either file\nchanges:\n- `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_judge.py` `_tree_b`: the default `state_line` gains `,peers-out` before\n  `,absent`;\n- `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py` `RECORD`: it becomes\n  `\" record=feat,exhausted,/x/docs/.bkit-memory.json,peers-out,\"`.\n\n`test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `ACTIVE_RE` becomes the grammar above. The new fixture is a today-shape\nline → `judge-error`.\n\n2600→**No gate test is unreachable.** This was checked by enumerating the stub sites at `29ae3e7b`:\n- `_tree_b(` matches 9 lines in `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_judge.py`, one `def` and 8 call sites. One of\n  the 8 passes its own `state_line`, whose lines are none of them `TDD-STATE: active`.\n- `_stub_outcome(` matches 6 lines in `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py`, one `def` and 5 call\n  sites.\n\nEvery one reaches its asserted outcome with author `peers-out`:\n- the judge-verb stubs reach the judge exactly as today;\n- `missing-field`, `unknown-kind` and `position-zero` still fail the grammar on their header;\n- the range check and the tag-agreement check still run, because `peers-out` reaches step 3.\n2610→\nThe real-judge gate tests build `PATH` as `<bin_dir>:/usr/bin:/bin` (`_gate`'s `path_tail`), with\nno `agy`, so their outcomes are FR-2's codex-only outcomes. So no contradiction is reported here.\n\n**Mutation rows that follow the line (R-1).** At `29ae3e7b`:\n- 56 rows name the hook and 36 name the judge;\n- M-3's pattern matches 8 rows in 3 specs (`claude_gate_judge_wiring.json` 5,\n  `tdd_judge_chain.json` 2, `tdd_gate_fallback_agent.json` 1), which reproduces the plan's figure;\n- 13 rows anchor inside the regions this design rewrites. In the hook that is 8: 7 in `_read_state`\n  and 1 in the authorship block. In the judge it is 5: 1 in `_active_records` and 4 in\n2620→  `format_state_line`.\n\nThe re-anchoring Task reads every row of both files, not only these, because the regions above are\na proxy. The census is owed as a committed probe (§\"Measurements\").\n\n### FR-6 — the shared gate policy (DO-5)\n\n**Placement: `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module. This is OQ-A, decided\nby the operator (spec D-12).** The tree holds hand-built trees that copy the Codex gate beside a stub judge\nand, at most, the identity module:\n2630→- `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason` copies the identity\n  module in and expects `kind=judge-timeout`;\n- the same file's `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]` builds one with a raising judge and **no**\n  identity module, and expects `kind=judge-error`.\n\nThe committed probe `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr6_import_failure_pair.py`\nrebuilds both trees in a temporary directory it removes, and runs the gate as those tests do.\nInvocation, from the repository root:\n`python3 docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr6_import_failure_pair.py`. One reading at\n`200ffd52`, `PROBE: PASS`:\n2640→- `timeout-tree/unmodified`: exit 0, a deny with `kind=judge-timeout`;\n- `timeout-tree/new-module` (one top-level `from h_mad_gate_policy import _safe_shell_command`\n  added): exit 1, no stdout, `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'h_mad_gate_policy'`;\n- `error-tree/unmodified`: exit 0, a deny with `kind=judge-error`;\n- `error-tree/G2-mutant` (row `G2`'s `replace` applied): exit 1, no stdout,\n  `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'h_mad_target_identity'`.\n\nThe last pair is what the binding below rests on: today the `judge-error` tree's deny is produced\nby `main`'s `except Exception as exc:  # M:G2` catching the identity module's import failure,\nraised from `_load_identity` inside `_main_guarded`. That is the only route by which row `G2`'s\n2650→test reaches `# M:G2`.\n\nSo a **new** module that the gate imports breaks a test that FR-6 says passes unmodified. The\nidentity module is in that tree already, as a copy of the live file, so the move keeps the test\ngreen. It also keeps the spec's letter: a shared module, imported by both gates. The cost is\ncohesion. The module's docstring, `Canonical filesystem identity shared by the H-MAD TDD gates.`,\nis widened to say that it also holds the policy both write gates apply.\n\n**The moved set.** It is 25 names, each moved byte for byte except where noted.\n- The 10 functions of `_safe_shell_command`'s call closure, re-derived with `ast` at `29ae3e7b` as\n2660→  the plan's M-4 does: `_contained_venv_executable`, `_cwd_escapes_root`, `_load_judge`,\n  `_option_value`, `_path_within`, `_payload_cwd_base`, `_safe_argv`, `_safe_hmad_script`,\n  `_safe_shell_command` and `_trusted_executable`.\n- 7 more functions the agy gate needs, so that none of them is copied:\n  - the operator allowance: `_shell_allowance`, `_operator_allowed`, `_is_shell_allowance`, and\n    `_read_regular_text`, which `_shell_allowance` reads through;\n  - the phase-5 activity predicate: `_state_status` and `_any_phase5_status`;\n  - the production predicate: `_is_production_python`. Its `_load_identity().fold_py_suffix` call\n    becomes a direct call to the module's own `fold_py_suffix`, the one non-verbatim edit.\n- The 8 module constants that those functions read: `SIMPLE_SHELL_COMMAND`, `READ_ONLY_COMMANDS`,\n2670→  `SAFE_DISPATCH_VERBS`, `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS`, `TEMP_OUTPUT_OPTIONS`, `SHELL_ALLOW_FILE`,\n  `TRUSTED_BIN_DIRS` and `MALFORMED_WORKDIR`.\n\nNot moved: `_load_identity`, which loads this very module; `_relative_target`, `_tmp_report_target`\nand `_command_cwd`, which are Codex-payload specific (see the binding rule); `_main_guarded`'s\ncomposition of safe-command → allowance → operator check, which carries the Codex-specific refusal\ntext and the `M:F2-WIRE` and `M:SA-WIRE` anchors; and the self-check's `unsafe` tuple in `main`.\n\nThe moved functions' `Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]` still names `h-mad/`, because\n`scripts/` and `hooks/` are siblings. So `hmad-dispatch` and the scripts root resolve as before.\n2680→\n**Binding.** The Codex gate inserts its `scripts` directory into `sys.path`, as `_load_judge`\nalready does, and imports all 25 names by name at module level, inside\n`try: … except Exception as exc: _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR = exc` (`_POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR = None`\nbefore it).\n- **The failure is re-raised inside `_main_guarded`, never answered in `main`.** The first\n  statement of `_main_guarded` is `if _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR is not None: raise\n  _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR`. The existing `except Exception as exc:  # M:G2` in `main` turns it into\n  the same `kind=judge-error` deny it gives today. A deny issued in `main` before `_main_guarded`\n  would also print `judge-error`, but `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]` would then pass with\n2690→  `# M:G2` mutated, so row `G2` would survive (§\"FR-6\" probe, `error-tree/G2-mutant`). Rule over\n  the axis: no new early return is added on any path between `main`'s entry and the `try` that\n  holds `# M:G2`; every new failure is raised inside `_main_guarded`.\n- **`--self-check` under a failed import.** The self-check branch runs before that `try` and\n  calls `_safe_shell_command`. When `_POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR` is set it prints\n  `CODEX-TDD-GATE: FAIL policy-import` to stderr and returns 2, instead of raising `NameError`.\n- **Fixtures.** `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]`, unmodified, is the import-failure fixture:\n  its tree has no identity module, so the guarded import fails and the gate still prints one\n  deny. A new fixture copies the gate beside a real judge and **no** identity module and runs\n  `--self-check`, expecting `FAIL policy-import` and exit 2.\n2700→\nOf the moved names, an existing test reaches these four through the Codex gate module, and\nbinding all 25 covers them:\n- `_safe_shell_command`: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` calls `module._safe_shell_command`;\n- `_payload_cwd_base`: the same file monkeypatches it on the module, then calls\n  `module._relative_target`. `_relative_target` stays in the Codex gate, so it reads the patched\n  binding;\n- `SIMPLE_SHELL_COMMAND`: read from `runpy.run_path(...)` globals, in the same file;\n- `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS`: read as `hook.SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS` in `test_h_mad_codex_runtime.py`.\n\n2710→The four were found with `grep -l \"\\b<name>\\b\"` over `git ls-files 'h-mad/tests/*.py'`, once per\nmoved name. Residual: a test that builds a name at run time is not found by that grep.\n\n**Rows re-pointed (R-3, AC-6.1).** At `29ae3e7b`, 62 rows name the Codex gate. A row is counted when\nits `find` starts inside a moved definition's span. 31 rows qualify, in 4 specs:\n`codex_gate_judge_wiring.json` 21, `codex_gate_shell_allowance.json` 6,\n`assemble_tdd_mutation_rows.json` 2 and `tdd_gate_differential.json` 2. 0 anchors are absent and 0\nare non-unique. The plan's 19, which is the 10-function closure alone, is a subset. The 12 added\nrows come from the allowance functions, `_read_regular_text`, `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS` and\n`_is_production_python`. Each re-pointed row's `file` becomes `h_mad_target_identity.py` under\n2720→`h-mad/scripts/` (written relative to `h-mad/`, as every row's `file` is),\nand each spec holding one is gated on `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n- **The identity module's own rows are rechecked too.** Adding the moved code can make one of the\n  module's existing `find` strings occur twice, and the harness then refuses it. The move Task runs\n  the anchor precheck over every spec naming that file.\n- **AC-6.1's \"one definition\".** A test asserts that each of the 17 moved functions has exactly one\n  `def` across `git ls-files h-mad`.\n\n### FR-4 — the agy write gate (DO-2)\n\n2730→**File and contract.** `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` reads stdin and prints exactly one JSON\nobject, `{\"decision\": \"allow\"}` or `{\"decision\": \"deny\", \"reason\": \"<text> kind=<kind>\"}`, then\nexits 0. `main` builds the decision inside one `try` and prints from a `finally` that emits a\n`judge-error` deny if nothing was decided. The judge, the identity module and the policy are\nimported inside that `try`. So a failed import still yields JSON (AC-4.2).\n\n**The order of evaluation.**\n1. Parse stdin, read with a 2 s bound like the Claude hook's `READER` (the Codex gate's `_payload`\n   calls `json.load(sys.stdin)` unbounded). `toolCall.name` must be a `str`, matched exactly and\n   case-sensitively. **A malformed payload** is exactly: stdin that is not a JSON object, a\n2740→   `toolCall` that is not an object, or a `toolCall.name` that is not a `str`. A `toolCall.args`\n   that is absent or not an object is read as `{}`, as the Codex gate's `_targets` reads a\n   non-`dict` `tool_input`; it is not malformed.\n2. **Class (a) is allowed before any state is read.** Class (a) is allowed whether or not step5 is\n   active, so no state read can change its answer.\n3. **The root.** It is `git -C <handler cwd>/.. rev-parse --show-toplevel`, canonicalised with the\n   identity module's `canonical_directory`.\n   A root that cannot be resolved, or a malformed payload, denies `kind=judge-error`. After the\n   root is resolved the gate calls `os.chdir(root)` once, so that every relative path the shared\n   policy resolves against `Path.cwd()` starts from the root rather than from `<root>/.agents`\n2750→   (class (c) refines this per command).\n4. **Activity.** `phase5_status = _any_phase5_status(root)`, the predicate the Codex gate computes\n   before it looks at the tool. It is an **input** to classes (b), (c) and (d), never a global\n   shortcut: the Codex gate judges a production `.py` target through `read_chain` even when the\n   `rglob` scan reads `inactive` (its `for raw in targets:  # M:G5` loop has no status filter, and\n   row `G5`'s test `test_unsearchable_docs_on_the_chain_denies_although_the_scan_is_inactive`\n   kills a mutant that adds one). `rglob` skips a directory it cannot search, so an `inactive` scan\n   does not prove the chain inactive. v1.0's \"`inactive` allows\" before class (b) was that mutant.\n5. **The `workspacePaths` cross-check, only under `S`** (`S` stands for `phase5_status in\n   {\"active\", \"unknown\"}` throughout this section). When `S` holds, `workspacePaths` is a list,\n2760→   and no entry resolves inside the root, the gate denies `kind=judge-error`. When `phase5_status`\n   is `inactive` the check stands down (spec FR-4 §\"Project root\", AC-4.1), and evaluation goes\n   on to step 6. v1.1 made this deny unconditional, so `run_command` `ls` with\n   `workspacePaths=[]` and an inactive state was refused (design audit cycle 2).\n6. **Classes (b), (c) and (d)**, as below. For (c) and (d), `inactive` allows and `unknown`\n   denies `kind=judge-error`. The Codex gate also refuses a shell command under `unknown`, but its\n   reason carries no `kind=` token (`H-MAD state is unreadable; refusing shell execution\n   fail-closed.`); the agy gate adds the kind, and the Codex gate has no counterpart for class (d).\n   Class (b) passes the status into its per-target sequence. An exception anywhere after step 2\n   denies `kind=judge-error`.\n2770→\n**The deny kinds: a closed set of five gate kinds plus the judge's.** Every deny the gate prints\ncarries exactly one `kind=` token, from this list:\n- `judge-error`: the gate could not reach a judgement (the rows of the table below that say so);\n- `tool-not-admitted`: class (d) under `active` (spec FR-4's table);\n- `target-outside-root`: T4's refusal;\n- `allowance-file-write`: T5's refusal;\n- `shell-not-admitted`: class (c)'s policy refusal (`_safe_shell_command` false and\n  `_operator_allowed` false);\n- at T7 only, the judge's own `verdict.kind`, passed through unchanged. It is a member of\n2780→  `h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `KINDS`, which holds 12 names at `cff71b49` (`red-measured`,\n  `no-test-resolved`, `test-missing`, `venv-escapes-root`, `pytest-missing`, `pytest-error`,\n  `no-tests-ran`, `no-summary`, `test-passing`, `timeout`, `judge-timeout`, `judge-error`).\n\nResidual: a kind the judge adds to `KINDS` later is passed through without a change here, so the\nclosed set over the gate's own denies is five names, and over T7 it is whatever `KINDS` holds.\nWhere the Codex gate's deny carries a kind (`judge-error` on an unresolvable target, an unreadable\nchain, a bad root; the judge's kind), the agy gate's carries the same one. Where the Codex deny\ncarries none (out-of-root target, allowance-file write, no write target, shell under `unknown`,\nan unreadable allowance file, the shell policy: 6 of the 13 `_deny(` call sites in\n2790→`h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py` at `cff71b49`, counted from `grep -n '_deny(' ` minus the\n`def _deny` line, with the one multi-line call read whole, as spec v1.8 FR-4 counts them), the\nagy kind is the one in the table below. Spec v1.8 FR-4 §\"Every deny carries a kind\" owns\n`judge-error`, `tool-not-admitted` and the judge's kind; the other three names are this design's.\n\n**Every deny, and when it is gated on step5.** The rule over the class: a deny that depends on\nthe payload's tool or targets fires only under `S`, except the two that follow the Codex gate in\nrunning whatever the scan reads (T7, because the chain is the authority, `M:G5`); a deny that\nfires before the status can be known, or because judging failed, is unconditional.\n\n2800→| Deny | When | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| malformed payload (step 1) | always: no tool is named, so no class applies | `judge-error` |\n| root cannot be resolved (step 3) | always: the status cannot be read without a root (a deliberate difference, below) | `judge-error` |\n| an exception after step 2, and the `finally` with nothing decided | always, as the Codex gate's `M:G2` | `judge-error` |\n| `workspacePaths` names nothing inside the root (step 5) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| class (b) with no target (no recognised key, or no recognised value that is a non-empty `str`) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T2 unresolvable | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T2a an existing directory (OD-2) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T4 outside the root, not admitted | `S` | `target-outside-root` |\n2810→| T5 the allowance file | `S` | `allowance-file-write` |\n| T7 chain unreadable | whatever the status (`M:G5`) | `judge-error` |\n| T7 judge non-ALLOW | whatever the status; reached only on an `active` chain | the judge's kind |\n| class (c) `CommandLine` missing or not a `str` | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) under `unknown` | `unknown` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) `_shell_allowance` returns a `str` (the allowance file unreadable or unparsable) | `active` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) policy refusal | `active` | `shell-not-admitted` |\n| class (d) | `active` | `tool-not-admitted` |\n| class (d) | `unknown` | `judge-error` |\n\n2820→The table is the whole set: 17 rows (v1.4 adds T2a), counted from this table, and every `deny` this section\nnames elsewhere is one of them. Under `inactive` the only denies left are the three unconditional\nrows and the two T7 rows; AC-4.1's \"allows every tool\" holds for every payload whose root resolves\nand whose chain is not `active` or `unreadable`. The T7 exception is v1.1's (step 4): an\n`inactive` scan does not prove the chain inactive.\n\n**Class (a), the read-only allowlist: 11 names.** `view_file`, `list_dir`, `grep_search`,\n`find_by_name`, `command_status`, `read_url_content`, `search_web`, `ask_question`, `finish`,\n`wait` and `wait_5_seconds`.\n- **The rule over the class.** A tool is in (a) only if it appears in a recorded `init.tools`\n2830→  inventory, and only if it writes nothing to the filesystem, starts no process, and drives no\n  other agent, MCP server or browser.\n- `finish` and the two waits are included because denying them leaves a headless agy unable to\n  end or pace its turn, and they touch nothing.\n- **Inventory.** The `init.tools` array of\n  `docs/03-analysis/probes/multi-host-runtime/rehearsal/live-agy-2026-10-01.log` holds 58 distinct\n  names (parsed as the first `init` event's `tools`, at `29ae3e7b`). All 11 are in it.\n- **That log carries no agy version.** Its `init` object has only `cwd`, `tools` and\n  `permission_mode`. The installed agy reports `1.4.0` (spec v1.7 FR-8 §\"Version\" records the\n  same at `200ffd52`; spec v1.6 and plan v1.4 read `1.3.6`).\n2840→  So the inventory is re-taken, with `agy --version`, by FR-8 step 1 (§\"FR-8\").\n- **Residual.** Membership is judged from the tool's name and agy's documentation, not observed. A\n  tool whose name reads as read-only but which writes would be admitted. The 11 names are the whole\n  exposure, and egress over the network is outside this gate.\n\n**Class (b), path-write: 5 names.** `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content`,\n`multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file` and `notebook_edit`.\n- **Path keys.** A per-tool table, `PATH_KEYS`, holds only observed keys. At `29ae3e7b` it holds\n  `write_to_file` → `TargetFile`. That key was seen in stream-json `tool_info.parameters` in 4\n  committed logs under `docs/03-analysis/probes/multi-host-runtime/rehearsal/`\n2850→  (`agy-G7-tool-write-then-link-read.log`, `agy-L3-unmapped-tool.log`, `agy-L9-no-step-index.log`,\n  `agy-L11-duplicate-index.log`), and never in a hook payload.\n- **Fail-closed.** The call's targets are the values of the keys from its table entry that are\n  present **and are a non-empty `str`** (spec v1.8 FR-4 §\"Target keys\"). A recognised key whose\n  value is anything else (`\"\"`, `null`, a number, a list) counts as no path key, and `\"\"` is never\n  canonicalised: v1.1 passed `\"\"` to T1, which design audit cycle 2 (agy) traced to the root\n  directory, no production name at T6, and an allow during active RED. A class-(b) call\n  with no target is denied `kind=judge-error` under `S` and allowed under `inactive`, as the Codex\n  gate denies a `Write`/`Edit`/`apply_patch` with no target (`[path] if path else []`, then\n  `not targets and phase5_status in {\"active\", \"unknown\"}`). That covers the other four tools until\n2860→  FR-8 step 1 records their keys. The non-`str` arm is stricter than the Codex gate, which\n  stringifies a truthy non-`str` value (`str(args.get(\"file_path\") or …)`) and judges it as a path;\n  that is a deliberate difference toward refusal.\n- **Judging a target: the Codex gate's per-target sequence, step for step.** The class is \"every\n  rule the Codex gate's `_main_guarded` applies to one target\" (its `_relative_target` and the\n  body of `for raw in targets:  # M:G5`), not the two rules the audit named. Each recognised\n  key's value `raw` goes through these steps, in this order, and the first that decides wins.\n  `S` stands for `phase5_status in {\"active\", \"unknown\"}`.\n  - **T1 canonicalise.** `canonicalise(str(root), str(Path(raw).expanduser()), str(root))`, the\n    Codex gate's call with the root as the base: agy sends no payload cwd.\n2870→  - **T2 unresolvable.** `resolved.unresolvable` → if `S`, deny `kind=judge-error`; else allow.\n    This runs before containment, as in `_relative_target`, which returns an unresolvable identity\n    before it tests `relative_to(root)`.\n  - **T2a an existing directory (OD-2, operator, 2026-10-16; v1.4).** `Path(resolved.target).is_dir()`\n    → if `S`, deny `kind=judge-error` with a reason naming the directory target; else allow. It runs\n    right after T2 and before T3, so an out-of-root directory under `S` denies `judge-error` too.\n    Without it the sequence allows a directory under `active`: `canonicalise` gives a directory\n    target (`.`, `src/`, `src`, the root itself) `unresolvable=False` and `names=()`, so T2 does not\n    fire, T3 and T5 pass, and T6 has no name to judge (scratch call at `84ec2133`, deleted). A\n    symlink to a directory is a directory here, since `is_dir` follows it. Residual: a target\n2880→    ending in `/` that names no existing directory is not a directory; `canonicalise` gives it\n    `names=('nodir',)` (same scratch call), and it goes through T3–T7 as a file name.\n  - **T3 containment, before anything that assumes it.** `Path(resolved.target).relative_to(root)`\n    raising `ValueError` means outside the root, and the target goes to T4 and never to T5–T7.\n    The order is load-bearing: `_is_shell_allowance` calls `candidate.relative_to(root)` without\n    catching `ValueError`, so calling it on an outside target raises (a scratch call at\n    `200ffd52` on a canonicalised `/tmp/audit-report.txt` raised `ValueError`), and the step-6\n    catch would turn every `/tmp` write into `judge-error`. The moved helper stays byte for byte;\n    the order makes it safe.\n  - **T4 outside the root.** Allowed when **no** name in `resolved.names` folds to `.py` under\n2890→    `fold_py_suffix`, and `resolved.target` is within `/tmp` by `_path_within`, or within the\n    payload's `artifactDirectoryPath` when that is a non-empty `str` (anything else is treated as\n    absent: `Path(\"\").resolve() == Path.cwd()` held in a scratch call at `200ffd52`, so an empty\n    value would make the allowance the process's working directory instead of anything the payload\n    named). Otherwise, if `S`, deny `kind=target-outside-root`; else allow. The artifact\n    allowance is computed before `S` is read, as `artifact = Path(adp) if isinstance(adp, str)\n    and adp else None` with `adp = payload.get(\"artifactDirectoryPath\")`, so a non-`str` value\n    never reaches `Path` (`Path(5)`, `Path([1])`, `Path({})` and `Path(True)` each raise\n    `TypeError`, scratch call at `cff71b49`, deleted).\n  - **T5 the allowance file.** If `S` and `_is_shell_allowance(root, resolved)`, deny\n2900→    `kind=allowance-file-write`. The helper\n    already tests the target and every same-parent hard-link name, casefolded.\n  - **T6 production, over every name.** For each `name` in `resolved.names`, `relative =\n    (Path(resolved.target).parent / name).relative_to(root).as_posix()`; collect those for which\n    `_is_production_python(relative)` holds. None → allow. Otherwise take `min` of the collected\n    `(name, absolute, relative)` tuples, as the Codex gate does. Judging only `resolved.target`\n    would miss production: `resolved.target` is `names[0]`, so with `src/test_a.py` hard-linked to\n    `src/z.py` the identity is `names=('test_a.py', 'z.py')` and the target names the test file\n    (scratch call at `200ffd52`: target-only `False`, any-name `True`).\n  - **T7 the chain.** `chain = read_chain(root, absolute)`. `chain.value == \"unreadable\"` → deny\n2910→    `kind=judge-error`; `chain.value != \"active\"` → allow; else `judge(root, absolute,\n    chain.records)`, and a non-ALLOW denies with the judge's kind. T6–T7 run whatever\n    `phase5_status` is (step 4).\n- **Deliberate differences from the Codex gate's target sequence**, each failing toward refusal or\n  forced by the payload:\n  - the base for a relative `raw` is the root, never a payload cwd (agy sends none);\n  - T4 admits `artifactDirectoryPath` as well as `/tmp` (spec FR-4);\n  - T4 tests every name in `resolved.names` for `.py`, where the Codex gate's `_tmp_report_target`\n    tests only the spelled path's resolved name. The spelled name and the referent's name are both\n    same-directory links, so they are in `names`, and the agy refusal set contains the Codex one;\n2920→  - a root that cannot be canonicalised denies outright (step 3); the Codex gate inspects the\n    component and allows when its scan reads `inactive` (`M:CX-ROOT-ENTER`);\n  - (v1.2) every deny carries a kind from the closed set above, where 6 of the Codex gate's 13 do\n    not; spec v1.8 FR-4 adopts the four above and this one as its fifth;\n  - (v1.2) a recognised path key whose value is a truthy non-`str` is no target here (spec v1.8\n    FR-4 §\"Target keys\"), where the Codex gate stringifies it;\n  - (v1.4) T2a refuses an existing-directory target under `S` (OD-2); the Codex gate's per-target\n    sequence has no such step (its three `is_dir` calls test a root candidate or a payload cwd,\n    never a write target).\n  Residual: any rule the Codex gate gains later is not inherited, because the per-target\n2930→  composition is written in both gates, like the shell composition below. The target\n  differential test (§\"Test Plan\", AC-4.1) is what catches that drift.\n\n**Class (c): `run_command`.** The command is `args.CommandLine`. A missing or non-`str` value\ndenies `kind=judge-error` under `S` and is allowed under `inactive` (v1.1 denied it whatever the\nstatus, against AC-4.1; design audit cycle 2). The Codex gate has no counterpart: a shell tool\nwith no command reaches none of its shell denies.\n- **The command's directory, `cwd`, computed as the Codex gate's `_command_cwd` computes it**,\n  with the root standing in for the session cwd agy does not send. `args.Cwd` was observed beside\n  `CommandLine` in 4 of 630 stream-json `run_command` parameter objects across the committed\n2940→  probes (counted as objects, not distinct calls).\n  - `Cwd` present and not a `str` (`0`, a list, a dict, `true`) → `MALFORMED_WORKDIR`;\n  - `Cwd` absent, `None` or `\"\"` → the root, as `_command_cwd` returns the session for an empty\n    workdir;\n  - a relative `Cwd` → `str(root / Path(Cwd).expanduser())`, joined against the root, never\n    against the handler's directory;\n  - an absolute `Cwd` → `str(Path(Cwd).expanduser())`.\n\n  `cwd` is therefore always `MALFORMED_WORKDIR` or an absolute `str`, so the process directory\n  cannot change what `_cwd_escapes_root` and `_payload_cwd_base` decide.\n2950→- **The process directory, for relative script and argument tokens.** `_safe_argv` resolves a\n  relative script, and `_path_within` a relative argument, against `Path.cwd()`. The shell runs the\n  command in `cwd`, so the gate resolves them there: when `cwd` is a `str` naming an existing\n  directory, the gate calls `os.chdir(cwd)`; in every other case (`MALFORMED_WORKDIR`, a path that\n  does not exist or is not a directory, or a `chdir` that raises `OSError`) it stays in the root\n  (step 3) and still judges with that `cwd`. **No case denies `judge-error`** — v1.0's \"a failed\n  change denies `judge-error`\" diverged from the Codex gate, which never changes directory, still\n  admits a read-only command such as `ls` under a bad workdir, and refuses a relative `.venv`\n  token there as a policy refusal through `_cwd_escapes_root` (`M:F2-ESCAPE`, `M:F2-MALFORMED`).\n  Why not stay in the root always: a relative script token would then be judged at\n2960→  `root/<token>` while the shell runs `<cwd>/<token>`, and a test-directory file named like an\n  h-mad script would be judged as the real one.\n- **The composition is the Codex gate's**, reading the shared names: `phase5_status == \"unknown\"`\n  denies `kind=judge-error`; `active` runs `_safe_shell_command(command, root, cwd)`, then\n  `_shell_allowance` (a `str` result denies `kind=judge-error`), then `_operator_allowed` (false\n  denies `kind=shell-not-admitted`); `inactive` allows. The refusal tells agy to use the\n  class-(b) tools, and names `SHELL_ALLOW_FILE`.\n- **Fixtures matching the Codex gate's workdir tests**, each run through the agy gate with `Cwd`\n  in place of `workdir`: `test_a_workdir_outside_the_root_stays_refused`'s three shapes\n  (`outside-absolute`, `dotdot-escape`, `absent` = `str(root / \"no-such-dir\")`) deny a relative\n2970→  `.venv` interpreter with the root's own venv as the positive control;\n  `test_a_present_but_malformed_workdir_fails_closed`'s four values deny; `Cwd=\"\"` behaves as\n  absent; and `ls` under each bad `Cwd` is allowed. Each of these denies carries\n  `kind=shell-not-admitted`, never `kind=judge-error`.\n- **Residual of the process directory.** When `cwd` does not exist the gate judges relative tokens\n  against the root, but the shell cannot run the command there either.\n- **Residual.** This six-line composition is the one piece of policy written in both gates. It\n  carries different refusal text, and each copy has its own mutation rows. A differential test pins\n  them equal: a corpus of commands × allowance-file states, judged by both gates, with the same\n  admit/deny in every cell.\n2980→\n**Class (d): everything else → deny, `kind=tool-not-admitted`** when `phase5_status` is\n`active` (`unknown` denies `kind=judge-error` and `inactive` allows, step 6). At the inventory above that is\n41 names: `ask_custom_permission`, `ask_permission`, `browser_click_element`,\n`browser_drag_pixel_to_pixel`, `browser_get_dom`, `browser_get_network_request`, `browser_input`,\n`browser_list_network_requests`, `browser_mouse_down`, `browser_mouse_up`, `browser_move_mouse`,\n`browser_press_key`, `browser_refresh_page`, `browser_resize_window`, `browser_scroll`,\n`browser_scroll_dom`, `browser_select_option`, `browser_subagent`, `call_mcp_tool`,\n`capture_browser_console_logs`, `capture_browser_screenshot`, `click_browser_pixel`,\n`define_subagent`, `delete_knowledge`, `execute_browser_javascript`, `generate_image`,\n2990→`invoke_subagent`, `list_browser_pages`, `list_permissions`, `list_resources`, `manage_inbox`,\n`manage_subagents`, `manage_task`, `notebook_execution`, `open_browser_url`, `read_browser_page`,\n`read_resource`, `run_workflow`, `schedule`, `send_command_input` and `send_message`. The 41 are\n58 − 11 − 5 − 1, derived as a set difference over the parsed inventory. Any name absent from the\ninventory, which includes tools added later, also falls here.\n\n**`--self-check`.** It checks four things: the classifier puts `view_file` in (a) and\n`call_mcp_tool` in (d); `_safe_shell_command` refuses `python3 -c \"open('x','w').write('x')\"`; the\npolicy names import; and `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` is readable. It prints exactly one line on stdout.\n**Rule over the axis (v1.4, orchestrator decision):** a FAIL that is a verdict of a check that ran\n3000→prints its token and exits 0, like PASS; the token carries the verdict. Only a FAIL meaning the\ncheck could not run exits 2. So `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS` and the two verdict FAILs,\n`FAIL classification` and `FAIL shell-policy`, exit 0. `FAIL policy-import` (the judge, the\nidentity module or the policy names do not import, or `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` cannot be read) exits 2.\nFR-6's Codex `FAIL policy-import` exits 2 for the same reason. FR-5's wiring check is unaffected:\nit requires the exact line `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS` with exit 0, so a FAIL line with exit 0 still reads\nas `self-check-failed`. The Codex gate's own `--self-check` breaks this rule today: its verdict\nFAILs `FAIL parser` and `FAIL shell-policy` print to stderr and return 2. The impl-plan's FR-6\nTask (Task 4) corrects `FAIL shell-policy` by orchestrator decision. `FAIL parser` is the same\nclass and is owed (§\"Owed by other documents\"). Residual: a self-check FAIL added later is\n3010→classed by this rule, not listed here. AC-6.1's parity with the Codex list is a test, not this self-check.\nThe test locates the `unsafe` tuple assigned inside the Codex gate's `main` with `ast`, requires\nexactly one, and asserts that the agy gate refuses each member.\n\n**Overwrite residual.** agy merges handlers, and a sibling's `overwrite` is a shallow merge into the\ntool's arguments (hooks.md §\"PreToolUse Contract\"). The gate judges the arguments it receives. A\nsibling that rewrites a path key after this gate judged it is not seen. At `29ae3e7b` the operator's\nglobal `~/.gemini/config/hooks.json` holds one `PreToolUse` group, `orca-status` with matcher `*`,\nwhose script prints `{\"decision\":\"ask\"}` for `PreToolUse` and never `overwrite`.\n\n3020→**Roles.** The gate cannot tell an implementing agy from a reviewing agy. During active step5, a\n5e-review agy in a wired project is therefore held to the same tool and shell rules, as a Codex\nverifier is today. 6a-prime runs after step5 ends, so it is unaffected.\n\n**Inherited residual.** The gate decides \"active\" from the root's own state, as both existing gates\ndo. `docs/.bkit-memory.json` is untracked in this repository and in HemaSuite\n(`git ls-files docs/.bkit-memory.json` prints nothing in either at `29ae3e7b`). How Phase 5c\nprovisions state inside a worktree was not read for this design, so whether the three gates stand\ndown in a worktree is not claimed either way.\n\n3030→**Hook timeout.** The floor is `math.ceil(JUDGE_BUDGET_S + 20)`, read from the module. At\n`29ae3e7b` `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` is `40.0`, so the floor is 60.\n\n### FR-5 — the wiring check, the assembler, and the worktree (DO-6)\n\n**The check.** `h_mad_hook_wiring.py` gains `--agy`. It calls a new `check_agy(project_root)`,\nprints the one line that function returns, and exits with the code beside it. Without `--agy`,\nbehaviour is unchanged. The reasons are evaluated in FR-5's order, and the first one found is\nprinted:\n1. `<root>/.agents/hooks.json` is absent → `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file`, exit 0. When it is\n3040→   present but not JSON, or not a JSON object, the line is `UNREADABLE file=<pct> error=<name>`\n   with exit 2, and the name is `not-an-object` for the second case.\n2. There is no top-level key `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate`, or its value is not an object → `no-entry`.\n3. `enabled` is present and is not `true` → `disabled`.\n4. No `PreToolUse` group has a `matcher` of exactly `*` or `\"\"` → `matcher-narrow`. An absent or\n   empty `PreToolUse` counts here too.\n5. No handler in such a group has a `command` whose `shlex.split` equals\n   `[\"python3\", \"~/.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py\"]` → `bad-command`.\n6. That handler's `timeout` is not an `int` ≥ the floor; an absent timeout means agy's default of\n   30 → `timeout-below-floor`.\n3050→7. `<python3 on PATH> <expanduser(path)> --self-check` must exit 0 and print exactly\n   `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS`, within 30 s. Otherwise → `self-check-failed`. When it passes, the line is\n   `AGY-GATE: WIRED`.\n\nResidual: an equivalent spelling of the command (an absolute path, `$HOME`) reads `bad-command`.\nThat fails toward the halt.\n\n**The assembler.** `--agent` gains `agy`, and the timeout default becomes a table,\n`{\"codex\": 900, \"grok\": 1500, \"agy\": 1500}`, with `--timeout` still winning. The choices line\nbecomes `ap.add_argument(\"--agent\", choices=(\"codex\", \"grok\", \"agy\"), default=\"codex\")`, and the\n3060→timeout line keeps the substring `args.timeout is not None` exactly once\n(`timeout = args.timeout if args.timeout is not None else TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS[args.agent]`), so the\nexisting mutation anchors on it survive (§\"Mutation rows\", \"Existing rows the edits move\"). For `--agent agy`,\n`main` runs the check before `assemble`, as a subprocess:\n`[sys.executable, AGY_WIRING_CHECK, \"--agy\", \"--project-root\", <root>]`, with a 60 s bound.\n`AGY_WIRING_CHECK` is a module constant and the tests' seam. Then FR-5's table applies:\n- `WIRED` → continue to PASS;\n- `UNWIRED` → print `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified`, then the check line indented by\n  two spaces, as existing HALT details are. Write no prompt, exit 0;\n- `UNREADABLE` → the same two lines, exit 2;\n3070→- no `AGY-GATE:` line, or a timeout → `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token`,\n  exit 2.\n\nThe verdict branch does not raise `Halt`, because every `Halt` exits 2. The module docstring's\n`exit 0 on PASS, 2 on any HALT` becomes: `exit 0 on PASS and on HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified after\nAGY-GATE: UNWIRED; 2 on every other HALT`. A test pins that sentence, and a test pins that the old\none is absent. `command_block`'s existing prefix rewrite already emits `hmad-dispatch exec agy`, and\n`exec agy` accepts `--model`, `--effort` and `--timeout` (`hmad-dispatch.sh` `_cmd_exec`).\n\n**DO-6: no override path in code.**\n3080→- **Tests.** Tests of `check_agy` set `HOME` to a fixture directory holding\n  `.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` as a symlink to the gate under test.\n  `os.path.expanduser` honours `HOME`, so the shipped code has no worktree switch.\n- **Any test that runs the assembler with `--agent agy` and expects a prompt** needs that `HOME`\n  fixture **and** a `<root>/.agents/hooks.json` holding FR-5's exact entry in its project root.\n  A bare `tmp_path` reads `no-hooks-file` and halts with no prompt written, which is the designed\n  outcome, not a defect. The fixture is called the **WIRED fixture** below. The check runs in a\n  subprocess, so `AGY_WIRING_CHECK` cannot be monkeypatched from a test that also runs the\n  assembler as a subprocess (as `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py` `run_cli` does); the fixture is\n  the only seam such a test has.\n3090→- **This feature's own Phase-5 worktree.** It ships no `.agents/hooks.json`, so `--agent agy` there\n  reads `no-hooks-file` and halts. That is correct: this feature's implementer is Codex.\n- **An adopting project's worktree.** Its tracked `.agents/hooks.json` reaches the worktree, and\n  the installed path is the skills repository's main checkout. So the check reads `WIRED` once this\n  feature has merged.\n- **This repository's own `.agents/hooks.json`** is committed only by FR-8's post-merge stage, and\n  only after its real refusal passes (§\"FR-8\"). Until then the halt stands here mechanically,\n  because the file is absent. Evidence that tracking it needs no `git add -f`: at `29ae3e7b`,\n  `.git/info/exclude` holds `/.agents/skills/`, and `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints\n  nothing.\n3100→\n### FR-7 — the `review_independence` add (DO-3) and the Phase-7 warnings\n\n**The add.** `h_mad_state_write.py` gains `--append KEY=JSON`, which is repeatable.\n- **Strict decoding.** `json.loads` with no string fallback. A decode failure prints `ERROR: …`\n  and exits 2, and the store is unchanged.\n- **One write, accumulated in command-line order.** `--set` fields and `--append` entries apply\n  inside **one** `_mutate` call, through `merge_fields`, which gains an `appends` parameter. Its\n  `apply` keeps its existing statements byte for byte (`record = {**records[feature], **fields}`,\n  then the owner-heartbeat `if` and its assignment) and runs the appends after them, so the two\n3110→  `heartbeat_beat.json` rows anchored on that `if` still match once (§\"Mutation rows\").\n  `appends` is the list of `(key, entry)` pairs in the order given. Below, `prior` is\n  `records[feature]` and `record` is the merged dict; for each pair in order, `current = record.get(key, [])`\n  is read from the **updated** `record`, and `record[key] = list(current) + [entry]`. Two\n  `--append` flags on one key in one call therefore keep every earlier entry and add both, in\n  order. Reading `prior[key]` instead would keep only the last addition.\n- **`--set` and `--append` on the same key in one call are refused** (`--set and --append name\n  the same key: <key>`), exit 2, store unchanged. `--set` replaces a whole value and `--append`\n  adds to it, so the pair has no single reading; refusing is the defined interaction.\n- **The append-only keys refuse `--set` (v1.3).** `review_independence` and\n3120→  `implementation_authors` are append-only: an entry, once written, is never removed or altered.\n  The writer operations that could replace a declared value are `--set` on the command line and\n  `merge_fields` / `set_fields` for library callers (`set_fields` calls `merge_fields`);\n  `create_feature` leaves an existing record alone and `drop_undeclared` drops only undeclared\n  keys, so neither can touch a declared one. The rule is therefore one check at the top of\n  `merge_fields`, before its `def apply`: a key of `fields` in the module constant\n  `APPEND_ONLY = (\"review_independence\", \"implementation_authors\")` raises `StateWriteError`\n  (`<key> is append-only: use --append`), exit 2, store unchanged. It sits outside `apply`, so\n  `apply`'s statements and the heartbeat rows anchored on them are unmoved. `--append` itself goes\n  through the `appends` parameter, never through `fields`, so it is not refused. Residual,\n3130→  exactly: a writer that bypasses `_mutate` (the same residual as below), and a key added to the\n  schema later as an array of history that is not added to `APPEND_ONLY`.\n- **Refusals.** An existing value that is present and is not a list is refused\n  (`cannot append to a non-array value`). The result is validated by `classify`, so FR-1's item\n  schema applies on both validator paths. A refusal raises before `_atomic_write` and prints the\n  writer's existing `ERROR:` refusal with exit 2.\n- **Heartbeat.** An owner's `--session-id` refreshes the heartbeat, as `merge_fields` does.\n- **Output.** The `STATE-WRITE: OK` line lists the appended keys with the set keys.\n- **Lost-entry detection is not needed, and that is why this mechanism was chosen.** `_mutate`\n  holds `fcntl.flock` on the sidecar lock across load, apply and write, so no other writer that\n3140→  goes through `_mutate` can interleave between the read and the write. Residual: a writer that\n  bypasses `_mutate`, such as a hand edit or another tool's raw write, can still race. That is true\n  of every field.\n- **The orchestrator's step (SKILL.md, FR-10).** After each 5e-review, 5e-verify and 6a-prime leg,\n  it runs `--append review_independence='{\"leg\":…,\"module\":…,\"reviewer\":…,\"implementer\":…,\"independent\":…}'`.\n  `independent` is `reviewer != implementer`. This applies for every implementer.\n- **Attribution: `implementer` is read from the dispatch, as spec FR-7 §\"Attribution\" requires.**\n  It is never read from `implementer_switch.to` or from the `implementer` field, which record\n  routing, not authorship. Counter-example the rule exists for: Codex out → agy dispatched → switch\n  stored; Codex back → Codex dispatched with `switch=none`, and the stored `.to` still says `agy`.\n3150→  - **The authors of a module** are the agents of that module's **accepted implementation\n    dispatches** (spec v1.8 FR-7, E4): the 5e GREEN dispatch the orchestrator accepted, and every\n    later fix dispatch for that module it accepted. A 5d RED dispatch is never one: it writes\n    tests, not the reviewed code (`codex-implementer-prompt.md` assigns GREEN to 5e, `For GREEN\n    phase (5e):`). A dispatch that halted, timed out or was rejected authored nothing. The agent of\n    a dispatch is the value of the `TDD-IMPLEMENTER:` token the orchestrator resolved just before\n    staging it, which is the `--agent` it passed to `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` (`--phase green` for\n    the 5e GREEN dispatch; `--phase` takes `red` or `green`); for the Claude fallback, where no\n    dispatch leaves the session, it is the same token's `claude` value.\n  - **The durable author record (v1.3, OD-E2 option (a), spec FR-1 D-15).** Each accepted\n3160→    implementation dispatch is recorded as one `implementation_authors` entry, through the same\n    `--append` operation as above:\n    `--append implementation_authors='{\"module\":\"<module>\",\"agent\":\"<agent>\"}'`. The writer gains\n    no code for it beyond FR-1's schema entry and the `APPEND_ONLY` refusal: `--append` is\n    key-agnostic, and `classify` applies the item schema on both validator paths.\n  - **Where in the procedure the append happens.** The rule over the axis is an ordering:\n    **every accepted implementation dispatch for a module is appended before any review leg of\n    its code runs** (spec FR-1 D-15), and so before that leg's `review_independence` entry. Its\n    members in the procedure:\n    - **5e GREEN.** In SKILL.md's 5e bullet, when the orchestrator accepts a module's GREEN\n3170→      dispatch, that is when it reads the `STATUS:` token `DONE` or `DONE_WITH_CONCERNS` and stops\n      re-dispatching GREEN for that module. It appends there, before the module's 5e spec-review\n      leg runs. A GREEN that is\n      re-dispatched (the path toward `step5e:green_unreachable:<module>`) is not accepted and is\n      not appended.\n    - **Every accepted fix dispatch** for the module, wherever it is dispatched (a 5e fix after a\n      review or verification finding, or a fix after a 6a-prime `WITH_FIXES`): appended once it\n      is accepted, before the next leg that reviews the fixed code.\n    - **Never 5d RED.** The 5d bullet gains no append.\n\n3180→    An accepted GREEN that a later step halts on (`step5e:verify_failed:<module>`, a failed revert\n    test) keeps its entry, because entries are append-only and its code is still in the tree. That\n    direction is the safe one: an extra author can only turn an entry `independent=false`, never\n    `true`. Residual, exactly: the order is orchestrator prose (SKILL.md, FR-10), and no code\n    refuses a `review_independence` append made before the author append it should follow. A\n    refused author append (an invalid entry) prints the writer's `ERROR:` refusal with the store\n    unchanged; the orchestrator corrects the entry and appends again before the review leg runs\n    (spec FR-1).\n  - **Reading the complete author set.** For module `M`, the set is the `agent` values of the\n    well-formed entries of the record's `implementation_authors` whose `module == M`. Well-formed\n3190→    is `property_valid(\"implementation_authors\", [entry])` (§\"The warnings\" below), the definition\n    the orchestrator applies, since no script reads the field (spec FR-1). The latest\n    accepted implementation dispatch is the last such entry in array order, since `--append` adds\n    at the end and the orchestrator appends at acceptance. The set is read from this field and\n    from nothing else: never from earlier `review_independence` entries, which are a projection of\n    it with one `implementer` per entry. Counter-example: with authors `[agy, codex]`, a grok\n    5e-review stores `implementer=codex` and a Codex 5e-verify stores `implementer=codex`, so a\n    set rebuilt from those entries is `{codex}` and an agy 6a-prime would read as independent\n    (design audit cycle 2, codex). The orchestrator's in-context list of dispatches is not read\n    either: the record is the only source, so a resumed orchestrator reads exactly what an\n3200→    uninterrupted one reads. v1.2 kept the set \"in the procedure\" and is replaced by this.\n  - **A 5e leg** reviews one module. If the reviewer is in that module's author set,\n    `implementer` is the reviewer and `independent` is `false`. Otherwise `implementer` is the\n    agent of the module's latest entry, and `independent` is `true` (spec v1.8 FR-7, S5).\n  - **6a-prime** reviews the whole diff. The orchestrator writes one 6a-prime entry per module,\n    with `module` set, applying the same rule to that module's complete author set.\n  - **When no author can be read, no entry is written.** The leg writes no `review_independence`\n    entry for module `M`, rather than one whose `independent` it cannot establish, in exactly\n    these cases:\n    - the record has no `implementation_authors` field: a record whose implementation dispatches\n3210→      were accepted before this field existed (spec v1.8 FR-7's \"no entry\" rule, kept for those\n      records only). An absent field is never read as an empty author set, which would make\n      every reviewer independent; AC-7.3's pre-field fixture pins this (spec v1.9);\n    - the field is present and holds no well-formed entry for `M`: an author append that was\n      skipped, the residual of the ordering rule above;\n    - the field is present and is not a list, or holds an entry that is not well-formed: a hand\n      edit, since the writer refuses both. One malformed entry may be the lost record of `M`'s\n      author, so the set is not established for any module of that record.\n\n    A resumed orchestrator whose dispatch context is gone is **not** a member: it reads the field\n3220→    like any other. Residual, as the spec states: `review_independence_unrecorded` fires only when\n    no `leg=6a-prime` entry exists at all, so one module missing among several recorded ones has\n    no warning of its own.\n  - **AC-7.3's multi-author recovered fixture, from durable state.** The record holds\n    `implementation_authors` `[{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"agy\"},{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"codex\"}]`. A\n    grok 5e-review: grok is not in `{agy, codex}`, so the entry is `implementer=codex` (the latest\n    entry) and `independent=true`. A Codex 5e-verify: codex is in the set, so the entry is\n    `implementer=codex` and `independent=false`. Then the dispatch context is discarded, and only\n    the record is kept. An agy 6a-prime: agy is in the set read from the record, so the entry is\n    `implementer=agy` and `independent=false`, the outcome AC-7.3 expects. The mutant AC-7.3 names\n3230→    (authors recovered from earlier entries' `implementer` values only) yields `{codex}` and\n    `independent=true`, so the fixture is RED against it. The other three v1.8 AC-7.3 fixtures follow\n    from the same field: a 5d RED appends nothing, so the agy reviewer of a Codex GREEN reads\n    `{codex}`; a rejected agy GREEN appends nothing, so the same holds; and the author set never\n    reads `implementer_switch.to`. The pre-field fixture (spec v1.9) runs the same reviews on a\n    record with no `implementation_authors`, and the 6a-prime leg writes no entry for the module;\n    its mutant reads the absent field as an empty set, so the agy reviewer reads as independent.\n    Whether attribution gets a code surface for these fixtures to\n    drive is a separate open decision, still the impl-plan's (§\"Owed by other documents\").\n  - The writer does not check attribution; the Phase-7 consistency check below verifies only that\n3240→    `independent == (reviewer != implementer)`.\n  - Residual: a module's authors are the agents of accepted dispatches only. Code a reviewer wrote\n    during its own leg (outside an implementation dispatch) is not attributed to it.\n\n**The warnings.** `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` `check` calls a new\n`_review_independence_warnings(record)` and extends `warnings`. It never touches `blockers`, so\n`ready` does not move.\n- **Well-formed means validated by the schema, not by a second copy of the value sets.**\n  `h_mad_state_validate.py` gains `property_valid(name, value)`, which validates `value` against the\n  strict schema's `properties[name]` through `_make_validator`, on both paths. For\n3250→  `implementer_switch` it also applies `rfc3339_datetime` to `ts`, and only when `value` is a\n  `dict`: it calls `_semantic_ok({\"implementer_switch\": value})`, the same predicate `classify`\n  uses, so `None` (the schema's `[\"object\", \"null\"]`) and every other non-`dict` go to the schema\n  alone and never raise (v1.1 applied the predicate unguarded; design audit cycle 2). An entry is well-formed when\n  `property_valid(\"review_independence\", [entry])` holds **and** `entry[\"independent\"] ==\n  (entry[\"reviewer\"] != entry[\"implementer\"])`. A boolean that contradicts its own two agents is\n  malformed, so it is never read as `independent=true`.\n- **`review_independence_malformed`** is emitted for each entry that is not well-formed, and once\n  when the field is present and not a list.\n- **`review_non_independent`** is emitted for each well-formed entry with `independent=false`,\n3260→  naming the leg, the module and the agent.\n- **`review_independence_unrecorded`** is emitted when `record.get(\"archreview\")` is in\n  `{\"READY_TO_MERGE\", \"WITH_FIXES\", \"NO\"}` and no **well-formed** entry has `leg == \"6a-prime\"`.\n  - The code path has no implementer condition (D-11), and it reads neither `implementer` nor\n    `implementer_switch`, nor `implementation_authors` (an authorship record no script reads,\n    spec FR-1 D-15), so AC-7.2's per-implementer fixtures (`grok` and `claude` through\n    `implementer_switch.to`) all reach the same branch.\n  - The detail text names the agents the record's entries attest (a projection, which is why it\n    is only detail text and never an author set the attribution reads): the distinct `implementer` values\n    of the well-formed 5e entries, sorted, or `authors=unrecorded` when there are none. It never\n3270→    names `implementer_switch.to` or `implementer`: they record routing, and the same attribution\n    rule as the entries applies to every surface that names an author.\n  - A malformed 6a-prime entry does not count as recorded, so it raises both warnings.\n- Each warning prints through the existing `  WARNING <code>: <detail>` loop in `main`.\n\n### FR-8 — the tracer, after the merge (DO-6, R-2) — OQ-B, spec D-13\n\n**Why after the merge.** The tracer needs agy to run the gate at the installed path,\n`~/.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/` → `/Users/kimhawk/orca/skills/h-mad`\n(`ls -la ~/.gemini/config/skills` at `29ae3e7b`). That path is main, and main gains the gate only\n3280→when this feature merges. An override flag would ship a code path that points the check away from\nthe installed gate. A hand edit of the symlink would also change every live session.\n\n**The stage.** It runs after Phase 7's integrate and before the feature is recorded COMPLETE.\n1. **A fixture project.** It is a throwaway git repository with one module task. It has its own\n   `docs/.bkit-memory.json` step5 record and its own `.agents/hooks.json`, holding FR-5's exact\n   entry. It is created beside this checkout, never under `/tmp`, because agy's trust decision for\n   `.agents/hooks.json` may depend on location.\n2. **Step 1, payload capture.** The real class-(b) payload keys are recorded, and so is\n   `init.tools` with `agy --version`. They land as one commit on main that fills `PATH_KEYS` and\n3290→   re-checks the class-(a) inventory. That commit carries tests built from the captured payloads,\n   written RED then GREEN, and it lands before step 2. The capture hook is then deleted.\n   **D-FR8-1 (2026-10-17):** on agy `1.4.0` only `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content` and\n   `run_command` are declared to the model; `multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file` and\n   `notebook_edit` appear in `init.tools` only. Step 1 captures the three declared tools; the\n   other three keep no `PATH_KEYS` entry and deny under active step5 (impl-plan Task 20 v1.3).\n3. **Steps 2, 2a and 3** in the fixture project, as FR-8 states them. Step 2a's delay hook is\n   added to, and removed from, the fixture's `hooks.json`, and the before and after\n   `shasum -a 256` are recorded.\n4. **This repository's adoption.** `.agents/hooks.json` is committed here, and step 2 is re-run\n3300→   once in this repository to settle trust at this location.\n5. The derived timeout replaces `agy`'s `1500` in the assembler table, as FR-8's formula sets.\n\nEvery reading is stamped with the sha and `agy --version` under\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`.\n\n**AC-8.1's formula check is a probe check, not a suite test.** A committed script asserts that the\nassembler's `agy` default equals `max(900, 3 × slower)` rounded up to a multiple of 60 over the","content_concise":"2308→               │        │                 │\n   TDD-STATE: line   TDD-IMPLEMENTER:     ├── h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py ──┐\n2310→               │        │                 └── h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py ────┤ both import the\n   h-mad-tdd-gate.sh  orchestrator ──► h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --agent    │ gate policy from\n   (Claude's writes)                    └─ agy: h_mad_hook_wiring.py --agy\n                                                                        │\n                                          h_mad_target_identity.py ◄────┘\n```\n\n- The judge is the only place `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE`, `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE` and `PATH` are read\n  for authorship. It runs as a child of the Claude hook, so it sees the hook's environment.\n- The Claude hook parses the line and refuses. It re-derives nothing.\n2320→- The agy gate and the Codex gate share one copy of the shell policy, the allowance file rules,\n  the phase-5 activity predicate and the production predicate.\n\n## Detailed Design\n\n### FR-1 — state fields, and the timestamp refusal (DO-4)\n\n**Schema.** Five properties are added to `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json`. `required` does\nnot change, and `additionalProperties` stays `false`. Every construct below is in the bundled\nvalidator's supported set (`_MiniDraft7`'s docstring, the `Deliberately supports ONLY` paragraph):\n2330→`type`, `enum`, `required`, `properties`, `additionalProperties`, `items`, and (v1.3, for\n`implementation_authors`) `minLength`. No `oneOf`, `anyOf`,\n`const` or `pattern` is used, so the two validator paths cannot disagree on structure.\n\n**Property order.** The five are appended, in the order listed below, after `fallback_agent`, which is the schema's last\nproperty today (`list(properties)` ends `…, 'codex_status', 'fallback_agent'` at `200ffd52`).\n`test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts`\nasserts `list(properties).index(\"fallback_agent\") == list(properties).index(\"codex_status\") + 1`,\nso any new property placed between those two breaks AC-1.1. Rule over the axis: no property is\ninserted anywhere before `fallback_agent`.\n2340→\n- `implementer`: `enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", null]`.\n- `agy_status`: `enum` `[\"available\", \"unavailable\", \"exhausted\", null]`.\n- `implementer_switch`: `type` `[\"object\", \"null\"]`. Properties `from` and `to` take `enum`\n  `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`, `reason` takes `enum` `[\"env\", \"status\", \"cli-absent\"]`, and\n  `ts` is `type` `string` with `\"format\": \"date-time\"` as an annotation only. All four are\n  `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`.\n- `review_independence`: `type` `array`. Its `items` is an object with `leg`\n  (`enum` `[\"5e-review\", \"5e-verify\", \"6a-prime\"]`), `module` (`type` `[\"string\", \"null\"]`),\n  `reviewer` and `implementer` (each `enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`) and `independent`\n2350→  (`type` `boolean`). All five are `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`.\n- `implementation_authors` (v1.3, operator decision OD-E2, spec FR-1 D-15): `type` `array`. Its\n  `items` is an object with `module` (`type` `string`, `minLength` `1`: it is the `--module` value\n  the assembler requires) and `agent` (`enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`). Both are\n  `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`. The array is a log, not a set: duplicates\n  are kept, and array order is acceptance order (spec FR-1 D-15). One entry is one accepted implementation dispatch; §\"FR-7\" says who writes it and when,\n  and how attribution reads it. `module` is a `string`, not `[\"string\", \"null\"]` as in\n  `review_independence`, because an implementation dispatch is always for one module (5e stages\n  one module per GREEN). It is placed last, after `review_independence`, so the AC-1.1 index\n  assertion on `fallback_agent` is untouched. `minLength` is in `_MiniDraft7`'s supported set (its\n2360→  docstring's `Deliberately supports ONLY` paragraph names it, and its `_ok` checks it on a\n  `str`), and the schema already uses it, so the two validator paths cannot disagree on its\n  structure. Fixtures: AC-1.3 (§\"Test Plan\").\n\n**The `.ts` refusal: a predicate outside the validator backend.** The option chosen is the third of\nthe three the spec lists, placed in the validator module rather than in the writer, so that a\nhand-edited record is refused by `h_mad_state_validate.py` too.\n- **The probe it rests on.** Reading at `29ae3e7b`, the plan's command\n  (`/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3 -B -c '… Draft7Validator(s).is_valid(x) … v._MiniDraft7(s).is_valid(x)'`\n  with `s={\"type\":\"string\",\"format\":\"date-time\"}` and `x=\"not-a-date\"`) prints\n2370→  `Draft7Validator: True` and `bundled: True`. `format` is enforced on neither path, so declaring it\n  refuses nothing.\n- **The predicate.** `h_mad_state_validate.py` gains `rfc3339_datetime(value) -> bool`:\n  - `re.fullmatch` with `re.ASCII` over\n    `([0-9]{4})-([0-9]{2})-([0-9]{2})[Tt]([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2})(\\.[0-9]+)?([Zz]|[+-]([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2}))`;\n  - then `datetime.datetime(y, mo, d, h, mi, s)` must construct, which refuses `2026-02-30`;\n  - then an offset's hours must be at most 23 and its minutes at most 59;\n  - a non-`str` is refused.\n\n  `fullmatch` and `[0-9]` are deliberate: `$` admits a trailing newline, and `\\d` admits non-ASCII\n2380→  digits. A scratch probe at `29ae3e7b`, since deleted, returned these results under both\n  `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3` (3.11.8) and `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` (3.15.0):\n  - refused: `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`, `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z`, an offset of `+24:00`, a seconds\n    field of 60, a value with\n    full-width digits, the writer's form followed by `\\n`, `5` and `None`;\n  - accepted: `2026-10-16T07:05:09Z` and `2026-10-16T07:05:09.123+09:00`.\n- **Where it applies.** A private `_semantic_ok(record)` returns `False` exactly when `record` is\n  a `dict` whose `implementer_switch` is a `dict` whose `ts` fails `rfc3339_datetime`; it returns\n  `True` for anything else, a non-`dict` record included, so it never raises.\n  `classify` keeps its existing first statement (`if not isinstance(record, dict): return\n2390→  \"invalid\"`, which is what `test_h_mad_state_validate_fallback.py` `CORPUS`'s\n  `(\"not an object\", [\"nope\"])` row exercises), then calls `_semantic_ok` and returns `invalid`\n  when it fails, before either schema tier is tried. That runs whichever\n  backend `_make_validator` chose, so both paths refuse. The writer refuses through `classify` as it\n  does today (`_mutate`: `classify(record) != \"strict\"`), and it prints its existing refusal.\n- **No library format checker is used.** The `rfc3339-validator` hazard therefore does not bind,\n  and AC-1.2's package-absent fixture is not owed, since AC-1.2 makes it conditional on that\n  mechanism. Recorded for the reader: at `29ae3e7b`, `rfc3339_validator` imports in\n  `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3`, and `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` has no `jsonschema`.\n- **Not schema-wide.** `started_ts`, `owner_heartbeat_ts` and `halt_ts` stay annotation-only, so\n2400→  no existing record can change class. The `classify_store` run the spec asks of a schema-wide\n  mechanism is not owed. A reading anyway, taken 2026-10-16 with this repository at `29ae3e7b` and\n  HemaSuite at `77fb16f51` (both files are untracked, so the sha does not pin their content): this\n  repository's `docs/.bkit-memory.json` has 54 records and HemaSuite's has 7. Zero records in either file carry\n  any of the four FR-1 keys. That zero is load-bearing, not incidental: the strict schema has\n  `additionalProperties: false`, so the writer has refused those keys until now. A hand-edited\n  record is the residual. The fifth key, `implementation_authors` (v1.3), has the same zero for\n  the same load-bearing reason: one reading on 2026-10-16, this repository at `3ad735f7` and\n  HemaSuite at `02d52e4e7`, over each file's `orchestrator_state` object, gives 54 and 8 records,\n  and 0 records in either file carry any of the five keys.\n2410→- **A seconds field of 60 is refused, as spec FR-1 requires (D-14).** RFC 3339 admits a leap\n  second; spec v1.7 FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`, because every `.ts`\n  is machine-written by h-mad's own writer through `datetime`, which cannot hold second 60. The predicate refuses it at the `datetime` construction\n  step, and a fixture pins `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` refused and `2016-12-31T23:59:59Z` accepted on\n  both paths.\n\n**Fixtures (AC-1.2).** One fixture per value per path, with the bundled path forced through the\nexisting `_FORCE_FALLBACK` seam. Refused: `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`, `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z`, and\n`2026-10-16T00:00:00Zjunk` and `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z`. `…Zjunk` kills a `fullmatch` → `match`\nmutation. Accepted: the writer's `_utc_now` form and `2016-12-31T23:59:59Z`. Each alternative of the zone group has its own fixture, run with that\n2420→branch alone: `…Z`, `…z`, `…+09:00` accepted, and `…+24:00` refused.\n\n**The `fallback_agent` description, and the same class on `codex_status`.** Spec FR-1 names the\n`fallback_agent` description and its `state-schema.md` row. The class is \"a surface that says what\nan unavailable Codex lets Claude do\". It has two more members in the tree:\n- the `codex_status` schema `description`, which ends \"permitting Claude to write the fallback\n  (still test-first)\" and \"One switch, both effects\";\n- its `state-schema.md` row, \"An unavailable or exhausted status permits a Phase-5 authoring\n  fallback\".\n\n2430→Both are false once agy is available. All four surfaces are rewritten to the FR-2 condition.\n`test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts` keeps\nits four substrings, for example \"read only when codex is out and agy is out too\". No test pins the\n`codex_status` wording: `git ls-files 'h-mad/tests/*.py' | xargs grep -ln 'permitting Claude\\|One switch, both effects'`\nprints nothing at `29ae3e7b`. The spec does not list the `codex_status` pair; see §\"Owed by other\ndocuments\".\n\n### FR-2 — `resolve_implementer` and the `implementer` verb\n\n**Record.** `Record` gains two trailing fields with defaults: `implementer: str = \"absent\"` and\n2440→`agy_status: str = \"available\"`. The defaults keep every positional four-argument construction\nvalid. One exists in the tree: the replace text of a `codex_gate_judge_wiring.json` row\n(`git ls-files -z h-mad | xargs -0 grep -l 'Record('` → `h_mad_tdd_judge.py` and that spec,\n2 files). `_active_records` fills both through two helpers:\n- `_implementer_tag(state)` returns `absent`, `null`, `codex`, `agy` or `invalid:<json>`, built like\n  `_fallback_tag`;\n- `agy_status` is read the way `codex_status` is: `\"available\"` when the value is `None` or the key\n  is absent, else `str(value)`.\n\n**Resolution.** `resolve_implementer(record, env=None, which=None) -> Implementer` is the only\n2450→resolver. `env` defaults to `os.environ` and `which` to `shutil.which`. `Implementer` is a\n`NamedTuple` with these fields: `resolved` (`codex|agy|grok|claude|invalid`), `chosen`\n(`codex|agy`, or `None` when invalid), `reason` (`env|status|cli-absent`, or `None`),\n`invalid_field` (`implementer|fallback_agent`, or `None`) and `invalid_value` (the JSON text, or\n`\"\"`).\n1. An `invalid:` implementer tag gives `resolved=\"invalid\"` and `invalid_field=\"implementer\"`.\n2. `chosen` is `agy` for the tag `agy`, else `codex`.\n3. `_cause(peer)` returns the first cause in FR-2's order:\n   - `env`: `env.get(\"HMAD_<PEER>_UNAVAILABLE\", \"\") != \"\"`;\n   - `status`: the record's `<peer>_status` is in `ESCAPE_STATUSES`;\n2460→   - `cli-absent`: `which(peer)` is `None`.\n\n   It returns `None` when the peer is available.\n4. If `chosen` is available it is resolved. Otherwise the other peer is resolved if it is\n   available. Otherwise the record's fallback tag decides: `grok` resolves `grok`; `absent`, `null`\n   and `claude` resolve `claude`; an invalid tag gives `invalid_field=\"fallback_agent\"`.\n   `reason` is `_cause(chosen)` whenever the resolved author is not `chosen`.\n\n`shutil.which` replaces the hook's `command -v codex`. The equivalence is checked by the existing\ndifferential `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py` `test_absent_null_claude_match_the_base_hook`,\n2470→which compares the current hook's bytes with the hook at `8ef6009f` over the PATH × env × status\nmatrix. Residual: that matrix holds only the PATH forms the test builds. Other forms, such as an\nempty `PATH` element or a non-executable `codex` file, are not compared.\n\n**The `implementer` verb.** It is a new `implementer` subparser in `main`, with `--root`,\n`--feature` and `--record`. It has no `--target`. It reads `<root>/docs/.bkit-memory.json` through\n`_state_at`, so it has the same reader and the same error names as the chain. It looks up\n`orchestrator_state[feature]` **whatever its phase**, because staging must not depend on the record\nalready being in `step5`. Exactly one line is printed:\n\n2480→| condition | line |\n|---|---|\n| file absent, or the key absent | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: no-record feature=<pct>` |\n| `_state_at` gives `unreadable`, or the record is not an object | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: unreadable file=<pct> error=<name>` (`not-an-object` for the second) |\n| `invalid_field` is set | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: invalid field=<field> value=<pct>` |\n| `resolved == chosen` | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <resolved> chosen=<chosen> switch=none` |\n| a peer switch | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <resolved> chosen=<chosen> switch=<chosen>-><resolved> reason=<reason>` |\n| peers out | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <grok\\|claude> chosen=<chosen> switch=peers-out reason=<reason>` |\n\n`<pct>` is the judge's existing `_enc`. With `--record`, any switch other than `none` calls\n2490→`h_mad_state_write.merge_fields(state_file, feature, {\"implementer_switch\": {...}})` with\n`from=chosen`, `to=resolved`, `reason` and `ts=_utc_now()`. That is the same write path `--set`\nuses. The write is skipped when the stored value has the same `from`, `to` and `reason`. A\n`StateWriteError` or an `OSError` appends ` record=failed` to the line. The verb always exits 0\nafter printing a line, and the caller reads the token.\n\n**The caller's halts (v1.4).** The orchestrator (SKILL.md, §\"FR-10\") reads the token and never\ndispatches on these three outcomes, adopting the impl-plan's names:\n- a line ending ` record=failed`, whatever precedes it, read before any other field →\n  `step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded` (the name spec FR-2 \"CLI verb\" already gives);\n2500→- `invalid field=` → `step5:implementer_invalid`;\n- `no-record` or `unreadable` → `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n\nEach prints its `[H-MAD] <feature> halt <name>` marker. The verb itself raises no halt; the names\nlive in the caller's prose, and the impl-plan's SKILL.md Task pins them.\n\nResidual: the verb reads the root's own state file. The gate folds the whole chain from the target\nup to the root. A nested sub-project state file that holds a record with the same key is read by\nthe gate and not by the verb.\n\n2510→### FR-3 — the `TDD-STATE:` line (DO-1, DO-1a) and the hook's fold\n\n**The shape.** Each record gains a fifth comma field, the **author field**, between the state file\nand the fallback tag. The header does not change.\n\n```\nTDD-STATE: active codex-escape=<yes|no> blocker=<N> records=<N> fallback=<fold> record=<key>,<codex_status>,<state_file>,<author>,<fallback_tag> …\n```\n\n- `<author>` is one of `codex`, `agy`, `peers-out` or `invalid:<pct-json>`. `format_state_line`\n2520→  computes it per record through `resolve_implementer`, evaluating `env` and `which` once per call.\n  `codex` and `agy` mean that peer is resolved. `invalid:` means the record's `implementer` is\n  invalid. `peers-out` means both peers are out, and the fallback tag then decides.\n- The new `STATE_ACTIVE_RE`, which is the one literal in `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh` matching\n  `^STATE_ACTIVE_RE='(.*)'$`:\n\n  ```\n  ^TDD-STATE: active codex-escape=(yes|no) blocker=(0|[1-9][0-9]*) records=([1-9][0-9]*) fallback=(none|(grok|invalid):([1-9][0-9]*))( record=[^ ,]+,[^ ,]+,[^ ,]+,(codex|agy|peers-out|invalid:[^ ,]+),(absent|null|grok|claude|invalid:[^ ,]+))+$\n  ```\n\n2530→  The new capture group follows `group(4)`, and groups 1 to 6, which the hook reads, keep their\n  numbers. No group before it changes, and the grammar uses no construct that Python `re` and POSIX\n  ERE read differently.\n\n**P-1..P-5 hold, checked against candidate lines.** The committed probe\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr3_state_line_grammar.py` derives the new grammar from\nthe hook's current `STATE_ACTIVE_RE` literal (it refuses if that literal's tail has drifted), builds\nlines in this shape, and matches each one with Python `re.fullmatch` and with `[[ =~ ]]` under\nevery bash named on its command line. Invocation, from the repository root:\n`python3 docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr3_state_line_grammar.py /bin/bash /opt/homebrew/bin/bash`.\n2540→One reading at `200ffd52`: `PROBE: PASS`, under `/bin/bash` 3.2.57, the hook's shebang, and\nHomebrew bash 5.3.20. A control copy that expects the today-shape line to match prints\n`PROBE: FAIL`, so the probe can fail. What it shows:\n- P-1: the header is unchanged, so ` records=1 fallback=grok:1 record=` stays contiguous.\n- P-2: Python and both bash versions gave the same groups 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6 on a grok fold, an agy\n  record and a two-record line whose first record has an invalid implementer, and `group(4)`\n  equalled the fold.\n- P-3: the fallback tag is still the last comma token.\n- P-4: the header is unchanged.\n- P-5: fields 0, 1 and 2 after the first ` record=` are still the key, the status and the file.\n2550→\nA today-shape line, and a line whose author field is `grok`, matched neither engine. So a missing\nor unknown author field refuses `judge-error`, which is the rule FR-3 requires.\n\n**Why the author field's value is right whatever `PATH` holds.** P-1 and P-2 run in-process with\nthe ambient `PATH`, where `agy` resolves (`which agy` → `/Users/kimhawk/.local/bin/agy` at\n`29ae3e7b`). Neither assertion reads the author field, and the grammar admits all four values, so\nboth stay byte-identical and green. The `fallback=` fold is still computed over the records alone\n(`_fallback_fold` is untouched).\n\n2560→**The hook.** `_read_state` keeps its header checks unchanged: the record count, escape against\nblocker, and both range checks. `codex-escape=` and `blocker=` are no longer decision inputs. They\nstay because P-4 pins them and because they still check the line's integrity. In the record loop,\nthe author field is taken by stripping three leading fields (`${rest#*,}` three times, then `%%,*`).\nThe key (`%%,*`), the file (the third field) and the tag (`##*,`) are read exactly as P-5 needs.\nThe loop sets two values:\n- `INVALID_RECORD`: the first record whose author starts with `invalid:`;\n- `PEER_RECORD` and `PEER`: the first record whose author is `codex` or `agy`.\n\nThe `BLOCKER_RECORD` capture, which only the old authorship block read, is removed. After\n2570→`_read_state --target`, the block that tested `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE`, `ESCAPE` and\n`command -v codex` is replaced by FR-3's precedence:\n1. **`INVALID_RECORD` set → `implementer-invalid`.** The text is new:\n   `implementer=$VALUE is not valid (valid: codex|agy|null) — refusing the write, fail-closed.` and\n   then ``  Fix it: python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_write.py --feature $KEY --set implementer=codex|agy|null \"$FILE\"``.\n   `$VALUE` is the decoded JSON.\n2. **`PEER_RECORD` set → `codex-authorship` or `agy-authorship`.** The `codex-authorship` text is\n   the current literal, byte for byte. Only the variables it interpolates are renamed, from\n   `BLOCKER_KEY`/`BLOCKER_FILE` to `PEER_KEY`/`PEER_FILE`, so the rendered bytes for a given record\n   are unchanged. The `agy-authorship` text is new and mirrors it. It names\n2580→   `hmad-dispatch exec agy <promptfile>` with `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --agent agy`, the\n   `--set agy_status=exhausted` command for `$PEER_KEY`/`$PEER_FILE`,\n   `export HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE=1 for a one-off`, and `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer` as the way to\n   see who resolves.\n3. **Otherwise, the existing `fallback_agent` block runs unchanged**, including the `M:F1`\n   tag-agreement check. It is reached only when every record's author is `peers-out`, which is\n   FR-3's \"both peers out\" condition. The comment above it,\n   `codex-authorship has already refused every case where codex is available`, is rewritten to\n   name both peers.\n\n2590→**Fixture consequence (AC-3.2).** Two literals are re-shaped, and nothing else in either file\nchanges:\n- `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_judge.py` `_tree_b`: the default `state_line` gains `,peers-out` before\n  `,absent`;\n- `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py` `RECORD`: it becomes\n  `\" record=feat,exhausted,/x/docs/.bkit-memory.json,peers-out,\"`.\n\n`test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `ACTIVE_RE` becomes the grammar above. The new fixture is a today-shape\nline → `judge-error`.\n\n2600→**No gate test is unreachable.** This was checked by enumerating the stub sites at `29ae3e7b`:\n- `_tree_b(` matches 9 lines in `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_judge.py`, one `def` and 8 call sites. One of\n  the 8 passes its own `state_line`, whose lines are none of them `TDD-STATE: active`.\n- `_stub_outcome(` matches 6 lines in `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py`, one `def` and 5 call\n  sites.\n\nEvery one reaches its asserted outcome with author `peers-out`:\n- the judge-verb stubs reach the judge exactly as today;\n- `missing-field`, `unknown-kind` and `position-zero` still fail the grammar on their header;\n- the range check and the tag-agreement check still run, because `peers-out` reaches step 3.\n2610→\nThe real-judge gate tests build `PATH` as `<bin_dir>:/usr/bin:/bin` (`_gate`'s `path_tail`), with\nno `agy`, so their outcomes are FR-2's codex-only outcomes. So no contradiction is reported here.\n\n**Mutation rows that follow the line (R-1).** At `29ae3e7b`:\n- 56 rows name the hook and 36 name the judge;\n- M-3's pattern matches 8 rows in 3 specs (`claude_gate_judge_wiring.json` 5,\n  `tdd_judge_chain.json` 2, `tdd_gate_fallback_agent.json` 1), which reproduces the plan's figure;\n- 13 rows anchor inside the regions this design rewrites. In the hook that is 8: 7 in `_read_state`\n  and 1 in the authorship block. In the judge it is 5: 1 in `_active_records` and 4 in\n2620→  `format_state_line`.\n\nThe re-anchoring Task reads every row of both files, not only these, because the regions above are\na proxy. The census is owed as a committed probe (§\"Measurements\").\n\n### FR-6 — the shared gate policy (DO-5)\n\n**Placement: `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module. This is OQ-A, decided\nby the operator (spec D-12).** The tree holds hand-built trees that copy the Codex gate beside a stub judge\nand, at most, the identity module:\n2630→- `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason` copies the identity\n  module in and expects `kind=judge-timeout`;\n- the same file's `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]` builds one with a raising judge and **no**\n  identity module, and expects `kind=judge-error`.\n\nThe committed probe `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr6_import_failure_pair.py`\nrebuilds both trees in a temporary directory it removes, and runs the gate as those tests do.\nInvocation, from the repository root:\n`python3 docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr6_import_failure_pair.py`. One reading at\n`200ffd52`, `PROBE: PASS`:\n2640→- `timeout-tree/unmodified`: exit 0, a deny with `kind=judge-timeout`;\n- `timeout-tree/new-module` (one top-level `from h_mad_gate_policy import _safe_shell_command`\n  added): exit 1, no stdout, `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'h_mad_gate_policy'`;\n- `error-tree/unmodified`: exit 0, a deny with `kind=judge-error`;\n- `error-tree/G2-mutant` (row `G2`'s `replace` applied): exit 1, no stdout,\n  `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'h_mad_target_identity'`.\n\nThe last pair is what the binding below rests on: today the `judge-error` tree's deny is produced\nby `main`'s `except Exception as exc:  # M:G2` catching the identity module's import failure,\nraised from `_load_identity` inside `_main_guarded`. That is the only route by which row `G2`'s\n2650→test reaches `# M:G2`.\n\nSo a **new** module that the gate imports breaks a test that FR-6 says passes unmodified. The\nidentity module is in that tree already, as a copy of the live file, so the move keeps the test\ngreen. It also keeps the spec's letter: a shared module, imported by both gates. The cost is\ncohesion. The module's docstring, `Canonical filesystem identity shared by the H-MAD TDD gates.`,\nis widened to say that it also holds the policy both write gates apply.\n\n**The moved set.** It is 25 names, each moved byte for byte except where noted.\n- The 10 functions of `_safe_shell_command`'s call closure, re-derived with `ast` at `29ae3e7b` as\n2660→  the plan's M-4 does: `_contained_venv_executable`, `_cwd_escapes_root`, `_load_judge`,\n  `_option_value`, `_path_within`, `_payload_cwd_base`, `_safe_argv`, `_safe_hmad_script`,\n  `_safe_shell_command` and `_trusted_executable`.\n- 7 more functions the agy gate needs, so that none of them is copied:\n  - the operator allowance: `_shell_allowance`, `_operator_allowed`, `_is_shell_allowance`, and\n    `_read_regular_text`, which `_shell_allowance` reads through;\n  - the phase-5 activity predicate: `_state_status` and `_any_phase5_status`;\n  - the production predicate: `_is_production_python`. Its `_load_identity().fold_py_suffix` call\n    becomes a direct call to the module's own `fold_py_suffix`, the one non-verbatim edit.\n- The 8 module constants that those functions read: `SIMPLE_SHELL_COMMAND`, `READ_ONLY_COMMANDS`,\n2670→  `SAFE_DISPATCH_VERBS`, `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS`, `TEMP_OUTPUT_OPTIONS`, `SHELL_ALLOW_FILE`,\n  `TRUSTED_BIN_DIRS` and `MALFORMED_WORKDIR`.\n\nNot moved: `_load_identity`, which loads this very module; `_relative_target`, `_tmp_report_target`\nand `_command_cwd`, which are Codex-payload specific (see the binding rule); `_main_guarded`'s\ncomposition of safe-command → allowance → operator check, which carries the Codex-specific refusal\ntext and the `M:F2-WIRE` and `M:SA-WIRE` anchors; and the self-check's `unsafe` tuple in `main`.\n\nThe moved functions' `Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]` still names `h-mad/`, because\n`scripts/` and `hooks/` are siblings. So `hmad-dispatch` and the scripts root resolve as before.\n2680→\n**Binding.** The Codex gate inserts its `scripts` directory into `sys.path`, as `_load_judge`\nalready does, and imports all 25 names by name at module level, inside\n`try: … except Exception as exc: _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR = exc` (`_POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR = None`\nbefore it).\n- **The failure is re-raised inside `_main_guarded`, never answered in `main`.** The first\n  statement of `_main_guarded` is `if _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR is not None: raise\n  _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR`. The existing `except Exception as exc:  # M:G2` in `main` turns it into\n  the same `kind=judge-error` deny it gives today. A deny issued in `main` before `_main_guarded`\n  would also print `judge-error`, but `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]` would then pass with\n2690→  `# M:G2` mutated, so row `G2` would survive (§\"FR-6\" probe, `error-tree/G2-mutant`). Rule over\n  the axis: no new early return is added on any path between `main`'s entry and the `try` that\n  holds `# M:G2`; every new failure is raised inside `_main_guarded`.\n- **`--self-check` under a failed import.** The self-check branch runs before that `try` and\n  calls `_safe_shell_command`. When `_POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR` is set it prints\n  `CODEX-TDD-GATE: FAIL policy-import` to stderr and returns 2, instead of raising `NameError`.\n- **Fixtures.** `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]`, unmodified, is the import-failure fixture:\n  its tree has no identity module, so the guarded import fails and the gate still prints one\n  deny. A new fixture copies the gate beside a real judge and **no** identity module and runs\n  `--self-check`, expecting `FAIL policy-import` and exit 2.\n2700→\nOf the moved names, an existing test reaches these four through the Codex gate module, and\nbinding all 25 covers them:\n- `_safe_shell_command`: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` calls `module._safe_shell_command`;\n- `_payload_cwd_base`: the same file monkeypatches it on the module, then calls\n  `module._relative_target`. `_relative_target` stays in the Codex gate, so it reads the patched\n  binding;\n- `SIMPLE_SHELL_COMMAND`: read from `runpy.run_path(...)` globals, in the same file;\n- `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS`: read as `hook.SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS` in `test_h_mad_codex_runtime.py`.\n\n2710→The four were found with `grep -l \"\\b<name>\\b\"` over `git ls-files 'h-mad/tests/*.py'`, once per\nmoved name. Residual: a test that builds a name at run time is not found by that grep.\n\n**Rows re-pointed (R-3, AC-6.1).** At `29ae3e7b`, 62 rows name the Codex gate. A row is counted when\nits `find` starts inside a moved definition's span. 31 rows qualify, in 4 specs:\n`codex_gate_judge_wiring.json` 21, `codex_gate_shell_allowance.json` 6,\n`assemble_tdd_mutation_rows.json` 2 and `tdd_gate_differential.json` 2. 0 anchors are absent and 0\nare non-unique. The plan's 19, which is the 10-function closure alone, is a subset. The 12 added\nrows come from the allowance functions, `_read_regular_text`, `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS` and\n`_is_production_python`. Each re-pointed row's `file` becomes `h_mad_target_identity.py` under\n2720→`h-mad/scripts/` (written relative to `h-mad/`, as every row's `file` is),\nand each spec holding one is gated on `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n- **The identity module's own rows are rechecked too.** Adding the moved code can make one of the\n  module's existing `find` strings occur twice, and the harness then refuses it. The move Task runs\n  the anchor precheck over every spec naming that file.\n- **AC-6.1's \"one definition\".** A test asserts that each of the 17 moved functions has exactly one\n  `def` across `git ls-files h-mad`.\n\n### FR-4 — the agy write gate (DO-2)\n\n2730→**File and contract.** `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` reads stdin and prints exactly one JSON\nobject, `{\"decision\": \"allow\"}` or `{\"decision\": \"deny\", \"reason\": \"<text> kind=<kind>\"}`, then\nexits 0. `main` builds the decision inside one `try` and prints from a `finally` that emits a\n`judge-error` deny if nothing was decided. The judge, the identity module and the policy are\nimported inside that `try`. So a failed import still yields JSON (AC-4.2).\n\n**The order of evaluation.**\n1. Parse stdin, read with a 2 s bound like the Claude hook's `READER` (the Codex gate's `_payload`\n   calls `json.load(sys.stdin)` unbounded). `toolCall.name` must be a `str`, matched exactly and\n   case-sensitively. **A malformed payload** is exactly: stdin that is not a JSON object, a\n2740→   `toolCall` that is not an object, or a `toolCall.name` that is not a `str`. A `toolCall.args`\n   that is absent or not an object is read as `{}`, as the Codex gate's `_targets` reads a\n   non-`dict` `tool_input`; it is not malformed.\n2. **Class (a) is allowed before any state is read.** Class (a) is allowed whether or not step5 is\n   active, so no state read can change its answer.\n3. **The root.** It is `git -C <handler cwd>/.. rev-parse --show-toplevel`, canonicalised with the\n   identity module's `canonical_directory`.\n   A root that cannot be resolved, or a malformed payload, denies `kind=judge-error`. After the\n   root is resolved the gate calls `os.chdir(root)` once, so that every relative path the shared\n   policy resolves against `Path.cwd()` starts from the root rather than from `<root>/.agents`\n2750→   (class (c) refines this per command).\n4. **Activity.** `phase5_status = _any_phase5_status(root)`, the predicate the Codex gate computes\n   before it looks at the tool. It is an **input** to classes (b), (c) and (d), never a global\n   shortcut: the Codex gate judges a production `.py` target through `read_chain` even when the\n   `rglob` scan reads `inactive` (its `for raw in targets:  # M:G5` loop has no status filter, and\n   row `G5`'s test `test_unsearchable_docs_on_the_chain_denies_although_the_scan_is_inactive`\n   kills a mutant that adds one). `rglob` skips a directory it cannot search, so an `inactive` scan\n   does not prove the chain inactive. v1.0's \"`inactive` allows\" before class (b) was that mutant.\n5. **The `workspacePaths` cross-check, only under `S`** (`S` stands for `phase5_status in\n   {\"active\", \"unknown\"}` throughout this section). When `S` holds, `workspacePaths` is a list,\n2760→   and no entry resolves inside the root, the gate denies `kind=judge-error`. When `phase5_status`\n   is `inactive` the check stands down (spec FR-4 §\"Project root\", AC-4.1), and evaluation goes\n   on to step 6. v1.1 made this deny unconditional, so `run_command` `ls` with\n   `workspacePaths=[]` and an inactive state was refused (design audit cycle 2).\n6. **Classes (b), (c) and (d)**, as below. For (c) and (d), `inactive` allows and `unknown`\n   denies `kind=judge-error`. The Codex gate also refuses a shell command under `unknown`, but its\n   reason carries no `kind=` token (`H-MAD state is unreadable; refusing shell execution\n   fail-closed.`); the agy gate adds the kind, and the Codex gate has no counterpart for class (d).\n   Class (b) passes the status into its per-target sequence. An exception anywhere after step 2\n   denies `kind=judge-error`.\n2770→\n**The deny kinds: a closed set of five gate kinds plus the judge's.** Every deny the gate prints\ncarries exactly one `kind=` token, from this list:\n- `judge-error`: the gate could not reach a judgement (the rows of the table below that say so);\n- `tool-not-admitted`: class (d) under `active` (spec FR-4's table);\n- `target-outside-root`: T4's refusal;\n- `allowance-file-write`: T5's refusal;\n- `shell-not-admitted`: class (c)'s policy refusal (`_safe_shell_command` false and\n  `_operator_allowed` false);\n- at T7 only, the judge's own `verdict.kind`, passed through unchanged. It is a member of\n2780→  `h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `KINDS`, which holds 12 names at `cff71b49` (`red-measured`,\n  `no-test-resolved`, `test-missing`, `venv-escapes-root`, `pytest-missing`, `pytest-error`,\n  `no-tests-ran`, `no-summary`, `test-passing`, `timeout`, `judge-timeout`, `judge-error`).\n\nResidual: a kind the judge adds to `KINDS` later is passed through without a change here, so the\nclosed set over the gate's own denies is five names, and over T7 it is whatever `KINDS` holds.\nWhere the Codex gate's deny carries a kind (`judge-error` on an unresolvable target, an unreadable\nchain, a bad root; the judge's kind), the agy gate's carries the same one. Where the Codex deny\ncarries none (out-of-root target, allowance-file write, no write target, shell under `unknown`,\nan unreadable allowance file, the shell policy: 6 of the 13 `_deny(` call sites in\n2790→`h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py` at `cff71b49`, counted from `grep -n '_deny(' ` minus the\n`def _deny` line, with the one multi-line call read whole, as spec v1.8 FR-4 counts them), the\nagy kind is the one in the table below. Spec v1.8 FR-4 §\"Every deny carries a kind\" owns\n`judge-error`, `tool-not-admitted` and the judge's kind; the other three names are this design's.\n\n**Every deny, and when it is gated on step5.** The rule over the class: a deny that depends on\nthe payload's tool or targets fires only under `S`, except the two that follow the Codex gate in\nrunning whatever the scan reads (T7, because the chain is the authority, `M:G5`); a deny that\nfires before the status can be known, or because judging failed, is unconditional.\n\n2800→| Deny | When | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| malformed payload (step 1) | always: no tool is named, so no class applies | `judge-error` |\n| root cannot be resolved (step 3) | always: the status cannot be read without a root (a deliberate difference, below) | `judge-error` |\n| an exception after step 2, and the `finally` with nothing decided | always, as the Codex gate's `M:G2` | `judge-error` |\n| `workspacePaths` names nothing inside the root (step 5) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| class (b) with no target (no recognised key, or no recognised value that is a non-empty `str`) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T2 unresolvable | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T2a an existing directory (OD-2) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T4 outside the root, not admitted | `S` | `target-outside-root` |\n2810→| T5 the allowance file | `S` | `allowance-file-write` |\n| T7 chain unreadable | whatever the status (`M:G5`) | `judge-error` |\n| T7 judge non-ALLOW | whatever the status; reached only on an `active` chain | the judge's kind |\n| class (c) `CommandLine` missing or not a `str` | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) under `unknown` | `unknown` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) `_shell_allowance` returns a `str` (the allowance file unreadable or unparsable) | `active` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) policy refusal | `active` | `shell-not-admitted` |\n| class (d) | `active` | `tool-not-admitted` |\n| class (d) | `unknown` | `judge-error` |\n\n2820→The table is the whole set: 17 rows (v1.4 adds T2a), counted from this table, and every `deny` this section\nnames elsewhere is one of them. Under `inactive` the only denies left are the three unconditional\nrows and the two T7 rows; AC-4.1's \"allows every tool\" holds for every payload whose root resolves\nand whose chain is not `active` or `unreadable`. The T7 exception is v1.1's (step 4): an\n`inactive` scan does not prove the chain inactive.\n\n**Class (a), the read-only allowlist: 11 names.** `view_file`, `list_dir`, `grep_search`,\n`find_by_name`, `command_status`, `read_url_content`, `search_web`, `ask_question`, `finish`,\n`wait` and `wait_5_seconds`.\n- **The rule over the class.** A tool is in (a) only if it appears in a recorded `init.tools`\n2830→  inventory, and only if it writes nothing to the filesystem, starts no process, and drives no\n  other agent, MCP server or browser.\n- `finish` and the two waits are included because denying them leaves a headless agy unable to\n  end or pace its turn, and they touch nothing.\n- **Inventory.** The `init.tools` array of\n  `docs/03-analysis/probes/multi-host-runtime/rehearsal/live-agy-2026-10-01.log` holds 58 distinct\n  names (parsed as the first `init` event's `tools`, at `29ae3e7b`). All 11 are in it.\n- **That log carries no agy version.** Its `init` object has only `cwd`, `tools` and\n  `permission_mode`. The installed agy reports `1.4.0` (spec v1.7 FR-8 §\"Version\" records the\n  same at `200ffd52`; spec v1.6 and plan v1.4 read `1.3.6`).\n2840→  So the inventory is re-taken, with `agy --version`, by FR-8 step 1 (§\"FR-8\").\n- **Residual.** Membership is judged from the tool's name and agy's documentation, not observed. A\n  tool whose name reads as read-only but which writes would be admitted. The 11 names are the whole\n  exposure, and egress over the network is outside this gate.\n\n**Class (b), path-write: 5 names.** `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content`,\n`multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file` and `notebook_edit`.\n- **Path keys.** A per-tool table, `PATH_KEYS`, holds only observed keys. At `29ae3e7b` it holds\n  `write_to_file` → `TargetFile`. That key was seen in stream-json `tool_info.parameters` in 4\n  committed logs under `docs/03-analysis/probes/multi-host-runtime/rehearsal/`\n2850→  (`agy-G7-tool-write-then-link-read.log`, `agy-L3-unmapped-tool.log`, `agy-L9-no-step-index.log`,\n  `agy-L11-duplicate-index.log`), and never in a hook payload.\n- **Fail-closed.** The call's targets are the values of the keys from its table entry that are\n  present **and are a non-empty `str`** (spec v1.8 FR-4 §\"Target keys\"). A recognised key whose\n  value is anything else (`\"\"`, `null`, a number, a list) counts as no path key, and `\"\"` is never\n  canonicalised: v1.1 passed `\"\"` to T1, which design audit cycle 2 (agy) traced to the root\n  directory, no production name at T6, and an allow during active RED. A class-(b) call\n  with no target is denied `kind=judge-error` under `S` and allowed under `inactive`, as the Codex\n  gate denies a `Write`/`Edit`/`apply_patch` with no target (`[path] if path else []`, then\n  `not targets and phase5_status in {\"active\", \"unknown\"}`). That covers the other four tools until\n2860→  FR-8 step 1 records their keys. The non-`str` arm is stricter than the Codex gate, which\n  stringifies a truthy non-`str` value (`str(args.get(\"file_path\") or …)`) and judges it as a path;\n  that is a deliberate difference toward refusal.\n- **Judging a target: the Codex gate's per-target sequence, step for step.** The class is \"every\n  rule the Codex gate's `_main_guarded` applies to one target\" (its `_relative_target` and the\n  body of `for raw in targets:  # M:G5`), not the two rules the audit named. Each recognised\n  key's value `raw` goes through these steps, in this order, and the first that decides wins.\n  `S` stands for `phase5_status in {\"active\", \"unknown\"}`.\n  - **T1 canonicalise.** `canonicalise(str(root), str(Path(raw).expanduser()), str(root))`, the\n    Codex gate's call with the root as the base: agy sends no payload cwd.\n2870→  - **T2 unresolvable.** `resolved.unresolvable` → if `S`, deny `kind=judge-error`; else allow.\n    This runs before containment, as in `_relative_target`, which returns an unresolvable identity\n    before it tests `relative_to(root)`.\n  - **T2a an existing directory (OD-2, operator, 2026-10-16; v1.4).** `Path(resolved.target).is_dir()`\n    → if `S`, deny `kind=judge-error` with a reason naming the directory target; else allow. It runs\n    right after T2 and before T3, so an out-of-root directory under `S` denies `judge-error` too.\n    Without it the sequence allows a directory under `active`: `canonicalise` gives a directory\n    target (`.`, `src/`, `src`, the root itself) `unresolvable=False` and `names=()`, so T2 does not\n    fire, T3 and T5 pass, and T6 has no name to judge (scratch call at `84ec2133`, deleted). A\n    symlink to a directory is a directory here, since `is_dir` follows it. Residual: a target\n2880→    ending in `/` that names no existing directory is not a directory; `canonicalise` gives it\n    `names=('nodir',)` (same scratch call), and it goes through T3–T7 as a file name.\n  - **T3 containment, before anything that assumes it.** `Path(resolved.target).relative_to(root)`\n    raising `ValueError` means outside the root, and the target goes to T4 and never to T5–T7.\n    The order is load-bearing: `_is_shell_allowance` calls `candidate.relative_to(root)` without\n    catching `ValueError`, so calling it on an outside target raises (a scratch call at\n    `200ffd52` on a canonicalised `/tmp/audit-report.txt` raised `ValueError`), and the step-6\n    catch would turn every `/tmp` write into `judge-error`. The moved helper stays byte for byte;\n    the order makes it safe.\n  - **T4 outside the root.** Allowed when **no** name in `resolved.names` folds to `.py` under\n2890→    `fold_py_suffix`, and `resolved.target` is within `/tmp` by `_path_within`, or within the\n    payload's `artifactDirectoryPath` when that is a non-empty `str` (anything else is treated as\n    absent: `Path(\"\").resolve() == Path.cwd()` held in a scratch call at `200ffd52`, so an empty\n    value would make the allowance the process's working directory instead of anything the payload\n    named). Otherwise, if `S`, deny `kind=target-outside-root`; else allow. The artifact\n    allowance is computed before `S` is read, as `artifact = Path(adp) if isinstance(adp, str)\n    and adp else None` with `adp = payload.get(\"artifactDirectoryPath\")`, so a non-`str` value\n    never reaches `Path` (`Path(5)`, `Path([1])`, `Path({})` and `Path(True)` each raise\n    `TypeError`, scratch call at `cff71b49`, deleted).\n  - **T5 the allowance file.** If `S` and `_is_shell_allowance(root, resolved)`, deny\n2900→    `kind=allowance-file-write`. The helper\n    already tests the target and every same-parent hard-link name, casefolded.\n  - **T6 production, over every name.** For each `name` in `resolved.names`, `relative =\n    (Path(resolved.target).parent / name).relative_to(root).as_posix()`; collect those for which\n    `_is_production_python(relative)` holds. None → allow. Otherwise take `min` of the collected\n    `(name, absolute, relative)` tuples, as the Codex gate does. Judging only `resolved.target`\n    would miss production: `resolved.target` is `names[0]`, so with `src/test_a.py` hard-linked to\n    `src/z.py` the identity is `names=('test_a.py', 'z.py')` and the target names the test file\n    (scratch call at `200ffd52`: target-only `False`, any-name `True`).\n  - **T7 the chain.** `chain = read_chain(root, absolute)`. `chain.value == \"unreadable\"` → deny\n2910→    `kind=judge-error`; `chain.value != \"active\"` → allow; else `judge(root, absolute,\n    chain.records)`, and a non-ALLOW denies with the judge's kind. T6–T7 run whatever\n    `phase5_status` is (step 4).\n- **Deliberate differences from the Codex gate's target sequence**, each failing toward refusal or\n  forced by the payload:\n  - the base for a relative `raw` is the root, never a payload cwd (agy sends none);\n  - T4 admits `artifactDirectoryPath` as well as `/tmp` (spec FR-4);\n  - T4 tests every name in `resolved.names` for `.py`, where the Codex gate's `_tmp_report_target`\n    tests only the spelled path's resolved name. The spelled name and the referent's name are both\n    same-directory links, so they are in `names`, and the agy refusal set contains the Codex one;\n2920→  - a root that cannot be canonicalised denies outright (step 3); the Codex gate inspects the\n    component and allows when its scan reads `inactive` (`M:CX-ROOT-ENTER`);\n  - (v1.2) every deny carries a kind from the closed set above, where 6 of the Codex gate's 13 do\n    not; spec v1.8 FR-4 adopts the four above and this one as its fifth;\n  - (v1.2) a recognised path key whose value is a truthy non-`str` is no target here (spec v1.8\n    FR-4 §\"Target keys\"), where the Codex gate stringifies it;\n  - (v1.4) T2a refuses an existing-directory target under `S` (OD-2); the Codex gate's per-target\n    sequence has no such step (its three `is_dir` calls test a root candidate or a payload cwd,\n    never a write target).\n  Residual: any rule the Codex gate gains later is not inherited, because the per-target\n2930→  composition is written in both gates, like the shell composition below. The target\n  differential test (§\"Test Plan\", AC-4.1) is what catches that drift.\n\n**Class (c): `run_command`.** The command is `args.CommandLine`. A missing or non-`str` value\ndenies `kind=judge-error` under `S` and is allowed under `inactive` (v1.1 denied it whatever the\nstatus, against AC-4.1; design audit cycle 2). The Codex gate has no counterpart: a shell tool\nwith no command reaches none of its shell denies.\n- **The command's directory, `cwd`, computed as the Codex gate's `_command_cwd` computes it**,\n  with the root standing in for the session cwd agy does not send. `args.Cwd` was observed beside\n  `CommandLine` in 4 of 630 stream-json `run_command` parameter objects across the committed\n2940→  probes (counted as objects, not distinct calls).\n  - `Cwd` present and not a `str` (`0`, a list, a dict, `true`) → `MALFORMED_WORKDIR`;\n  - `Cwd` absent, `None` or `\"\"` → the root, as `_command_cwd` returns the session for an empty\n    workdir;\n  - a relative `Cwd` → `str(root / Path(Cwd).expanduser())`, joined against the root, never\n    against the handler's directory;\n  - an absolute `Cwd` → `str(Path(Cwd).expanduser())`.\n\n  `cwd` is therefore always `MALFORMED_WORKDIR` or an absolute `str`, so the process directory\n  cannot change what `_cwd_escapes_root` and `_payload_cwd_base` decide.\n2950→- **The process directory, for relative script and argument tokens.** `_safe_argv` resolves a\n  relative script, and `_path_within` a relative argument, against `Path.cwd()`. The shell runs the\n  command in `cwd`, so the gate resolves them there: when `cwd` is a `str` naming an existing\n  directory, the gate calls `os.chdir(cwd)`; in every other case (`MALFORMED_WORKDIR`, a path that\n  does not exist or is not a directory, or a `chdir` that raises `OSError`) it stays in the root\n  (step 3) and still judges with that `cwd`. **No case denies `judge-error`** — v1.0's \"a failed\n  change denies `judge-error`\" diverged from the Codex gate, which never changes directory, still\n  admits a read-only command such as `ls` under a bad workdir, and refuses a relative `.venv`\n  token there as a policy refusal through `_cwd_escapes_root` (`M:F2-ESCAPE`, `M:F2-MALFORMED`).\n  Why not stay in the root always: a relative script token would then be judged at\n2960→  `root/<token>` while the shell runs `<cwd>/<token>`, and a test-directory file named like an\n  h-mad script would be judged as the real one.\n- **The composition is the Codex gate's**, reading the shared names: `phase5_status == \"unknown\"`\n  denies `kind=judge-error`; `active` runs `_safe_shell_command(command, root, cwd)`, then\n  `_shell_allowance` (a `str` result denies `kind=judge-error`), then `_operator_allowed` (false\n  denies `kind=shell-not-admitted`); `inactive` allows. The refusal tells agy to use the\n  class-(b) tools, and names `SHELL_ALLOW_FILE`.\n- **Fixtures matching the Codex gate's workdir tests**, each run through the agy gate with `Cwd`\n  in place of `workdir`: `test_a_workdir_outside_the_root_stays_refused`'s three shapes\n  (`outside-absolute`, `dotdot-escape`, `absent` = `str(root / \"no-such-dir\")`) deny a relative\n2970→  `.venv` interpreter with the root's own venv as the positive control;\n  `test_a_present_but_malformed_workdir_fails_closed`'s four values deny; `Cwd=\"\"` behaves as\n  absent; and `ls` under each bad `Cwd` is allowed. Each of these denies carries\n  `kind=shell-not-admitted`, never `kind=judge-error`.\n- **Residual of the process directory.** When `cwd` does not exist the gate judges relative tokens\n  against the root, but the shell cannot run the command there either.\n- **Residual.** This six-line composition is the one piece of policy written in both gates. It\n  carries different refusal text, and each copy has its own mutation rows. A differential test pins\n  them equal: a corpus of commands × allowance-file states, judged by both gates, with the same\n  admit/deny in every cell.\n2980→\n**Class (d): everything else → deny, `kind=tool-not-admitted`** when `phase5_status` is\n`active` (`unknown` denies `kind=judge-error` and `inactive` allows, step 6). At the inventory above that is\n41 names: `ask_custom_permission`, `ask_permission`, `browser_click_element`,\n`browser_drag_pixel_to_pixel`, `browser_get_dom`, `browser_get_network_request`, `browser_input`,\n`browser_list_network_requests`, `browser_mouse_down`, `browser_mouse_up`, `browser_move_mouse`,\n`browser_press_key`, `browser_refresh_page`, `browser_resize_window`, `browser_scroll`,\n`browser_scroll_dom`, `browser_select_option`, `browser_subagent`, `call_mcp_tool`,\n`capture_browser_console_logs`, `capture_browser_screenshot`, `click_browser_pixel`,\n`define_subagent`, `delete_knowledge`, `execute_browser_javascript`, `generate_image`,\n2990→`invoke_subagent`, `list_browser_pages`, `list_permissions`, `list_resources`, `manage_inbox`,\n`manage_subagents`, `manage_task`, `notebook_execution`, `open_browser_url`, `read_browser_page`,\n`read_resource`, `run_workflow`, `schedule`, `send_command_input` and `send_message`. The 41 are\n58 − 11 − 5 − 1, derived as a set difference over the parsed inventory. Any name absent from the\ninventory, which includes tools added later, also falls here.\n\n**`--self-check`.** It checks four things: the classifier puts `view_file` in (a) and\n`call_mcp_tool` in (d); `_safe_shell_command` refuses `python3 -c \"open('x','w').write('x')\"`; the\npolicy names import; and `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` is readable. It prints exactly one line on stdout.\n**Rule over the axis (v1.4, orchestrator decision):** a FAIL that is a verdict of a check that ran\n3000→prints its token and exits 0, like PASS; the token carries the verdict. Only a FAIL meaning the\ncheck could not run exits 2. So `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS` and the two verdict FAILs,\n`FAIL classification` and `FAIL shell-policy`, exit 0. `FAIL policy-import` (the judge, the\nidentity module or the policy names do not import, or `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` cannot be read) exits 2.\nFR-6's Codex `FAIL policy-import` exits 2 for the same reason. FR-5's wiring check is unaffected:\nit requires the exact line `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS` with exit 0, so a FAIL line with exit 0 still reads\nas `self-check-failed`. The Codex gate's own `--self-check` breaks this rule today: its verdict\nFAILs `FAIL parser` and `FAIL shell-policy` print to stderr and return 2. The impl-plan's FR-6\nTask (Task 4) corrects `FAIL shell-policy` by orchestrator decision. `FAIL parser` is the same\nclass and is owed (§\"Owed by other documents\"). Residual: a self-check FAIL added later is\n3010→classed by this rule, not listed here. AC-6.1's parity with the Codex list is a test, not this self-check.\nThe test locates the `unsafe` tuple assigned inside the Codex gate's `main` with `ast`, requires\nexactly one, and asserts that the agy gate refuses each member.\n\n**Overwrite residual.** agy merges handlers, and a sibling's `overwrite` is a shallow merge into the\ntool's arguments (hooks.md §\"PreToolUse Contract\"). The gate judges the arguments it receives. A\nsibling that rewrites a path key after this gate judged it is not seen. At `29ae3e7b` the operator's\nglobal `~/.gemini/config/hooks.json` holds one `PreToolUse` group, `orca-status` with matcher `*`,\nwhose script prints `{\"decision\":\"ask\"}` for `PreToolUse` and never `overwrite`.\n\n3020→**Roles.** The gate cannot tell an implementing agy from a reviewing agy. During active step5, a\n5e-review agy in a wired project is therefore held to the same tool and shell rules, as a Codex\nverifier is today. 6a-prime runs after step5 ends, so it is unaffected.\n\n**Inherited residual.** The gate decides \"active\" from the root's own state, as both existing gates\ndo. `docs/.bkit-memory.json` is untracked in this repository and in HemaSuite\n(`git ls-files docs/.bkit-memory.json` prints nothing in either at `29ae3e7b`). How Phase 5c\nprovisions state inside a worktree was not read for this design, so whether the three gates stand\ndown in a worktree is not claimed either way.\n\n3030→**Hook timeout.** The floor is `math.ceil(JUDGE_BUDGET_S + 20)`, read from the module. At\n`29ae3e7b` `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` is `40.0`, so the floor is 60.\n\n### FR-5 — the wiring check, the assembler, and the worktree (DO-6)\n\n**The check.** `h_mad_hook_wiring.py` gains `--agy`. It calls a new `check_agy(project_root)`,\nprints the one line that function returns, and exits with the code beside it. Without `--agy`,\nbehaviour is unchanged. The reasons are evaluated in FR-5's order, and the first one found is\nprinted:\n1. `<root>/.agents/hooks.json` is absent → `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file`, exit 0. When it is\n3040→   present but not JSON, or not a JSON object, the line is `UNREADABLE file=<pct> error=<name>`\n   with exit 2, and the name is `not-an-object` for the second case.\n2. There is no top-level key `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate`, or its value is not an object → `no-entry`.\n3. `enabled` is present and is not `true` → `disabled`.\n4. No `PreToolUse` group has a `matcher` of exactly `*` or `\"\"` → `matcher-narrow`. An absent or\n   empty `PreToolUse` counts here too.\n5. No handler in such a group has a `command` whose `shlex.split` equals\n   `[\"python3\", \"~/.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py\"]` → `bad-command`.\n6. That handler's `timeout` is not an `int` ≥ the floor; an absent timeout means agy's default of\n   30 → `timeout-below-floor`.\n3050→7. `<python3 on PATH> <expanduser(path)> --self-check` must exit 0 and print exactly\n   `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS`, within 30 s. Otherwise → `self-check-failed`. When it passes, the line is\n   `AGY-GATE: WIRED`.\n\nResidual: an equivalent spelling of the command (an absolute path, `$HOME`) reads `bad-command`.\nThat fails toward the halt.\n\n**The assembler.** `--agent` gains `agy`, and the timeout default becomes a table,\n`{\"codex\": 900, \"grok\": 1500, \"agy\": 1500}`, with `--timeout` still winning. The choices line\nbecomes `ap.add_argument(\"--agent\", choices=(\"codex\", \"grok\", \"agy\"), default=\"codex\")`, and the\n3060→timeout line keeps the substring `args.timeout is not None` exactly once\n(`timeout = args.timeout if args.timeout is not None else TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS[args.agent]`), so the\nexisting mutation anchors on it survive (§\"Mutation rows\", \"Existing rows the edits move\"). For `--agent agy`,\n`main` runs the check before `assemble`, as a subprocess:\n`[sys.executable, AGY_WIRING_CHECK, \"--agy\", \"--project-root\", <root>]`, with a 60 s bound.\n`AGY_WIRING_CHECK` is a module constant and the tests' seam. Then FR-5's table applies:\n- `WIRED` → continue to PASS;\n- `UNWIRED` → print `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified`, then the check line indented by\n  two spaces, as existing HALT details are. Write no prompt, exit 0;\n- `UNREADABLE` → the same two lines, exit 2;\n3070→- no `AGY-GATE:` line, or a timeout → `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token`,\n  exit 2.\n\nThe verdict branch does not raise `Halt`, because every `Halt` exits 2. The module docstring's\n`exit 0 on PASS, 2 on any HALT` becomes: `exit 0 on PASS and on HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified after\nAGY-GATE: UNWIRED; 2 on every other HALT`. A test pins that sentence, and a test pins that the old\none is absent. `command_block`'s existing prefix rewrite already emits `hmad-dispatch exec agy`, and\n`exec agy` accepts `--model`, `--effort` and `--timeout` (`hmad-dispatch.sh` `_cmd_exec`).\n\n**DO-6: no override path in code.**\n3080→- **Tests.** Tests of `check_agy` set `HOME` to a fixture directory holding\n  `.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` as a symlink to the gate under test.\n  `os.path.expanduser` honours `HOME`, so the shipped code has no worktree switch.\n- **Any test that runs the assembler with `--agent agy` and expects a prompt** needs that `HOME`\n  fixture **and** a `<root>/.agents/hooks.json` holding FR-5's exact entry in its project root.\n  A bare `tmp_path` reads `no-hooks-file` and halts with no prompt written, which is the designed\n  outcome, not a defect. The fixture is called the **WIRED fixture** below. The check runs in a\n  subprocess, so `AGY_WIRING_CHECK` cannot be monkeypatched from a test that also runs the\n  assembler as a subprocess (as `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py` `run_cli` does); the fixture is\n  the only seam such a test has.\n3090→- **This feature's own Phase-5 worktree.** It ships no `.agents/hooks.json`, so `--agent agy` there\n  reads `no-hooks-file` and halts. That is correct: this feature's implementer is Codex.\n- **An adopting project's worktree.** Its tracked `.agents/hooks.json` reaches the worktree, and\n  the installed path is the skills repository's main checkout. So the check reads `WIRED` once this\n  feature has merged.\n- **This repository's own `.agents/hooks.json`** is committed only by FR-8's post-merge stage, and\n  only after its real refusal passes (§\"FR-8\"). Until then the halt stands here mechanically,\n  because the file is absent. Evidence that tracking it needs no `git add -f`: at `29ae3e7b`,\n  `.git/info/exclude` holds `/.agents/skills/`, and `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints\n  nothing.\n3100→\n### FR-7 — the `review_independence` add (DO-3) and the Phase-7 warnings\n\n**The add.** `h_mad_state_write.py` gains `--append KEY=JSON`, which is repeatable.\n- **Strict decoding.** `json.loads` with no string fallback. A decode failure prints `ERROR: …`\n  and exits 2, and the store is unchanged.\n- **One write, accumulated in command-line order.** `--set` fields and `--append` entries apply\n  inside **one** `_mutate` call, through `merge_fields`, which gains an `appends` parameter. Its\n  `apply` keeps its existing statements byte for byte (`record = {**records[feature], **fields}`,\n  then the owner-heartbeat `if` and its assignment) and runs the appends after them, so the two\n3110→  `heartbeat_beat.json` rows anchored on that `if` still match once (§\"Mutation rows\").\n  `appends` is the list of `(key, entry)` pairs in the order given. Below, `prior` is\n  `records[feature]` and `record` is the merged dict; for each pair in order, `current = record.get(key, [])`\n  is read from the **updated** `record`, and `record[key] = list(current) + [entry]`. Two\n  `--append` flags on one key in one call therefore keep every earlier entry and add both, in\n  order. Reading `prior[key]` instead would keep only the last addition.\n- **`--set` and `--append` on the same key in one call are refused** (`--set and --append name\n  the same key: <key>`), exit 2, store unchanged. `--set` replaces a whole value and `--append`\n  adds to it, so the pair has no single reading; refusing is the defined interaction.\n- **The append-only keys refuse `--set` (v1.3).** `review_independence` and\n3120→  `implementation_authors` are append-only: an entry, once written, is never removed or altered.\n  The writer operations that could replace a declared value are `--set` on the command line and\n  `merge_fields` / `set_fields` for library callers (`set_fields` calls `merge_fields`);\n  `create_feature` leaves an existing record alone and `drop_undeclared` drops only undeclared\n  keys, so neither can touch a declared one. The rule is therefore one check at the top of\n  `merge_fields`, before its `def apply`: a key of `fields` in the module constant\n  `APPEND_ONLY = (\"review_independence\", \"implementation_authors\")` raises `StateWriteError`\n  (`<key> is append-only: use --append`), exit 2, store unchanged. It sits outside `apply`, so\n  `apply`'s statements and the heartbeat rows anchored on them are unmoved. `--append` itself goes\n  through the `appends` parameter, never through `fields`, so it is not refused. Residual,\n3130→  exactly: a writer that bypasses `_mutate` (the same residual as below), and a key added to the\n  schema later as an array of history that is not added to `APPEND_ONLY`.\n- **Refusals.** An existing value that is present and is not a list is refused\n  (`cannot append to a non-array value`). The result is validated by `classify`, so FR-1's item\n  schema applies on both validator paths. A refusal raises before `_atomic_write` and prints the\n  writer's existing `ERROR:` refusal with exit 2.\n- **Heartbeat.** An owner's `--session-id` refreshes the heartbeat, as `merge_fields` does.\n- **Output.** The `STATE-WRITE: OK` line lists the appended keys with the set keys.\n- **Lost-entry detection is not needed, and that is why this mechanism was chosen.** `_mutate`\n  holds `fcntl.flock` on the sidecar lock across load, apply and write, so no other writer that\n3140→  goes through `_mutate` can interleave between the read and the write. Residual: a writer that\n  bypasses `_mutate`, such as a hand edit or another tool's raw write, can still race. That is true\n  of every field.\n- **The orchestrator's step (SKILL.md, FR-10).** After each 5e-review, 5e-verify and 6a-prime leg,\n  it runs `--append review_independence='{\"leg\":…,\"module\":…,\"reviewer\":…,\"implementer\":…,\"independent\":…}'`.\n  `independent` is `reviewer != implementer`. This applies for every implementer.\n- **Attribution: `implementer` is read from the dispatch, as spec FR-7 §\"Attribution\" requires.**\n  It is never read from `implementer_switch.to` or from the `implementer` field, which record\n  routing, not authorship. Counter-example the rule exists for: Codex out → agy dispatched → switch\n  stored; Codex back → Codex dispatched with `switch=none`, and the stored `.to` still says `agy`.\n3150→  - **The authors of a module** are the agents of that module's **accepted implementation\n    dispatches** (spec v1.8 FR-7, E4): the 5e GREEN dispatch the orchestrator accepted, and every\n    later fix dispatch for that module it accepted. A 5d RED dispatch is never one: it writes\n    tests, not the reviewed code (`codex-implementer-prompt.md` assigns GREEN to 5e, `For GREEN\n    phase (5e):`). A dispatch that halted, timed out or was rejected authored nothing. The agent of\n    a dispatch is the value of the `TDD-IMPLEMENTER:` token the orchestrator resolved just before\n    staging it, which is the `--agent` it passed to `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` (`--phase green` for\n    the 5e GREEN dispatch; `--phase` takes `red` or `green`); for the Claude fallback, where no\n    dispatch leaves the session, it is the same token's `claude` value.\n  - **The durable author record (v1.3, OD-E2 option (a), spec FR-1 D-15).** Each accepted\n3160→    implementation dispatch is recorded as one `implementation_authors` entry, through the same\n    `--append` operation as above:\n    `--append implementation_authors='{\"module\":\"<module>\",\"agent\":\"<agent>\"}'`. The writer gains\n    no code for it beyond FR-1's schema entry and the `APPEND_ONLY` refusal: `--append` is\n    key-agnostic, and `classify` applies the item schema on both validator paths.\n  - **Where in the procedure the append happens.** The rule over the axis is an ordering:\n    **every accepted implementation dispatch for a module is appended before any review leg of\n    its code runs** (spec FR-1 D-15), and so before that leg's `review_independence` entry. Its\n    members in the procedure:\n    - **5e GREEN.** In SKILL.md's 5e bullet, when the orchestrator accepts a module's GREEN\n3170→      dispatch, that is when it reads the `STATUS:` token `DONE` or `DONE_WITH_CONCERNS` and stops\n      re-dispatching GREEN for that module. It appends there, before the module's 5e spec-review\n      leg runs. A GREEN that is\n      re-dispatched (the path toward `step5e:green_unreachable:<module>`) is not accepted and is\n      not appended.\n    - **Every accepted fix dispatch** for the module, wherever it is dispatched (a 5e fix after a\n      review or verification finding, or a fix after a 6a-prime `WITH_FIXES`): appended once it\n      is accepted, before the next leg that reviews the fixed code.\n    - **Never 5d RED.** The 5d bullet gains no append.\n\n3180→    An accepted GREEN that a later step halts on (`step5e:verify_failed:<module>`, a failed revert\n    test) keeps its entry, because entries are append-only and its code is still in the tree. That\n    direction is the safe one: an extra author can only turn an entry `independent=false`, never\n    `true`. Residual, exactly: the order is orchestrator prose (SKILL.md, FR-10), and no code\n    refuses a `review_independence` append made before the author append it should follow. A\n    refused author append (an invalid entry) prints the writer's `ERROR:` refusal with the store\n    unchanged; the orchestrator corrects the entry and appends again before the review leg runs\n    (spec FR-1).\n  - **Reading the complete author set.** For module `M`, the set is the `agent` values of the\n    well-formed entries of the record's `implementation_authors` whose `module == M`. Well-formed\n3190→    is `property_valid(\"implementation_authors\", [entry])` (§\"The warnings\" below), the definition\n    the orchestrator applies, since no script reads the field (spec FR-1). The latest\n    accepted implementation dispatch is the last such entry in array order, since `--append` adds\n    at the end and the orchestrator appends at acceptance. The set is read from this field and\n    from nothing else: never from earlier `review_independence` entries, which are a projection of\n    it with one `implementer` per entry. Counter-example: with authors `[agy, codex]`, a grok\n    5e-review stores `implementer=codex` and a Codex 5e-verify stores `implementer=codex`, so a\n    set rebuilt from those entries is `{codex}` and an agy 6a-prime would read as independent\n    (design audit cycle 2, codex). The orchestrator's in-context list of dispatches is not read\n    either: the record is the only source, so a resumed orchestrator reads exactly what an\n3200→    uninterrupted one reads. v1.2 kept the set \"in the procedure\" and is replaced by this.\n  - **A 5e leg** reviews one module. If the reviewer is in that module's author set,\n    `implementer` is the reviewer and `independent` is `false`. Otherwise `implementer` is the\n    agent of the module's latest entry, and `independent` is `true` (spec v1.8 FR-7, S5).\n  - **6a-prime** reviews the whole diff. The orchestrator writes one 6a-prime entry per module,\n    with `module` set, applying the same rule to that module's complete author set.\n  - **When no author can be read, no entry is written.** The leg writes no `review_independence`\n    entry for module `M`, rather than one whose `independent` it cannot establish, in exactly\n    these cases:\n    - the record has no `implementation_authors` field: a record whose implementation dispatches\n3210→      were accepted before this field existed (spec v1.8 FR-7's \"no entry\" rule, kept for those\n      records only). An absent field is never read as an empty author set, which would make\n      every reviewer independent; AC-7.3's pre-field fixture pins this (spec v1.9);\n    - the field is present and holds no well-formed entry for `M`: an author append that was\n      skipped, the residual of the ordering rule above;\n    - the field is present and is not a list, or holds an entry that is not well-formed: a hand\n      edit, since the writer refuses both. One malformed entry may be the lost record of `M`'s\n      author, so the set is not established for any module of that record.\n\n    A resumed orchestrator whose dispatch context is gone is **not** a member: it reads the field\n3220→    like any other. Residual, as the spec states: `review_independence_unrecorded` fires only when\n    no `leg=6a-prime` entry exists at all, so one module missing among several recorded ones has\n    no warning of its own.\n  - **AC-7.3's multi-author recovered fixture, from durable state.** The record holds\n    `implementation_authors` `[{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"agy\"},{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"codex\"}]`. A\n    grok 5e-review: grok is not in `{agy, codex}`, so the entry is `implementer=codex` (the latest\n    entry) and `independent=true`. A Codex 5e-verify: codex is in the set, so the entry is\n    `implementer=codex` and `independent=false`. Then the dispatch context is discarded, and only\n    the record is kept. An agy 6a-prime: agy is in the set read from the record, so the entry is\n    `implementer=agy` and `independent=false`, the outcome AC-7.3 expects. The mutant AC-7.3 names\n3230→    (authors recovered from earlier entries' `implementer` values only) yields `{codex}` and\n    `independent=true`, so the fixture is RED against it. The other three v1.8 AC-7.3 fixtures follow\n    from the same field: a 5d RED appends nothing, so the agy reviewer of a Codex GREEN reads\n    `{codex}`; a rejected agy GREEN appends nothing, so the same holds; and the author set never\n    reads `implementer_switch.to`. The pre-field fixture (spec v1.9) runs the same reviews on a\n    record with no `implementation_authors`, and the 6a-prime leg writes no entry for the module;\n    its mutant reads the absent field as an empty set, so the agy reviewer reads as independent.\n    Whether attribution gets a code surface for these fixtures to\n    drive is a separate open decision, still the impl-plan's (§\"Owed by other documents\").\n  - The writer does not check attribution; the Phase-7 consistency check below verifies only that\n3240→    `independent == (reviewer != implementer)`.\n  - Residual: a module's authors are the agents of accepted dispatches only. Code a reviewer wrote\n    during its own leg (outside an implementation dispatch) is not attributed to it.\n\n**The warnings.** `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` `check` calls a new\n`_review_independence_warnings(record)` and extends `warnings`. It never touches `blockers`, so\n`ready` does not move.\n- **Well-formed means validated by the schema, not by a second copy of the value sets.**\n  `h_mad_state_validate.py` gains `property_valid(name, value)`, which validates `value` against the\n  strict schema's `properties[name]` through `_make_validator`, on both paths. For\n3250→  `implementer_switch` it also applies `rfc3339_datetime` to `ts`, and only when `value` is a\n  `dict`: it calls `_semantic_ok({\"implementer_switch\": value})`, the same predicate `classify`\n  uses, so `None` (the schema's `[\"object\", \"null\"]`) and every other non-`dict` go to the schema\n  alone and never raise (v1.1 applied the predicate unguarded; design audit cycle 2). An entry is well-formed when\n  `property_valid(\"review_independence\", [entry])` holds **and** `entry[\"independent\"] ==\n  (entry[\"reviewer\"] != entry[\"implementer\"])`. A boolean that contradicts its own two agents is\n  malformed, so it is never read as `independent=true`.\n- **`review_independence_malformed`** is emitted for each entry that is not well-formed, and once\n  when the field is present and not a list.\n- **`review_non_independent`** is emitted for each well-formed entry with `independent=false`,\n3260→  naming the leg, the module and the agent.\n- **`review_independence_unrecorded`** is emitted when `record.get(\"archreview\")` is in\n  `{\"READY_TO_MERGE\", \"WITH_FIXES\", \"NO\"}` and no **well-formed** entry has `leg == \"6a-prime\"`.\n  - The code path has no implementer condition (D-11), and it reads neither `implementer` nor\n    `implementer_switch`, nor `implementation_authors` (an authorship record no script reads,\n    spec FR-1 D-15), so AC-7.2's per-implementer fixtures (`grok` and `claude` through\n    `implementer_switch.to`) all reach the same branch.\n  - The detail text names the agents the record's entries attest (a projection, which is why it\n    is only detail text and never an author set the attribution reads): the distinct `implementer` values\n    of the well-formed 5e entries, sorted, or `authors=unrecorded` when there are none. It never\n3270→    names `implementer_switch.to` or `implementer`: they record routing, and the same attribution\n    rule as the entries applies to every surface that names an author.\n  - A malformed 6a-prime entry does not count as recorded, so it raises both warnings.\n- Each warning prints through the existing `  WARNING <code>: <detail>` loop in `main`.\n\n### FR-8 — the tracer, after the merge (DO-6, R-2) — OQ-B, spec D-13\n\n**Why after the merge.** The tracer needs agy to run the gate at the installed path,\n`~/.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/` → `/Users/kimhawk/orca/skills/h-mad`\n(`ls -la ~/.gemini/config/skills` at `29ae3e7b`). That path is main, and main gains the gate only\n3280→when this feature merges. An override flag would ship a code path that points the check away from\nthe installed gate. A hand edit of the symlink would also change every live session.\n\n**The stage.** It runs after Phase 7's integrate and before the feature is recorded COMPLETE.\n1. **A fixture project.** It is a throwaway git repository with one module task. It has its own\n   `docs/.bkit-memory.json` step5 record and its own `.agents/hooks.json`, holding FR-5's exact\n   entry. It is created beside this checkout, never under `/tmp`, because agy's trust decision for\n   `.agents/hooks.json` may depend on location.\n2. **Step 1, payload capture.** The real class-(b) payload keys are recorded, and so is\n   `init.tools` with `agy --version`. They land as one commit on main that fills `PATH_KEYS` and\n3290→   re-checks the class-(a) inventory. That commit carries tests built from the captured payloads,\n   written RED then GREEN, and it lands before step 2. The capture hook is then deleted.\n   **D-FR8-1 (2026-10-17):** on agy `1.4.0` only `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content` and\n   `run_command` are declared to the model; `multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file` and\n   `notebook_edit` appear in `init.tools` only. Step 1 captures the three declared tools; the\n   other three keep no `PATH_KEYS` entry and deny under active step5 (impl-plan Task 20 v1.3).\n3. **Steps 2, 2a and 3** in the fixture project, as FR-8 states them. Step 2a's delay hook is\n   added to, and removed from, the fixture's `hooks.json`, and the before and after\n   `shasum -a 256` are recorded.\n4. **This repository's adoption.** `.agents/hooks.json` is committed here, and step 2 is re-run\n3300→   once in this repository to settle trust at this location.\n5. The derived timeout replaces `agy`'s `1500` in the assembler table, as FR-8's formula sets.\n\nEvery reading is stamped with the sha and `agy --version` under\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`.\n\n**AC-8.1's formula check is a probe check, not a suite test.** A committed script asserts that the\nassembler's `agy` default equals `max(900, 3 × slower)` rounded up to a multiple of 60 over the","absolute_path":"/Users/kimhawk/.grok/sessions/%2FUsers%2Fkimhawk%2F.local%2Fstate%2Fh-mad%2Fgrok-audit-surface%2Frun-ab2c85dc2e25-20261024T132452Z%2Froots%2Fgas-root-d9-s2v1-oesswhhq/01a16e92-2377-7a81-9b1e-b1c30274d035/prompts/prompt_0.txt","offset":2308,"limit":1000,"raw_output":"               │        │                 │\n   TDD-STATE: line   TDD-IMPLEMENTER:     ├── h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py ──┐\n               │        │                 └── h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py ────┤ both import the\n   h-mad-tdd-gate.sh  orchestrator ──► h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --agent    │ gate policy from\n   (Claude's writes)                    └─ agy: h_mad_hook_wiring.py --agy\n                                                                        │\n                                          h_mad_target_identity.py ◄────┘\n```\n\n- The judge is the only place `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE`, `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE` and `PATH` are read\n  for authorship. It runs as a child of the Claude hook, so it sees the hook's environment.\n- The Claude hook parses the line and refuses. It re-derives nothing.\n- The agy gate and the Codex gate share one copy of the shell policy, the allowance file rules,\n  the phase-5 activity predicate and the production predicate.\n\n## Detailed Design\n\n### FR-1 — state fields, and the timestamp refusal (DO-4)\n\n**Schema.** Five properties are added to `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json`. `required` does\nnot change, and `additionalProperties` stays `false`. Every construct below is in the bundled\nvalidator's supported set (`_MiniDraft7`'s docstring, the `Deliberately supports ONLY` paragraph):\n`type`, `enum`, `required`, `properties`, `additionalProperties`, `items`, and (v1.3, for\n`implementation_authors`) `minLength`. No `oneOf`, `anyOf`,\n`const` or `pattern` is used, so the two validator paths cannot disagree on structure.\n\n**Property order.** The five are appended, in the order listed below, after `fallback_agent`, which is the schema's last\nproperty today (`list(properties)` ends `…, 'codex_status', 'fallback_agent'` at `200ffd52`).\n`test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts`\nasserts `list(properties).index(\"fallback_agent\") == list(properties).index(\"codex_status\") + 1`,\nso any new property placed between those two breaks AC-1.1. Rule over the axis: no property is\ninserted anywhere before `fallback_agent`.\n\n- `implementer`: `enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", null]`.\n- `agy_status`: `enum` `[\"available\", \"unavailable\", \"exhausted\", null]`.\n- `implementer_switch`: `type` `[\"object\", \"null\"]`. Properties `from` and `to` take `enum`\n  `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`, `reason` takes `enum` `[\"env\", \"status\", \"cli-absent\"]`, and\n  `ts` is `type` `string` with `\"format\": \"date-time\"` as an annotation only. All four are\n  `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`.\n- `review_independence`: `type` `array`. Its `items` is an object with `leg`\n  (`enum` `[\"5e-review\", \"5e-verify\", \"6a-prime\"]`), `module` (`type` `[\"string\", \"null\"]`),\n  `reviewer` and `implementer` (each `enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`) and `independent`\n  (`type` `boolean`). All five are `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`.\n- `implementation_authors` (v1.3, operator decision OD-E2, spec FR-1 D-15): `type` `array`. Its\n  `items` is an object with `module` (`type` `string`, `minLength` `1`: it is the `--module` value\n  the assembler requires) and `agent` (`enum` `[\"codex\", \"agy\", \"grok\", \"claude\"]`). Both are\n  `required`, and `additionalProperties` is `false`. The array is a log, not a set: duplicates\n  are kept, and array order is acceptance order (spec FR-1 D-15). One entry is one accepted implementation dispatch; §\"FR-7\" says who writes it and when,\n  and how attribution reads it. `module` is a `string`, not `[\"string\", \"null\"]` as in\n  `review_independence`, because an implementation dispatch is always for one module (5e stages\n  one module per GREEN). It is placed last, after `review_independence`, so the AC-1.1 index\n  assertion on `fallback_agent` is untouched. `minLength` is in `_MiniDraft7`'s supported set (its\n  docstring's `Deliberately supports ONLY` paragraph names it, and its `_ok` checks it on a\n  `str`), and the schema already uses it, so the two validator paths cannot disagree on its\n  structure. Fixtures: AC-1.3 (§\"Test Plan\").\n\n**The `.ts` refusal: a predicate outside the validator backend.** The option chosen is the third of\nthe three the spec lists, placed in the validator module rather than in the writer, so that a\nhand-edited record is refused by `h_mad_state_validate.py` too.\n- **The probe it rests on.** Reading at `29ae3e7b`, the plan's command\n  (`/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3 -B -c '… Draft7Validator(s).is_valid(x) … v._MiniDraft7(s).is_valid(x)'`\n  with `s={\"type\":\"string\",\"format\":\"date-time\"}` and `x=\"not-a-date\"`) prints\n  `Draft7Validator: True` and `bundled: True`. `format` is enforced on neither path, so declaring it\n  refuses nothing.\n- **The predicate.** `h_mad_state_validate.py` gains `rfc3339_datetime(value) -> bool`:\n  - `re.fullmatch` with `re.ASCII` over\n    `([0-9]{4})-([0-9]{2})-([0-9]{2})[Tt]([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2})(\\.[0-9]+)?([Zz]|[+-]([0-9]{2}):([0-9]{2}))`;\n  - then `datetime.datetime(y, mo, d, h, mi, s)` must construct, which refuses `2026-02-30`;\n  - then an offset's hours must be at most 23 and its minutes at most 59;\n  - a non-`str` is refused.\n\n  `fullmatch` and `[0-9]` are deliberate: `$` admits a trailing newline, and `\\d` admits non-ASCII\n  digits. A scratch probe at `29ae3e7b`, since deleted, returned these results under both\n  `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3` (3.11.8) and `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` (3.15.0):\n  - refused: `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`, `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z`, an offset of `+24:00`, a seconds\n    field of 60, a value with\n    full-width digits, the writer's form followed by `\\n`, `5` and `None`;\n  - accepted: `2026-10-16T07:05:09Z` and `2026-10-16T07:05:09.123+09:00`.\n- **Where it applies.** A private `_semantic_ok(record)` returns `False` exactly when `record` is\n  a `dict` whose `implementer_switch` is a `dict` whose `ts` fails `rfc3339_datetime`; it returns\n  `True` for anything else, a non-`dict` record included, so it never raises.\n  `classify` keeps its existing first statement (`if not isinstance(record, dict): return\n  \"invalid\"`, which is what `test_h_mad_state_validate_fallback.py` `CORPUS`'s\n  `(\"not an object\", [\"nope\"])` row exercises), then calls `_semantic_ok` and returns `invalid`\n  when it fails, before either schema tier is tried. That runs whichever\n  backend `_make_validator` chose, so both paths refuse. The writer refuses through `classify` as it\n  does today (`_mutate`: `classify(record) != \"strict\"`), and it prints its existing refusal.\n- **No library format checker is used.** The `rfc3339-validator` hazard therefore does not bind,\n  and AC-1.2's package-absent fixture is not owed, since AC-1.2 makes it conditional on that\n  mechanism. Recorded for the reader: at `29ae3e7b`, `rfc3339_validator` imports in\n  `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3`, and `/opt/homebrew/bin/python3` has no `jsonschema`.\n- **Not schema-wide.** `started_ts`, `owner_heartbeat_ts` and `halt_ts` stay annotation-only, so\n  no existing record can change class. The `classify_store` run the spec asks of a schema-wide\n  mechanism is not owed. A reading anyway, taken 2026-10-16 with this repository at `29ae3e7b` and\n  HemaSuite at `77fb16f51` (both files are untracked, so the sha does not pin their content): this\n  repository's `docs/.bkit-memory.json` has 54 records and HemaSuite's has 7. Zero records in either file carry\n  any of the four FR-1 keys. That zero is load-bearing, not incidental: the strict schema has\n  `additionalProperties: false`, so the writer has refused those keys until now. A hand-edited\n  record is the residual. The fifth key, `implementation_authors` (v1.3), has the same zero for\n  the same load-bearing reason: one reading on 2026-10-16, this repository at `3ad735f7` and\n  HemaSuite at `02d52e4e7`, over each file's `orchestrator_state` object, gives 54 and 8 records,\n  and 0 records in either file carry any of the five keys.\n- **A seconds field of 60 is refused, as spec FR-1 requires (D-14).** RFC 3339 admits a leap\n  second; spec v1.7 FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`, because every `.ts`\n  is machine-written by h-mad's own writer through `datetime`, which cannot hold second 60. The predicate refuses it at the `datetime` construction\n  step, and a fixture pins `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` refused and `2016-12-31T23:59:59Z` accepted on\n  both paths.\n\n**Fixtures (AC-1.2).** One fixture per value per path, with the bundled path forced through the\nexisting `_FORCE_FALLBACK` seam. Refused: `not-a-date`, `2026-10-16`, `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z`, and\n`2026-10-16T00:00:00Zjunk` and `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z`. `…Zjunk` kills a `fullmatch` → `match`\nmutation. Accepted: the writer's `_utc_now` form and `2016-12-31T23:59:59Z`. Each alternative of the zone group has its own fixture, run with that\nbranch alone: `…Z`, `…z`, `…+09:00` accepted, and `…+24:00` refused.\n\n**The `fallback_agent` description, and the same class on `codex_status`.** Spec FR-1 names the\n`fallback_agent` description and its `state-schema.md` row. The class is \"a surface that says what\nan unavailable Codex lets Claude do\". It has two more members in the tree:\n- the `codex_status` schema `description`, which ends \"permitting Claude to write the fallback\n  (still test-first)\" and \"One switch, both effects\";\n- its `state-schema.md` row, \"An unavailable or exhausted status permits a Phase-5 authoring\n  fallback\".\n\nBoth are false once agy is available. All four surfaces are rewritten to the FR-2 condition.\n`test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts` keeps\nits four substrings, for example \"read only when codex is out and agy is out too\". No test pins the\n`codex_status` wording: `git ls-files 'h-mad/tests/*.py' | xargs grep -ln 'permitting Claude\\|One switch, both effects'`\nprints nothing at `29ae3e7b`. The spec does not list the `codex_status` pair; see §\"Owed by other\ndocuments\".\n\n### FR-2 — `resolve_implementer` and the `implementer` verb\n\n**Record.** `Record` gains two trailing fields with defaults: `implementer: str = \"absent\"` and\n`agy_status: str = \"available\"`. The defaults keep every positional four-argument construction\nvalid. One exists in the tree: the replace text of a `codex_gate_judge_wiring.json` row\n(`git ls-files -z h-mad | xargs -0 grep -l 'Record('` → `h_mad_tdd_judge.py` and that spec,\n2 files). `_active_records` fills both through two helpers:\n- `_implementer_tag(state)` returns `absent`, `null`, `codex`, `agy` or `invalid:<json>`, built like\n  `_fallback_tag`;\n- `agy_status` is read the way `codex_status` is: `\"available\"` when the value is `None` or the key\n  is absent, else `str(value)`.\n\n**Resolution.** `resolve_implementer(record, env=None, which=None) -> Implementer` is the only\nresolver. `env` defaults to `os.environ` and `which` to `shutil.which`. `Implementer` is a\n`NamedTuple` with these fields: `resolved` (`codex|agy|grok|claude|invalid`), `chosen`\n(`codex|agy`, or `None` when invalid), `reason` (`env|status|cli-absent`, or `None`),\n`invalid_field` (`implementer|fallback_agent`, or `None`) and `invalid_value` (the JSON text, or\n`\"\"`).\n1. An `invalid:` implementer tag gives `resolved=\"invalid\"` and `invalid_field=\"implementer\"`.\n2. `chosen` is `agy` for the tag `agy`, else `codex`.\n3. `_cause(peer)` returns the first cause in FR-2's order:\n   - `env`: `env.get(\"HMAD_<PEER>_UNAVAILABLE\", \"\") != \"\"`;\n   - `status`: the record's `<peer>_status` is in `ESCAPE_STATUSES`;\n   - `cli-absent`: `which(peer)` is `None`.\n\n   It returns `None` when the peer is available.\n4. If `chosen` is available it is resolved. Otherwise the other peer is resolved if it is\n   available. Otherwise the record's fallback tag decides: `grok` resolves `grok`; `absent`, `null`\n   and `claude` resolve `claude`; an invalid tag gives `invalid_field=\"fallback_agent\"`.\n   `reason` is `_cause(chosen)` whenever the resolved author is not `chosen`.\n\n`shutil.which` replaces the hook's `command -v codex`. The equivalence is checked by the existing\ndifferential `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py` `test_absent_null_claude_match_the_base_hook`,\nwhich compares the current hook's bytes with the hook at `8ef6009f` over the PATH × env × status\nmatrix. Residual: that matrix holds only the PATH forms the test builds. Other forms, such as an\nempty `PATH` element or a non-executable `codex` file, are not compared.\n\n**The `implementer` verb.** It is a new `implementer` subparser in `main`, with `--root`,\n`--feature` and `--record`. It has no `--target`. It reads `<root>/docs/.bkit-memory.json` through\n`_state_at`, so it has the same reader and the same error names as the chain. It looks up\n`orchestrator_state[feature]` **whatever its phase**, because staging must not depend on the record\nalready being in `step5`. Exactly one line is printed:\n\n| condition | line |\n|---|---|\n| file absent, or the key absent | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: no-record feature=<pct>` |\n| `_state_at` gives `unreadable`, or the record is not an object | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: unreadable file=<pct> error=<name>` (`not-an-object` for the second) |\n| `invalid_field` is set | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: invalid field=<field> value=<pct>` |\n| `resolved == chosen` | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <resolved> chosen=<chosen> switch=none` |\n| a peer switch | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <resolved> chosen=<chosen> switch=<chosen>-><resolved> reason=<reason>` |\n| peers out | `TDD-IMPLEMENTER: <grok\\|claude> chosen=<chosen> switch=peers-out reason=<reason>` |\n\n`<pct>` is the judge's existing `_enc`. With `--record`, any switch other than `none` calls\n`h_mad_state_write.merge_fields(state_file, feature, {\"implementer_switch\": {...}})` with\n`from=chosen`, `to=resolved`, `reason` and `ts=_utc_now()`. That is the same write path `--set`\nuses. The write is skipped when the stored value has the same `from`, `to` and `reason`. A\n`StateWriteError` or an `OSError` appends ` record=failed` to the line. The verb always exits 0\nafter printing a line, and the caller reads the token.\n\n**The caller's halts (v1.4).** The orchestrator (SKILL.md, §\"FR-10\") reads the token and never\ndispatches on these three outcomes, adopting the impl-plan's names:\n- a line ending ` record=failed`, whatever precedes it, read before any other field →\n  `step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded` (the name spec FR-2 \"CLI verb\" already gives);\n- `invalid field=` → `step5:implementer_invalid`;\n- `no-record` or `unreadable` → `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n\nEach prints its `[H-MAD] <feature> halt <name>` marker. The verb itself raises no halt; the names\nlive in the caller's prose, and the impl-plan's SKILL.md Task pins them.\n\nResidual: the verb reads the root's own state file. The gate folds the whole chain from the target\nup to the root. A nested sub-project state file that holds a record with the same key is read by\nthe gate and not by the verb.\n\n### FR-3 — the `TDD-STATE:` line (DO-1, DO-1a) and the hook's fold\n\n**The shape.** Each record gains a fifth comma field, the **author field**, between the state file\nand the fallback tag. The header does not change.\n\n```\nTDD-STATE: active codex-escape=<yes|no> blocker=<N> records=<N> fallback=<fold> record=<key>,<codex_status>,<state_file>,<author>,<fallback_tag> …\n```\n\n- `<author>` is one of `codex`, `agy`, `peers-out` or `invalid:<pct-json>`. `format_state_line`\n  computes it per record through `resolve_implementer`, evaluating `env` and `which` once per call.\n  `codex` and `agy` mean that peer is resolved. `invalid:` means the record's `implementer` is\n  invalid. `peers-out` means both peers are out, and the fallback tag then decides.\n- The new `STATE_ACTIVE_RE`, which is the one literal in `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh` matching\n  `^STATE_ACTIVE_RE='(.*)'$`:\n\n  ```\n  ^TDD-STATE: active codex-escape=(yes|no) blocker=(0|[1-9][0-9]*) records=([1-9][0-9]*) fallback=(none|(grok|invalid):([1-9][0-9]*))( record=[^ ,]+,[^ ,]+,[^ ,]+,(codex|agy|peers-out|invalid:[^ ,]+),(absent|null|grok|claude|invalid:[^ ,]+))+$\n  ```\n\n  The new capture group follows `group(4)`, and groups 1 to 6, which the hook reads, keep their\n  numbers. No group before it changes, and the grammar uses no construct that Python `re` and POSIX\n  ERE read differently.\n\n**P-1..P-5 hold, checked against candidate lines.** The committed probe\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr3_state_line_grammar.py` derives the new grammar from\nthe hook's current `STATE_ACTIVE_RE` literal (it refuses if that literal's tail has drifted), builds\nlines in this shape, and matches each one with Python `re.fullmatch` and with `[[ =~ ]]` under\nevery bash named on its command line. Invocation, from the repository root:\n`python3 docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr3_state_line_grammar.py /bin/bash /opt/homebrew/bin/bash`.\nOne reading at `200ffd52`: `PROBE: PASS`, under `/bin/bash` 3.2.57, the hook's shebang, and\nHomebrew bash 5.3.20. A control copy that expects the today-shape line to match prints\n`PROBE: FAIL`, so the probe can fail. What it shows:\n- P-1: the header is unchanged, so ` records=1 fallback=grok:1 record=` stays contiguous.\n- P-2: Python and both bash versions gave the same groups 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6 on a grok fold, an agy\n  record and a two-record line whose first record has an invalid implementer, and `group(4)`\n  equalled the fold.\n- P-3: the fallback tag is still the last comma token.\n- P-4: the header is unchanged.\n- P-5: fields 0, 1 and 2 after the first ` record=` are still the key, the status and the file.\n\nA today-shape line, and a line whose author field is `grok`, matched neither engine. So a missing\nor unknown author field refuses `judge-error`, which is the rule FR-3 requires.\n\n**Why the author field's value is right whatever `PATH` holds.** P-1 and P-2 run in-process with\nthe ambient `PATH`, where `agy` resolves (`which agy` → `/Users/kimhawk/.local/bin/agy` at\n`29ae3e7b`). Neither assertion reads the author field, and the grammar admits all four values, so\nboth stay byte-identical and green. The `fallback=` fold is still computed over the records alone\n(`_fallback_fold` is untouched).\n\n**The hook.** `_read_state` keeps its header checks unchanged: the record count, escape against\nblocker, and both range checks. `codex-escape=` and `blocker=` are no longer decision inputs. They\nstay because P-4 pins them and because they still check the line's integrity. In the record loop,\nthe author field is taken by stripping three leading fields (`${rest#*,}` three times, then `%%,*`).\nThe key (`%%,*`), the file (the third field) and the tag (`##*,`) are read exactly as P-5 needs.\nThe loop sets two values:\n- `INVALID_RECORD`: the first record whose author starts with `invalid:`;\n- `PEER_RECORD` and `PEER`: the first record whose author is `codex` or `agy`.\n\nThe `BLOCKER_RECORD` capture, which only the old authorship block read, is removed. After\n`_read_state --target`, the block that tested `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE`, `ESCAPE` and\n`command -v codex` is replaced by FR-3's precedence:\n1. **`INVALID_RECORD` set → `implementer-invalid`.** The text is new:\n   `implementer=$VALUE is not valid (valid: codex|agy|null) — refusing the write, fail-closed.` and\n   then ``  Fix it: python3 ~/.claude/skills/h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_write.py --feature $KEY --set implementer=codex|agy|null \"$FILE\"``.\n   `$VALUE` is the decoded JSON.\n2. **`PEER_RECORD` set → `codex-authorship` or `agy-authorship`.** The `codex-authorship` text is\n   the current literal, byte for byte. Only the variables it interpolates are renamed, from\n   `BLOCKER_KEY`/`BLOCKER_FILE` to `PEER_KEY`/`PEER_FILE`, so the rendered bytes for a given record\n   are unchanged. The `agy-authorship` text is new and mirrors it. It names\n   `hmad-dispatch exec agy <promptfile>` with `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py --agent agy`, the\n   `--set agy_status=exhausted` command for `$PEER_KEY`/`$PEER_FILE`,\n   `export HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE=1 for a one-off`, and `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer` as the way to\n   see who resolves.\n3. **Otherwise, the existing `fallback_agent` block runs unchanged**, including the `M:F1`\n   tag-agreement check. It is reached only when every record's author is `peers-out`, which is\n   FR-3's \"both peers out\" condition. The comment above it,\n   `codex-authorship has already refused every case where codex is available`, is rewritten to\n   name both peers.\n\n**Fixture consequence (AC-3.2).** Two literals are re-shaped, and nothing else in either file\nchanges:\n- `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_judge.py` `_tree_b`: the default `state_line` gains `,peers-out` before\n  `,absent`;\n- `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py` `RECORD`: it becomes\n  `\" record=feat,exhausted,/x/docs/.bkit-memory.json,peers-out,\"`.\n\n`test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `ACTIVE_RE` becomes the grammar above. The new fixture is a today-shape\nline → `judge-error`.\n\n**No gate test is unreachable.** This was checked by enumerating the stub sites at `29ae3e7b`:\n- `_tree_b(` matches 9 lines in `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_judge.py`, one `def` and 8 call sites. One of\n  the 8 passes its own `state_line`, whose lines are none of them `TDD-STATE: active`.\n- `_stub_outcome(` matches 6 lines in `test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py`, one `def` and 5 call\n  sites.\n\nEvery one reaches its asserted outcome with author `peers-out`:\n- the judge-verb stubs reach the judge exactly as today;\n- `missing-field`, `unknown-kind` and `position-zero` still fail the grammar on their header;\n- the range check and the tag-agreement check still run, because `peers-out` reaches step 3.\n\nThe real-judge gate tests build `PATH` as `<bin_dir>:/usr/bin:/bin` (`_gate`'s `path_tail`), with\nno `agy`, so their outcomes are FR-2's codex-only outcomes. So no contradiction is reported here.\n\n**Mutation rows that follow the line (R-1).** At `29ae3e7b`:\n- 56 rows name the hook and 36 name the judge;\n- M-3's pattern matches 8 rows in 3 specs (`claude_gate_judge_wiring.json` 5,\n  `tdd_judge_chain.json` 2, `tdd_gate_fallback_agent.json` 1), which reproduces the plan's figure;\n- 13 rows anchor inside the regions this design rewrites. In the hook that is 8: 7 in `_read_state`\n  and 1 in the authorship block. In the judge it is 5: 1 in `_active_records` and 4 in\n  `format_state_line`.\n\nThe re-anchoring Task reads every row of both files, not only these, because the regions above are\na proxy. The census is owed as a committed probe (§\"Measurements\").\n\n### FR-6 — the shared gate policy (DO-5)\n\n**Placement: `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module. This is OQ-A, decided\nby the operator (spec D-12).** The tree holds hand-built trees that copy the Codex gate beside a stub judge\nand, at most, the identity module:\n- `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason` copies the identity\n  module in and expects `kind=judge-timeout`;\n- the same file's `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]` builds one with a raising judge and **no**\n  identity module, and expects `kind=judge-error`.\n\nThe committed probe `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr6_import_failure_pair.py`\nrebuilds both trees in a temporary directory it removes, and runs the gate as those tests do.\nInvocation, from the repository root:\n`python3 docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/fr6_import_failure_pair.py`. One reading at\n`200ffd52`, `PROBE: PASS`:\n- `timeout-tree/unmodified`: exit 0, a deny with `kind=judge-timeout`;\n- `timeout-tree/new-module` (one top-level `from h_mad_gate_policy import _safe_shell_command`\n  added): exit 1, no stdout, `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'h_mad_gate_policy'`;\n- `error-tree/unmodified`: exit 0, a deny with `kind=judge-error`;\n- `error-tree/G2-mutant` (row `G2`'s `replace` applied): exit 1, no stdout,\n  `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'h_mad_target_identity'`.\n\nThe last pair is what the binding below rests on: today the `judge-error` tree's deny is produced\nby `main`'s `except Exception as exc:  # M:G2` catching the identity module's import failure,\nraised from `_load_identity` inside `_main_guarded`. That is the only route by which row `G2`'s\ntest reaches `# M:G2`.\n\nSo a **new** module that the gate imports breaks a test that FR-6 says passes unmodified. The\nidentity module is in that tree already, as a copy of the live file, so the move keeps the test\ngreen. It also keeps the spec's letter: a shared module, imported by both gates. The cost is\ncohesion. The module's docstring, `Canonical filesystem identity shared by the H-MAD TDD gates.`,\nis widened to say that it also holds the policy both write gates apply.\n\n**The moved set.** It is 25 names, each moved byte for byte except where noted.\n- The 10 functions of `_safe_shell_command`'s call closure, re-derived with `ast` at `29ae3e7b` as\n  the plan's M-4 does: `_contained_venv_executable`, `_cwd_escapes_root`, `_load_judge`,\n  `_option_value`, `_path_within`, `_payload_cwd_base`, `_safe_argv`, `_safe_hmad_script`,\n  `_safe_shell_command` and `_trusted_executable`.\n- 7 more functions the agy gate needs, so that none of them is copied:\n  - the operator allowance: `_shell_allowance`, `_operator_allowed`, `_is_shell_allowance`, and\n    `_read_regular_text`, which `_shell_allowance` reads through;\n  - the phase-5 activity predicate: `_state_status` and `_any_phase5_status`;\n  - the production predicate: `_is_production_python`. Its `_load_identity().fold_py_suffix` call\n    becomes a direct call to the module's own `fold_py_suffix`, the one non-verbatim edit.\n- The 8 module constants that those functions read: `SIMPLE_SHELL_COMMAND`, `READ_ONLY_COMMANDS`,\n  `SAFE_DISPATCH_VERBS`, `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS`, `TEMP_OUTPUT_OPTIONS`, `SHELL_ALLOW_FILE`,\n  `TRUSTED_BIN_DIRS` and `MALFORMED_WORKDIR`.\n\nNot moved: `_load_identity`, which loads this very module; `_relative_target`, `_tmp_report_target`\nand `_command_cwd`, which are Codex-payload specific (see the binding rule); `_main_guarded`'s\ncomposition of safe-command → allowance → operator check, which carries the Codex-specific refusal\ntext and the `M:F2-WIRE` and `M:SA-WIRE` anchors; and the self-check's `unsafe` tuple in `main`.\n\nThe moved functions' `Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]` still names `h-mad/`, because\n`scripts/` and `hooks/` are siblings. So `hmad-dispatch` and the scripts root resolve as before.\n\n**Binding.** The Codex gate inserts its `scripts` directory into `sys.path`, as `_load_judge`\nalready does, and imports all 25 names by name at module level, inside\n`try: … except Exception as exc: _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR = exc` (`_POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR = None`\nbefore it).\n- **The failure is re-raised inside `_main_guarded`, never answered in `main`.** The first\n  statement of `_main_guarded` is `if _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR is not None: raise\n  _POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR`. The existing `except Exception as exc:  # M:G2` in `main` turns it into\n  the same `kind=judge-error` deny it gives today. A deny issued in `main` before `_main_guarded`\n  would also print `judge-error`, but `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]` would then pass with\n  `# M:G2` mutated, so row `G2` would survive (§\"FR-6\" probe, `error-tree/G2-mutant`). Rule over\n  the axis: no new early return is added on any path between `main`'s entry and the `try` that\n  holds `# M:G2`; every new failure is raised inside `_main_guarded`.\n- **`--self-check` under a failed import.** The self-check branch runs before that `try` and\n  calls `_safe_shell_command`. When `_POLICY_IMPORT_ERROR` is set it prints\n  `CODEX-TDD-GATE: FAIL policy-import` to stderr and returns 2, instead of raising `NameError`.\n- **Fixtures.** `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]`, unmodified, is the import-failure fixture:\n  its tree has no identity module, so the guarded import fails and the gate still prints one\n  deny. A new fixture copies the gate beside a real judge and **no** identity module and runs\n  `--self-check`, expecting `FAIL policy-import` and exit 2.\n\nOf the moved names, an existing test reaches these four through the Codex gate module, and\nbinding all 25 covers them:\n- `_safe_shell_command`: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` calls `module._safe_shell_command`;\n- `_payload_cwd_base`: the same file monkeypatches it on the module, then calls\n  `module._relative_target`. `_relative_target` stays in the Codex gate, so it reads the patched\n  binding;\n- `SIMPLE_SHELL_COMMAND`: read from `runpy.run_path(...)` globals, in the same file;\n- `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS`: read as `hook.SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS` in `test_h_mad_codex_runtime.py`.\n\nThe four were found with `grep -l \"\\b<name>\\b\"` over `git ls-files 'h-mad/tests/*.py'`, once per\nmoved name. Residual: a test that builds a name at run time is not found by that grep.\n\n**Rows re-pointed (R-3, AC-6.1).** At `29ae3e7b`, 62 rows name the Codex gate. A row is counted when\nits `find` starts inside a moved definition's span. 31 rows qualify, in 4 specs:\n`codex_gate_judge_wiring.json` 21, `codex_gate_shell_allowance.json` 6,\n`assemble_tdd_mutation_rows.json` 2 and `tdd_gate_differential.json` 2. 0 anchors are absent and 0\nare non-unique. The plan's 19, which is the 10-function closure alone, is a subset. The 12 added\nrows come from the allowance functions, `_read_regular_text`, `SAFE_HMAD_SCRIPT_OPTIONS` and\n`_is_production_python`. Each re-pointed row's `file` becomes `h_mad_target_identity.py` under\n`h-mad/scripts/` (written relative to `h-mad/`, as every row's `file` is),\nand each spec holding one is gated on `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n- **The identity module's own rows are rechecked too.** Adding the moved code can make one of the\n  module's existing `find` strings occur twice, and the harness then refuses it. The move Task runs\n  the anchor precheck over every spec naming that file.\n- **AC-6.1's \"one definition\".** A test asserts that each of the 17 moved functions has exactly one\n  `def` across `git ls-files h-mad`.\n\n### FR-4 — the agy write gate (DO-2)\n\n**File and contract.** `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` reads stdin and prints exactly one JSON\nobject, `{\"decision\": \"allow\"}` or `{\"decision\": \"deny\", \"reason\": \"<text> kind=<kind>\"}`, then\nexits 0. `main` builds the decision inside one `try` and prints from a `finally` that emits a\n`judge-error` deny if nothing was decided. The judge, the identity module and the policy are\nimported inside that `try`. So a failed import still yields JSON (AC-4.2).\n\n**The order of evaluation.**\n1. Parse stdin, read with a 2 s bound like the Claude hook's `READER` (the Codex gate's `_payload`\n   calls `json.load(sys.stdin)` unbounded). `toolCall.name` must be a `str`, matched exactly and\n   case-sensitively. **A malformed payload** is exactly: stdin that is not a JSON object, a\n   `toolCall` that is not an object, or a `toolCall.name` that is not a `str`. A `toolCall.args`\n   that is absent or not an object is read as `{}`, as the Codex gate's `_targets` reads a\n   non-`dict` `tool_input`; it is not malformed.\n2. **Class (a) is allowed before any state is read.** Class (a) is allowed whether or not step5 is\n   active, so no state read can change its answer.\n3. **The root.** It is `git -C <handler cwd>/.. rev-parse --show-toplevel`, canonicalised with the\n   identity module's `canonical_directory`.\n   A root that cannot be resolved, or a malformed payload, denies `kind=judge-error`. After the\n   root is resolved the gate calls `os.chdir(root)` once, so that every relative path the shared\n   policy resolves against `Path.cwd()` starts from the root rather than from `<root>/.agents`\n   (class (c) refines this per command).\n4. **Activity.** `phase5_status = _any_phase5_status(root)`, the predicate the Codex gate computes\n   before it looks at the tool. It is an **input** to classes (b), (c) and (d), never a global\n   shortcut: the Codex gate judges a production `.py` target through `read_chain` even when the\n   `rglob` scan reads `inactive` (its `for raw in targets:  # M:G5` loop has no status filter, and\n   row `G5`'s test `test_unsearchable_docs_on_the_chain_denies_although_the_scan_is_inactive`\n   kills a mutant that adds one). `rglob` skips a directory it cannot search, so an `inactive` scan\n   does not prove the chain inactive. v1.0's \"`inactive` allows\" before class (b) was that mutant.\n5. **The `workspacePaths` cross-check, only under `S`** (`S` stands for `phase5_status in\n   {\"active\", \"unknown\"}` throughout this section). When `S` holds, `workspacePaths` is a list,\n   and no entry resolves inside the root, the gate denies `kind=judge-error`. When `phase5_status`\n   is `inactive` the check stands down (spec FR-4 §\"Project root\", AC-4.1), and evaluation goes\n   on to step 6. v1.1 made this deny unconditional, so `run_command` `ls` with\n   `workspacePaths=[]` and an inactive state was refused (design audit cycle 2).\n6. **Classes (b), (c) and (d)**, as below. For (c) and (d), `inactive` allows and `unknown`\n   denies `kind=judge-error`. The Codex gate also refuses a shell command under `unknown`, but its\n   reason carries no `kind=` token (`H-MAD state is unreadable; refusing shell execution\n   fail-closed.`); the agy gate adds the kind, and the Codex gate has no counterpart for class (d).\n   Class (b) passes the status into its per-target sequence. An exception anywhere after step 2\n   denies `kind=judge-error`.\n\n**The deny kinds: a closed set of five gate kinds plus the judge's.** Every deny the gate prints\ncarries exactly one `kind=` token, from this list:\n- `judge-error`: the gate could not reach a judgement (the rows of the table below that say so);\n- `tool-not-admitted`: class (d) under `active` (spec FR-4's table);\n- `target-outside-root`: T4's refusal;\n- `allowance-file-write`: T5's refusal;\n- `shell-not-admitted`: class (c)'s policy refusal (`_safe_shell_command` false and\n  `_operator_allowed` false);\n- at T7 only, the judge's own `verdict.kind`, passed through unchanged. It is a member of\n  `h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `KINDS`, which holds 12 names at `cff71b49` (`red-measured`,\n  `no-test-resolved`, `test-missing`, `venv-escapes-root`, `pytest-missing`, `pytest-error`,\n  `no-tests-ran`, `no-summary`, `test-passing`, `timeout`, `judge-timeout`, `judge-error`).\n\nResidual: a kind the judge adds to `KINDS` later is passed through without a change here, so the\nclosed set over the gate's own denies is five names, and over T7 it is whatever `KINDS` holds.\nWhere the Codex gate's deny carries a kind (`judge-error` on an unresolvable target, an unreadable\nchain, a bad root; the judge's kind), the agy gate's carries the same one. Where the Codex deny\ncarries none (out-of-root target, allowance-file write, no write target, shell under `unknown`,\nan unreadable allowance file, the shell policy: 6 of the 13 `_deny(` call sites in\n`h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py` at `cff71b49`, counted from `grep -n '_deny(' ` minus the\n`def _deny` line, with the one multi-line call read whole, as spec v1.8 FR-4 counts them), the\nagy kind is the one in the table below. Spec v1.8 FR-4 §\"Every deny carries a kind\" owns\n`judge-error`, `tool-not-admitted` and the judge's kind; the other three names are this design's.\n\n**Every deny, and when it is gated on step5.** The rule over the class: a deny that depends on\nthe payload's tool or targets fires only under `S`, except the two that follow the Codex gate in\nrunning whatever the scan reads (T7, because the chain is the authority, `M:G5`); a deny that\nfires before the status can be known, or because judging failed, is unconditional.\n\n| Deny | When | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| malformed payload (step 1) | always: no tool is named, so no class applies | `judge-error` |\n| root cannot be resolved (step 3) | always: the status cannot be read without a root (a deliberate difference, below) | `judge-error` |\n| an exception after step 2, and the `finally` with nothing decided | always, as the Codex gate's `M:G2` | `judge-error` |\n| `workspacePaths` names nothing inside the root (step 5) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| class (b) with no target (no recognised key, or no recognised value that is a non-empty `str`) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T2 unresolvable | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T2a an existing directory (OD-2) | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| T4 outside the root, not admitted | `S` | `target-outside-root` |\n| T5 the allowance file | `S` | `allowance-file-write` |\n| T7 chain unreadable | whatever the status (`M:G5`) | `judge-error` |\n| T7 judge non-ALLOW | whatever the status; reached only on an `active` chain | the judge's kind |\n| class (c) `CommandLine` missing or not a `str` | `S` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) under `unknown` | `unknown` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) `_shell_allowance` returns a `str` (the allowance file unreadable or unparsable) | `active` | `judge-error` |\n| class (c) policy refusal | `active` | `shell-not-admitted` |\n| class (d) | `active` | `tool-not-admitted` |\n| class (d) | `unknown` | `judge-error` |\n\nThe table is the whole set: 17 rows (v1.4 adds T2a), counted from this table, and every `deny` this section\nnames elsewhere is one of them. Under `inactive` the only denies left are the three unconditional\nrows and the two T7 rows; AC-4.1's \"allows every tool\" holds for every payload whose root resolves\nand whose chain is not `active` or `unreadable`. The T7 exception is v1.1's (step 4): an\n`inactive` scan does not prove the chain inactive.\n\n**Class (a), the read-only allowlist: 11 names.** `view_file`, `list_dir`, `grep_search`,\n`find_by_name`, `command_status`, `read_url_content`, `search_web`, `ask_question`, `finish`,\n`wait` and `wait_5_seconds`.\n- **The rule over the class.** A tool is in (a) only if it appears in a recorded `init.tools`\n  inventory, and only if it writes nothing to the filesystem, starts no process, and drives no\n  other agent, MCP server or browser.\n- `finish` and the two waits are included because denying them leaves a headless agy unable to\n  end or pace its turn, and they touch nothing.\n- **Inventory.** The `init.tools` array of\n  `docs/03-analysis/probes/multi-host-runtime/rehearsal/live-agy-2026-10-01.log` holds 58 distinct\n  names (parsed as the first `init` event's `tools`, at `29ae3e7b`). All 11 are in it.\n- **That log carries no agy version.** Its `init` object has only `cwd`, `tools` and\n  `permission_mode`. The installed agy reports `1.4.0` (spec v1.7 FR-8 §\"Version\" records the\n  same at `200ffd52`; spec v1.6 and plan v1.4 read `1.3.6`).\n  So the inventory is re-taken, with `agy --version`, by FR-8 step 1 (§\"FR-8\").\n- **Residual.** Membership is judged from the tool's name and agy's documentation, not observed. A\n  tool whose name reads as read-only but which writes would be admitted. The 11 names are the whole\n  exposure, and egress over the network is outside this gate.\n\n**Class (b), path-write: 5 names.** `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content`,\n`multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file` and `notebook_edit`.\n- **Path keys.** A per-tool table, `PATH_KEYS`, holds only observed keys. At `29ae3e7b` it holds\n  `write_to_file` → `TargetFile`. That key was seen in stream-json `tool_info.parameters` in 4\n  committed logs under `docs/03-analysis/probes/multi-host-runtime/rehearsal/`\n  (`agy-G7-tool-write-then-link-read.log`, `agy-L3-unmapped-tool.log`, `agy-L9-no-step-index.log`,\n  `agy-L11-duplicate-index.log`), and never in a hook payload.\n- **Fail-closed.** The call's targets are the values of the keys from its table entry that are\n  present **and are a non-empty `str`** (spec v1.8 FR-4 §\"Target keys\"). A recognised key whose\n  value is anything else (`\"\"`, `null`, a number, a list) counts as no path key, and `\"\"` is never\n  canonicalised: v1.1 passed `\"\"` to T1, which design audit cycle 2 (agy) traced to the root\n  directory, no production name at T6, and an allow during active RED. A class-(b) call\n  with no target is denied `kind=judge-error` under `S` and allowed under `inactive`, as the Codex\n  gate denies a `Write`/`Edit`/`apply_patch` with no target (`[path] if path else []`, then\n  `not targets and phase5_status in {\"active\", \"unknown\"}`). That covers the other four tools until\n  FR-8 step 1 records their keys. The non-`str` arm is stricter than the Codex gate, which\n  stringifies a truthy non-`str` value (`str(args.get(\"file_path\") or …)`) and judges it as a path;\n  that is a deliberate difference toward refusal.\n- **Judging a target: the Codex gate's per-target sequence, step for step.** The class is \"every\n  rule the Codex gate's `_main_guarded` applies to one target\" (its `_relative_target` and the\n  body of `for raw in targets:  # M:G5`), not the two rules the audit named. Each recognised\n  key's value `raw` goes through these steps, in this order, and the first that decides wins.\n  `S` stands for `phase5_status in {\"active\", \"unknown\"}`.\n  - **T1 canonicalise.** `canonicalise(str(root), str(Path(raw).expanduser()), str(root))`, the\n    Codex gate's call with the root as the base: agy sends no payload cwd.\n  - **T2 unresolvable.** `resolved.unresolvable` → if `S`, deny `kind=judge-error`; else allow.\n    This runs before containment, as in `_relative_target`, which returns an unresolvable identity\n    before it tests `relative_to(root)`.\n  - **T2a an existing directory (OD-2, operator, 2026-10-16; v1.4).** `Path(resolved.target).is_dir()`\n    → if `S`, deny `kind=judge-error` with a reason naming the directory target; else allow. It runs\n    right after T2 and before T3, so an out-of-root directory under `S` denies `judge-error` too.\n    Without it the sequence allows a directory under `active`: `canonicalise` gives a directory\n    target (`.`, `src/`, `src`, the root itself) `unresolvable=False` and `names=()`, so T2 does not\n    fire, T3 and T5 pass, and T6 has no name to judge (scratch call at `84ec2133`, deleted). A\n    symlink to a directory is a directory here, since `is_dir` follows it. Residual: a target\n    ending in `/` that names no existing directory is not a directory; `canonicalise` gives it\n    `names=('nodir',)` (same scratch call), and it goes through T3–T7 as a file name.\n  - **T3 containment, before anything that assumes it.** `Path(resolved.target).relative_to(root)`\n    raising `ValueError` means outside the root, and the target goes to T4 and never to T5–T7.\n    The order is load-bearing: `_is_shell_allowance` calls `candidate.relative_to(root)` without\n    catching `ValueError`, so calling it on an outside target raises (a scratch call at\n    `200ffd52` on a canonicalised `/tmp/audit-report.txt` raised `ValueError`), and the step-6\n    catch would turn every `/tmp` write into `judge-error`. The moved helper stays byte for byte;\n    the order makes it safe.\n  - **T4 outside the root.** Allowed when **no** name in `resolved.names` folds to `.py` under\n    `fold_py_suffix`, and `resolved.target` is within `/tmp` by `_path_within`, or within the\n    payload's `artifactDirectoryPath` when that is a non-empty `str` (anything else is treated as\n    absent: `Path(\"\").resolve() == Path.cwd()` held in a scratch call at `200ffd52`, so an empty\n    value would make the allowance the process's working directory instead of anything the payload\n    named). Otherwise, if `S`, deny `kind=target-outside-root`; else allow. The artifact\n    allowance is computed before `S` is read, as `artifact = Path(adp) if isinstance(adp, str)\n    and adp else None` with `adp = payload.get(\"artifactDirectoryPath\")`, so a non-`str` value\n    never reaches `Path` (`Path(5)`, `Path([1])`, `Path({})` and `Path(True)` each raise\n    `TypeError`, scratch call at `cff71b49`, deleted).\n  - **T5 the allowance file.** If `S` and `_is_shell_allowance(root, resolved)`, deny\n    `kind=allowance-file-write`. The helper\n    already tests the target and every same-parent hard-link name, casefolded.\n  - **T6 production, over every name.** For each `name` in `resolved.names`, `relative =\n    (Path(resolved.target).parent / name).relative_to(root).as_posix()`; collect those for which\n    `_is_production_python(relative)` holds. None → allow. Otherwise take `min` of the collected\n    `(name, absolute, relative)` tuples, as the Codex gate does. Judging only `resolved.target`\n    would miss production: `resolved.target` is `names[0]`, so with `src/test_a.py` hard-linked to\n    `src/z.py` the identity is `names=('test_a.py', 'z.py')` and the target names the test file\n    (scratch call at `200ffd52`: target-only `False`, any-name `True`).\n  - **T7 the chain.** `chain = read_chain(root, absolute)`. `chain.value == \"unreadable\"` → deny\n    `kind=judge-error`; `chain.value != \"active\"` → allow; else `judge(root, absolute,\n    chain.records)`, and a non-ALLOW denies with the judge's kind. T6–T7 run whatever\n    `phase5_status` is (step 4).\n- **Deliberate differences from the Codex gate's target sequence**, each failing toward refusal or\n  forced by the payload:\n  - the base for a relative `raw` is the root, never a payload cwd (agy sends none);\n  - T4 admits `artifactDirectoryPath` as well as `/tmp` (spec FR-4);\n  - T4 tests every name in `resolved.names` for `.py`, where the Codex gate's `_tmp_report_target`\n    tests only the spelled path's resolved name. The spelled name and the referent's name are both\n    same-directory links, so they are in `names`, and the agy refusal set contains the Codex one;\n  - a root that cannot be canonicalised denies outright (step 3); the Codex gate inspects the\n    component and allows when its scan reads `inactive` (`M:CX-ROOT-ENTER`);\n  - (v1.2) every deny carries a kind from the closed set above, where 6 of the Codex gate's 13 do\n    not; spec v1.8 FR-4 adopts the four above and this one as its fifth;\n  - (v1.2) a recognised path key whose value is a truthy non-`str` is no target here (spec v1.8\n    FR-4 §\"Target keys\"), where the Codex gate stringifies it;\n  - (v1.4) T2a refuses an existing-directory target under `S` (OD-2); the Codex gate's per-target\n    sequence has no such step (its three `is_dir` calls test a root candidate or a payload cwd,\n    never a write target).\n  Residual: any rule the Codex gate gains later is not inherited, because the per-target\n  composition is written in both gates, like the shell composition below. The target\n  differential test (§\"Test Plan\", AC-4.1) is what catches that drift.\n\n**Class (c): `run_command`.** The command is `args.CommandLine`. A missing or non-`str` value\ndenies `kind=judge-error` under `S` and is allowed under `inactive` (v1.1 denied it whatever the\nstatus, against AC-4.1; design audit cycle 2). The Codex gate has no counterpart: a shell tool\nwith no command reaches none of its shell denies.\n- **The command's directory, `cwd`, computed as the Codex gate's `_command_cwd` computes it**,\n  with the root standing in for the session cwd agy does not send. `args.Cwd` was observed beside\n  `CommandLine` in 4 of 630 stream-json `run_command` parameter objects across the committed\n  probes (counted as objects, not distinct calls).\n  - `Cwd` present and not a `str` (`0`, a list, a dict, `true`) → `MALFORMED_WORKDIR`;\n  - `Cwd` absent, `None` or `\"\"` → the root, as `_command_cwd` returns the session for an empty\n    workdir;\n  - a relative `Cwd` → `str(root / Path(Cwd).expanduser())`, joined against the root, never\n    against the handler's directory;\n  - an absolute `Cwd` → `str(Path(Cwd).expanduser())`.\n\n  `cwd` is therefore always `MALFORMED_WORKDIR` or an absolute `str`, so the process directory\n  cannot change what `_cwd_escapes_root` and `_payload_cwd_base` decide.\n- **The process directory, for relative script and argument tokens.** `_safe_argv` resolves a\n  relative script, and `_path_within` a relative argument, against `Path.cwd()`. The shell runs the\n  command in `cwd`, so the gate resolves them there: when `cwd` is a `str` naming an existing\n  directory, the gate calls `os.chdir(cwd)`; in every other case (`MALFORMED_WORKDIR`, a path that\n  does not exist or is not a directory, or a `chdir` that raises `OSError`) it stays in the root\n  (step 3) and still judges with that `cwd`. **No case denies `judge-error`** — v1.0's \"a failed\n  change denies `judge-error`\" diverged from the Codex gate, which never changes directory, still\n  admits a read-only command such as `ls` under a bad workdir, and refuses a relative `.venv`\n  token there as a policy refusal through `_cwd_escapes_root` (`M:F2-ESCAPE`, `M:F2-MALFORMED`).\n  Why not stay in the root always: a relative script token would then be judged at\n  `root/<token>` while the shell runs `<cwd>/<token>`, and a test-directory file named like an\n  h-mad script would be judged as the real one.\n- **The composition is the Codex gate's**, reading the shared names: `phase5_status == \"unknown\"`\n  denies `kind=judge-error`; `active` runs `_safe_shell_command(command, root, cwd)`, then\n  `_shell_allowance` (a `str` result denies `kind=judge-error`), then `_operator_allowed` (false\n  denies `kind=shell-not-admitted`); `inactive` allows. The refusal tells agy to use the\n  class-(b) tools, and names `SHELL_ALLOW_FILE`.\n- **Fixtures matching the Codex gate's workdir tests**, each run through the agy gate with `Cwd`\n  in place of `workdir`: `test_a_workdir_outside_the_root_stays_refused`'s three shapes\n  (`outside-absolute`, `dotdot-escape`, `absent` = `str(root / \"no-such-dir\")`) deny a relative\n  `.venv` interpreter with the root's own venv as the positive control;\n  `test_a_present_but_malformed_workdir_fails_closed`'s four values deny; `Cwd=\"\"` behaves as\n  absent; and `ls` under each bad `Cwd` is allowed. Each of these denies carries\n  `kind=shell-not-admitted`, never `kind=judge-error`.\n- **Residual of the process directory.** When `cwd` does not exist the gate judges relative tokens\n  against the root, but the shell cannot run the command there either.\n- **Residual.** This six-line composition is the one piece of policy written in both gates. It\n  carries different refusal text, and each copy has its own mutation rows. A differential test pins\n  them equal: a corpus of commands × allowance-file states, judged by both gates, with the same\n  admit/deny in every cell.\n\n**Class (d): everything else → deny, `kind=tool-not-admitted`** when `phase5_status` is\n`active` (`unknown` denies `kind=judge-error` and `inactive` allows, step 6). At the inventory above that is\n41 names: `ask_custom_permission`, `ask_permission`, `browser_click_element`,\n`browser_drag_pixel_to_pixel`, `browser_get_dom`, `browser_get_network_request`, `browser_input`,\n`browser_list_network_requests`, `browser_mouse_down`, `browser_mouse_up`, `browser_move_mouse`,\n`browser_press_key`, `browser_refresh_page`, `browser_resize_window`, `browser_scroll`,\n`browser_scroll_dom`, `browser_select_option`, `browser_subagent`, `call_mcp_tool`,\n`capture_browser_console_logs`, `capture_browser_screenshot`, `click_browser_pixel`,\n`define_subagent`, `delete_knowledge`, `execute_browser_javascript`, `generate_image`,\n`invoke_subagent`, `list_browser_pages`, `list_permissions`, `list_resources`, `manage_inbox`,\n`manage_subagents`, `manage_task`, `notebook_execution`, `open_browser_url`, `read_browser_page`,\n`read_resource`, `run_workflow`, `schedule`, `send_command_input` and `send_message`. The 41 are\n58 − 11 − 5 − 1, derived as a set difference over the parsed inventory. Any name absent from the\ninventory, which includes tools added later, also falls here.\n\n**`--self-check`.** It checks four things: the classifier puts `view_file` in (a) and\n`call_mcp_tool` in (d); `_safe_shell_command` refuses `python3 -c \"open('x','w').write('x')\"`; the\npolicy names import; and `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` is readable. It prints exactly one line on stdout.\n**Rule over the axis (v1.4, orchestrator decision):** a FAIL that is a verdict of a check that ran\nprints its token and exits 0, like PASS; the token carries the verdict. Only a FAIL meaning the\ncheck could not run exits 2. So `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS` and the two verdict FAILs,\n`FAIL classification` and `FAIL shell-policy`, exit 0. `FAIL policy-import` (the judge, the\nidentity module or the policy names do not import, or `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` cannot be read) exits 2.\nFR-6's Codex `FAIL policy-import` exits 2 for the same reason. FR-5's wiring check is unaffected:\nit requires the exact line `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS` with exit 0, so a FAIL line with exit 0 still reads\nas `self-check-failed`. The Codex gate's own `--self-check` breaks this rule today: its verdict\nFAILs `FAIL parser` and `FAIL shell-policy` print to stderr and return 2. The impl-plan's FR-6\nTask (Task 4) corrects `FAIL shell-policy` by orchestrator decision. `FAIL parser` is the same\nclass and is owed (§\"Owed by other documents\"). Residual: a self-check FAIL added later is\nclassed by this rule, not listed here. AC-6.1's parity with the Codex list is a test, not this self-check.\nThe test locates the `unsafe` tuple assigned inside the Codex gate's `main` with `ast`, requires\nexactly one, and asserts that the agy gate refuses each member.\n\n**Overwrite residual.** agy merges handlers, and a sibling's `overwrite` is a shallow merge into the\ntool's arguments (hooks.md §\"PreToolUse Contract\"). The gate judges the arguments it receives. A\nsibling that rewrites a path key after this gate judged it is not seen. At `29ae3e7b` the operator's\nglobal `~/.gemini/config/hooks.json` holds one `PreToolUse` group, `orca-status` with matcher `*`,\nwhose script prints `{\"decision\":\"ask\"}` for `PreToolUse` and never `overwrite`.\n\n**Roles.** The gate cannot tell an implementing agy from a reviewing agy. During active step5, a\n5e-review agy in a wired project is therefore held to the same tool and shell rules, as a Codex\nverifier is today. 6a-prime runs after step5 ends, so it is unaffected.\n\n**Inherited residual.** The gate decides \"active\" from the root's own state, as both existing gates\ndo. `docs/.bkit-memory.json` is untracked in this repository and in HemaSuite\n(`git ls-files docs/.bkit-memory.json` prints nothing in either at `29ae3e7b`). How Phase 5c\nprovisions state inside a worktree was not read for this design, so whether the three gates stand\ndown in a worktree is not claimed either way.\n\n**Hook timeout.** The floor is `math.ceil(JUDGE_BUDGET_S + 20)`, read from the module. At\n`29ae3e7b` `JUDGE_BUDGET_S` is `40.0`, so the floor is 60.\n\n### FR-5 — the wiring check, the assembler, and the worktree (DO-6)\n\n**The check.** `h_mad_hook_wiring.py` gains `--agy`. It calls a new `check_agy(project_root)`,\nprints the one line that function returns, and exits with the code beside it. Without `--agy`,\nbehaviour is unchanged. The reasons are evaluated in FR-5's order, and the first one found is\nprinted:\n1. `<root>/.agents/hooks.json` is absent → `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file`, exit 0. When it is\n   present but not JSON, or not a JSON object, the line is `UNREADABLE file=<pct> error=<name>`\n   with exit 2, and the name is `not-an-object` for the second case.\n2. There is no top-level key `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate`, or its value is not an object → `no-entry`.\n3. `enabled` is present and is not `true` → `disabled`.\n4. No `PreToolUse` group has a `matcher` of exactly `*` or `\"\"` → `matcher-narrow`. An absent or\n   empty `PreToolUse` counts here too.\n5. No handler in such a group has a `command` whose `shlex.split` equals\n   `[\"python3\", \"~/.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py\"]` → `bad-command`.\n6. That handler's `timeout` is not an `int` ≥ the floor; an absent timeout means agy's default of\n   30 → `timeout-below-floor`.\n7. `<python3 on PATH> <expanduser(path)> --self-check` must exit 0 and print exactly\n   `AGY-TDD-GATE: PASS`, within 30 s. Otherwise → `self-check-failed`. When it passes, the line is\n   `AGY-GATE: WIRED`.\n\nResidual: an equivalent spelling of the command (an absolute path, `$HOME`) reads `bad-command`.\nThat fails toward the halt.\n\n**The assembler.** `--agent` gains `agy`, and the timeout default becomes a table,\n`{\"codex\": 900, \"grok\": 1500, \"agy\": 1500}`, with `--timeout` still winning. The choices line\nbecomes `ap.add_argument(\"--agent\", choices=(\"codex\", \"grok\", \"agy\"), default=\"codex\")`, and the\ntimeout line keeps the substring `args.timeout is not None` exactly once\n(`timeout = args.timeout if args.timeout is not None else TIMEOUT_DEFAULTS[args.agent]`), so the\nexisting mutation anchors on it survive (§\"Mutation rows\", \"Existing rows the edits move\"). For `--agent agy`,\n`main` runs the check before `assemble`, as a subprocess:\n`[sys.executable, AGY_WIRING_CHECK, \"--agy\", \"--project-root\", <root>]`, with a 60 s bound.\n`AGY_WIRING_CHECK` is a module constant and the tests' seam. Then FR-5's table applies:\n- `WIRED` → continue to PASS;\n- `UNWIRED` → print `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified`, then the check line indented by\n  two spaces, as existing HALT details are. Write no prompt, exit 0;\n- `UNREADABLE` → the same two lines, exit 2;\n- no `AGY-GATE:` line, or a timeout → `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token`,\n  exit 2.\n\nThe verdict branch does not raise `Halt`, because every `Halt` exits 2. The module docstring's\n`exit 0 on PASS, 2 on any HALT` becomes: `exit 0 on PASS and on HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified after\nAGY-GATE: UNWIRED; 2 on every other HALT`. A test pins that sentence, and a test pins that the old\none is absent. `command_block`'s existing prefix rewrite already emits `hmad-dispatch exec agy`, and\n`exec agy` accepts `--model`, `--effort` and `--timeout` (`hmad-dispatch.sh` `_cmd_exec`).\n\n**DO-6: no override path in code.**\n- **Tests.** Tests of `check_agy` set `HOME` to a fixture directory holding\n  `.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` as a symlink to the gate under test.\n  `os.path.expanduser` honours `HOME`, so the shipped code has no worktree switch.\n- **Any test that runs the assembler with `--agent agy` and expects a prompt** needs that `HOME`\n  fixture **and** a `<root>/.agents/hooks.json` holding FR-5's exact entry in its project root.\n  A bare `tmp_path` reads `no-hooks-file` and halts with no prompt written, which is the designed\n  outcome, not a defect. The fixture is called the **WIRED fixture** below. The check runs in a\n  subprocess, so `AGY_WIRING_CHECK` cannot be monkeypatched from a test that also runs the\n  assembler as a subprocess (as `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py` `run_cli` does); the fixture is\n  the only seam such a test has.\n- **This feature's own Phase-5 worktree.** It ships no `.agents/hooks.json`, so `--agent agy` there\n  reads `no-hooks-file` and halts. That is correct: this feature's implementer is Codex.\n- **An adopting project's worktree.** Its tracked `.agents/hooks.json` reaches the worktree, and\n  the installed path is the skills repository's main checkout. So the check reads `WIRED` once this\n  feature has merged.\n- **This repository's own `.agents/hooks.json`** is committed only by FR-8's post-merge stage, and\n  only after its real refusal passes (§\"FR-8\"). Until then the halt stands here mechanically,\n  because the file is absent. Evidence that tracking it needs no `git add -f`: at `29ae3e7b`,\n  `.git/info/exclude` holds `/.agents/skills/`, and `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints\n  nothing.\n\n### FR-7 — the `review_independence` add (DO-3) and the Phase-7 warnings\n\n**The add.** `h_mad_state_write.py` gains `--append KEY=JSON`, which is repeatable.\n- **Strict decoding.** `json.loads` with no string fallback. A decode failure prints `ERROR: …`\n  and exits 2, and the store is unchanged.\n- **One write, accumulated in command-line order.** `--set` fields and `--append` entries apply\n  inside **one** `_mutate` call, through `merge_fields`, which gains an `appends` parameter. Its\n  `apply` keeps its existing statements byte for byte (`record = {**records[feature], **fields}`,\n  then the owner-heartbeat `if` and its assignment) and runs the appends after them, so the two\n  `heartbeat_beat.json` rows anchored on that `if` still match once (§\"Mutation rows\").\n  `appends` is the list of `(key, entry)` pairs in the order given. Below, `prior` is\n  `records[feature]` and `record` is the merged dict; for each pair in order, `current = record.get(key, [])`\n  is read from the **updated** `record`, and `record[key] = list(current) + [entry]`. Two\n  `--append` flags on one key in one call therefore keep every earlier entry and add both, in\n  order. Reading `prior[key]` instead would keep only the last addition.\n- **`--set` and `--append` on the same key in one call are refused** (`--set and --append name\n  the same key: <key>`), exit 2, store unchanged. `--set` replaces a whole value and `--append`\n  adds to it, so the pair has no single reading; refusing is the defined interaction.\n- **The append-only keys refuse `--set` (v1.3).** `review_independence` and\n  `implementation_authors` are append-only: an entry, once written, is never removed or altered.\n  The writer operations that could replace a declared value are `--set` on the command line and\n  `merge_fields` / `set_fields` for library callers (`set_fields` calls `merge_fields`);\n  `create_feature` leaves an existing record alone and `drop_undeclared` drops only undeclared\n  keys, so neither can touch a declared one. The rule is therefore one check at the top of\n  `merge_fields`, before its `def apply`: a key of `fields` in the module constant\n  `APPEND_ONLY = (\"review_independence\", \"implementation_authors\")` raises `StateWriteError`\n  (`<key> is append-only: use --append`), exit 2, store unchanged. It sits outside `apply`, so\n  `apply`'s statements and the heartbeat rows anchored on them are unmoved. `--append` itself goes\n  through the `appends` parameter, never through `fields`, so it is not refused. Residual,\n  exactly: a writer that bypasses `_mutate` (the same residual as below), and a key added to the\n  schema later as an array of history that is not added to `APPEND_ONLY`.\n- **Refusals.** An existing value that is present and is not a list is refused\n  (`cannot append to a non-array value`). The result is validated by `classify`, so FR-1's item\n  schema applies on both validator paths. A refusal raises before `_atomic_write` and prints the\n  writer's existing `ERROR:` refusal with exit 2.\n- **Heartbeat.** An owner's `--session-id` refreshes the heartbeat, as `merge_fields` does.\n- **Output.** The `STATE-WRITE: OK` line lists the appended keys with the set keys.\n- **Lost-entry detection is not needed, and that is why this mechanism was chosen.** `_mutate`\n  holds `fcntl.flock` on the sidecar lock across load, apply and write, so no other writer that\n  goes through `_mutate` can interleave between the read and the write. Residual: a writer that\n  bypasses `_mutate`, such as a hand edit or another tool's raw write, can still race. That is true\n  of every field.\n- **The orchestrator's step (SKILL.md, FR-10).** After each 5e-review, 5e-verify and 6a-prime leg,\n  it runs `--append review_independence='{\"leg\":…,\"module\":…,\"reviewer\":…,\"implementer\":…,\"independent\":…}'`.\n  `independent` is `reviewer != implementer`. This applies for every implementer.\n- **Attribution: `implementer` is read from the dispatch, as spec FR-7 §\"Attribution\" requires.**\n  It is never read from `implementer_switch.to` or from the `implementer` field, which record\n  routing, not authorship. Counter-example the rule exists for: Codex out → agy dispatched → switch\n  stored; Codex back → Codex dispatched with `switch=none`, and the stored `.to` still says `agy`.\n  - **The authors of a module** are the agents of that module's **accepted implementation\n    dispatches** (spec v1.8 FR-7, E4): the 5e GREEN dispatch the orchestrator accepted, and every\n    later fix dispatch for that module it accepted. A 5d RED dispatch is never one: it writes\n    tests, not the reviewed code (`codex-implementer-prompt.md` assigns GREEN to 5e, `For GREEN\n    phase (5e):`). A dispatch that halted, timed out or was rejected authored nothing. The agent of\n    a dispatch is the value of the `TDD-IMPLEMENTER:` token the orchestrator resolved just before\n    staging it, which is the `--agent` it passed to `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` (`--phase green` for\n    the 5e GREEN dispatch; `--phase` takes `red` or `green`); for the Claude fallback, where no\n    dispatch leaves the session, it is the same token's `claude` value.\n  - **The durable author record (v1.3, OD-E2 option (a), spec FR-1 D-15).** Each accepted\n    implementation dispatch is recorded as one `implementation_authors` entry, through the same\n    `--append` operation as above:\n    `--append implementation_authors='{\"module\":\"<module>\",\"agent\":\"<agent>\"}'`. The writer gains\n    no code for it beyond FR-1's schema entry and the `APPEND_ONLY` refusal: `--append` is\n    key-agnostic, and `classify` applies the item schema on both validator paths.\n  - **Where in the procedure the append happens.** The rule over the axis is an ordering:\n    **every accepted implementation dispatch for a module is appended before any review leg of\n    its code runs** (spec FR-1 D-15), and so before that leg's `review_independence` entry. Its\n    members in the procedure:\n    - **5e GREEN.** In SKILL.md's 5e bullet, when the orchestrator accepts a module's GREEN\n      dispatch, that is when it reads the `STATUS:` token `DONE` or `DONE_WITH_CONCERNS` and stops\n      re-dispatching GREEN for that module. It appends there, before the module's 5e spec-review\n      leg runs. A GREEN that is\n      re-dispatched (the path toward `step5e:green_unreachable:<module>`) is not accepted and is\n      not appended.\n    - **Every accepted fix dispatch** for the module, wherever it is dispatched (a 5e fix after a\n      review or verification finding, or a fix after a 6a-prime `WITH_FIXES`): appended once it\n      is accepted, before the next leg that reviews the fixed code.\n    - **Never 5d RED.** The 5d bullet gains no append.\n\n    An accepted GREEN that a later step halts on (`step5e:verify_failed:<module>`, a failed revert\n    test) keeps its entry, because entries are append-only and its code is still in the tree. That\n    direction is the safe one: an extra author can only turn an entry `independent=false`, never\n    `true`. Residual, exactly: the order is orchestrator prose (SKILL.md, FR-10), and no code\n    refuses a `review_independence` append made before the author append it should follow. A\n    refused author append (an invalid entry) prints the writer's `ERROR:` refusal with the store\n    unchanged; the orchestrator corrects the entry and appends again before the review leg runs\n    (spec FR-1).\n  - **Reading the complete author set.** For module `M`, the set is the `agent` values of the\n    well-formed entries of the record's `implementation_authors` whose `module == M`. Well-formed\n    is `property_valid(\"implementation_authors\", [entry])` (§\"The warnings\" below), the definition\n    the orchestrator applies, since no script reads the field (spec FR-1). The latest\n    accepted implementation dispatch is the last such entry in array order, since `--append` adds\n    at the end and the orchestrator appends at acceptance. The set is read from this field and\n    from nothing else: never from earlier `review_independence` entries, which are a projection of\n    it with one `implementer` per entry. Counter-example: with authors `[agy, codex]`, a grok\n    5e-review stores `implementer=codex` and a Codex 5e-verify stores `implementer=codex`, so a\n    set rebuilt from those entries is `{codex}` and an agy 6a-prime would read as independent\n    (design audit cycle 2, codex). The orchestrator's in-context list of dispatches is not read\n    either: the record is the only source, so a resumed orchestrator reads exactly what an\n    uninterrupted one reads. v1.2 kept the set \"in the procedure\" and is replaced by this.\n  - **A 5e leg** reviews one module. If the reviewer is in that module's author set,\n    `implementer` is the reviewer and `independent` is `false`. Otherwise `implementer` is the\n    agent of the module's latest entry, and `independent` is `true` (spec v1.8 FR-7, S5).\n  - **6a-prime** reviews the whole diff. The orchestrator writes one 6a-prime entry per module,\n    with `module` set, applying the same rule to that module's complete author set.\n  - **When no author can be read, no entry is written.** The leg writes no `review_independence`\n    entry for module `M`, rather than one whose `independent` it cannot establish, in exactly\n    these cases:\n    - the record has no `implementation_authors` field: a record whose implementation dispatches\n      were accepted before this field existed (spec v1.8 FR-7's \"no entry\" rule, kept for those\n      records only). An absent field is never read as an empty author set, which would make\n      every reviewer independent; AC-7.3's pre-field fixture pins this (spec v1.9);\n    - the field is present and holds no well-formed entry for `M`: an author append that was\n      skipped, the residual of the ordering rule above;\n    - the field is present and is not a list, or holds an entry that is not well-formed: a hand\n      edit, since the writer refuses both. One malformed entry may be the lost record of `M`'s\n      author, so the set is not established for any module of that record.\n\n    A resumed orchestrator whose dispatch context is gone is **not** a member: it reads the field\n    like any other. Residual, as the spec states: `review_independence_unrecorded` fires only when\n    no `leg=6a-prime` entry exists at all, so one module missing among several recorded ones has\n    no warning of its own.\n  - **AC-7.3's multi-author recovered fixture, from durable state.** The record holds\n    `implementation_authors` `[{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"agy\"},{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"codex\"}]`. A\n    grok 5e-review: grok is not in `{agy, codex}`, so the entry is `implementer=codex` (the latest\n    entry) and `independent=true`. A Codex 5e-verify: codex is in the set, so the entry is\n    `implementer=codex` and `independent=false`. Then the dispatch context is discarded, and only\n    the record is kept. An agy 6a-prime: agy is in the set read from the record, so the entry is\n    `implementer=agy` and `independent=false`, the outcome AC-7.3 expects. The mutant AC-7.3 names\n    (authors recovered from earlier entries' `implementer` values only) yields `{codex}` and\n    `independent=true`, so the fixture is RED against it. The other three v1.8 AC-7.3 fixtures follow\n    from the same field: a 5d RED appends nothing, so the agy reviewer of a Codex GREEN reads\n    `{codex}`; a rejected agy GREEN appends nothing, so the same holds; and the author set never\n    reads `implementer_switch.to`. The pre-field fixture (spec v1.9) runs the same reviews on a\n    record with no `implementation_authors`, and the 6a-prime leg writes no entry for the module;\n    its mutant reads the absent field as an empty set, so the agy reviewer reads as independent.\n    Whether attribution gets a code surface for these fixtures to\n    drive is a separate open decision, still the impl-plan's (§\"Owed by other documents\").\n  - The writer does not check attribution; the Phase-7 consistency check below verifies only that\n    `independent == (reviewer != implementer)`.\n  - Residual: a module's authors are the agents of accepted dispatches only. Code a reviewer wrote\n    during its own leg (outside an implementation dispatch) is not attributed to it.\n\n**The warnings.** `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` `check` calls a new\n`_review_independence_warnings(record)` and extends `warnings`. It never touches `blockers`, so\n`ready` does not move.\n- **Well-formed means validated by the schema, not by a second copy of the value sets.**\n  `h_mad_state_validate.py` gains `property_valid(name, value)`, which validates `value` against the\n  strict schema's `properties[name]` through `_make_validator`, on both paths. For\n  `implementer_switch` it also applies `rfc3339_datetime` to `ts`, and only when `value` is a\n  `dict`: it calls `_semantic_ok({\"implementer_switch\": value})`, the same predicate `classify`\n  uses, so `None` (the schema's `[\"object\", \"null\"]`) and every other non-`dict` go to the schema\n  alone and never raise (v1.1 applied the predicate unguarded; design audit cycle 2). An entry is well-formed when\n  `property_valid(\"review_independence\", [entry])` holds **and** `entry[\"independent\"] ==\n  (entry[\"reviewer\"] != entry[\"implementer\"])`. A boolean that contradicts its own two agents is\n  malformed, so it is never read as `independent=true`.\n- **`review_independence_malformed`** is emitted for each entry that is not well-formed, and once\n  when the field is present and not a list.\n- **`review_non_independent`** is emitted for each well-formed entry with `independent=false`,\n  naming the leg, the module and the agent.\n- **`review_independence_unrecorded`** is emitted when `record.get(\"archreview\")` is in\n  `{\"READY_TO_MERGE\", \"WITH_FIXES\", \"NO\"}` and no **well-formed** entry has `leg == \"6a-prime\"`.\n  - The code path has no implementer condition (D-11), and it reads neither `implementer` nor\n    `implementer_switch`, nor `implementation_authors` (an authorship record no script reads,\n    spec FR-1 D-15), so AC-7.2's per-implementer fixtures (`grok` and `claude` through\n    `implementer_switch.to`) all reach the same branch.\n  - The detail text names the agents the record's entries attest (a projection, which is why it\n    is only detail text and never an author set the attribution reads): the distinct `implementer` values\n    of the well-formed 5e entries, sorted, or `authors=unrecorded` when there are none. It never\n    names `implementer_switch.to` or `implementer`: they record routing, and the same attribution\n    rule as the entries applies to every surface that names an author.\n  - A malformed 6a-prime entry does not count as recorded, so it raises both warnings.\n- Each warning prints through the existing `  WARNING <code>: <detail>` loop in `main`.\n\n### FR-8 — the tracer, after the merge (DO-6, R-2) — OQ-B, spec D-13\n\n**Why after the merge.** The tracer needs agy to run the gate at the installed path,\n`~/.gemini/config/skills/h-mad/` → `/Users/kimhawk/orca/skills/h-mad`\n(`ls -la ~/.gemini/config/skills` at `29ae3e7b`). That path is main, and main gains the gate only\nwhen this feature merges. An override flag would ship a code path that points the check away from\nthe installed gate. A hand edit of the symlink would also change every live session.\n\n**The stage.** It runs after Phase 7's integrate and before the feature is recorded COMPLETE.\n1. **A fixture project.** It is a throwaway git repository with one module task. It has its own\n   `docs/.bkit-memory.json` step5 record and its own `.agents/hooks.json`, holding FR-5's exact\n   entry. It is created beside this checkout, never under `/tmp`, because agy's trust decision for\n   `.agents/hooks.json` may depend on location.\n2. **Step 1, payload capture.** The real class-(b) payload keys are recorded, and so is\n   `init.tools` with `agy --version`. They land as one commit on main that fills `PATH_KEYS` and\n   re-checks the class-(a) inventory. That commit carries tests built from the captured payloads,\n   written RED then GREEN, and it lands before step 2. The capture hook is then deleted.\n   **D-FR8-1 (2026-10-17):** on agy `1.4.0` only `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content` and\n   `run_command` are declared to the model; `multi_replace_file_content`, `sed_file` and\n   `notebook_edit` appear in `init.tools` only. Step 1 captures the three declared tools; the\n   other three keep no `PATH_KEYS` entry and deny under active step5 (impl-plan Task 20 v1.3).\n3. **Steps 2, 2a and 3** in the fixture project, as FR-8 states them. Step 2a's delay hook is\n   added to, and removed from, the fixture's `hooks.json`, and the before and after\n   `shasum -a 256` are recorded.\n4. **This repository's adoption.** `.agents/hooks.json` is committed here, and step 2 is re-run\n   once in this repository to settle trust at this location.\n5. The derived timeout replaces `agy`'s `1500` in the assembler table, as FR-8's formula sets.\n\nEvery reading is stamped with the sha and `agy --version` under\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`.\n\n**AC-8.1's formula check is a probe check, not a suite test.** A committed script asserts that the\nassembler's `agy` default equals `max(900, 3 × slower)` rounded up to a multiple of 60 over the\n","total_lines":4240}},"locations":[]}
{"type":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"call-7ff6ad45-bf66-4fbd-9947-667549568e2e-3","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"3308→readings it names. A suite test that read a probe file would break when the probe directory is\narchived.\n3310→\n### FR-9 — the carve-out stays prose; the corpus, the replay, and AC-9.4 (DO-7)\n\n**Applied by prose.** The verdict needs two facts: which symbol the task creates, and whether the\ncallee resolves at the RED base. The `WIRE:` value is free text: `h_mad_wire_pin_gate.py`\n`_parse_tasks` returns it as one unparsed string (probed at `29ae3e7b` on a sample task, where a\n`WIRE:` line naming two dotted symbols came back as a single `wire` string). So a mechanical classifier\nwould have to guess the caller and the callee from prose. The orchestrator therefore keeps applying the rule at 5d from the RED report, which\nmust name both. **Residual:** the corpus shows that the rule's **text** selects exactly\n`{new-caller}`. It does not show that any one 5d application follows the text.\n3320→\n**The canonical block.** It is held once, as `CANONICAL` in the equivalence test, and it equals\nspec FR-9's quoted block. It was extracted at `29ae3e7b` by joining the `>` lines and collapsing\nwhitespace:\n\n> A missing-symbol RED on the pin is valid **only** when the missing symbol is the caller the task\n> creates, and the callee resolves at the RED base. The RED report must name both. GREEN must prove\n> the wire by a wire-cut: delete the call only, keep the caller and the callee, and confirm the pin\n> fails.\n\n3330→**Placement of each copy.** Each copy is appended after an existing sentence, so every pinned\nliteral stays. Each of the context sentences below occurs exactly once in its file at `29ae3e7b`\n(Python `str.count`):\n1. Prompt RED item 1, after `Say which case each failing test is.`\n2. The prompt's WIRE-PIN paragraph, after `report that and stop rather than accepting it.`\n3. The SKILL.md 5d bullet, after ``halt `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>`.``\n4. The `failure-recovery.md` 5d row, inside the remedy cell's quotes, after\n   `only the failure mode can.`\n5. `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` `assemble()`, inside the wiring branch's `lines.append(...)` that holds\n   `The WIRE-PIN is this task's single load-bearing test.` The existing literal `\"symbol.\"` becomes\n3340→   `\"symbol. \"`, and the block follows it as further adjacent literals. `never a missing` stays.\n6. The prompt's GREEN paragraph, after `keeping tests green.`\n\n**The equivalence test.** It is new: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_new_caller_carveout_copies.py`.\n- **How a copy is compared.** `collapse(s)` is `\" \".join(s.split())`. Each locator returns one span,\n  and the test asserts `collapse(span).count(collapse(CANONICAL)) == 1`.\n- **Locators fail rather than pass.** Each locator raises when its anchor matches zero spans or more\n  than one, so the test fails.\n\nThe six locators:\n3350→- **L1, prompt RED item 1.** The span runs from the unique line that starts `1. For each FAILING test:`\n  to the next `\\n2. `. It does not pin the item's number or position beyond items 1 and 2.\n- **L2, the WIRE-PIN paragraph.** The prompt is split on blank lines (`\\n[ \\t]*\\n`), and the one\n  block whose left-stripped text starts ``**If the dispatch carries a `WIRE:` / `WIRE-PIN:` line``\n  is taken. It does not pin where the block sits in the file.\n- **L6, the GREEN paragraph.** The same split, taking the one block starting `For GREEN phase (5e):`.\n- **L3, the SKILL.md 5d bullet.** `docsections.titled_section(skill, \"Phase 5 (Implementation) sub-steps\")`\n  is split on blank lines, and the one block whose stripped text starts\n  ``**The third task shape is `wiring`, and counts cannot gate it.**`` is taken. At `29ae3e7b` that\n  selection holds exactly 1 block. It does not pin which 5d list item holds the block.\n3360→- **L4, the failure-recovery row.** It is the one table line whose third `|`-cell, stripped, equals\n  `` `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` ``. The span is that line's fourth cell. The row holds no\n  escaped `\\|` at `29ae3e7b`, and the locator splits on unescaped `|` only. It does not pin the\n  row's position in the table.\n- **L5, the assembler copy.** `ast.parse` the assembler and take the top-level `FunctionDef` named\n  `assemble`. Among its `ast.Constant` `str` nodes, take the one containing\n  `The WIRE-PIN is this task's single load-bearing test.` The parser joins the adjacent literals.\n  This does not pin the literal split, and the mutation row is what pins that.\n\nNo span holds more than one copy, so a healthy copy cannot mask a sick one.\n3370→\n**The control.** The new spec `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/new_caller_carveout_copies.json` holds 8\nrows, each with `symbol` set. For a `.md` file that is the enclosing heading, and for the `.py` file\nit is `assemble`. Each row's `test` is the equivalence test, and each row is run alone.\n- **Six per-copy rows.** Each changes `valid **only** when` to `valid when` in one copy, with a\n  `find` that starts at that copy's context sentence above. That context makes the `find` unique in\n  its file, which the harness `anchor_status` requires. The `.py` row's `find` is written against\n  the committed source bytes, including the adjacent-literal split.\n- **Two per-sentence rows**, on the `failure-recovery.md` copy, which is its file's only copy:\n  - sentence 2: delete `The RED report must name both. `;\n3380→  - sentence 3: change `delete the call only, keep the caller and the callee` to\n    `delete the callee only, keep the caller`.\n\n  Sentence 1 in that copy is covered by its per-copy row.\n- AC-9.4 asks for six per-copy runs plus one run per sentence in one copy. 8 = 6 + 2, because the\n  per-copy row for `failure-recovery.md` is also its sentence-1 row.\n\n**Residual.** The test shows that each copy carries the block. It does not show that no copy has a\ncontradicting sentence beside it, or that a seventh surface exists. A seventh surface held in a\n`str` constant, a comment or non-`.py` text is found by re-running FR-9's union sweep (AC-9.1). One\n3390→built at run time is found only by reading the code that builds the dispatch text.\n\n**The differential corpus (AC-9.2).** It is a new suite test,\n`h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_wire_pin_new_caller_corpus.py`.\n- **The five cases.** For each of `new-caller`, `missing-callee`, `both-missing`,\n  `unrelated-import` and `other-symbol`, it builds a fixture repository in `tmp_path` and runs real\n  pytest with `sys.executable`, recording the RED's exception line.\n- **Callee resolution is executed, never parsed.** A subprocess runs `import <mod>` and\n  `getattr(<mod>, \"<callee>\")` at the RED base.\n- **The missing-symbol role** comes from the fixture's declaration. The test asserts that the\n3400→  exception line names that symbol.\n- **Two classifiers transcribe the two texts.**\n  - `old_rule`: a missing-symbol RED (`ImportError`, `ModuleNotFoundError`, `AttributeError` or\n    `NameError`) is a halt.\n  - `new_rule`: such a RED is valid iff the missing symbol is the caller and the callee resolves.\n  - The test also asserts that `CANONICAL`, imported from the equivalence test, contains\n    `the missing symbol is the caller the task creates` and `the callee resolves at the RED base`.\n    That ties the transcription to the words.\n- **Pass:** the set of cases whose verdict changed equals `{new-caller}`.\n- **Control:** two rows in the same new spec, each run alone. One drops the callee condition, and\n3410→  `both-missing` then changes. The other drops the caller condition, and `other-symbol` and\n  `unrelated-import` then change.\n- The cases' exception lines and verdicts are also written to\n  `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/` as the committed reading.\n\n**The incident replay (AC-9.3).** It is a committed probe under\n`docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, following FR-9's four steps through `git archive`\nexports, so the live clone is never checked out. The premises were re-checked at `29ae3e7b`:\nHemaSuite `HEAD` is `77fb16f51`, and `531a6296b`, `7aa0d9dec` and `c2d671f6b` are each ancestors\n(`git merge-base --is-ancestor`). Their subjects begin `…Task 4 FR-1 resolver`,\n3420→`…Task 5 FR-5 initiali…` and `…Task 6 CLI surface…`. The probe stamps the HemaSuite sha it reads.\n\n### FR-10 — documentation surfaces\n\nThese follow the spec's FR-10 list and the plan's doc-pin census. Each rewrite keeps every pinned\nliteral the census lists, or changes that test's literal in the same commit.\n- **SKILL.md §\"Codex authors Phase 5 — enforced, not just instructed\"** becomes implementer-neutral.\n  It states FR-2 and FR-3 and names `agy-authorship`, `implementer-invalid` and\n  `h_mad_tdd_judge.py implementer`. The heading and the end anchor\n  `Phase 5 implementation is **Codex's job**` stay, or `_phase_5e_text` and `PHASE5_HEADING` move\n3430→  in the same commit.\n- **SKILL.md §\"Exit-code dispatch for 5d/5e\"** gains a line that holds both `exec agy` and\n  \"implement\", plus the `TDD-IMPLEMENTER:` staging step.\n- **SKILL.md, Phase 7 closure item 7** carries every `WARNING` line, and adds the FR-7 `--append`\n  step after each leg, for every implementer.\n- **SKILL.md's 5e bullet, and each fix-dispatch step** (v1.3), gain the FR-7 author append at\n  acceptance, ordered before the leg that reviews the code; the 5d bullet gains none.\n- **The SKILL.md teammate-audit bullet** separates audit-leg selection from resolved authorship.\n- **SKILL.md** states the FR-5 re-run trigger: after any agy upgrade, re-run FR-8 step 2 and step\n  2a, and re-take the class-(a) inventory.\n3440→- **`agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD gate\"** names `h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` and `AGY-GATE:`, and keeps the\n  halt. Its `SessionStart` claim, on two surfaces, is re-checked against the installed agy, which\n  is `1.4.0` at `29ae3e7b`.\n- **The prompt** gets a neutral opening and a `Hook:` line that names each gate by implementer,\n  with no `<`, `>` or `->`.\n- **`state-schema.md` and the schema** get the FR-1 rows, plus the four description rewrites in\n  §\"FR-1\".\n- **`failure-recovery.md`** gets copy 4.\n\n## Components Changed / Added\n3450→\n| Path | Change | FR |\n|---|---|---|\n| `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json` | five properties; `fallback_agent` and `codex_status` descriptions | FR-1 |\n| `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_validate.py` | `rfc3339_datetime`, `_semantic_ok` in `classify`, `property_valid` | FR-1, FR-7 |\n| `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_tdd_judge.py` | `Record` fields, `_implementer_tag`, `Implementer`, `resolve_implementer`, author field, `implementer` verb | FR-2, FR-3 |\n| `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh` | `STATE_ACTIVE_RE`, author-field parse, FR-3 precedence | FR-3 |\n| `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py` | receives the 25 moved names | FR-6 |\n| `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py` | guarded import of the 25 names; definitions removed | FR-6 |\n| `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py` (new) | agy write gate | FR-4 |\n3460→| `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_hook_wiring.py` | `--agy`, `check_agy` | FR-5 |\n| `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` | `agy` choice, timeout table, check run, docstring, copy 5 | FR-5, FR-8, FR-9 |\n| `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_write.py` | `--append`; the `APPEND_ONLY` refusal in `merge_fields` | FR-7 |\n| `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` | `_review_independence_warnings` | FR-7 |\n| `h-mad/references/codex-implementer-prompt.md` | neutral opening, `Hook:` line, copies 1, 2 and 6 | FR-9 |\n| `h-mad/SKILL.md`, `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md`, `h-mad/references/state-schema.md`, `h-mad/references/failure-recovery.md` | §\"FR-10\" | FR-9, FR-10 |\n| `.agents/hooks.json` (new, committed by FR-8's stage) | wiring | FR-5, D-2 |\n\n## Implementation Order\n\n3470→1. FR-1: the schema, the validator predicate and `property_valid`.\n2. FR-2: the resolver and the verb.\n3. FR-3: the line