on `.py`; a test file; `src/test_a.py` hard-linked to `src/z.py`; the allowance file in two case spellings; `docs/x.py`; `/tmp` non-`.py`; `/tmp` `.py`; an outside non-`/tmp` path; an unresolvable path; a production `.py` whose chain holds a `chmod 000` `docs/` that the `rglob` scan cannot see) × `phase5_status` (`active`, `inactive`, `unknown`), each judged by the Codex gate (`Write` with `file_path`, no payload cwd) and by the agy gate (`write_to_file` with `TargetFile`), with the same admit/deny in every cell outside the stated deliberate differences, and the same kind wherever the Codex deny carries one; plus the class-(c) workdir fixtures and the shell differential (§\"FR-4\"); plus, under `inactive`, `run_command` `ls` with `workspacePaths=[]`, `run_command` with no `CommandLine`, and `write_to_file` with `TargetFile: \"\"` each allowed, and the same three under `active` and under `unknown` each denied `kind=judge-error`; plus one fixture per row of §\"FR-4\" \"Every deny\" asserting that row's kind, and a scan of every deny the suite produces asserting exactly one `kind=` token from the closed set |\n| AC-5.1, AC-5.2 | new `test_h_mad_hook_wiring_agy.py` (a `HOME` fixture), plus `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py` |\n| AC-6.1 | the existing Codex gate tests unmodified (`test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]` is the import-failure fixture); the new `--self-check` `FAIL policy-import` fixture; the one-`def` test; the `unsafe` tuple parity test; the shell differential |\n| AC-7.1, AC-7.2 | `test_h_mad_state_write.py` (`--append`: the two-entry fixture, the two-appends-in-one-call fixture, the same-key `--set`/`--append` refusal, and the `APPEND_ONLY` refusal once per key with that key alone: `--set review_independence=[]` and `--set implementation_authors=[]`, each exit 2 with the store unchanged) and `test_h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py`; the FR-7 attribution rule is orchestrator prose (SKILL.md, FR-10), pinned by a doc test that the FR-7 step's text states the dispatch as the source; the one code surface that names an author, the `unrecorded` detail text, gets the switch/recovery fixture: `implementer_switch.to == \"agy\"` with a well-formed 5e entry whose `implementer` is `codex`, and the detail must name `codex` and not `agy`; `property_valid(\"implementer_switch\", None)` is `True` and does not raise. AC-7.3's attribution fixtures (spec v1.9: the four of v1.8 plus the pre-field fixture) are each derivable from the record alone, the multi-author recovered one included, since the author set is read from `implementation_authors` (§\"FR-7\", OD-E2 decided); they still have no code surface to drive, since attribution is orchestrator prose, and that surface is owed to the impl-plan (§\"Owed by other documents\") |\n| AC-8.1, AC-8.2 | probes, §\"FR-8\" |\n| AC-9.1 | `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd_agent.py` `test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent` is extended: the WIRED fixture (§\"FR-5\") is provisioned in its `tmp_path` root once, before the loop, so all three agents read the same root; the loop becomes `(\"codex\", \"grok\", \"agy\")`; `run_cli` gains an env override so the `agy` run sees the fixture `HOME`; and the assertion becomes `len(hashes) == 3 and len(set(hashes)) == 1`. Today it loops over `(\"codex\", \"grok\")` and asserts `hashes[0] == hashes[1]`, which would leave a third hash unchecked. Plus the union sweep re-run and the three pin files staying green |\n3500→| AC-9.2, AC-9.4 | §\"FR-9\" |\n| AC-9.3 | probe |\n| AC-X | §\"Mutation rows\" |\n\n## Mutation rows (AC-X)\n\nEach new guard below gets one row. The fixture that kills it is named beside it.\n- **FR-1**\n  - `_semantic_ok` call removed from `classify` → `not-a-date` on both paths.\n  - The `datetime` construction removed → `2026-02-30T00:00:00Z`.\n3510→  - `fullmatch` → `match` → `…Zjunk`.\n- **FR-2**\n  - Each `_cause` branch removed → that branch's own fixture, with only that cause present.\n  - The `other` peer step removed → `chosen=codex` out, agy available.\n- **FR-3**\n  - The `implementer-invalid` branch removed → the invalid fixture.\n  - The `INVALID_RECORD` scan stopped at the first record → `[codex-available, invalid]` expects\n    `implementer-invalid`.\n  - The `agy` arm removed → the `agy-authorship` fixture.\n  - The grammar's `peers-out` alternative removed → every stub test.\n3520→- **FR-6**\n  - The import guard removed (a bare top-level import) → `test_codex_gate_kind[judge-error]`,\n    whose tree has no identity module: the gate then exits 1 with no JSON.\n  - The self-check's `FAIL policy-import` branch removed → the new self-check fixture, which then\n    sees a `NameError` traceback and exit 1.\n  - Row `G2` itself stays killed through the re-raise (§\"FR-6\", Binding).\n  - No row for removing the `_main_guarded` re-raise, because that mutant is equivalent in\n    decision: without it, every path of `_main_guarded` that can allow first calls a moved name\n    (`_any_phase5_status` runs before any allow), which raises `NameError` into `# M:G2` and\n    denies `judge-error`; the one earlier deny (`M:CX-ROOT-ENTER`) is a deny either way. Only the\n3530→    reason text differs. The re-raise exists so the reason names the import failure.\n- **FR-4**\n  - A class-(a) name moved to (b) or (d) → that class's member fixture.\n  - The class-(d) default replaced by allow → an unknown-name fixture.\n  - The no-path-key deny removed → that fixture.\n  - The `artifactDirectoryPath` `.py` refusal removed → its fixture.\n  - The `finally` deny removed → malformed stdin.\n  - T3 moved after T5 (allowance check before containment) → a `/tmp` non-`.py` write, which then\n    denies `judge-error` instead of allowing.\n  - (v1.4) T2a removed → a `write_to_file` whose `TargetFile` names an existing in-root directory\n3540→    under `active`, which then allows instead of denying `judge-error` (the impl-plan's\n    `od2-directory-target-check-dropped`).\n  - T6 judging `resolved.target` alone instead of every name → the `test_a.py`/`z.py` hard-link\n    fixture.\n  - T6–T7 skipped when `phase5_status == \"inactive\"` → the `chmod 000` `docs/` chain fixture.\n  - T7's `unreadable` branch removed → the same fixture.\n  - T4's `str` check on `artifactDirectoryPath` (`isinstance(adp, str) and adp` → `adp is not\n    None`) → `artifactDirectoryPath: 5` with a non-`.py` target outside both `/tmp` and the root,\n    under `inactive`: the rule allows; the mutant reaches `Path(5)`, raises `TypeError`, and denies\n    `kind=judge-error`. v1.1's fixture (`\"\"`, from a process directory containing the target)\n3550→    could not bite: step 3's `os.chdir(root)` makes `Path(\"\")` the root, and T3 has already sent\n    every in-root target away, so `\"\"` admits nothing either way (design audit cycle 2, codex,\n    run on `_path_within` as a controlled pair). **The empty-string arm is therefore an\n    equivalent mutant at the full gate** and has no row; it stays as defence should step 3's\n    `chdir` move. Residual: that arm is unpinned.\n  - Step 5's `S` condition removed → `run_command` `ls`, `workspacePaths=[]`, `inactive`, which\n    then denies.\n  - Class (c)'s missing-`CommandLine` `S` condition removed → `run_command` with no `CommandLine`,\n    `inactive`, which then denies.\n  - Class (b)'s non-empty-`str` target test weakened to key presence → `TargetFile: \"\"` under\n3560→    `active`, which then allows (no production name) instead of denying `kind=judge-error`.\n  - Each of the three design-owned kinds replaced by `judge-error` at its site\n    (`target-outside-root`, `allowance-file-write`, `shell-not-admitted`) → that row's kind\n    fixture.\n  - Class (c) denying `judge-error` when `cwd` is not an existing directory → `ls` with\n    `Cwd=str(root / \"no-such-dir\")`.\n  - Class (c) joining a relative `Cwd` against the handler's directory → a relative `Cwd` naming\n    a sub-project whose `.venv` interpreter the command uses.\n- **FR-5:** each of the seven reason checks removed → its fixture; `UNWIRED` returning 2 → the\n  AC-5.2 exit fixture.\n3570→- **FR-7**\n  - Each appended value built from `[]` instead of the current value → AC-7.1's two-entry\n    fixture (both earlier entries, compared as `json.dumps` text, must survive).\n  - Accumulation read from `prior` instead of the updated `record` → the two-appends-in-one-call\n    fixture (both earlier entries and both additions, in order).\n  - The same-key `--set`/`--append` refusal removed → its fixture, which expects exit 2 and an\n    unchanged store.\n  - (v1.3) Each `APPEND_ONLY` member removed from the tuple → that key's own `--set …=[]` fixture,\n    run with that key alone, which expects exit 2 and an unchanged store.\n  - v1.0's row \"`--append` mutating the prior list in place\" is dropped: the prior record is\n3580→    discarded after the write, so the persisted bytes are identical in both modes and no\n    observation the suite makes can see it (codex audit cycle 1, run on a controlled pair).\n  - The `independent` consistency check removed → a lying-boolean fixture.\n  - The `unrecorded` trigger restricted to `implementer == \"agy\"` → the `codex` fixture (AC-7.2).\n  - A malformed 6a-prime entry counted as recorded → its fixture.\n  - The `unrecorded` detail text reading `implementer_switch.to` → the switch/recovery fixture.\n  - `property_valid`'s `dict` guard removed (the predicate applied to `value[\"ts\"]` directly) →\n    `property_valid(\"implementer_switch\", None)`, which then raises.\n- **FR-9:** the 8 + 2 rows in §\"FR-9\".\n\n3590→**Existing rows the edits move (E7).** The rule over the class: after each Task, every committed\nmutation row whose `file` the Task edits must still match its `find` exactly once\n(`h_mad_mutation_harness.py --check-anchors`, the harness's own `source.count(find)` test), and a\nrow the edit breaks is re-anchored **in that Task**, to the same intent and the same `test`. The\nsweep that found them: every row of `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/*.json` whose `file` is one this\ndesign edits outside FR-3's and FR-6's files (which §\"FR-3\" and §\"FR-6\" already re-anchor\nwhole), with each `find` located in the file at `cff71b49`:\n- `assemble_tdd_agent.json` `w10a-grok-by-default` (FR-5 Task): its `find` is the `--agent`\n  choices line, which FR-5 rewrites. Re-anchor: `find`\n  `ap.add_argument(\"--agent\", choices=(\"codex\", \"grok\", \"agy\"), default=\"codex\")`, `replace` the\n3600→  same with `default=\"grok\"`, `test` unchanged.\n- `assemble_tdd_agent.json` `t1-timeout-judged-by-value` (FR-5 Task): `find`\n  `args.timeout is not None` sits on the timeout line FR-5 rewrites; the line form in §\"FR-5\"\n  keeps it once, and the `replace` (`args.timeout not in (None, 900)`) still bites\n  `test_agent_grok_explicit_timeout_wins`. No re-anchor if that form is kept.\n- `grok_wire_reverts.json` `wr3-assembler-drops-agent` (FR-5 Task): `find` `        agent=args.agent,`\n  must stay once; the check run FR-5 adds to `main` must not repeat that line.\n- `heartbeat_beat.json` `the-owners-set-no-longer-beats` and `a-bystanders-set-beats-too` (FR-7\n  Task): `find` is `merge_fields`' owner-heartbeat `if` and its assignment; §\"FR-7\" keeps both\n  byte for byte, so no re-anchor. The other two rows of that spec anchor in `beat`, which FR-7\n3610→  does not edit.\n- SKILL.md and `failure-recovery.md` rows: none of their `find` texts lies in a span FR-9/FR-10\n  rewrites (the nearest are `phase7_integrate_blockers_documented.json`'s three rows inside Phase 7\n  item 7 (the closure list, around its 7f integrate step), which the item-7 edit only adds to, and\n  `baseline_sha.json`/`wire_registry_key.json` in §\"Phase 5 (Implementation) sub-steps\" on the 5f\n  bullet, not the 5d bullet FR-9 edits). Every other row on\n  `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`, `h_mad_state_write.py`, `h_mad_phase7_preconditions.py` and\n  `h_mad_hook_wiring.py` anchors on code this design does not rewrite. `h_mad_state_validate.py`,\n  `h_mad_state_schema.json` and `codex-implementer-prompt.md` have no rows.\nResidual: the sweep reads the design's edit sites as described here; an implementation that\n3620→rewrites more than it describes moves rows this list does not name, which `--check-anchors` then\ncatches in that Task.\n\n## Residuals\n\nEach is stated where it arises. Collected here:\n- the `which` / `command -v` matrix (FR-2);\n- the verb reads the root only (FR-2);\n- the class-(a) judgement (FR-4);\n- the six-line shell composition in both gates (FR-4);\n3630→- a sibling `overwrite` (FR-4);\n- the review-leg agy is gated too (FR-4);\n- worktree state, not claimed either way (FR-4);\n- an equivalent command spelling (FR-5);\n- a writer that bypasses `_mutate` (FR-7);\n- the deliberate differences from the Codex gate's target sequence, and any rule it gains later\n  (FR-4);\n- relative shell tokens under a `Cwd` that does not exist (FR-4);\n- code a reviewer wrote outside an implementation dispatch (FR-7);\n- prose application (FR-9);\n3640→- a contradicting sentence or a seventh surface (FR-9);\n- (v1.2) a judge kind added to `KINDS` later passes through unlisted (FR-4);\n- (v1.2) T4's empty-string arm is an equivalent mutant, unpinned (Mutation rows);\n- (v1.2) a module missing its 6a-prime entry among recorded ones has no warning of its own (FR-7);\n- (v1.3) no entry is written for a record with no `implementation_authors` field, a module with\n  no well-formed author entry, or a record holding a malformed one (FR-7);\n- (v1.3) the author-before-review ordering is prose, not refused by code (FR-7);\n- (v1.3) a history array added to the schema later and not added to `APPEND_ONLY` (FR-7);\n- (v1.2) rows moved by an edit wider than this design describes (Mutation rows, \"Existing rows\");\n- (v1.4) a class-(b) target ending in `/` that names no existing directory passes T2a and is\n3650→  judged as a file name (FR-4, OD-2);\n- (v1.4) a self-check FAIL added later is classed by the verdict/could-not-run rule, not listed\n  (FR-4 `--self-check`).\n\n## Measurements\n\nTwo behavioural premises carry committed probes under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`,\neach with its invocation in its docstring and one reading at `200ffd52`:\n- `fr3_state_line_grammar.py` (P-1..P-5, §\"FR-3\"): `PROBE: PASS`;\n- `fr6_import_failure_pair.py` (the hand-built gate trees, §\"FR-6\"): `PROBE: PASS`.\n3660→\nThe census figures below are one reading at `29ae3e7b` each. Their committed probes are owed to\nthe impl-plan (§\"Owed by other documents\"); the scratch scripts that produced them were deleted,\nand the method is stated beside each figure so a probe can re-derive it.\n\n| Figure | Method | Reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| DO-1a line census | `git ls-files -z h-mad \\| xargs -0 grep -l 'TDD-STATE: active'`, and the plan's four-pattern variant | 6 files; 7 files |\n| M-3, rows over the contract | the plan's pattern over the `find` + `replace` of rows naming the two FR-3 files | 8 rows, 3 specs |\n| rows inside the regions FR-3 rewrites | the `find` offset inside `_read_state`, the authorship block, `_active_records` or `format_state_line` | 13 rows |\n3670→| M-4 over the DO-5 set | the `find` start inside a moved name's `ast` span, for rows naming the Codex gate | 31 of 62 rows, 4 specs |\n| class inventory | the first `init` event's `tools` | 58 names: (a) 11, (b) 5, (c) 1, (d) 41 |\n| live records with FR-1 keys | a key scan over two state files | 0 of 61 records |\n\n## Operator decisions\n\n- **OQ-A — where the shared policy lives (DO-5): decided, spec D-12.** `h_mad_target_identity.py`.\n  A **new** module breaks `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason` unmodified (§\"FR-6\", committed\n  probe). The alternatives v1.0 listed (a new module plus an edited test tree; loading the Codex\n  gate file through `importlib`) are not taken.\n3680→- **OQ-B — FR-8 after the merge (DO-6): decided, spec D-13.** The post-merge stage in §\"FR-8\",\n  with the feature recorded COMPLETE only after it, and this repository's `.agents/hooks.json`\n  committed only on a passed refusal. The override-flag alternative is not taken.\n- **OD-E2 — a durable author record (FR-7): decided 2026-10-16, option (a), spec FR-1 D-15.** The\n  `implementation_authors` field (§\"FR-1\"), appended through `--append` at each accepted\n  implementation dispatch and read as the only source of a module's author set (§\"FR-7\"). Option\n  (b), amending AC-7.3's expected outcome to \"no entry\", is not taken.\n- **OD-2 — a class-(b) directory target: decided 2026-10-16 (operator), impl-plan Task 6.** An\n  existing-directory target is refused `kind=judge-error` under `S` and allowed under `inactive`,\n  as T2a (§\"FR-4\"). Residual: a trailing-`/` path naming no existing directory.\n3690→- **Self-check exits: decided 2026-10-16 (orchestrator, impl-plan cycles 1–2).** A verdict FAIL\n  exits 0 and a could-not-run FAIL exits 2 (§\"FR-4\" `--self-check`); the Codex gate's\n  `FAIL shell-policy` is corrected in impl-plan Task 4.\n\n## Owed by other documents\n\nSpec v1.7 (`c117a695`) settled the v1.0 items it owned: the `codex_status` description pair\n(FR-1), the agy version (FR-8 §\"Version\", `1.4.0` at `200ffd52`), D-12, D-13, D-14 and FR-7\n§\"Attribution\". Still owed:\n- **Plan.** v1.1's plan items (M-4's 31 rows, DO-2's log version, D-12/D-13) are landed in plan\n3700→  v1.6 (`8e242d06`); v1.2 contradicts nothing in it. Plan stage 7's attribution fixture is spec\n  AC-7.3's first; the plan does not yet carry AC-7.3's other three or the E7 re-anchors (§\"Mutation\n  rows\", \"Existing rows the edits move\"), which the impl-plan's FR-5 and FR-7 Tasks must carry.\n- **Spec v1.8 (`bd508fbf`)** adopted the deliberate differences (FR-4), the kind rule, S5 and E4.\n  It names only `judge-error`, `tool-not-admitted` and the judge's kind; the closed set it defers\n  to is §\"FR-4\" \"The deny kinds\" (five gate kinds plus the judge's).\n- **Spec v1.9 (D-15)** carries `implementation_authors`, AC-1.3 and the pre-field fixture, and\n  scopes FR-7's \"no entry\" rule to the three closed cases this design follows. This design adds\n  the `APPEND_ONLY` `--set` refusal over it and over `review_independence` (§\"FR-7\"), which\n  spec v1.9 FR-7 §\"Record\" does not state; the spec owes either that rule or a reason it is out\n3710→  of scope.\n- **Impl-plan (v1.3).** The FR-1 Task carries AC-1.3's `implementation_authors` fixtures; the\n  FR-7 Task carries the two `APPEND_ONLY` fixtures and their mutation row, and AC-7.3's pre-field\n  fixture beside its other four; the FR-10 Task\n  carries the SKILL.md author-append step at 5e GREEN acceptance and at each accepted fix\n  dispatch.\n- **Impl-plan, AC-7.3's surface.** Attribution is orchestrator prose; AC-7.3's five fixtures need\n  a code surface to drive, which spec v1.8 also files as an OPEN-DECISION. OD-E2 leaves this open:\n  it makes the fixtures' inputs durable, not executable.\n- **Spec (v1.4 items).** Lines recording OD-2 (T2a, with its residual); FR-2 naming\n3720→  `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved`, or adopting them from the\n  impl-plan (§\"FR-2\" \"The caller's halts\"); FR-4/FR-5 self-check: a verdict FAIL exits 0.\n- **Impl-plan (v1.4 item).** The Codex gate's `FAIL parser` is a verdict FAIL that returns 2 like\n  `FAIL shell-policy`; Task 4 corrects only the latter by name. Either Task 4 corrects both, or the\n  impl-plan states why `FAIL parser` stays.\n- **Impl-plan.** These become committed probes: the M-3/M-4 censuses, the class inventory, the FR-9\n  corpus reading and the replay. The FR-3 grammar probe and the FR-6 import-failure probe are\n  committed by this design. Each FR-9/FR-10 Task's RED names the doc-pin census entries it moves.\n\n## Version History\n3730→- v1.0: Initial design (2026-10-16) from spec v1.6 (c853b207) and plan v1.4 (29ae3e7b), tree read at 29ae3e7b. Settles DO-1..DO-7: per-record author field on TDD-STATE (P-1..P-5 probed in Python re and bash 3.2/5.3 ERE); 11-name class-(a) allowlist over the 58-name init.tools inventory; locked --append writer operation; stdlib RFC 3339 predicate in classify on both validator paths, scoped to implementer_switch.ts; 25 moved names into h_mad_target_identity.py with guarded import (M-4 re-derived: 31 rows, 4 specs); HOME-fixture tests and post-merge FR-8 stage; prose-applied carve-out with six per-copy locators and 8 control rows. Two operator questions (OQ-A placement, OQ-B post-merge tracer); codex_status description owed to the spec.\n- v1.1: Design audit cycle 1 (2026-10-16), against spec v1.7 (c117a695), tree at 200ffd52. C1: leap second refused per spec FR-1/D-14, residual framing dropped. C2/C3: FR-4 class (b) is the Codex gate per-target sequence T1-T7 (unresolvable, containment before _is_shell_allowance, every hard-link name for production, read_chain unreadable/inactive branches, no rglob-inactive shortcut per M:G5, non-empty artifactDirectoryPath), deliberate differences listed, target differential test. C4/C11: AC-9.1 WIRED fixture and three-hash assertion. C5: in-place append row dropped as unbitable, replaced by loss and accumulation mutants. C6: FR-7 attribution from accepted implementation dispatches per spec FR-7 Attribution, per-module 6a-prime entries, unrecorded detail text no longer reads implementer_switch. C7: --append accumulates from the updated record; same-key --set/--append refused. C8: import failure re-raised inside _main_guarded so row G2 stays killed; self-check FAIL policy-import. C9: class (c) cwd computed like _command_cwd, chdir only to an existing command directory, no judge-error on a bad Cwd. C10: _semantic_ok never raises; new properties after fallback_agent. M1: committed probes fr3_state_line_grammar.py and fr6_import_failure_pair.py (both PROBE: PASS at 200ffd52). OQ-A/OQ-B recorded as spec D-12/D-13.\n- v1.2: Design audit cycle 2, final corrective revision after the round cap, not re-audited (2026-10-16); spec v1.8 (bd508fbf), plan v1.6 (8e242d06), tree at cff71b49. E1: step-5 workspacePaths cross-check and class (c) missing CommandLine gated on S (active|unknown); every FR-4 deny tabulated with its gating (16 rows). E5: class (b) targets are recognised values that are a non-empty str, '' never canonicalised. E6: closed kind set - judge-error, tool-not-admitted, target-outside-root, allowance-file-write, shell-not-admitted, plus the judge's KINDS passthrough at T7; 'as the Codex gate does' claim corrected (6 of 13 Codex _deny calls kindless). E3: T4 row re-fixtured on a non-str artifactDirectoryPath under inactive; the empty-string arm declared an equivalent mutant (step-3 chdir). E2/E4: authors = accepted 5e GREEN + accepted fix dispatches, never 5d RED; complete set kept in procedure, never rebuilt from entries; lost list -> no entry; OD-E2 (AC-7.3 multi-author recovery needs a durable FR-1 record) to the impl-plan. E7: re-anchor of assemble_tdd_agent w10a-grok-by-default; t1, wr3 and two heartbeat_beat rows kept by stated line forms; --check-anchors rule per Task. E8: property_valid guards dict via _semantic_ok. New rows for the S-gates, the target test, the three design kinds, the dict guard.\n- v1.3: Operator decision OD-E2 option (a) (2026-10-16), aligned to spec v1.9 D-15; tree at 3ad735f7. FR-1: fifth property implementation_authors, array of {module (string, minLength 1), agent (codex|agy|grok|claude)}, both required, no extra keys, placed after review_independence; minLength confirmed in _MiniDraft7's supported set; zero-record reading for the fifth key (54 + 8 records, 0 carrying any of the five keys). FR-7: the author record appended through the existing --append at each accepted 5e GREEN and accepted fix dispatch, never 5d RED, before any review leg of that code runs; the author set and the latest author are read from the field only (v1.2's in-procedure list retired); no entry only for an absent field, a module with no well-formed entry, or a malformed field; the AC-7.3 multi-author recovered and pre-field fixtures derived from durable state. New: APPEND_ONLY --set refusal in merge_fields over review_independence and implementation_authors, with a per-key mutation row (owed to the spec). OD-E2 closed in Operator decisions; Test Plan gains AC-1.3; Residuals, Components, FR-10 and Owed updated. AC-7.3's code surface stays the impl-plan's open decision.\n- v1.4: Owed lines from Phase 5b impl-plan audit cycle 2, final corrective, not re-audited (2026-10-16); impl-plan v1.1 (fc92becf), tree at 84ec2133. Q1: OD-2 recorded as FR-4 T2a (existing-directory target -> judge-error under S, allow under inactive; before T3), deny table 17 rows, deliberate difference, mutation row, residual (trailing '/' naming no directory), Operator decisions. Q2: --self-check rule - a verdict FAIL exits 0, a could-not-run FAIL (policy-import) exits 2; the Codex gate's FAIL shell-policy corrected in impl-plan Task 4, FAIL parser owed. Q3: FR-2 caller halts step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded, step5:implementer_invalid, step5:implementer_unresolved adopted from the impl-plan. Owed: spec lines for all three; impl-plan FAIL parser.\n\n\nAudit rubric (TWO axes, both mandatory):\n\nAxis A — Generic adversarial:\n3740→- Contradictions inside the doc\n- Gaps (missing error paths, untestable AC, unstated assumptions)\n- Weak claims (load-bearing decisions without justification)\n- Scope creep (work outside the stated feature boundary)\n\nAxis B — Invariant compliance (any breach = must-fix, you cannot downgrade).\nTwo layers, both binding:\n\n### Base invariants (workflow-universal; non-overridable by any project file — but the operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar still applies to base findings):\n\n3750→# H-MAD Base Invariants — Axis B (workflow-universal)\n\n> Shipped with the `/h-mad` skill. The orchestrator inlines this file verbatim into the\n> `INLINE_BASE_INVARIANTS` slot (name written bare — a bracketed mention inside an inlined\n> file survives substitution and reaches the reviewer as an unfilled-looking placeholder)\n> of every plan / design / impl-plan audit, **before** the\n> per-project `<PROJECT_ROOT>/.h-mad/invariants.md` domain layer. These rules apply to every\n> project that uses `/h-mad` and are **non-overridable by any project file**: a project's\n> invariants file may add domain rules but may not downgrade or delete a base rule. The\n> operator `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar escape hatch still applies to base findings\n3760→> (a base item listed under that section in a sidecar `.audit.v<N+1>.md` is a conscious,\n> audited deferral — it is excluded from the gate count exactly like any other layer's item).\n>\n> Axis A (generic adversarial review) is covered by the audit-prompt template — not repeated here.\n\n## Audit-gate signal discipline\n- Any gate/check whose verdict the orchestrator consumes MUST communicate PASS/FAIL via an\n  explicit **stdout token** and **exit 0** on a normal verdict (PASS or FAIL). Using a non-zero\n  process exit to mean \"FAIL\" is a violation — a non-zero exit registers as a Claude Code tool\n  failure (`PostToolUseFailure`) and leaks into coexisting plugins' error handling. A non-zero\n3770→  exit is permitted ONLY for genuine operational errors (missing/unreadable input).\n\n## Single-source contract\n- A rule applied by more than one surface (e.g. an audit blocking-item count read by both the\n  orchestrator gate step and a precondition checker) MUST have exactly one authoritative\n  implementation that all surfaces call, OR a test asserting byte-equivalence across surfaces.\n  Independent re-implementations that can silently diverge are a violation.\n- A contract stated in a template (e.g. an empty-section sentinel) MUST match what the consuming\n  gate logic treats as that case. Template guidance and gate behavior disagreeing is a violation.\n\n3780→## Standalone / no plugin dependency\n- The skill MUST NOT acquire a **runtime dependency** on any other plugin (OMC, bkit,\n  context-mode, etc.) or external skill (spec-kit, b-mad, pdca). Coexistence accommodations\n  (sharing a state filename, conforming doc structure to an external validator's required\n  sections) are allowed; *requiring another plugin installed at runtime* is a violation.\n\n## No new external dependency\n- Scripts MUST use only Python stdlib plus tooling already depended upon by the skill (the\n  agent dispatch substrate (cmux or orca, via `hmad-dispatch`); `jq` where the existing hook\n  already uses it; `pytest`).\n3790→  Introducing a new third-party package or new CLI is a violation.\n- **Dispatched agent CLIs are not script dependencies.** `codex`, `agy` and `grok` are optional\n  runtime *agents*: reached only through a `hmad-dispatch` verb (`exec`, `send`, …), never imported,\n  required, or invoked by a script or test for its own work — tests reach them through stubs, and an\n  absent agent CLI is a dispatch-time condition the verb reports, never a failure of the skill.\n  The list is closed: adding an agent to it is an operator decision recorded here, not something a\n  document or a dispatch may assume. (`grok` added 2026-09-28, operator decision on\n  `grok-codex-fallback` design audit v2.)\n\n## Portable time bounds\n3800→- A time-bounded command MUST NOT be written as `timeout <s> <cmd>` or `gtimeout <s> <cmd>`.\n  **Neither is a macOS system component**, so the form is unportable in both directions.\n  A stock box ships neither and the call fails at 127; a box carrying `brew install coreutils`\n  runs it fine and the bound silently rests on a CLI h-mad cannot assume is there.\n  **What your box has is not an input to this rule.** h-mad already owns a time-bounder that\n  reachable wherever `hmad-dispatch` is, so the form is forbidden unconditionally — not because\n  of what happens downstream. (Reachable, not universal: where the wrapper itself cannot be\n  called the rule below still applies — halt, do not drop the bound.) Do not reason from a local probe to an exemption. For anything committed\n  or dispatched the form is additionally a new external CLI dependency (§\"No new external\n  dependency\"); a downstream 127 is how that eventually surfaces, never the reason.\n3810→  Do not settle the question by probing: a local `command -v timeout` that succeeds is not\n  licence, it proves only that this box has coreutils, and it means the 127 that would have\n  exposed the improvisation never fires. Use `hmad-dispatch run --timeout <s> -- <cmd...>`,\n  which exits 124 at the deadline.\n- Where a 127 does fire, its aftermath is worse than the 127 itself. Measured: the reflex after `timeout: command not found`\n  is to re-run the same command **unbounded** and call it \"checking directly\". That does not fail\n  at the deadline, it hangs the phase, and in every log h-mad reads a hang and slow work are the\n  same bytes. A plan, a design, or a prompt that leaves an agent no reachable time-bounder is a\n  violation; the correct behaviour when none exists is to **halt**, not to drop the bound.\n\n3820→## Doc-template superset compliance\n- Generated phase documents whose type is validated by an external doc-structure validator\n  (e.g. bkit PDCA: `plan` → `docs/01-plan/...`, `design` → `docs/02-design/...`,\n  `report` → `docs/04-report/...`) MUST be supersets that satisfy that validator's required\n  sections AND retain the existing h-mad sections. Dropping an h-mad section or failing the\n  validator is a violation.\n- The contract binds the **saved document**, not only the template it came from. Where such a\n  validator reclassifies a document by a substring of its *content* — bkit's `isPlanPlus` promotes\n  a plan to a larger required-section list on seeing `Plan-Plus`, `Plan Plus`, `plan-plus`,\n  `Brainstorming-Enhanced`, or `Intent Discovery` anywhere in the file — a generated document\n3830→  carrying that substring is a violation even though the template is clean. Guard it mechanically\n  (`scripts/h_mad_doc_shape_check.py`); the literals are unremarkable prose, so an author cannot\n  be expected to avoid them from memory.\n\n## Operator-override preservation\n- The `## Acknowledged-not-fixed` sidecar override mechanism MUST remain functional for all\n  layers (base + project). Any gate change that causes overridden items to still count as\n  blocking, or that ignores the sidecar, is a violation.\n\n## Backward compatibility\n3840→- A change to the audit gate MUST preserve the PASS verdict for audit docs that passed before\n  the change. Flipping a historically-passing audit to FAIL (absent a real new blocking item)\n  is a violation.\n\n## Marker discipline\n- Orchestrator phase transitions and halts MUST emit `[H-MAD]` log markers so a run is\n  diagnosable from logs alone. Silent state transitions are a violation.\n\n## Mutation verification\n- A step that mutates state (a git operation, a file write, a remote/CLI call) MUST verify the\n3850→  mutation by **re-reading the resulting state**; an **exit code is not evidence that a mutation\n  occurred**. A plan, design, or implementation that treats a zero exit — or an echoed success\n  string such as `Sent N bytes` or `{\"ok\":true}` — as proof of the intended effect is a violation.\n- The failure this blocks is silent and looks exactly like success: two `zsh` no-ops (backtick\n  execution inside `-m`, a leading-dash path) both exited 0 while doing nothing, and a dispatch\n  reported `Sent 7293 bytes` into a dead pane. Where a command reports on its own behaviour, the\n  check must read the *thing it was supposed to change*, from a separate call.\n- **When several mutations target the SAME line, vary one field and keep the anchor shared.** A\n  guard whose failure has separable parts — exit code, stream routing, message content — needs one\n  mutation per part, each replacing the same anchor. That shape is what proves a content assertion\n3860→  load-bearing: the mutants that keep exit and stream and strip only the text are exactly the ones a\n  returncode-only test survives. Two discriminators worth writing into the spec: a\n  first-vs-last-occurrence mutant survives whenever the sought item is last in BOTH regions, so the\n  discriminating fixture must put the decoys between the markers and leave the tail empty; and\n  `survived` has four distinct causes — missing guard, equivalent mutant, weak test, and a mutant\n  that never ran — which the verdict token collapses into one word. Diagnose which before acting.\n\n## Test discrimination\n- A test or guard MUST be **observed failing against the unfixed code** before it is trusted.\n  For a regression test, revert the fix and re-run; for a guard, stub it to its permissive value\n3870→  and re-run the suite. **Zero failures is a finding, not a reassurance** — it means the check is\n  unenforced, not that the code is safe. Keeping a check that has never been seen to fail is a\n  violation.\n- A check that cannot fail is decoration, and it is worse than no check because it reports\n  coverage that does not exist. Two guards shipped green this way and were caught only by\n  stubbing: one passed solely because an unrelated helper stripped the env var its subject read,\n  and a documentation test passed with the documented guidance deleted, because both of its\n  component words already appeared in nearby prose. Neither was visible to review or to a green run.\n- The mutation must itself be verified (§\"Mutation verification\"): a `.replace()` that matches\n  nothing exits 0 and reports the guard as enforced.\n3880→- **A criterion that cannot discriminate in EITHER direction is vacuous, and it survives both a\n  green run and a mutation battery.** The stronger case of the bullet above: not \"this check never\n  fails\" but \"this check returns the same observation whether the behaviour is present or absent\".\n  Measured on AC-2.5b, which spied on `is_cli_available` *returning* `False` — the forced-`True`\n  mutation emits an identical record and returns the same value, so neither the passing run nor the\n  mutated run could tell the two states apart. AC-7.5c was the same shape. **Neither audit leg saw\n  either one**; the revising author found AC-2.5b while fixing an unrelated must. A document audit\n  reads prose and cannot ask \"does this pin discriminate?\", so the question has to be asked where the\n  criterion is written: **name the observation that differs between the implemented and unimplemented\n  states, and if you cannot name one, the criterion is decoration** — delete it or replace it with a\n3890→  positive assertion only the implemented behaviour produces. Spying on a return value that both\n  states produce is the recurring form.\n- **Mutating a path-resolution function can disable the suite's own isolation.** Tests usually\n  isolate by pointing an env override at a temp path, and that override is honoured by a branch —\n  the same kind of branch a mutation deletes. Stubbing the override branch in `_pin_file` redirected\n  every pin write in the suite onto the developer's live session file and replaced two real agent\n  handles, while the run reported 642 passed. Before mutating anything that decides *where* state is\n  written, snapshot the real target and restore it, or run in a sandboxed working directory.\n- **A stub must model the step the real system CONSUMES.** The rules above are about asserting the\n  right thing; this one is about the fixture telling the truth. A stub that replays state the real\n3900→  system consumes once makes a test pass before the fix exists: an orca stub replayed an acked\n  delivery forever, so a sibling-cache test re-matched from the queue, pinned nothing, and passed\n  against unmodified code. Cardinality is part of the model — a fake that writes a path once where\n  production writes it twice let a verb never dispatch with 57 tests green. Before trusting a stub,\n  ask which of its effects are destructive in production and make the stub destroy them too.\n\n## Verifying a review finding\n- **A review finding has three separable parts — facts, concern, prescription — and they fail\n  INDEPENDENTLY.** Before applying a prescription, diff it against the RED tests and the spec's\n  acceptance criteria. A finding that matches the design doc but breaks tests means the DESIGN\n3910→  drifted, not the implementation. Measured: one pass's prescription broke a guard (1 failed, 53\n  passed) while its concern was entirely real, and another review's prescription was backwards.\n- Test a prescription by applying it as a mutation and reverting it. When two passes dissent, ask\n  what the codebase ALREADY does — twice the house pattern beat both readings.\n\n## Guard narrowing\n- When a change **deliberately makes a guard accept something it used to reject**, the relaxation\n  MUST be shown to be *exactly* the intended case: run a corpus of inputs through the old and new\n  logic and diff the verdicts, then account for **every** input whose verdict softened. A green\n  suite is not evidence here — it encodes the cases someone already thought of, which is the wrong\n3920→  population when the question is \"what else did this let through?\". This is the inverse of\n  §\"Mutation verification\": that proves a guard still bites, this proves a loosening did not widen.\n- The relaxation MUST rest on a **guarantee of the thing being parsed**, not on a heuristic that\n  re-implements it. Narrowing a scanner by \"ignoring quoted regions\" means re-deriving the target\n  language's quoting rules in a regex, which is how a bypass gets introduced; narrowing it by a rule\n  the language itself guarantees is safe. Verify that guarantee **against the real interpreter**,\n  not its documentation (§\"Assumption verification\").\n- Both halves were load-bearing when this was written. Narrowing a heredoc scanner so it stopped\n  denying inert prose, a 23-input differential corpus flagged one case as a regression; running the\n  shape through real `bash` showed the body genuinely never expands, so the *test expectation* was\n3930→  wrong and the code was right. Without the corpus that case ships as either an unnoticed hole or a\n  \"fix\" applied to correct behaviour — and nothing else would have distinguished them. The same run\n  confirmed the intended relaxations numbered exactly two, and that the remaining 21 verdicts were\n  untouched.\n- A narrowing is scoped by **which checks consult the reduced input**, not by the reduction itself.\n  Exempting the quoted region from *one* class of check while every other class kept scanning the\n  full input is what kept the change from widening; a blanket exemption would have disabled\n  detections that were still correct for reasons unrelated to the false positive.\n\n## Connection enforcement\n3940→- A task whose deliverable is a **connection** — a call site, an import, a registration, a route, a\n  flag or value propagated across a boundary — MUST ship a test that **fails when the connection\n  alone is removed and the callee is left intact**. A test that exercises the callee directly is not\n  evidence that the callee is reached. Shipping a connection with no such test is a violation.\n- **A whole-module revert cannot establish this**, because it removes both sides: the RED split\n  returns identically for a wired and an unwired build. The same blindness runs through every other\n  layer — a RED phase goes red because the callee is *absent*, an anti-gaming audit finds a\n  callee-scoped unit test perfectly discriminating, and a review of the diff sees a call site that is\n  *present* and therefore reads as correct. **Presence is not enforcement.** Measured across two\n  consecutive wiring tasks: each shipped its single load-bearing decision untested through every\n3950→  audit cycle and through the RED phase, and only a mutation scoped to the connection caught it.\n- Mutate the connection in **both directions**: remove it → the wire test must fail; force it to fire\n  unconditionally → the fall-through/negative test must fail. One direction certifies a connection\n  that exists but is unconditional. Verify the mutation landed before trusting the run\n  (§\"Mutation verification\") — a revert that never happened reports as a pass.\n\n## Incident replay\n- A fix motivated by a specific observed incident MUST be **replayed against the real artifacts\n  already on disk** that motivated it, not only against cases authored alongside the fix.\n  **Synthetic cases alone are a violation** whenever such artifacts exist and are reachable.\n3960→- Cases written next to a fix inherit the author's model of the bug, so they agree with it by\n  construction. A detector validated on 14 handcrafted samples rejected the real historical label\n  it was written to accept; the same replay then measured the true rate (7 of 13), which reclassified\n  the defect from a one-off into the majority case. Replay is how a fix is told from a belief.\n\n## Assumption verification\n- Every **load-bearing assumption** in a plan or design — an API's accepted inputs, a command's\n  output shape, a boundary, a default — MUST be **executed as a throwaway command before it is\n  written into the design**, and the design **cites the observed output**. An assumption asserted\n  without evidence, where evidence was one command away, is a violation.\n3970→- Design review cannot catch a wrong assumption: it reads as reasonable, and the implementation\n  and its tests are then both built from the same wrong model, so they agree with each other and\n  pass. Tracer-bulleting the assumptions of one feature confirmed a `--porcelain` boundary,\n  confirmed a base-ref chain, and found a truncation hole — all before any code existed. Separately,\n  a selector grammar assumed from a wrapper's own code was wrong in a way that had already let a\n  destructive verb run unguarded.\n- The evidence belongs in the document, not only in the author's terminal. A cited output is\n  checkable by a reviewer; \"I verified this\" is not.\n- **Attributing a failure to a cause requires the controlled PAIR, not just the repro.** Run the\n  case with the blamed step and the case without it, and require the observable to differ. A repro\n3980→  that reproduces confirms the symptom, never the cause. Measured four times: pane readiness was\n  blamed for an `injected:false` that a missing `--inject` flag fully explained, and that causality\n  shipped in a doc and a PR body; a title-only reading concluded \"no agents running\" and the\n  operator corrected it; and two carried repros were each falsified by a control that removed the\n  step they blamed. Re-run the MEASUREMENT as well as the claim — a brief's conclusion can be right\n  while its method is wrong.\n\n- **A contract to RECOGNISE something needs a real instance of it, captured from the running\n  system, before any gate scores it clean** — a banner, a log line, a response shape.\n  `pin-agents-tail-banner` passed 2663 tests, 49 mutations and 53 audit cycles while its matcher\n3990→  hit 0 of 5 real agent banners: all 12 corpus positives were written by hand. The fixture corpus must include at\n  least one captured sample with its capture command cited; an idealised fixture is not a sample.\n\n## Behavioural premises carry their command\n- A premise about **behaviour** — \"X currently does Y\", \"this guard fires when Z\", \"the suite\n  collects N\", \"the parser rejects W\" — MUST carry the exact command that produced it, inline and\n  runnable, beside the observed output. An output recorded without its command is not\n  re-derivable, and a premise that cannot be cheaply re-run gets re-READ instead, cycle after\n  cycle, by every reviewer in turn. Measured: one false behavioural premise survived **10** audit\n  cycles that way.\n4000→- Narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it fails at a different moment: there the evidence\n  was never gathered; here it was gathered once, written down as a conclusion, and thereby made\n  uncheckable. Both readers are then reasoning about prose.\n- **The command belongs in the document even when the answer is one number**, because the number\n  alone does not say what was counted. Measured: two readers measuring \"the same\" census got **3**\n  and **23**, having run different commands, and neither could tell which was wrong until the\n  command was written down beside it. `git grep -l '```' -- '*.py'` is the census; \"21 files\" is\n  a claim about one.\n- A premise whose command has become expensive to re-run is not exempt. Say so, and say what the\n  cheap proxy is — an unrunnable premise is a premise no later cycle can falsify.\n4010→\n## Counts a dispatch reports\n- **Re-derive every COUNT a dispatched agent reports; never carry it into a document, a commit\n  message or a verdict on the agent's word.** A report that is right about its actions can still be\n  wrong about its numbers, and the two failures look identical from outside — the prose is accurate,\n  the figures are confident, and nothing in the report signals which is which.\n- Measured 2026-08-26: an `exec codex` report stated an anchor sweep as `mutations=238` when the\n  tree held **244**, with every spec byte-identical to HEAD and every other figure in the same\n  report checking out. Nothing had changed; the number was invented. The cheap control existed the\n  whole time — `--check-anchors` prints the count in about a second.\n4020→- This is narrower than §\"Assumption verification\" and it applies at a different moment: there the\n  risk is an assumption written without evidence, here it is evidence that was produced, looks\n  right, and is false. Prefer a count you can re-derive with one command over any count a\n  dispatch hands you, and when a count is load-bearing for a verdict, re-derive it before recording\n  the verdict rather than after.\n\n## Wrapper–runtime reconciliation\n- A wrapper verb over an **external runtime's CLI** MUST be exercised **live against that runtime**\n  — a full create → list → remove cycle, or the verb's own equivalent round trip — before it ships,\n  and its **output-key extraction fixed against the observed envelope**. Shipping a verb whose\n4030→  response shape is known only from stub fixtures is a violation, however green the suite is.\n- Stub fixtures encode the author's model of the envelope, so the wrapper and its tests agree with\n  each other and both are wrong together — the Incident-replay gap, applied to response parsing.\n  Four occurrences: a create-response `.id` read from the envelope (a per-request correlation uuid\n  that always exists, so it yielded a plausible but useless handle) in two different verbs; then\n  probing a live response for a delivery-id field surfaced `run_required` — the whole structured\n  path had been **dead at its first mutation**, and no stub test could see it because the stub had\n  no concept of a Run binding. Later the same day the real ack key proved to be `deliveryId` while\n  the extraction chain led with `delivery_id`, and the only test covering that loop pinned the\n  spelling the runtime never sends.\n4040→- The live probe is what finds these, not the review: each was invisible to design review, to the\n  suite, and to a careful reading of the vendor guide. Where a live run is impossible (destructive\n  verb, no credentials), say so in the doc and name what stayed unverified — an unmeasured shape is\n  an open risk, not a pass.\n\n## Regression provenance\n- When a change makes an existing test fail, the plan or design MUST establish whether that test\n  **asserts current behaviour that the change is fixing** before proposing to edit it. **Changing an\n  existing test to pass is a violation unless** the doc states that the test pinned a defect (or\n  stale contract) as correct, and cites what it asserted.\n4050→- The reflex \"make the failing test pass\" preserves the bug when the test *was* the bug. Three tests\n  in one session asserted the defect as an acceptance criterion — a forwarded selector a live\n  runtime rejects, a create-response handle the pane never has, a cwd-relative path — and each would\n  have survived a naive \"adjust the test\" edit. A red pre-existing test is evidence about the test as\n  often as about the change.\n\n## Both halves of a doc change\n- Removing a documented instruction, flag, or capability MUST be paired with **the executable\n  replacement landed** (or an explicit statement that the capability is intentionally dropped, with\n  the reason). A test asserting only that the old text is *gone* passes for a deletion that silently\n4060→  lost the capability.\n- An unexecutable instruction was deleted in one session; a test checking only its absence would\n  have gone green while the run lost the ability it named. The gate is: assert the new path works in\n  the same change that removes the old one.\n\n## Reimplementation parity\n- Replacing a third-party dependency (validator, parser, formatter) with an in-tree implementation\n  MUST ship a **differential test asserting identical results against the original** across a corpus\n  that covers every construct in use, AND against the **real artifacts on disk**. Shipping the\n  reimplementation with tests that exercise only itself is a violation.\n4070→- A hand-rolled reimplementation is worth exactly what its differential test catches. A bundled\n  JSON-Schema validator agreed with the library on a construct-complete corpus and on the live state\n  records; the traps it had to match (annotation-only `format`, `bool` is not `integer`) were\n  invisible without the library beside it. Parity on a synthetic corpus alone repeats the\n  Incident-replay gap: the corpus shares the author's model of the reimplementation.\n\n---\n\n## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this verbatim as the **base** portion of the Axis B rubric, before the project layer.\n4080→Any finding that violates a base rule is auto-classified `## Must-fix` and cannot be downgraded.\n\n\n### Project invariants (domain rules for this repository):\n\n# H-MAD Project Invariants — Axis B (BrightGold70/skills repo, domain layer)\n\n> Inlined by `/h-mad` as the **project (domain)** portion of Axis B, AFTER the\n> workflow-universal `invariants.base.md`. The base layer already enforces the\n> universal rules (audit-gate signal discipline, single-source contract,\n4090→> no-plugin-dependency, doc-template superset compliance, operator-override\n> preservation, backward-compatibility, marker discipline) — they are NOT repeated\n> here. This file holds only rules specific to this repository (a collection of\n> Claude Code skills: `SKILL.md` + `references/*.md` + `scripts/*.py` + `hooks/*.sh`).\n\n## Skill self-containment\n- A skill MUST remain runnable from a bare clone: no import of another skill's\n  internals, no hardcoded path outside the skill's own directory (except documented\n  host install locations: `~/.claude/...` for Claude Code, `~/.agents/skills/...` for\n  Codex, `~/.gemini/config/skills/...` for the Antigravity CLI (agy), each overridable\n4100→  by its documented environment variable). Cross-skill coupling is a violation.\n  (Operator decision 2026-09-28, multi-host-runtime.)\n\n## Skill manifest integrity\n- Every skill's `SKILL.md` MUST carry valid frontmatter with `name` and `description`.\n  A skill whose entry behavior is changed without updating its `SKILL.md` contract is\n  a violation.\n\n---\n\n4110→## How agy uses this file\nagy reads this as the domain portion of Axis B, after the base layer. Domain-rule\nviolations are `## Must-fix`. If this repo has no domain-specific concern for a given\nfeature, this layer may legitimately add nothing beyond the base rules.\n\n\n\nEffort contract (binding on you either way; MEASURED only on some dispatches):\n\n- **Sweep the whole document. Do not stop at the first blocking finding.** Measured\n4120→  over 83 design / 74 plan / 34 impl-plan cycles on one feature: the median pass\n  returned **one** must-fix against a prompt asking for all of them, which turns every\n  defect into its own round trip and is most of why those numbers are that large.\n- **Read the tree before asserting what is in it.** The documents are inlined above,\n  which is exactly why a pass can look thorough having opened nothing. Their claims are\n  *about* the tree — a `path:symbol`, a signature, a test name, a count against its own\n  list — and those are the claims that turn out to be wrong.\n- **When you re-derive a published figure, four things must match or your reading answers a\n  different question. Each has been got wrong here by an auditor, not only by the orchestrator:**\n  - *Commit.* Presence at a sha is not provenance. \"X was introduced in revision N\" needs the\n4130→    needle PRESENT at N's landing commit **and ABSENT at N-1's** — two readings return N and\n    N+1 alike. `git rev-list <base>..<sha> -- <path>` first: if that commit does not touch the\n    file, it is not the figure's provenance.\n  - *Corpus.* **Run the command the document DEFINES, not one that reproduces the document's\n    number.** A head-scoped series verified whole-blob agreed for four shas and then did not;\n    the gating auditor re-ran it, reported it reproduced, and the scope was wrong the whole\n    time. Agreement with a published value is evidence about arithmetic, never about scope.\n  - *Grammar.* `grep -c` answers \"how many lines contain this string\", never \"how many\n    instances of this concept exist\". Print the matching lines and classify them before you\n    publish a count — a Python kwarg and a diagnostic field spell identically. Over markdown,\n4140→    collapse newlines and admit the inline-code delimiters (`` ` ``): a needle that cannot\n    match across a backtick reported 2 sites where there are 4.\n  - *Shell.* Take each control in its own invocation; a `cd` in a chained command is not\n    scoped to that command.\n- **If a document publishes a screen with `# expect 0`, RUN IT** rather than reporting that it\n  is present. Three sheet entries certified \"no scoped census moved\" over a commit at which the\n  document's own trip-wire read 8.\n- **An ABSENCE claim is a figure.** \"No corpus instance …\", \"the corpus has none\", \"nothing in\n  this repository\" — hold each to the same command and sha as a count; both that shipped here\n  were false. A zero bounded only by some *unrelated* constraint is a defect: the document says\n4150→  why it is zero and whether that reason is load-bearing.\n- **A stated PROPERTY of a screen is a claim about code — execute it.** \"No working-file edit can\n  move this\" was false of the shipped line. Do the thing it says cannot move it, and re-run. A\n  control over an ALTERNATION tests the alternation, never its branches: a healthy sibling covers\n  a sick one (one boundary repair was half-applied three rounds running and passed every time).\n- **Your report is held to this too.** Every count carries its UNIT (`grep -n` lines are not\n  occurrences); a population you prescribe is derived once and cited twice; and your PRESCRIPTION\n  is a claim about the tree — one prescribed naming an enclosing function for a line that has\n  none. Check it or mark it unchecked, and give every alternative repair rather than the best-\n  reading one.\n4160→- **A pass at or below two successful tool calls cannot certify a clean.** Writing the\n  report and its marker costs those two by itself, so at that floor nothing was read.\n  When your transcript is captured — an `audit-cycle` dispatch — the combiner enforces\n  this and scores the cycle `UNVERIFIED` rather than clean. When it is not captured,\n  **nothing measures you and the rule binds you anyway**: say what you read. Findings\n  count at any effort; this bounds what a *clean* is worth, never what a finding is.\n- **State your evidence** on its own line in `## Summary`: `Evidence: <N> files opened,\n  <M> greps run.`\n\nOutput framing (mandatory — the orchestrator extracts on these markers):\n4170→\nEmit your report bracketed by these two lines, each alone on its line, with\nnothing before the first or after the second:\n\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-impl-plan-v1-BEGIN\n    ...your report...\n    AUDIT-agy-coder-fallback-impl-plan-v1-END\n\nYour report is read by scraping this terminal, and the previous cycle's report\nis usually still visible above the prompt. The markers are how the orchestrator\n4180→tells your new report from that old one — omit them and the audit cannot be\nscored. Emit them even if you have nothing to report.\n\nOutput schema (exact, no other top-level sections):\n\n## Summary\n<2-3 sentences>\n\n## Must-fix\n- <issue> — <why it breaks invariant or creates a hard gap>\n4190→  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n## Should-fix\n- <issue> — <why it matters but isn't a hard gate>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n## Nit\n- <style/clarity issue>\n  *(empty section: write the single word `None` on its own line — NOT a `- None` bullet; a leading `- ` would be counted as a blocking item)*\n\n4200→Class rule (Must-fix and Should-fix): **every bullet carries a `class:` continuation line**, `build` or\n`measurement`, under the bullet and never as a `- ` bullet of its own (a leading `- ` is a second\nfinding). The test is one question: **would the code or tests a 5d/5e implementer writes differ if this finding were fixed?** Yes → `class: build` (an interface, a contract, a\npredicate, a fixture, a test's assertion or RED/GREEN count, a false premise about the platform). No →\n`class: measurement` (a published number, stamp, ledger row, sha series, pointer or self-count that is\nstale or mis-derived; the tree is unchanged by fixing it). An untagged bullet is scored as `build`, so\nomitting the line never softens a finding — it only costs the orchestrator the triage. Put the `class:` line directly under its bullet (before or after `quote:`); do not indent a sub-bullet between a finding and its `class:` line, which leaves the finding untagged.\n"}}],"rawOutput":{"type":"ReadFile","FileContent":{"content":"3308→readings it names. A suite test that read a probe file would break when the probe directory is\narchived.\n3310→\n### FR-9 — the carve-out stays prose; the corpus, the replay, and AC-9.4 (DO-7)\n\n**Applied by prose.** The verdict needs two facts: which symbol the task creates, and whether the\ncallee resolves at the RED base. The `WIRE:` value is free text: `h_mad_wire_pin_gate.py`\n`_parse_tasks` returns it as one unparsed string (probed at `29ae3e7b` on a sample task, where a\n`WIRE:` line naming two dotted symbols came back as a single `wire` string). So a mechanical classifier\nwould have to guess the caller and the callee from prose. The orchestrator therefore keeps applying the rule at 5d from the RED report, which\nmust name both. **Residual:** the corpus shows that the rule's **text** selects exactly\n`{new-caller}`. It does not show that any one 5d application follows the text.\n3320→\n**The canonical block.** It is held once, as `CANONICAL` in the equivalence test, and it equals\nspec FR-9's quoted block. It was extracted at `29ae3e7b` by joining the `>` lines and collapsing\nwhitespace:\n\n> A missing-symbol RED on the pin is valid **only** when the missing symbol is the caller the task\n> creates, and the callee resolves at the RED base. The RED report must name both. GREEN must prove\n> the wire by a wire-cut: delete the call only, keep the caller and the callee, and confirm the pin\n> fails.\n\n3330→**Placement of each copy.** Each copy is appended after an existing sentence, so every pinned\nliteral stays. Each of the context sentences below occurs exactly once in its file at `29ae3e7b`\n(Python `str.count`):\n1. Prompt RED item 1, after `Say which case each failing test is.`\n2. The prompt's WIRE-PIN paragraph, after `report that and stop rather than accepting it.`\n3. The SKILL.md 5d bullet, after ``halt `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>`.``\n4. The `failure-recovery.md` 5d row, inside the remedy cell's quotes, after\n   `only the failure mode can.`\n5. `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` `assemble()`, inside the wiring branch's `lines.append(...)` that holds\n   `The WIRE-PIN is this task's single load-bearing test.` The existing literal `\"symbol.\"` becomes\n3340→   `\"symbol. \"`, and the block follows it as further adjacent literals. `never a missing` stays.\n6. The prompt's GREEN paragraph, after `keeping tests green.`\n\n**The equivalence test.** It is new: `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_new_caller_carveout_copies.py`.\n- **How a copy is compared.** `collapse(s)` is `\" \".join(s.split())`. Each locator returns one span,\n  and the test asserts `collapse(span).count(collapse(CANONICAL)) == 1`.\n- **Locators fail rather than pass.** Each locator raises when its anchor matches zero spans or more\n  than one, so the test fails.\n\nThe six locators:\n3350→- **L1, prompt RED item 1.** The span runs from the unique line that starts `1. For each FAILING test:`\n  to the next `\\n2. `. It does not pin the item's number or position beyond items 1 and 2.\n- **L2, the WIRE-PIN paragraph.** The prompt is split on blank lines (`\\n[ \\t]*\\n`), and the one\n  block whose left-stripped text starts ``**If the dispatch carries a `WIRE:` / `WIRE-PIN:` line``\n  is taken. It does not pin where the block sits in the file.\n- **L6, the GREEN paragraph.** The same split, taking the one block starting `For GREEN phase (5e):`.\n- **L3, the SKILL.md 5d bullet.** `docsections.titled_section(skill, \"Phase 5 (Implementation) sub-steps\")`\n  is split on blank lines, and the one block whose stripped text starts\n  ``**The third task shape is `wiring`, and counts cannot gate it.**`` is taken. At `29ae3e7b` that\n  selection holds exactly 1 block. It does not pin which 5d list item holds the block.\n3360→- **L4, the failure-recovery row.** It is the one table line whose third `|`-cell, stripped, equals\n  `` `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` ``. The span is that line's fourth cell. The row holds no\n  escaped `\\|` at `29ae3e7b`, and the locator splits on unescaped `|` only. It does not pin the\n  row's position in the table.\n- **L5, the assembler copy.** `ast.parse` the assembler and take the top-level `FunctionDef` named\n  `assemble`. Among its `ast.Constant` `str` nodes, take the one containing\n  `The WIRE-PIN is this task's single load-bearing test.` The parser joins the adjacent literals.\n  This does not pin the literal split, and the mutation row is what pins that.\n\nNo span holds more than one copy, so a healthy copy cannot mask a sick one.\n3370→\n**The control.** The new spec `h-mad/tests/mutation-specs/new_caller_carveout_copies.json` holds 8\nrows, each with `symbol` set. For a `.md` file that is the enclosing heading, and for the `.py` file\nit is `assemble`. Each row's `test` is the equivalence test, and each row is run alone.\n- **Six per-copy rows.** Each changes `valid **only** when` to `valid when` in one copy, with a\n  `find` that starts at that copy's context sentence above. That context makes the `find` unique in\n  its file, which the harness `anchor_status` requires. The `.py` row's `find` is written against\n  the committed source bytes, including the adjacent-literal split.\n- **Two per-sentence rows**, on the `failure-recovery.md` copy, which is its file's only copy:\n  - sentence 2: delete `The RED report must name both. `;\n3380→  - sentence 3: change `delete the call only, keep the caller and the callee` to\n    `delete the callee only, keep the caller`.\n\n  Sentence 1 in that copy is covered by its per-copy row.\n- AC-9.4 asks for six per-copy runs plus one run per sentence in one copy. 8 = 6 + 2, because the\n  per-copy row for `failure-recovery.md` is also its sentence-1 row.\n\n**Residual.** The test shows that each copy carries the block. It does not show that no copy has a\ncontradicting sentence beside it, or that a seventh surface exists. A seventh surface held in a\n`str` constant, a comment or non-`.py` text is found by re-running FR-9's union sweep (AC-9.1). One\n3390→built at run time is found only by reading the code that builds the dispatch text.\n\n**The differential corpus (AC-9.2).** It is a new suite test,\n`h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_wire_pin_new_caller_corpus.py`.\n- **The five cases.** For each of `new-caller`, `missing-callee`, `both-missing`,\n  `unrelated-import` and `other-symbol`, it builds a fixture repository in `tmp_path` and runs real\n  pytest with `sys.executable`, recording the RED's exception line.\n- **Callee resolution is executed, never parsed.** A subprocess runs `import <mod>` and\n  `getattr(<mod>, \"<callee>\")` at the RED base.\n- **The missing-symbol role** comes from the fixture's declaration. The test asserts that the\n3400→  exception line names that symbol.\n- **Two classifiers transcribe the two texts.**\n  - `old_rule`: a missing-symbol RED (`ImportError`, `ModuleNotFoundError`, `AttributeError` or\n    `NameError`) is a halt.\n  - `new_rule`: such a RED is valid iff the missing symbol is the caller and the callee resolves.\n  - The test also asserts that `CANONICAL`, imported from the equivalence test, contains\n    `the missing symbol is the caller the task creates` and `the callee resolves at the RED base`.\n    That ties the transcription to the words.\n- **Pass:** the set of cases whose verdict changed equals `{new-caller}`.\n- **Control:** two rows in the same new spec, each run alone. One drops the callee condition, and\n3410→  `both-missing` then changes. The other drops the caller condition, and `other-symbol` and\n  `unrelated-import` then change.\n- The cases' exception lines and